    <node id="&quot;POLYURETHANE (PU)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A plastic material that T. molitor larvae can consume, with different variations such as PU1 and PU2 tested for degradation rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PU) is a type of polymer, the biodegradation of which is a focus of various microorganisms and insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a common form of synthetic plastic with a high environmental resistance but low biodegradability, presenting significant waste disposal challenges."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a polymer that can be biodegraded by certain insects as shown in studies with T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a widely used polymer characterized by its resistance to physical and biological degradation due to its chemical composition, making recycling processes complex. It is used in foam products and is difficult to eliminate, leading to accumulation in landfills or incineration."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a type of plastic used as a diet for larvae in the study, with biodegradation observed through gut microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane, particularly in a foam form, targeted for biodegradation in studies involving mealworm larvae species."&lt;SEP&gt;"PU is one of the three types of plastics being studied for degradation in yellow mealworms, showing specific depolymerization characteristics and different microbial responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PU) is another type of foam plastic tested as a diet for larvae, having different effects on larval survival and gut microbiome composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a foam plastic used in the experiment to study its effects on yellow mealworm growth and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane is a type of polymerized plastic that undergoes biodegradation with the aid of gut microbial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ETHER BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical links in polyether-PU foam that are cleaved during mealworm digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ether bonds are a type of chemical bond present in polyether-PU foam, which can be cleaved during the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ether bonds are component bonds in polyether PU foam, whose biodegradation mechanism involves oxidative reactions and enzymatic processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;URETHANE BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Urethane bonds are chemical bonds in polyurethane, including polyether-PU foam, potentially cleaved during biodegradation by microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOURIER TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR is a technique indicating formation of functional groups and chemical modifications during PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical technique used to confirm the biodegradation of PE and WC films by lesser waxworms."&lt;SEP&gt;Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is a versatile and powerful analytical technique used to obtain infrared spectra of absorption and emission from solid, liquid, or gas samples. This method is predominantly used to identify organic, polymeric, and occasionally inorganic materials by analyzing their infrared light absorption. FTIR is instrumental in detecting chemical changes and modifications in polymers, such as polyurethane foam and synthetic plastics, during various degradation processes, including biodegradation and digestion by organisms like mealworms (Tenebrio molitor).

FTIR helps in analyzing changes in the chemical bonds and structural properties of materials. It can identify functional groups within samples, such as carbonyl groups, that are indicative of depolymerization, oxidation, and biodegradation. This technique provides detailed insights into molecular composition and changes, highlighting surface changes and the formation of new functional groups during material breakdown. Additionally, FTIR can be used alongside other techniques like TGA to identify decomposition products from materials. Through its application, FTIR has proven effective in monitoring the chemical modifications in polymers and characterizing the functional groups of residual materials, offering comprehensive information on polymer degradation and transformation processes.&lt;SEP&gt;"Technique used to identify major functional groups in samples by observing spectral patterns."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is used to identify major functional groups in PS feedstock and frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical method used in the study to identify the chemical properties and changes of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical technique used to characterize the change in functional groups and molecular weight of MPs after depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is used to analyze the changes in functional groups of degraded microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a technique used to characterize major functional groups in materials through infrared spectroscopy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;X-RAY PHOTOELECTRON SPECTROSCOPY (XPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"X-Ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy (XPS) is used to demonstrate the generation of new oxygen-containing functional groups on the surface of HDPE film."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a process that examines changes in the chemical structure of polyether-PU foam, used to confirm bond cleavages during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a technique used to evaluate the changes in surface chemical components and functional groups during plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a technique used to analyze changes in chemical components and functional groups on the surface of the PS film incubated with strain YT2."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is used to investigate the chemical components on the PS Film Sheet surface by analyzing spectra over a broadband energy range."&lt;SEP&gt;"X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a technology employed to analyze surface chemistry changes in PS films during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS (TGA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A method used to characterize changes in the thermal properties of polyurethane foam post-experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is a method of thermal analysis that measures the change in weight of a substance as it is heated, used in studying plastic stability."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is a process that assesses the thermal stability of materials like polyether-PU foam, revealing decreased stability after biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is a method utilized to analyze the thermal stability and composition changes in PS and PE-1 samples following diet consumption by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is used to analyze thermal changes and study the pyrolysis of polymers in this biodegradation experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is used to study thermal properties and decomposition of samples, analyzing the thermal variation between feedstocks and frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is used to assess the thermal stability of mask materials during degradation in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is used to suggest the effectiveness of polystyrene degradation by Tenebrio obscurus larvae based on PS residue."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (GPC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chromatography method used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polymers like PS."&lt;SEP&gt;Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a laboratory technique deployed primarily to determine the molecular weight and distribution of polymers. It is a type of size exclusion chromatography that separates analytes based on their size, making it a valuable analytical method for studying polymer structures. GPC is extensively used to analyze the depolymerization products in various studies, including those involving plastic degradation in the digestive processes of insects such as superworms and mealworms. This technique evaluates the molecular weight distribution of polymers, providing crucial insight into the degree of polymer degradation and biodegradation processes. It has been specifically utilized to assess changes in the molecular weight of polystyrene and polyethylene, indicative of the extent of their degradation. GPC also measures molecular weight modifications in other materials like Styrofoam, polyether-PU foam, and PET polymers during experimental studies, including those on plastic digestion and biodegradation. Furthermore, it has been applied to analyze the residual polymer fragments, fecula, and frass, thereby enhancing the understanding of molecular weight changes resulting from environmental or biological interactions.&lt;SEP&gt;"Analytical method used to determine changes in molecular weight of polymers extracted from larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A technique used to analyze molecular weights in the depolymerization process of polymers such as PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is a technique used to analyze the molecular weight of dissolved PS materials, aiding in understanding biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is utilized to measure molecular weights to understand the extent of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is an analytical method used to determine changes in molecular weight distributions following plastic depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel permeation chromatography is an analytical technique used to assess the depolymerization efficiency of microplastics in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is a technique used to characterize polymer depolymerization based on changes in molecular weight distributions."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is used in the analysis of molecular weight changes and biodegradation of PS and LDPE polymers. It helps determine polymer characteristics before and after the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"A laboratory technique used to analyze the molecular weight distribution of polymers, including the depolymerization and biodegradation of plastics by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is used to characterize changes in molecular weights of polymers during the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC is used to analyze the molecular weight and size distribution of polymers like PS in the mealworms' frass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
