    <node id="&quot;DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A regimen of feeding strategies used in experiments to study the effects on larvae growth and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet in the context of this text refers to the dietary elements such as fresh plant materials and corn straw fed to mealworms to observe their growth, nutrient uptake, and microbiome responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet in this study refers to the different feeding conditions of mealworm larvae, such as pure PE, PE + bran, and bran, to observe variations in physiological responses and biodegradation efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"The diet mentioned refers to the specific feeding practice involving polyether-PU foam, affecting the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet is a critical factor in the text, influencing enzyme activity levels and concentrations among different organisms and conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet refers to the composition of food consumed by organisms, which can affect the microbial communities in the digestive system, as seen in T. molitor when fed polystyrene and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet refers to the specific feeding regimen of mealworms, including bran and microplastics, to assess its effects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet is a nutritional component in the study consisting of different substrates like bran and various plastic products fed to T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet in this context refers to the typical food intake of insects, which was altered by the inclusion of plastic, leading to metabolic stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"The diet refers to the feed given to larvae, which is supplemented with antibiotics in certain experimental groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the food intake of mealworms, significant in studies examining the impact of nutrient-poor, plastic-only diets on survival and fertility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet in this context refers to the different food and plastic combinations fed to mealworms, such as bran, polyethylene, and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Various diets are examined, such as a control diet with only bran and others including mixtures of bran and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet is a critical factor in the text, influencing enzyme activity levels and concentrations among different organisms and conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL COMMUNITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A community of microorganisms, including bacteria and other microbes, within the gut that is sensitive to environmental changes and antibiotics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A group of microorganisms living in a shared environment, interacting with each other and their surroundings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities in the mealworm larval digestive tract change depending on the diet and assist in the digestion process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community involves the diverse population of microorganisms in the gut of yellow mealworms, which changes due to a specific diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the community of different microorganisms residing in the gut of larvae, analyzed for structural changes based on diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"A community of microorganisms, including bacteria, fungi, and others present in the soil and environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community refers to the collection of microorganisms, including bacteria and fungi, found in the gut of A. diaperinus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community refers to the ensemble of microorganisms in the gut of T. molitor larvae that aid in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial community in mealworm guts is analyzed to understand the biodegradation of PS MPs, influencing metabolism and health outcomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities in the mealworm larval digestive tract change depending on the diet and assist in the digestion process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradation involves the breakdown of materials by biological means; in this context, it refers to the degradation of polystyrene by mealworms and associated bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the breakdown of materials, such as polystyrene, by biological means, including the action of microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the natural process by which organic substances are broken down by biological means, such as through gut microorganisms in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation is the process by which organic substances are broken down by living organisms, used in the study of plastic consumption by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;Biodegradation is the natural process by which organic substances are broken down by living organisms, particularly microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi, as well as insects and their associated microbiomes. This process is crucial in the degradation of plastics and is environmentally friendly, helping to reduce plastic pollution and recycle nutrients within ecosystems. It involves converting complex materials into simpler, non-toxic compounds such as water, carbon dioxide (CO2), and biomass.

In the context of PS polymers (polystyrene), biodegradation refers to their breakdown by the mealworm gut microbiome. Organisms such as mealworms, particularly Tenebrio molitor, can decompose plastics like PS and Styrofoam into simpler substances, including CO2, lipids, depolymerized metabolites, and biomass. Other insects like superworms, waxworms, and particularly lesser waxworms, demonstrate the ability to break down polyethylene effectively through their gut microbes or unique enzymes. Additionally, microorganisms such as Enterobacter hormaechei, Priestia megaterium S1, and Bacillus sp. contribute significantly to the biodegradation of plastics, including high-density polyethylene (HDPE).

The efficiency of biodegradation is influenced by factors such as the plastic’s crystallinity and environmental conditions. It plays a significant role in waste management and environmental cleanup efforts by offering sustainable solutions that mitigate the harmful effects of plastic waste on ecosystems. For example, Tenebrio larvae and their microbiomes have shown the ability to degrade polyethylene polymers, as evidenced by changes in molecular weight and oxidation of the materials. The capabilities of yellow mealworms have also been observed in degrading materials such as surgical masks. Overall, biodegradation offers an environmentally sustainable strategy to alleviate the long-term ecological impacts of plastic waste, leading to the destabilization of harmful materials and the generation of more environmentally benign by-products.</data>
