      <data key="d1">"A key metric used to evaluate the impact of various polystyrene types on mealworms by measuring their change in body mass over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass loss in this context refers to the reduction in weight of polyether-PU foam as it is biodegraded by yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass loss is an indicator of plastic biodegradation, determined through weight loss measurement techniques."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass loss refers to the reduction in polymer weight due to ingestion by mealworms across different regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass loss refers to the reduction in weight of polymers like PS, PSp, and EPS when consumed by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass loss is used as an indicator of degradation or consumption of the LDPE film by the larvae, a key metric in the experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reduction in mass of plastic substrate observed in experimental setups, crucial for evaluating degradation efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"The decrease in plastic mass due to consumption by mealworms, measured throughout the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reduction in mealworm mass is observed due to the consumption of plastics lacking essential nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass loss refers to the reduction in weight observed in both larvae and plastic materials after being subjected to experimental conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BOND CLEAVAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bond cleavage is the process of breaking chemical bonds in polyether-PU foam during its biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">Depolymerization is a chemical and biological process that involves breaking down polymers into monomers or smaller units, such as oligomers. This process plays a critical role in the biodegradation of plastics, significantly contributing to environmental sustainability by mitigating plastic waste. Depolymerization can occur through chemical means or within biological systems, including the digestive systems of certain organisms like Tenebrio molitor larvae (mealworms) and superworms. These organisms have demonstrated the ability to metabolize and break down polymer chains, such as those found in polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), and microplastics, within their guts.

The process is facilitated by specific enzymes that dismantle long-chain polymers into lower molecular weight products, sometimes independently of traditional microbial activity. Gut microbes in beetles like T. obscurus and T. molitor also assist in breaking down plastics, highlighting the potential of biological systems to aid in plastic waste management through depolymerization. Depolymerization is particularly effective in amorphous polymer fractions and is observed in the degradation of various polymers, including polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polypropylene (PP), polystyrene (PS), and materials commonly used in masks such as polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and polyurethane (PU). The rate and extent of depolymerization can vary among different types of polyethylene. 

Overall, this process is crucial for the biodegradation of plastics, reducing complex polymer chains into more basic, biodegradable forms, and providing a promising approach to managing plastic waste sustainably.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d5a9fbe4803ded646c6a5e4d23ca93a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76