    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL RESISTANCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Environmental resistance describes the ability of polyether-PU foam to resist degradation by environmental factors, which biodegradation aims to overcome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC POLLUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A global environmental threat resulting from the accumulation of plastics, requiring sustainable management solutions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic pollution refers to the accumulation of plastic products in the environment, contributing to ecological damage and challenges."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic pollution is the accumulation of plastic products in the environment adversely affecting wildlife, wildlife habitat, and humans."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic pollution is the accumulation of plastic products in the environment, causing harm to wildlife, marine life, and humans."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the percentage of mealworms surviving over the 32-day experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate indicates the percentage of mealworm survival under different feeding conditions, including microplastics and bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate is the percentage of yellow mealworms remaining alive after being fed a diet of polyether-PU foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate refers to the percentage of larvae that remained alive after an 8-day observation period under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate refers to the percentage of living organisms after an experiment, used to measure the success or failure of different conditions in experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate is the percentage of mealworm larvae surviving the period of biodegradation experiments, measured for different feeding treatments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate refers to the percentage of mealworms surviving under different dietary conditions in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival rate refers to the percentage of mealworms that remain alive over the course of the experiment, indicating their adaptability to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate measures the percentage of larvae that remain alive during the experimental period, indicating the suitability of PET agar gel as an energy source."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival rate is a measure of the percentage of larvae that remain alive throughout the feeding trials, showing the impact of different diets on larval mortality."&lt;SEP&gt;"The percentage of live larvae over the course of the experiment, used as an indicator of health and viability under different feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The percentage of mealworms that remain alive during the experiment, measured under various dietary regimens."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival rate measures the proportion of mealworms that survive from the larval to the imago stage under different feeding treatments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate is the percentage of mealworms that survived under different feeding conditions over a specific period, indicating the impact of diet on larval health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate refers to the percentage of mealworms that remain alive after consuming PS over a set period, influenced by the material type."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate is a measure of the proportion of organisms alive after a certain period, used to assess the effect of exposure to substances like microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate (SR) is a measure often used in studies to determine the percentage of organisms that survive under certain conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival rate refers to the proportion of larvae remaining alive under different feeding conditions throughout the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e16c2c8ec44f02a1180cfb4b9e4fb90f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tenebrio molitor is a species of darkling beetle whose larvae are capable of dining on Styrofoam and are important in the context of polystyrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor, also known as the mealworm beetle, is a species of darkling beetle used in research on developmental plasticity and polystyrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as the yellow mealworm or mealworm beetle, is a species within the family Tenebrionidae. It is primarily studied for its remarkable ability to biodegrade various synthetic plastic polymers, which places it at the forefront of scientific research in environmental sustainability. The larvae of Tenebrio molitor, referred to as mealworms, can break down materials such as polystyrene, polyethylene, polypropylene, low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), cellulose, and polylactic acid. This capability is largely attributed to the diverse bacterial strains present in their gut microbiome, especially Bacillus sp. PELW2042, which aid in decomposing these substances.

The species undergoes a complete metamorphosis through four life stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult. In addition to its plastic-degrading abilities, Tenebrio molitor plays a role in ecological balance by fixing nitrogen and serves as a significant model organism for ecological and environmental research. The larvae's ability to depolymerize microplastics using their intestinal microorganisms and enzymes underscores their environmental protection potential.

Research extends to exploring Tenebrio molitor's potential in waste management and understanding its physiological mechanisms, ecological interactions, and the detoxification capabilities of its gut microbiota. Furthermore, the species offers potential as a sustainable, nutrient-rich protein source known as TMlp, which could significantly impact global food security and sustainable agriculture. With its dual functionality in both environmental protection and alternative protein development, Tenebrio molitor presents promising contributions to waste management, ecological balance, and the advancement of sustainable practices. Additionally, the immune defenses of Tenebrio molitor are also a topic of study, adding to its repertoire of scientific interest.</data>
