    <node id="&quot;ZOPHOBAS ATRATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zophobas atratus, or superworms, are involved in the biodegradation of polypropylene, relying on gut-microbe activity."&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, commonly known as superworms, is a species of darkling beetle belonging to the order Coleoptera and the family Tenebrionidae. This species has garnered substantial attention due to the remarkable capabilities of its larvae in biodegrading a variety of plastic materials, including polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene, and Styrofoam. The larvae of Zophobas atratus exhibit an exceptional ability to depolymerize polystyrene into low-molecular-weight substances while maintaining a strong survival rate and a high capacity for PS consumption.

The biodegradation process in Zophobas atratus is primarily mediated through its gut microbiota. The gut microbiome of these larvae includes specific microbes such as the Pseudomonas species, which play a crucial role in breaking down synthetic materials and converting them into mineral forms. Notably, the bacterium Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 has been isolated from superworms and has been the subject of detailed study for its effectiveness in polystyrene degradation.

Extensive research on Zophobas atratus has been conducted to compare its plastic degradation efficiency with other tenebrionid species like Tenebrio molitor larvae. The significant biodegradation capabilities of superworms underscore their potential as a sustainable solution for plastic waste management through natural decomposition processes. Furthermore, studies focusing on their unique genetic material highlight potential commercial applications, especially in the context of global plastic pollution challenges. Overall, Zophobas atratus is celebrated for its contribution to biodegradable plastics research, microbiome studies, and sustainable environmental solutions.</data>
