    <node id="&quot;GALLERIA MELLONELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of greater wax moth larvae involved in the study of gut microbiome changes due to diet supplementation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella larvae, known as greater wax moth larvae, aid in the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella, or the greater wax moth larva, is shown to digest polystyrene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;Galleria mellonella, commonly known as the greater wax moth or wax moth larvae, is a species of moth whose larvae are notable for their exceptional ability to biodegrade plastic materials, particularly polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS). These larvae have drawn significant attention in scientific research for their potential in environmental sustainability and biodegradation. This capability is attributed to both the larvae's innate metabolic mechanisms and the presence of specific gut microbiota, including bacteria such as Enterobacter sp. D1 and Masilia sp. FS1903. 

Importantly, Galleria mellonella larvae exhibit a unique metabolism that allows them to break down long-chain hydrocarbons found in beeswax and plastics even in the absence of their microbiota. Their saliva contains enzymes that are critical in the degradation of polyethylene, further enhancing their role in plastic biodegradation. As a result, Galleria mellonella has emerged as an important model organism in studies aimed at developing innovative bioremediation strategies for managing and reducing environmental waste. By showcasing this species' promise in reducing plastic waste, research on Galleria mellonella continues to make significant contributions to the fields of ecology and environmental management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACHATINA FULICA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A large land snail species reportedly involved in the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina Fulica, also known as the land snail, can ingest polystyrene and expel microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a land snail that can degrade PS effectively without depending on its intestinal microflora."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica or giant African land snail is involved in the biodegradation and disintegration of expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, commonly referred to as giant African snail, is identified as a species capable of ingesting plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, a type of land snail used in biodegradation of expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, a species of land snail, engages in biodegradation through limited extent depolymerization of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, commonly known as the giant African land snail, is studied for its interaction with environmental pollutants such as microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, commonly known as the giant African snail, is capable of biodegrading and disintegrating expanded polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica is a species of land snail studied for its ability to disintegrate expanded polystyrene, illustrating alternative biological approaches to plastic waste degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of land snail that biodegrades polystyrene foam into microplastics through its digestive processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica, or land snails, are studied for their ability to biodegrade and disintegrate expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina Fulica, a land snail species known to biodegrade and disintegrate expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Land snails known as Achatina fulica have shown ability to digest PS foam, demonstrating a similar biodegradation process to mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE (PE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) is a widely-used plastic polymer known for its versatility, durability, and chemical resistance. It is composed of long chains of carbon atoms, derived from the polymerization of ethylene, with a chemical representation of \([CH2 - CH2]n\). PE is a petroleum-based, hydrophobic, and inert material, which makes it resistant to both environmental and biological degradation. These characteristics contribute significantly to plastic pollution worldwide, as polyethylene is prevalent in numerous applications such as agricultural films, packaging materials, toys, bottles, wrapping materials, and fluid containers. It accounts for approximately 26.3% to 29.6% of global plastic production, making it the most used polymer globally.

Despite its robustness, polyethylene's resistance to degradation poses substantial challenges for waste management. However, research is increasingly focused on exploring the potential for its biodegradation under specific conditions. Studies have shown that certain microorganisms and the gut microbes of insects, such as lesser waxworms and mealworms, have demonstrated some ability to chemically and enzymatically degrade polyethylene, leading to chemical modifications and reductions in its molecular weight. Notably, mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor) can incorporate polyethylene into their diet, providing valuable insights into possible biodegradation methods and pathways. This process can produce byproducts such as long-chain alkanes and oxygen-containing compounds, and species like Tenebrio molitor larvae exhibit the ability to depolymerize polyethylene variants, such as PE-1 and PE-2.

Polyethylene degradation research aims to understand these mechanisms and improve waste management strategies to ultimately reduce its environmental footprint. As studies continue, insights gained from the interactions of polyethylene with enzymes, microbiota, and environmentally responsive species could lead to innovative approaches for mitigating the ecological challenges posed by polyethylene. However, it should be noted that the degradation processes, such as those facilitated by mealworm larvae, can induce oxidative stress in the organisms. Despite these challenges, ongoing research is crucial for developing effective methods to address the ecological impacts of polyethylene.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5af82472112cbc9e76b1fd1ac6e8c54e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-463ac9dfa68274d35700c61208f4ea85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-843d5746868067bce1a2f15dfc1571e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) is a widely utilized synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer derived from the monomer styrene, with its initial development dating back to the 1930s. Recognized by brand names such as Styrofoam, polystyrene is a petroleum-based material known for its versatility and desirable properties, including being lightweight, strong, durable, corrosion-resistant, waterproof, thermally insulating, and resistant to radiation. These characteristics make it a cost-effective choice for numerous applications such as building insulation, food containers, toys, glass frames, plastic cups, packaging, and electronics. Polystyrene is processed into various forms like expanded polystyrene (EPS) and extruded polystyrene (XPS).

Despite its advantageous properties and an annual global production exceeding 20 million tons, polystyrene poses significant environmental concerns. Its stable carbon-carbon backbone makes it hardly degradable, contributing to low recycling rates and substantial pollution in soil, waterways, and oceans. This resistance to natural degradation contributes significantly to the global plastic waste crisis, presenting potential risks to ecosystems and human health.

Scientific research has increasingly focused on the biodegradation of polystyrene, particularly through the involvement of insects such as mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms (Zophobas atratus). These insects can enzymatically break down polystyrene into carbon dioxide and other low molecular weight compounds, aided by enzymes such as monooxygenases and laccases. Mealworms, in particular, have shown a notable capability for decomposing this carbon-rich, nitrogen-deficient plastic. Additionally, the degradation processes in Zophobas atratus larvae involve reactive oxygen species and specific enzymes, adding to the understanding of biodegradation pathways. 

Research is also exploring the role of bacteria, especially those from the Enterobacteriaceae family, in assisting polystyrene biodegradation. These studies aim to elucidate the metabolic, physiological, and nutritional consequences of polystyrene consumption by these organisms. However, while biodegradation processes can break down polystyrene, they also lead to the fragmentation of polystyrene into microplastics and potentially nanoplastics, thereby complicating environmental concerns further. Understanding these processes is crucial in mitigating the environmental footprint of polystyrene while maintaining its economic significance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98a6f770f1d3a35f8611c5f2516c1e08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16