    <node id="&quot;POLYPROPYLENE (PP)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of petro-based polymer, comparable but distinct from those like PE, PET, and others in terms of degradation potential."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another form of plastic found as microplastics in various environmental and biological contexts along with other polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP is a thermoplastic polymer used in various applications and can be biodegraded by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP is a type of plastic similar to PVC that is mentioned in the context of potential biodegradation testing by T. obscurus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a common plastic noted for its carbon-rich and nitrogen-deficient composition, posing a nutritional challenge."&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene (PP) is a versatile and widely used synthetic plastic polymer known for its durability, resistance to chemical solvents, physical wear, and atmospheric oxidation. As a thermoplastic, it is characterized by high molecular weight and long carbon chains, contributing to its persistence and resistance to degradation. Globally, it accounts for 18.9% of plastic production and is utilized in various applications, including packaging, textiles, plastic films, containers, bags, and surgical masks. Despite its benefits, PP is a non-hydrolyzable plastic, resistant to enzymatic depolymerization, which leads to significant environmental challenges, primarily through plastic waste and microplastic pollution.

However, recent studies suggest that PP can undergo biodegradation, particularly by insect larvae such as Tenebrio molitor (commonly known as mealworms). Experiments have shown that PP used as a diet for these larvae affects larval growth and gut microbial communities. Specifically, PP biodegradation is influenced by the polymer's molecular weight distribution and is facilitated by shifts in the gut bacterial communities of yellow mealworms. Although PP is consumed at lower rates compared to other plastics like polystyrene, the biodegradation by organisms such as mealworms presents a potential method for mitigating its environmental impact. Research into this biodegradation process is ongoing, as it offers a promising avenue for addressing pollution issues associated with fossil fuel-derived plastics like polypropylene.&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a thermoplastic polymer used in a wide variety of applications including packaging, textiles, and automotive parts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a type of plastic that has been associated with degradation by Enterobacteriaceae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PVC is a synthetic plastic polymer, difficult to biodegrade, composed of chlorine and carbon, used as food for larvae in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride is a plastic polymer linked to degradation processes involving the Enterobacteriaceae family."&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is a widely used synthetic plastic polymer notable for its exceptional durability, chemical resistance, and versatility, making it an essential material across various industries, including construction (building materials), electrical insulation, plumbing, and other industrial applications. PVC is characterized by its unique chlorine content and hard texture, which contribute to its extensive utility. Despite these beneficial properties, PVC poses significant environmental challenges due to its resistance to natural degradation, leading to substantial plastic waste and associated hazardous environmental and health impacts.

Recent scientific research explores the biodegradation potential of PVC, particularly through the ingestion by certain insect species such as the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, commonly referred to as yellow mealworms, and possibly other organisms like fungi and microorganisms. These studies indicate that when consumed by mealworms, PVC undergoes some level of biodegradation, evidenced by chewed edges and a reduction in the polymer's molecular weight. However, this biodegradation process also causes the most significant oxidative stress to the mealworm larvae, along with higher mortality rates and reduced protein content, emphasizing the potential adverse physiological effects on the consumption organism.

While PVC's biodegradation is challenging compared to other polymers and demonstrates a lower degradation preference, the ongoing research is driven by the pressing environmental implications of PVC's persistence. These studies highlight the complex interplay between PVC's chemical structure and the biological processes of the organisms involved in its degradation, underscoring the necessity for further investigation into sustainable waste management and innovative degradation methods.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
