    <node id="&quot;ACINETOBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria prevalent in polyethylene-fed groups of Tenebrio species, linked to significant ecological responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria that has been identified as potentially playing a role in the biodegradation processes in certain larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter isolated from red flour beetle may contribute to the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria known for its ability to survive in diverse environments, including its ability to degrade polystyrene under certain conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter bacteria, isolated from red flour beetle, involved in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a dominant genus in PP-fed groups, significant for its association with plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a dominant genus in polyolefin-fed groups, distinguishing its gut bacterial community from polyester and copolymer-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is identified as a dominant genus in PP-fed groups, indicating its potential role in transforming or degrading PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter is a genus of bacteria known for its ability to degrade plastics like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The genus Acinetobacter includes bacteria with adhesive capabilities to abiotic surfaces, possibly aiding in EPS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d</data>
    </node>
