    <node id="&quot;POLYETHER-PU&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyether-PU is a type of polyurethane noted for being subject to degradation by certain fungi like Cladosporium tenuissimum and Cladosporium pseudocladosporioides."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyether-polyurethane, a type of plastic foam used in feeding experiments to test biodegradation by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworms are the larvae of Zophobas atratus known for their gut bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;Superworms, the larvae of Zophobas atratus and occasionally referred to as Zophobas morio, belong to the Tenebrionidae family and are notable for their significant role in plastic biodegradation. These larger, worm-like larvae are of particular interest in scientific research due to their remarkable ability to degrade various plastic polymers, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polyethylene (PE), and polypropylene. This biodegradation process is primarily facilitated by the microbial activity present in their gut, which leads to the depolymerization and reduction of the molecular weight of plastics, effectively transforming them into low molecular-weight products and eventually CO2. Superworms are extensively utilized in biodegradation research, showcasing their potential in mitigating plastic waste and contributing to environmental sustainability. Studies have shown that superworms can consume plastic-based diets, surviving and performing plastic degradation efficiently compared to conventional diets. Their physiological responses to various dietary conditions and their gut-microbe interactions are important areas of study in environmental science, emphasizing their potential application in reducing plastic pollution. Overall, superworms represent a vital focus in scientific research, demonstrating a significant potential role in addressing the global challenge of plastic waste.&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms, the larval stage of Zophobas atratus, involved in the biological degradation of polypropylene, playing a role in environmental plastic reduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms, which are the larvae of Zophobas atratus, are known to biodegrade plastics such as polypropylene via gut microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms, larvae of Zophobas morio, are capable of consuming polystyrene, although mealworms degrade it faster."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac55379b7db07587354ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b79a227bc8e127f6b71fd2edb0e650a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SNAILS (ACHATINA FULICA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Achatina fulica is a species of snail capable of ingesting and potentially degrading plastics."</data>
