    <node id="&quot;HONEYBEES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Honeybees are insects studied for their potential role in biodegrading plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Honeybees are mentioned as one of the plastic-munching species, although specific plastic types they consume are not detailed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbiota in Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a role in the breakdown of microplastics, contributing to the biodegradation process by altering chemical properties such as functional groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms living in the digestive tracts of insects, critical for nutrient assimilation and nitrogen fixation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The community of microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of mealworms, responsible for processes like nitrogen fixation and plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Gut Microbiota refers to the diverse and complex community of microorganisms, including bacteria, fungi, archaea, and viruses, residing in the digestive tracts of animals. This microbial community significantly influences the host's health, digestion, disease prevention, and immune function. The composition and function of gut microbiota can be affected by various dietary factors and environmental conditions.

In particular, the gut microbiota of insects like Alphitobius diaperinus and Tenebrio molitor (commonly known as mealworms) are studied for their role in the biodegradation of plastics such as polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), and low-density polyethylene (LDPE). In T. molitor, specific bacteria, including Kluyvera sp., Pediococcus sp., and Acinetobacter sp., secrete enzymes and bioemulsifying factors that facilitate the degradation and depolymerization of plastics and paper waste. This microbial activity is crucial for their digestive processes, showcasing a unique ecological function in reducing plastic waste through biodegradation.

Research shows that the interaction of these microbes within the intestines of mealworm larvae significantly contributes to the breakdown of feedstocks, with changes detectable through DGGE analysis, reflecting the type of polystyrene consumed. Such abilities of gut microbiota in mealworms and similar organisms highlight their potential role in ecological balance and waste management. Additionally, gut microbiota are involved in nutrient digestion and detoxification processes, further emphasizing their essential contribution to the health and sustainability of their host organisms.&lt;SEP&gt;"Microorganisms living in the digestive system of larvae, which shift in response to plastic biodegradation and diet changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiota represents the complex community of microorganisms residing in the digestive tracts of insects, providing various biological functions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f488d8d4434d57d1a775a22333caa04b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7a49f1c1e76d6e987e0aa499923d3f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RECALCITRANT PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Recalcitrant plastics are types of plastics that are resistant to breakdown by environmental processes and require specialized treatments for degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Recalcitrant plastics such as polyethylene and polystyrene are resistant to natural degradation and require specialized organisms for breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms refer to the larval stage of the beetle species Tenebrio molitor, which belongs to the Tenebrionidae family. These larvae are widely recognized for their remarkable ability to biodegrade various plastics, including polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), polyurethane (PU) foam, polyethylene (PE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polypropylene (PP), and cellulose. This capability is largely attributed to the unique microbiota within their digestive tract, which produces enzymes and emulsifying agents that facilitate the breakdown and mineralization of synthetic materials. As a result, mealworms act as efficient bioreactors, enabling the conversion of plastic waste into simpler, environmentally friendly compounds.

In addition to their role in plastic degradation, mealworms hold significant potential as a food source due to their high protein content. They are investigated for their nutritional value, making them a viable protein option for poultry and fish diets. Mealworms' flexibility in dietary intake and their adaptive digestive processes are also noteworthy, as research explores how different diets, ranging from grains to synthetic polymers, impact their survival, growth, and biomass conversion rates. The gut microbiome plays a crucial role in these adaptive digestion capabilities, highlighting its importance in the mealworms' ability to transform ingested plastic into biomass.

Beyond these uses, mealworms are of interest in agriculture due to their excrement, known as frass, which has potential applications in promoting plant growth, particularly in cactus cultivation. Overall, mealworms are at the forefront of ecological research and environmental sustainability. Their unique biodegradation abilities and potential applications in agriculture and animal nutrition underscore their versatility and ecological significance.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;G. MELLONELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"G. mellonella is a type of larvae also involved in the biodegradation and depolymerization of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"G. mellonella refers to a genus of larvae used in the study, showing different plastic degradation pathways compared to other larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella is a species of wax moth whose larvae prefer pristine plastics over waste electrical and electronic equipment plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"G. mellonella refers to the greater wax moth, a species used in the study to analyze gut microbial communities and PS consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"G. mellonella, commonly referred to as waxworm, is involved in the biodegradation of plastic materials like LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Greater waxworms, or Galleria mellonella, are capable of degrading PS polymers, comparable to mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;T. MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">T. molitor, commonly known as the mealworm or the mealworm beetle, is the larval form of a species in the darkling beetle family. This species is a prominent model organism in scientific research, with studies focusing on its life cycle, growth, and nutritional needs, particularly under varying dietary and feeding conditions. Notably, T. molitor has garnered attention for its remarkable ability to consume and efficiently break down a wide array of plastic polymers, such as low-density polyethylene (LDPE), polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene (EPS), linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polyurethane (PU), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). This biodegradation capability is largely attributed to its gut microbiota, which includes bacteria like Klebsiella oxytoca, facilitating the transformation of plastic waste into less harmful substances. Studies have also highlighted T. molitor's potential in biodegrading other materials, like disposable surgical masks, through investigations of its digestive microbial communities and physiological adaptations.

Research examining T. molitor under DEB models has focused on the larval stage, specifically exploring its growth and dietary habits. It is capable of thriving on synthetic polymer diets, such as polystyrene foam and bran, while maintaining biomass, with the nutritional content directly influencing its biodegradation efficacy through mechanisms like depolymerization and surface modification. With the potential to convert degraded polymers into valuable products like lipids, T. molitor plays a vital role in sustainable waste management and mitigating plastic pollution, supporting ecological sustainability and innovative waste reduction solutions. Its symbiotic bacterial community not only assists in nitrogen fixation and polystyrene degradation but also positions T. molitor as a crucial resource in environmental conservation and biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea4f92fecc6ac