    <node id="&quot;TOLUENE 2,4-DIISOCYANATE (TDI)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"TDI is an isocyanate used in the synthesis of polyether-PU foam, influencing its chemical structure and prone to study in degradation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WHEAT BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wheat Bran is a dietary component reviewed for its composition and nutritional quality."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat Bran is used as a dietary supplement in combination with polypropylene foam to enhance the consumption rates in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is used to feed T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae in control experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;Wheat bran is a versatile and essential nutritional component widely used in experimental and rearing settings for mealworms, particularly Tenebrio molitor. As a byproduct of the milling process, wheat bran constitutes the hard outer layer of the wheat kernel, rich in dietary fiber and various nutrients, which makes it a favorable choice for animal feed. In experiments, wheat bran often serves as a control or baseline diet to assess mealworms' responses to various factors, including unconventional diets like microplastics such as polystyrene (PS) and polyurethane (PU) foams. Its primary role is to provide a natural and sufficient source of nutrition, supporting mealworm growth, survival, and plastic degradation. 

Wheat bran is frequently used in studies in conjunction with plastics to enhance the consumption rates of plastics by mealworms, facilitating research on improving plastic utilization and degradation by these organisms. It provides a standard for comparison against other test diets, such as polyethylene, allowing researchers to evaluate the effects of alternative feeding practices on mealworms. The nutritional benefits of wheat bran, including its high fiber content, contribute positively to the growth and stress response of mealworm larvae, promoting low mortality rates and enhancing dietary effects on crude protein and fat content in larvae. Additionally, wheat bran has been noted to potentially accelerate mealworm development, helping advance their life cycle into adulthood, which further confirms its optimal role in mealworm rearing and experimentations.&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a component of the diet fed to T. molitor larvae before biodegradation tests, providing nutritional sustenance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is used as a food source for mealworms in experimental settings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a traditional and more effective food source for mealworms, compared to a diet based solely on plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a type of feedstock ingested by mealworms, facilitating their digestive processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a dietary component containing carbohydrates, fat, proteins, and vitamins, influencing the gut microbiome of bran-fed mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a706f2bd66291a3ef925114ffd9300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Frass is the digested and egested material from mealworms, used to determine digestibility and the degree of polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the excrement of mealworms, often used as a biomarker of digestion and nutrient absorption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the waste generated by mealworms after consuming PS MPs, used in the study to determine the ratio of frass produced versus PS MPs consumed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass refers to the debris and waste products ingested by mealworms that contribute to their gut microbial diversity during starvation."&lt;SEP&gt;FRASS is the waste material excreted by insect larvae, notably mealworms (Tenebrio molitor and T. obscurus) and waxworms. This granular by-product consists of excrement containing partially digested and undigested materials, including polymer residues from substrates like polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE) foam, polypropylene (PP) mixtures, polyether-PU foam, and cellulose-starch. The frass includes both biological waste and exoskeleton residues and is analyzed to determine residual plastic content after biodegradation processes such as depolymerization and oxidation. 

It serves as a critical indicator in biodegradation studies and waste management practices, providing insights into the efficiency of larvae in breaking down plastics. Various analytical techniques, including FTIR spectroscopy, are employed to examine its chemical composition, structural changes, and molecular weight shifts, thereby assessing the effectiveness of polymer breakdown and plastic consumption by the larvae.

Beyond biodegradation, frass holds potential for environmental and agricultural applications. It can influence soil microbial communities, affect metal bioavailability, and be used as a soil amendment and plant growth medium. It has even been used for cultivating crops, such as dragon fruit cacti, highlighting its promise in enhancing sustainable agricultural practices. Overall, frass is a valuable resource in advancing environmental sustainability and optimizing innovative ecological and agricultural developments.&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is the waste material excreted by mealworm larvae after digestion of various test substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass is waste produced by mealworm larvae, considered in calculating degradation efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e155a60beeedb5736450fdc1ffa4699e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-915331bc1ae85fe139629eaebc4042cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f2303f3680e9313daac77bb7e27e7da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a61