    <node id="&quot;BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bran is used as a supplement in the diet, reportedly enabling larvae to complete their life cycle."&lt;SEP&gt;Bran, the hard outer layer of cereal grains, is frequently utilized in scientific research as a crucial component of mealworm and superworm diets, particularly in studies focused on biodegradation and plastic degradation. It serves as a conventional mealworm diet rich in carbohydrates and proteins and is commonly employed as a cost-effective nutrient source in insect feed. Bran plays an essential role in studies involving Tenebrio molitor larvae, influencing their growth, consumption rates, biomass development, and nutrient uptake. Notably, it is used as a control diet to compare the effects of plastic diets on gut microbiota and serves as a standard in experiments assessing the impacts of microplastic and plastic consumption.

Bran's dietary fiber content enhances the ability of mealworms and superworms to consume and break down plastics, effectively doubling the consumption rate of polystyrene (PS) and improving biodegradation rates. Researchers frequently use bran alongside polystyrene (PS) or polyethylene (PE) foams to boost the degradation efficiency of these plastics, impacting the gut microbiome and microbial diversity of the worms. Additionally, bran contributes to studies on plastic degradation efficiency and plays a crucial role in modified frass products. It is also noted for yielding the highest fatty acid content and providing optimal nutritional ratios in mealworm diets. Overall, bran's multifaceted role in scientific studies and its impact on plastic degradation and nutritional development underscores its significance in advancing environmental research and the life cycle management of mealworms and superworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf662c8e784d14a3a4dba2e89041aa4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e96b716e9400e9e77e099de5cca7c548&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) is a technique for producing high-resolution images to study the surface of materials, used here to analyze PS/CS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) is a technique used to observe the morphological characteristics and surface topography changes of PE films."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) is used for examining the surface morphology of materials like polyether-PU foam after exposure to mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscopy used to elucidate structural changes on polymer bead surfaces during microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM, or Scanning Electron Microscope, is used to assess and observe the residual plastics in frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscope, used for detailed image capture and morphological comparison of plastics and frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is used to understand surface topography and composition of the gut after microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscopy, used to visualize the surface alterations of aged plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM is scanning electron microscopy, employed to visualize structural alterations as evidence of PU biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM, or Scanning Electron Microscopy, is used to observe the surface structures of PS films before and after microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM) is used to observe microscopic morphological changes on the surface of PS films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscope, used to observe particles under high vacuum conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5193c909193ddd73442615b27f4f9be3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LARVAE" refers to the immature, non-reproductive form of insects that serve as the primary biological subjects in a variety of scientific experiments, particularly in the study of biodegradation processes. The research focuses on several species, including Tenebrio molitor, Galleria mellonella, Zophobas atratus, and occasionally Plesiophthalus davidsi. These larvae are used to evaluate the impact of different diets on growth, survival, and physiological development, with specific attention given to the protein and fat content of these diets.

In these experiments, larvae are fed various components, such as synthetic polymers like polystyrene (PS), polyethylene terephthalate (PET), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC), as well as natural feeds like wheat bran, oatmeal, and jelly powder. Notably, larvae demonstrate varying efficiencies in degrading plastics, with PET being metabolized more effectively than other plastics. The studies further explore the role of gut microbiota in facilitating the biodegradation of PS and other plastics, focusing on microbial interactions and chemical processes such as depolymerization during digestion.

The research also examines survival rates and developmental changes in larvae when exposed to different feeding treatments, including the analysis of reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels when plastics are combined with bran. This comprehensive approach provides valuable insights into how dietary changes affect insects' developmental changes and larvae's potential environmental impact as biological models in biodegradation research.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-355ef52836a4c27f1290730187a78818&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Mealworms are the larval form of the darkling beetle, Tenebrio molitor, which belongs to the family Tenebrionidae and the order Coleoptera. They play a pivotal role in environmental science and sustainability due to their unique ability to biodegrade various plastic materials. Mealworms can consume and degrade plastics such as polystyrene (PS), expanded polystyrene, polyethylene (PE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), polyvinyl chloride (PVC), polylactic acid (PLA), and polyurethane (PU) foam. This degradation is primarily facilitated by their symbiotic gut microbiota, which transforms the plastics into carbon dioxide (CO₂) and microbial biomass, significantly aiding in plastic pollution reduction and waste management.

In scientific studies, mealworms are extensively utilized to explore biodegradation processes by examining factors such as survival rates, polystyrene-specific consumption under different conditions, the role of their gut microbiome, gene expression changes, and biochemical transformations during polymer breakdown. Although they are capable of ingesting microplastics, mealworms require organic matter like bran for optimal growth, survival, and pupation, as they are sensitive to environmental stress during their rapid growth phase.

In addition to their environmental benefits, mealworms are being recognized as a promising sustainable food source due to their high protein and fat content, offering a viable alternative protein option for supporting food sustainability and security. Their lipids are also being researched for potential biodiesel production, underscoring their importance in sustainable practices. Furthermore, research on mealworms encompasses their feed efficiency, nutritional value, and development under various dietary conditions, highlighting their significance in the realms of environmental science and sustainable food production. Typically weighing around 98.3 mg each, mealworms are raised without hormonal additives or antibiotics and are also involved in symbiosis with nitrogen-fixing bacteria, presenting another fascinating area of study concerning their ecological and environmental impact.

Moreover, mealworms are studied within the context of the Dynamic Energy Budget (DEB) model to assess the effects of different food types on their growth trajectories and metabolism, further illuminating their ecological importance and versatility in research related to environmental sustainability and food security.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671dc96f9c648fdc6d21397c515d171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c319