;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) analyzes modifications in functional groups of plastics after degradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, a method used to analyze changes in the chemical structure of polyether-PU foam after passing through mealworm guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to identify changes in the functional groups of residual PS in frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical technique used to identify organic, polymeric, and, in some cases, inorganic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is used for analyzing changes in chemical composition of samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, a method used to analyze structural changes in the biodegradation of PU."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared) is a spectroscopic method used to obtain the infrared spectrum of absorption of a material."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) is a technique used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid, or gas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy used to detect specific functional group peaks in previous studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR, or Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, is an analytical technique used to identify chemical modifications by detecting functional groups like alcohol and C-O bonds in plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is used to identify functional groups in plastic residues from frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analyzes oxidation and depolymerization of Styrofoam in larvae guts, revealing functional groups indicating chemical changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) is used to identify chemical changes and degradation in polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is used in the study to analyze chemical changes in PS as a result of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, a tool confirming chemical modifications during the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANNIBALISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A behavior observed in mealworm breeding where organisms consume their own kind, impacting experiment results."&lt;SEP&gt;"An observed factor during the experiment that may have influenced consumption rates, where larvae eat each other."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism among T. molitor larvae provides a supplementary source of energy and nutrients when other food sources are insufficient."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a noted behavior among mealworm larvae, observed regardless of the diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Occurs among mealworm larvae, particularly under nutrient-limited conditions, impacting their survival rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"The act of consuming individuals of the same species, observed as a behavior to acquire nutrients in nitrogen-deficient habitats."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalistic behavior observed among insects, impacting the efficiency of plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is the phenomenon observed among mealworms and adult beetles during experiments, impacting specimen survival and mass."&lt;SEP&gt;"A behavior observed to increase slightly in the context of a protein-deficient diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism in the context of this study refers to the innate behavior of larvae in the Tenebrio genus to consume each other."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism occurs among PE-fed lesser waxworms due to a shortage of nutrients, affecting survival and growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism among larvae is observed in certain feed groups and is attributed to hunger or reduced aggression."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is observed among larvae as a response to nutrient deficiency, leading them to consume other larvae or pupae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior observed among mealworms under starvation conditions due to insufficient nutritional intake."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a behavior observed in larvae under nutrient limitation, especially noted in large-sized or mature Z. atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The aggressive inter-species consumption observed in Z. atratus larvae under nutritional limitations."&lt;SEP&gt;"A process where larvae consume each other under certain conditions, observed during larvae incubation periods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibalism is a noted behavior among mealworm larvae, observed regardless of the diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47c76e5149ac059b1960b533c9e99a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;N-H STRETCHING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Refers to a specific bond vibration identified in the FTIR spectra of polyether-PU foam, indicative of molecular structure changes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C-O-C STRETCHING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bond within polyether-PU that disappears in frass, suggesting it is broken down during mealworm digestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYURETHANE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of synthetic polymer foam, including both polyester and polyether variants, used in biodegradation experiments with mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane foam is a flexible plastic material that can cause epigenetic modifications in mitochondrial DNA across different life stages of Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRITION-LIMITED CONDITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A state of nutrient deficiency affecting the mealworm larvae, influencing behaviors such as cannibalism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL STRUCTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The molecular composition of polyether-PU foam, which changes after passing through mealworm guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANNIBAL RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the tendency for mealworm larvae to consume each other under specific conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cannibal rate is the percentage of T. molitor larvae that engage in cannibalism under nutrient-limited conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"A measure representing the incidence of cannibalism among larvae during the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polyethylene (PE) is a widely used plastic polymer known for its durability, flexibility, and resistance to environmental and chemical breakdown. With a 'C-C' bond structure, high molecular weight, and hydrophobic properties, PE is inherently challenging to degrade through natural biological processes. It is commonly found in products such as packaging and microplastics. Despite its resistance to biodegradation, PE has been the focus of extensive studies aiming to understand and enhance its degradability. These investigations, often involving insect larvae such as mealworms, superworms, and darkling beetles, have shown that PE can undergo chemical modifications and limited depolymerization. When ingested by these larvae, aided by gut microbes, the material's surface structure alters, exhibiting changes from flat and flaky layers to bubble-like protrusions. The studies also consider factors like molecular weight, density (notably higher than polystyrene), and additives within PE, which influence degradation rates and larval weight. Different forms of PE, including low-density (LDPE) and high-density polyethylene (HDPE), demonstrate varying degradation levels, with high utilization rates observed in experimental settings. While some metal contents such as aluminum and calcium present in PE may further affect degradation patterns, overall research suggests that under specific conditions, PE can be degraded. This highlights its potential for contributing to the reduction of plastic waste in a controlled biodegradation context.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78cd8d4684fa9e0519635dba9f124a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polypropylene (PP) is a plastic polymer characterized by its high crystallinity and resistance to degradation, making it less consumable and biodegradable under normal conditions. However, specific studies have examined its potential for biodegradation through consumption by insect larvae, particularly mealworms such as T. molitor. In these experiments, polypropylene has been one of the plastics fed to mealworms to assess their ability to consume and degrade it. Despite being ingestible, it is not efficiently enough degraded by T. molitor larvae to provide significant nutritional value, partly due to its composition of only hydrogen and carbon, lacking essential nutrients like nitrogen, which can affect larval survival.

Efforts to study polypropylene's biodegradation often focus on understanding the effects of insect larvae on its depolymerization, with varying results depending on factors such as particle size and physicochemical properties. Although polypropylene can undergo limited depolymerization under specific conditions, its degradation is severely inhibited compared to other plastics like PET, given its non-hydrolyzable nature. As such, polypropylene is often studied alongside other plastics like polyethylene, PVC, and PET to explore its potential for biodegradation.

Polypropylene's role in these studies is crucial for determining its environmental impact and potential for breakdown through biological processes. Despite its resistance, it remains a significant target for biodegradation research, highlighting the adaptability and effectiveness of insect larvae, such as mealworms, in plastic degradation studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Polystyrene (PS) is a synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer derived from the monomer styrene, known for its rigidity, gloss, and resistance to natural biodegradation. It is extensively used in the production of foam and packaging materials. Despite its low natural degradability, it has been tested as a potential source of mass and energy for Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms. In these studies, PS is used as a diet component in experiments with mealworm larvae to evaluate its effects on growth, energy production, and physiological reactions.

The biodegradation of PS has primarily been explored through biological means involving insects such as mealworms and microorganisms. Mealworms, along with other insects like Galleria mellonella larvae, have demonstrated the ability to ingest PS, initiating its breakdown process with the aid of gut microorganisms such as Priestia megaterium S1 and other bacteria. This microbial digestion is crucial for the degradation process, leading to significant reductions in the molecular weight of PS, surface erosion, and structural modifications of the polymer. During biodegradation, changes such as alterations in the polymer's cross-linked structure and an increase in Bromine concentration have been observed.

While the efficacy of PS as a nutritional source is doubtful, its consumption by mealworms and the accompanying microbial activity are promising areas of study for potentially mitigating environmental plastic waste. The degradation rate of PS is variable and influenced by its density and form, with raw PS being particularly tough. Studies examine the physiological impact of PS on mealworms, which includes moderate mass loss and gain over specific intervals. Researchers continue to explore the impact of PS on enzyme activity levels, survival, growth, and reproduction of mealworm larvae. Additionally, regional variations in metabolic effects on mealworms are observed due to differences in their gut microbiota compositions.

Beyond aerobic degradation processes, PS can also be degraded anaerobically through microorganisms such as LG3. Ongoing research into these biological degradation pathways continues to yield insights into innovative strategies for waste mitigation and the broader environmental impacts of PS on microbial ecosystems. Overall, understanding polystyrene biodegradation by insects and microorganisms holds great promise for developing future solutions in managing plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02f21478b87845df31740b3dab37d197&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4b03ca32b0efbf0ca6a3c8761c0d03d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-065b846c5f5f23f64023995cdb21c3db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc613