    <node id="&quot;URETHANE BOND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A link within polyurethane foam that is broken down during mealworm digestion, as evidenced by FTIR analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Urethane bond is the type of chemical bond in polyether-PU Foam, which undergoes hydrolysis when digested by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Urethane bonds are chemical bonds in polyurethane (PU) foam that undergo hydrolysis and decomposition, particularly under thermal conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BENZENE RINGS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL STRUCTURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Benzene rings are a component of polystyrene whose presence reduces during biodegradation in mealworm fecula."&lt;SEP&gt;"Benzene rings are involved in the structural aspect of polyether-PU, specifically indicated by the -C=C- stretching."&lt;SEP&gt;"Structural components in polymers, the -C=C- stretching in benzene rings is absent in polyether-PU after digestion by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
