    <node id="&quot;HYDROLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction involving the breakdown of a compound in the presence of water. It is a key mechanism in the biodegradation of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrolysis is a chemical reaction involving the breakdown of urethane and urea bonds in PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYMES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biological molecules utilized by insects and their microbiomes to break down substances such as graphene oxide."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are biological molecules that degrade synthetic polymers, influenced by the covalent bonds in plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are biological molecules that facilitate the biodepolymerization and biodegradation process within the mealworms, specifically targeting polymer substances like plastics and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are proteins that potentially catalyze the degradation of ether, urethane, and urea bonds in polyurethane foams."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes like hydrolase and oxidase are produced in mealworm larvae to facilitate PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes secreted by gut bacteria in mealworm larvae catalyze the digestion of complex substrates into simpler molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are secreted by microorganisms found in the intestines of insects, facilitating the biodegradation of plastics such as PS and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes like cholesterol esterases, proteases, and lipases are involved in the biodegradation of polyurethane by breaking down its bonds. These enzymes are studied for their effectiveness in facilitating biological decomposition of PU."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes play a critical role in the biodegradation of plastics, facilitating the breakdown of complex polymers into simpler substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes in the gut microbiome of larvae such as those from Tenebrio molitor play a critical role in the degradation of plastic substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are catalysts used in the synthesis processes required for the biodegradation and growth of organisms, such as worms in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes synthesized by lesser waxworms are necessary for both growth and the biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are secreted in the gut of larvae and contribute to the adherence and decomposition of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extracellular enzymes secreted by gut bacteria in mealworm larvae catalyze the digestion of complex substrates into simpler molecules."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e56bce8206396081e0b7dccc47a10c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TGA (THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A technique used to study weight changes in the samples as a function of temperature, indicating the thermal degradation of components like PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of testing method used to determine the change in weight of a material as it is heated."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is a process used for measuring the mass of a sample over time as the temperature changes, to assess thermal stability and composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is a method used to evaluate the decomposition and thermal stability of plastic materials, as well as the frass obtained from superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is employed to confirm biodegradation and oxidation by measuring weight changes in materials like EPS and LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is an analytical technique used to measure the change in weight of a substance as a function of temperature, detecting thermal modifications of ingested PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is an analytical technique used to measure changes in physical and chemical properties as a material is heated, used here to assess PP biodegradation products."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA is used to assess the thermal stability and compositional changes of polypropylene during the degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VOLATILE COMPONENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Volatile components refer to substances that contribute to weight loss in PU foam at certain temperatures during decomposition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XPS (X-RAY PHOTOELECTRON SPECTROSCOPY)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A surface-sensitive quantitative spectroscopic technique that measures the elemental composition of materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a method used to analyze the elemental content and chemical composition of surfaces."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a technique used to analyze surface chemical composition and functional groups, showing changes in the surface of HDPE after treatment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE REACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An oxidative reaction involves the loss of electrons from a molecule, contributing to the degradation of ether bonds in polyether PU."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMAL STABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The ability of a substance to retain its structure and properties at high temperatures, important in assessing polymer blends."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermal stability refers to a material's ability to retain its properties at elevated temperatures, a major focus in the thermal analysis of polyether-PU foam."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UREA BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Urea bonds in polyurethane foams are subject to degradation, often through enzymatic hydrolysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTER LINKAGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ester linkages in polyether-PU foam are more resistant to thermal decomposition than urethane segments, contributing to weight loss at higher temperatures."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DECOMPOSITION STAGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Decomposition Stages refer to the phases of thermal breakdown experienced by a substance when exposed to heat."&lt;SEP&gt;"Decomposition stages in polyether-PU involve chemical reactions occurring at different temperatures, indicating changes due to thermal stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT LOSS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure used to assess the extent of plastic degradation by PM S1 over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss in polyether-PU foam is used to assess the material's degradation during thermal analysis such as TGA."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the reduction in weight observed in mealworms that are exposed to microplastic feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reduction in the weight of T. molitor larvae, often observed when feeding on plastics like PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight Loss in TGA refers to the reduction in mass of a sample upon heating, indicative of decomposition and compound volatility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss indicates the percentage reduction in mass of a sample during thermal decomposition, as seen in TG/DTG analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reduction in mass observed as plastics are biodegraded by microbial strains like PELW2042."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss of HDPE indicates the degree of its biodegradation, measured as a percentage of mass lost over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss of polystyrene samples is used as a measure of degradation efficiency when inoculated with strain YT2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss describes the reduction in mass during thermal decomposition, indicating formation of new compounds in mealworm frass compared to original plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss refers to the reduction in mass of mask materials due to degradation, measured during thermal analysis in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISOCYANATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Isocyanates are reactive chemicals in polyether-PU that influence the decomposition temperature range by acting as a part of the urethane segments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be14442602a613e0ddfef5b30f6d023d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-C=O- GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-C=O- is a carbonyl group identified in the chemical structure of polyether-PU Foam, which undergoes hydrolysis during digestion by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XPS SPECTRA OF C 1S&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"XPS Spectra of C 1s are used to identify the presence of various carbon bonds in polyether-PU Foam, revealing functional group changes after mealworm digestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-C-O- GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-C-O- is an ether group seen in the chemical structure of polyether-PU Foam, maintained in reduced intensity after mealworm digestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALPHA DIVERSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of diversity within a specific ecological community, calculated at the OTU level."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha Diversity refers to the variety within a single sample, such as those in microbiome studies, measured by indices like the Shannon index."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity is a measure of microbial community diversity within an environment, such as the mealworm gut, often assessed using metrics like the inverse Simpson index."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity measures species diversity within a single sample of the gut microbiome, using indices like Simpson and Shannon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity measures the diversity within a single ecosystem or sample, indicating species richness."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity is a measure of species diversity within a community, such as the gut microbiome of mealworms, and was not significantly altered by plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity is a measure of the variety of microorganisms within a single habitat, calculated through indices like ACE, Chao1, Simpson, and Shannon."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity refers to the variety of species within a particular area or ecosystem, representing species richness in a community."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alpha diversity measures the diversity of microbial species in larvae gut, influenced by microplastic feed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMATACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of bacteria in gut microbiota involved in the degradation of plastics in the larvae of T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of bacteria found in the gut microbiome of mealworms, dominating when fed with bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of bacteria that are part of the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family under the order Entomoplasmatales."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a bacterial family in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio species that plays a role in the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a bacterial family present in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio species, with changing abundance upon PS feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a family of gut-associated bacteria relevant to the Tenebrio gut microbiome and shows changes in relative abundance with diet shifts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae becomes dominant in PP-fed groups, indicating its specific association with the PP diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Spiroplasmataceae is a bacterial family part of the gut microbiome in mealworms, contributing to microbial diversity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of bacteria that includes Enterococcus hirae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of bacteria that includes species found in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of bacteria within enterococci known for plastic degradation capabilities under certain environmental conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a dominant bacterial family in the mealworm gut microbiome when fed with bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of lactic acid bacteria, including the genus Enterococcus, known for its resistance to environmental stresses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that includes the genus Enterococcus, noted for their presence in digestive tracts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is another family of bacteria with considerable relative abundance in the gut bacterium of bran-fed mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a bacterial family within the Tenebrio gut microbiome, with altered abundance related to PS ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a predominant bacterial family in the gut microbial community of Tenebrio species, linked to the biodegradation process of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is another family of gut-associated bacteria noted for its changed abundance in the Tenebrio gut microbiome in response to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a common bacterial family found in the gut microbiomes of different feeding groups, indicating its fundamental ecological presence."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a common bacterial family found in the gut microbiomes of different feeding groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a dominant bacterial family, whose relative abundance is negatively impacted by the presence of microplastics in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcaceae is a family of bacteria that show decreased relative abundance due to the harmful additives and components in polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of gut bacteria involved in the biodegradation process of LDPE in T. obscurus and T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STREPTOCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial family predominant in the intestines of mealworms that were previously fed with EPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"A bacterial family present in the gut microbiome of larvae that degrades LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of bacteria including Lactococcus species found in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria whose relative abundance increases significantly in the gut microbiome of mealworms fed with polyether-PU Foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of lactobacilli, which are spherical and often chained bacteria included in beneficial and pathogenic strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of lactic acid bacteria that includes genera such as Lactococcus, known for their role in various decomposition processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of bacteria showing increased population density in the EPS-fed group."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria that showed increased abundance in the gut microbiomes of mealworms fed with surgical mask layers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of bacteria with increased abundances in middle-fed and inner-fed group mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is another common bacterial family present across various diet types in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae shows prevalence in various study groups, contributing to the baseline understanding of mealworm gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family whose relative abundance is possibly promoted in the degradation process of aged microplastics by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a family of spherical bacteria that dominate in the biodegradation process of plastics in the larvae's intestines."&lt;SEP&gt;"Streptococcaceae is a bacterial family that was most abundant in the PS-L group of mealworms, indicating preference for low MW PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTERIACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial family found in large proportions in mealworm intestines when fed EPS in a previous study."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of gut bacteria found in T. obscurus and T. molitor larvae, associated with the biodegradation of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;Enterobacteriaceae is a large and diverse family of Gram-negative bacteria, notable for its significant role in the degradation of various plastics, including polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyether polyurethane (PU) foam, and other microplastics. This bacterial family encompasses a variety of genera, such as Escherichia, Shigella, Salmonella, Citrobacter, Kosakonia, and Proteus. Enterobacteriaceae is particularly prevalent in the gut microbiomes of insect larvae, including superworms, yellow mealworms, Tenebrio obscurus, and Tenebrio molitor. Research indicates that Enterobacteriaceae's relative abundance notably increases in these insects when they consume diets rich in plastics, such as PS, PU foam, and even components like surgical mask layers, compared to traditional diets. Unclassified members of Enterobacteriaceae are dominant in the gut microbiomes of larvae fed with PS, clearly marking their pivotal role in biodegradation. Additionally, Enterobacter hormaechei, a species within this family, is known to interact with microplastics in the intestinal tract. The significant proliferation of Enterobacteriaceae in conditions where larvae consume plastics underscores their crucial role in both environmental and intestinal microbial ecology, making them essential for advancing our understanding of bacterial involvement in plastic degradation processes and the ecological dynamics of insect larvae gut environments.&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriaceae is a family within the class Gammaproteobacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriaceae is one of the bacterial families classified under Gammaproteobacteria in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriaceae is a bacterial family associated with plastic biodegradation and was most abundant in the PS-M group."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AEROMONAS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A microorganism suggested to grow on polystyrene, although not observed in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aeromonas sp. is a bacterial strain from the Enterobacteriaceae family that can grow on PS and is found in the guts of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aeromonas sp. is a bacterial strain isolated from the guts of mealworms, capable of growing on PS and belonging to Enterobacteriaceae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aeromonas is a genus of bacteria that contributes to polystyrene degradation in the mealworm gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KLEBSIELLA PNEUMONIAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium within the Enterobacteriaceae family found in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a bacterial strain capable of growing on PS and present in the Enterobacteriaceae family within mealworm guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a bacterium found in the guts of mealworms capable of growing on PS, belonging to Enterobacteriaceae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species known to associate with EPS but not identified in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a gram-negative bacterium related to Klebsiella oxytoca, used as a reference in comparative studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a nitrogen-fixing bacterium found in the guts of certain insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a bacterium from mealworms involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b628b0a97e61f2c75e67585a9b07fc71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A technique used for obtaining an infrared spectrum of absorption, emission, or photoconductivity of solid materials like polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"A technique used to analyze frass in the study to determine the composition and changes after biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Analysis is a technique used to confirm the chemical changes in polyether-PU Foam, supporting findings from XPS analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR, or Fourier-Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, is used to analyze chemical modifications in PS by observing characteristic peaks in frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Analysis is used to reveal chemical alterations in PVC during the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis is a scientific method used to identify functional groups formed during hydrocarbon degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared) analysis is used to identify chemical bonds and functional groups in materials by measuring infrared absorption."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Analysis is a technique used to assess chemical changes, used here to demonstrate oxidation and structural changes in PET during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A technique used to confirm the biodegradation of PE by detecting the presence of new chemical groups in the egested frass of A. grisea larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis is a technique used to study the chemical modifications in the waxworm gut contributing to biodegradation, by observing changes in the intensity of native bonds."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) is a technique used to obtain the IR spectrum of absorption, helping confirm the oxidation and biodegradation of polyethylene types."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis is a spectroscopic technique used to identify chemical changes in materials like polystyrene foam mediated by bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis, or Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, is a method employed to assess the oxidation of PS films when exposed to bacterial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis is the process of using infrared spectroscopy to study and obtain spectra of various materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR analysis refers to Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, a method used to identify chemical bonds and characteristics in materials undergoing biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analysis is used to provide evidence of oxidation and depolymerization of LDPE foams."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS (POLYSTYRENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another common plastic polymer that undergoes transformation through biodegradation processes facilitated by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is a type of plastic material that is targeted for biodegradation by certain microorganisms like mealworms' gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene (PS) is a type of plastic whose biodegradation is studied using bacterial strains like LG3 and SCGB1, under anaerobic and aerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene (PS) is a synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer derived from the monomer styrene. It is commonly used in various consumer products, including electronic packaging, food containers, foam, and protective packaging due to its lightweight and excellent insulation properties. Comprised predominantly of 96% polystyrene with additional components like talc, calcium stearate, and butane, PS is a hard plastic notorious for being challenging to decompose, often contributing to microplastic pollution due to its durability and environmental persistence.

Recent research has focused on the potential biodegradation of PS through its consumption by insects, particularly mealworms (T. molitor larvae) and superworms. Studies have demonstrated that PS can be partially biodegraded by mealworms, aided by specific gut microbes which transform the polymer into biomass while inducing mass loss, surface alterations, and biochemical changes indicative of mineralization and conversion to byproducts such as styrene and CO₂. This process also produces mealworm frass, which retains some residual PS content, yet shows a potential for further post-digestive degradation.

Among the five types of plastics tested for consumption by mealworms, polystyrene exhibited the highest consumption rate, making it a focal point in scientific studies exploring environmental sustainability and innovative waste management solutions. The exploration of PS as a potential substrate in the larvae diet aims to understand the changes in molecular weight, chemical structure, and the overall biodegradation process, offering insights into enhancing sustainable waste management practices.&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is a type of plastic polymer analyzed for biodegradation when processed by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS or Polystyrene is a type of plastic foam that was fed to mealworms in this study for degradation assessment."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is synthetic plastic tested with mealworms, observed to be decomposed by microorganisms in the larvae's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS, or polystyrene, is a synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer used as a substrate in feeding mealworm larvae, impacting gut microbiota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d99a98d885ec6495c4dc902866c4ab9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e38968f22c4cd9bef3f1562e4ca47e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE (POLYETHYLENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A common plastic polymer whose molecular weight and properties change after biodegradation by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A common type of plastic polymer used in numerous applications, from bags to bottles."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE, or Polyethylene, is another type of plastic that has been analyzed for biodegradation, akin to polyether-PU Foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by superworms and yellow mealworms. During this process, its molecular weights decrease significantly through microbial activity in the larval gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is another type of plastic that mealworms have been observed to consume, indicating their adaptability to different plastic types."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene is another type of plastic experiencing biodegradation when exposed to microorganisms' enzymatic activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene, a type of plastic potentially subject to degradation similar to PS by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a type of plastic that can undergo biodegradation by microbial communities in the environment into carbon dioxide and water."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene (PE) is a type of plastic widely used in mulching films and other applications, known for its high molecular weight and resistance to degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE, or polyethylene, is a type of plastic film used in experiments, comprised of 70% PE and 30% starch with a molecular weight less than 10,000."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a nitrogen-free, carbon-rich polymer whose degradation is studied in larvae fed with different types."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE, or Polyethylene, is another type of plastic tested for consumption by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is a type of plastic that Aspergillus flavus PEDX3, isolated from Galleria mellonella larvae, is capable of degrading."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE or Polyethylene is a type of plastic with strong chemical bonds that can be degraded by certain organisms such as A. grisella larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE stands for polyethylene, another type of plastic that certain gut bacteria can degrade, related to the study on PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene is a widely used plastic polymer known for its stability and resistance to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE is another type of plastic polymer examined for biodegradation after being processed by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE or Polyethylene is a kind of plastic foam also used in the study to evaluate its breakdown by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-490fdb90ad8a65d35f3c2cd42a8c4c8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIMPSON INDEX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diversity index used to estimate species richness in samples, reflecting the probability that two individuals randomly selected belong to different species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Simpson Index is a metric used to measure the diversity and evenness of species within a microbial community."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson Index is a diversity index used to evaluate the dominance and richness of bacterial species in the gut microbiome of organisms under different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson index measures the dominance and evenness of different species within the gut microbiome of T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson Index measures the diversity of species in a community, providing insight into diet-induced variation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Simpson index indicates the probability that two individuals randomly selected from a sample will belong to the same species, providing insights into microbial dominance."&lt;SEP&gt;"An index that acts as a diversity measure assessing the probability that two individuals randomly selected from a sample belong to the same species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SHANNON INDEX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diversity index that measures the biodiversity of a community; in this context, it compares the microbial diversity in bran-fed and EPS-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"A measure of diversity in ecological studies, indicating species diversity in the gut microbiome analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon Index is another metric for evaluating species richness in a microbial community, often used in diversity studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon Index measures the diversity of species in a community, used here to assess the changes in bacterial richness in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index is an ecological metric used to measure the diversity and evenness of gut microbial communities in T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon index is a measure of diversity used in this study to assess different microbial treatment groups in T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon Index is a metric used to quantify the diversity within a microbial community, varying with diet changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shannon index is a measure of species richness and diversity within the gut microbiome of the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A measure of species diversity in a community, used in analyzing microbiome complexity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index is a measure of microbial diversity used to assess the richness and evenness of species in the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index is a metric used to summarize the diversity within the microbiome samples by quantifying the entropy."&lt;SEP&gt;"A diversity index used to estimate the bacterial and fungal diversity in environmental samples, with higher values indicating greater diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"A measure of biodiversity that quantifies species diversity within fungal communities of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon Index is a measure of diversity within the gut microbiome of mealworms under various feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Shannon index is used to evaluate the richness and diversity of the gut microbiome of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88706f91768bcf0eaa7a49ef2d4b771f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRAY-CURTIS DISSIMILARITY INDEX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bray-Curtis Dissimilarity Index is used in PCoA to differentiate between gut microbiomes of mealworms fed with different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Bray-Curtis dissimilarity index is used to measure community structure shifts in gut microbiomes due to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Bray-Curtis dissimilarity index is used in ecological studies to quantify the compositional dissimilarity between different sets of samples, applied here to microbial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PRINCIPAL COORDINATE ANALYSIS (PCOA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PCoA is a method for visualizing and analyzing differences in the gut microbiomes of mealworms based on diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is a statistical method used to visualize similarities and differences in microbial community structures."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is used to analyze microbial composition and demonstrate distinctions among diet groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is an analytical technique used to visualize similarities or differences in microbial communities based on specific distance matrices."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA was used to investigate changes in microbial community structure at the OTU level."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal Coordinate Analysis (PCoA) is a method used to visualize differences in the microbial community structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCoA is a method used to compare and visualize differences in gut microbial communities of mealworms based on diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal Coordinate Analysis is a method used to visualize differences in the gut microbiome composition among mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a6517509e242b0396ba75e2d6e09a1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df147b253e29abfa812d060407051e45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40</data>
    </node>
