    <node id="&quot;COMAMONAS ACIDOVORANS TB-35&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium known for its ability to degrade polyester urethane, as studied by Akutsu et al."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNGI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGI"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A group of eukaryotic organisms, including certain species used in the biodegradation of polyurethane foams."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi are microorganisms that play a significant role in the degradation of polyester polyurethane in soil environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi are microorganisms within the microbiome, impacted by changes in diet and surroundings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi have potential for degrading petroleum-based polymers, contributing to understanding biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi, such as Aspergillus species, play a vital role in the biodegradation of various synthetic polymers, aiding in environmental sustainability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Various environmental fungi known for their biodegradation activities on polyester urethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Non-symbiotic Endophytic Fungi colonizing grass species for ecological evaluation and phylogenetic analysis, contributing to plant health and soil ecology."&lt;SEP&gt;"Various fungi species have been studied for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene, though their capabilities appear limited in axenic and mixed microbial cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi are eukaryotic organisms that include microorganisms such as yeasts and molds, often associated with insects as endosymbionts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi in the yellow mealworm gut are influenced by the consumption of different layers of surgical masks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi are a group of organisms capable of degrading PVC plastic, indicating their potential role in plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi represent one of the components of the gut microbiome involved in plastic biodegradation in insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Certain free-living fungi have been described as efficient in degrading synthetic polymers and possibly contributing to polystyrene biodegradation in insect guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi are a group of organisms analyzed for their presence and abundance in polystyrene and control samples, showing notable differences in taxa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungi are another part of the microbial community in the gut of A. diaperinus larvae, with changes in presence when larvae are reared on polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2741fab96df80d452d2d4cc651730521&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER URETHANE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"POLYMER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of urethane used in various products, known for being biodegradable by certain microbes and fungi."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-165679126b891785de849b020c1548f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene is a plastic whose degradation in mealworms is uncertain and questioned for energy conversion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene is a synthetic aromatic polymer made from the monomer styrene, commonly used in various consumer products, and subject to biodegradation research."&lt;SEP&gt;Polystyrene is a synthetic aromatic hydrocarbon polymer made from the monomer styrene, known for its high purity, high molecular weight (approximately 345,000), remarkable structural stability, and excellent insulating properties. It is widely used across a variety of consumer and industrial products, including disposable cutlery, food containers, foam products, insulation materials, plastic models, CD and DVD cases, smoke detector housings, and as the foundational material for Expanded Polystyrene (EPS). Its long hydrocarbon chains make it a preferred choice for EPS buoys and various packaging materials.

Despite its versatility and extensive use, polystyrene poses significant environmental challenges due to its durability and resistance to natural biodegradation, contributing notably to microplastic pollution and complicating plastic waste management. However, ongoing research has identified potential biological pathways for the biodegradation of polystyrene. Certain microorganisms, including bacteria like Klebsiella oxytoca, and insect larvae such as Tenebrio molitor (mealworms), Zophobas atratus, and Galleria mellonella (Greater Wax Moth), as well as some fungi, have demonstrated the ability to break down polystyrene. This biodegradation is facilitated by the gut microbiomes and enzymes of these organisms, which metabolize polystyrene into fragments of low molecular weight.

Polystyrene is utilized as a substrate in experimental settings to explore its biodegradation potential and its role as an unconventional feed source for mealworm larvae. Although it offers minimal nutritional content and results in high larval mortality, once mineralized, it provides energy. These findings highlight the importance of polystyrene in scientific investigations concerning biodegradation pathways and the development of innovative environmental management strategies. Consequently, while polystyrene's resistance to degradation presents environmental obstacles, it offers potential for pioneering waste management solutions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c1bb645805a7158e2e18e95313565f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51daa29515cc5a431055291627bcb199&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8bb63b06976c3a8e6f2cea85c1ecf518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-372aa232f6e8aad1d8e0a93f16a42b0c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-165679126b891