    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworm (Zophobas atratus larva) participates in the study of plastic degradation, including PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is an insect species whose nutrient composition shows little change when fed Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm larvae, scientifically known as Zophobas atratus, are used in experiments to test plastic degradation effects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm refers to the larvae of Zophobas atratus, known for harboring bacteria capable of biodegrading polymers like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm, the larval form of Zophobas atratus, is recognized for its potential to biodegrade synthetic plastics like polystyrene and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are larvae used in the study to measure PS consumption and their frass is analyzed for its impact on cacti growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is the larval stage of Zophobas atratus, known for its capacity to biodegrade polystyrene and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm larvae are involved in experiments concerning the ingestion of different diets, including plastics, and show a higher plastic consumption rate than yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are the larvae of the Zophobas atratus beetle, known for their ability to biodegrade various plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm is one of the species observed in the study, whose gut microbiome includes Enterococcus, Enterobacteriaceae, Kluyvera, and Lactococcus; these microorganisms are involved in digesting polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm larvae have intestinal microorganisms with a high ability to degrade polystyrene (PS) into low-molecular-weight substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm refers to the larvae of Zophobas atratus, noted for their strong capacity to degrade polystyrene into low-molecular-weight substances and high survival rate on a PS diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworms are a type of larvae capable of feeding on polystyrene (PS) and are known for their high PS consumption rate and significant body weight changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superworm, scientifically known as Zophobas atratus, is studied for its plastic ingestion preferences and efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f8bb992751f8875d9449507e9c46ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSECT PESTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insect pests can cause significant damage to packaging materials made from plastic films, impacting stored product integrity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENDOPHYTIC FUNGI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGI"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Endophytic Fungi are non-symbiotic fungi that colonize plants such as timothy grass and perennial ryegrass, playing ecological roles in plant communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Endophytic fungi live within plants and are capable of breaking down synthetic compounds like polyester polyurethane."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACINETOBACTER BACTERIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A polystyrene-degrading bacterium isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum, known for its role in breaking down polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"A strain of bacteria isolated for its ability to degrade polystyrene, found in the larvae of Tribolium castaneum."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter bacterium, capable of degrading polystyrene, isolated from the larvae of Tribolium castaneum."&lt;SEP&gt;"Acinetobacter bacterium is a genus of Gram-negative bacteria, some species of which have been found to aid in the biodegradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIBOLIUM CASTANEUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A beetle species confirmed to biodegrade certain plastics including polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae host from which the polystyrene-degrading Acinetobacter bacterium was isolated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum, also known as the red flour beetle, involved in the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum, also known as the Red Flour Beetle, is an insect associated with the biodegradation of expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum, the red flour beetle, is studied for its ability to degrade Styrofoam, contributing to plastic waste reduction efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is a beetle species from which polystyrene-degrading Acinetobacterium was isolated, highlighting its ecological significance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is a species within Tenebrionidae known to participate in biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is a species of beetle commonly used in scientific research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is another pest species managed in studies using packaging and known for its significant role in stored product pest control."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum, part of the Coleoptera order, is managed through standard loose plastic packaging."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is used in microbiome studies for understanding plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum, also known as the red flour beetle, is a common pest of stored grains and a model organism in scientific research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum, the larvae from which the polystyrene-degrading Acinetobacter bacterium was isolated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum is a genus within darkling beetles that can biodegrade polyethylene materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tribolium castaneum, a species of beetle, has been associated with polystyrene degradation via its larval stage."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f45bee9c7111e3b95e966d23c3a6223d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYPROPYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polypropylene is a type of plastic polymer subject to degradation and biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene, abbreviated as PP, is a widely used thermoplastic polymer, the biodegradation of which is being studied."&lt;SEP&gt;Polypropylene, often abbreviated as PP, is a versatile and common thermoplastic polymer used in a wide variety of applications, including packaging and the production of objects such as mask layers. Known for its strength and resistance to chemicals, this plastic polymer is notable for its potential in waste management due to its ability to be biodegraded. Research has shown that polypropylene can be broken down by certain larval species and microorganisms. In particular, Tenebrio molitor (yellow mealworms) and superworms have been identified as capable of depolymerizing and biodegrading polypropylene through processes involving their gut microbiota. Additionally, various microorganisms, such as Bacillus sp. and Rhodococcus sp., have been reported to contribute to its biodeterioration. This biodegradation potential positions polypropylene as a focus of studies aimed at understanding and utilizing biological processes for managing plastic waste effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is subject to biodegradation by yellow mealworms and superworms, reliant on gut-microbe interactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by mealworms and superworms through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene is a versatile thermoplastic polymer used in various applications, known for its chemical resistance and recyclability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WAXWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A term generally used for larvae of wax moths like Galleria mellonella, studied for their ability to degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae of the greater wax moth that consume polyethylene and show signs of its biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are another species that show plastic degradation ability similar to mealworms and superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms host Bacillus strain YP1, which can degrade polyethylene, contributing to bioremediation efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae of certain moth species, previously identified in the laboratory for their capacity to biodegrade polyethylene films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae of the wax moth that have shown potential for breaking down polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are the larvae of the Indian mealmoth, capable of chewing and eating polyethylene (PE) films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms, particularly those of Galleria mellonella, are larvae known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae known for their polyethylene consumption ability, offering a biological avenue for plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are insects that are capable of biodegrading polyethylene through their gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae that have shown evidence of polyethylene biodegradation through associated bacterial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are a type of larvae capable of degrading polyethylene plastic, supported by bacterial strains in their gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae known for their ability to biodegrade plastic, specifically associated with the degradation of polyethylene in scientific studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-551c06d3562ff01465354b3a954b1145&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c</data>
    </node>
