    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b47eb2d42413e11dbd4e9bb9f2df12f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16328c3c685f35501c3639525e997d2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92bf00317a1d0d9ffccab121661a18a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic is a synthetic material that poses environmental challenges, as indicated by the effects of its consumption on insects causing metabolic stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic is a synthetic material used in the study as a sole feedstock for larvae to determine its effects on growth and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae can consume and degrade various plastics including PS and PLA."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic waste is a significant environmental problem that researchers are trying to degrade using natural and engineered solutions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic, especially Polystyrene (PS) and Polyethylene (PE), are used as part of mealworms' diet to investigate biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic refers to various synthetic materials consumed by mealworms, including PP, HDPE, LDPE, PVC, and PS, each with differing impacts on digestion and weight change."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic is the material being studied for its degradation and biodegradation properties under various processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic, generally referring to polymers such as PP, PS, and LDPE, is a material observed for degradation through biological processes."</data>
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TDI&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c</data>
      <data key="d1">"TDI is a component in the synthesis of polyether-PU foam, affecting its chemical properties and susceptibility to biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THF&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dfcb3f2424bd027b5888293a0d01d3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
      <data key="d1">"THF (Tetrahydrofuran) is a solvent used for the extraction of polystyrene (PS)."&lt;SEP&gt;"THF (Tetrahydrofuran) is a solvent used to extract frass and dissolve pristine Styrofoam for GPC analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"THF is used as a solvent for extracting plastic fragments from frass to evaluate the extent of biodegradation carried out by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is an organic solvent used for extracting components from samples, such as those from frass and PS feedstock in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"THF is used to extract degraded products from fecula for further analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tetrahydrofuran (THF) is used as a solvent in the extraction process of PS from frass material."&lt;SEP&gt;"THF (tetrahydrofuran) is used to extract residual plastic in the experiment to assess the extent of degradation."</data>
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL RATES&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae42540a18795e5e2ad9054b7a0edfc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
      <data key="d1">"Survival Rates indicate the percentage of mealworms that remained alive during the experiment, offering insight into the sustainability of plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rates measure the number of living larvae over a period, providing insights into the impact of different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rates refer to the percentage of larvae that remain alive under various dietary conditions during the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates indicate the proportion of living individuals in a population, affected by diet and hydration conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates measure the proportion of larvae that remain alive under different diet conditions over the test period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates refer to the percentage of insect larvae that remain alive after being fed different diets, including plastic, over a period."&lt;SEP&gt;"The measure indicating the percentage of larvae that continue living throughout the experiment, potentially affected by their diet and consumption ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival rates of larvae are measured to evaluate the effects of different feeding treatments with polyether-PU and bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"The percentage of T. molitor larvae surviving under specific dietary conditions, such as PET and agar diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates indicate the percentage of organisms that continue to live under certain environmental conditions or stressors."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates monitored to assess the impact of different diets and microplastic ingestion over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"The rate at which larvae survive under different feeding conditions during the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates refer to the percentage of lesser waxworms that survive over a given period while being fed on different diets, including wax comb and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rates indicate the percentage of mealworms that survived throughout the experimental duration while being fed different PS MP diets."</data>
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-bae14ee5ac26fe661b539bccaa825fb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a47a21d3773bb9ea3318d47bb077597f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9</data>
      <data key="d1">"A form of polystyrene plastic subject to biodegradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of foam plastic subject to biodegradation, observed in studies involving Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of plastic foam material known for its insulation and cushioning properties and subject to studies on biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achatina fulica snail is instrumental in the biodegradation and physical disintegration of expanded polystyrene, promoting waste reduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight plastic material commonly used for insulation and packaging due to its cushioning properties and versatility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene is used as a component in mealworm powder to evaluate its effects on animal feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a major pollutant that Tenebrio molitor can biodegrade, highlighting its potential environmental utility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a lightweight plastic foam material used for various applications, including packaging, and can be biodegraded by specific organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a form of polystyrene plastic often used for packaging and can be biodegraded under certain conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a lightweight foam used in various applications, with known issues related to environmental pollution and toxicity in aquatic ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene, a type of foam plastic, is notably investigated for its biodegradation by organisms such as mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a foam plastic material that can be biodegraded under specific conditions by organisms such as Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a form of polystyrene used for various products, notable for being a lightweight, rigid, and closed-cell material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a form of polystyrene used in products like foam, which can undergo biodegradation by mealworm larvae and microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a form of plastic used as a nutrition source in the study for microorganism growth and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of lightweight plastic, studied for biodegradation and disintegration by land snails."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene is a type of plastic commonly used in environmental impact studies on biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight cellular plastic material used in packaging, insulation, and numerous applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene is a type of plastic subject to biodegradation and disintegration by Achatina Fulica, a land snail."</data>
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
    </node>
