    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE (PE) BIODEGRADATION BY MEALWORM LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The breakdown of polyethylene (PE) microplastics by mealworm larvae, involving depolymerization and other biochemical processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f7d44b3f99233bb4389ceea0cd62617</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradation of plastics involves the breaking down of plastic materials by microorganisms or enzymes, leading to a reduction in environmental plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic biodegradation involves breaking down plastics like polystyrene and polyethylene into smaller molecular intermediates, eventually mineralizing them into carbon dioxide and water."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic biodegradation is the process by which microorganisms break down plastic materials, leading to the complete or partial conversion of those materials into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic biodegradation measures the degree to which plastics are broken down by biological processes, such as the formation of carbon dioxide."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of breaking down plastic materials into simpler compounds using biological organisms and enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The microbial decomposition or breakdown of plastic materials into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic biodegradation is the process of breaking down plastic materials through biological means, such as by microorganisms in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic biodegradation refers to the breakdown of plastics by biological means, in this case, by gut microbes in Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic biodegradation is the breakdown of plastic substances by microbial action, thereby reducing environmental plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic biodegradation involves the breakdown of plastics by biological entities such as organisms and microorganisms, including mealworms and bacterial strains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07aae06c64778453da4bda48f5347de5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE FOAM PRODUCTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Low-Density Polyethylene foam products used as a feed for mealworm larvae to study plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE microplastics refer to polyethylene particles of micro size used in the study to evaluate mealworm larvae's degradation abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE microplastics refer to the small polyethylene particles used in the study to assess their biodegradability by mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene microplastics with a medium molecular weight capable of being depolymerized by mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene (PE) microplastics are a type of plastic ingested by mealworms, exhibiting limited depolymerization in their gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES (ROS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of oxidative stress resulting from exposure to microplastics, indicative of cellular damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical species involved in the oxidative stress response during plastic degradation in mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen that are a byproduct of oxygen metabolism and play roles in cell signaling and homeostasis."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS levels indicate oxidative stress in mealworm larvae, with higher levels seen in PE-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive chemical species containing oxygen, which are known to cause oxidative stress and damage within organisms like mealworm larvae during plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, which play a significant role in cell signaling and homeostasis."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen that can indicate oxidative stress within cells."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species, particularly superoxide radicals, are indicators of oxidative stress in cells."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen that increase as plastic degradability decreases, indicating oxidative stress in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS are highly reactive molecules involved in oxidative stress. Their levels increase in response to plastic mp exposure in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS are molecules involved in oxidative stress, analyzed to understand stress responses in macroinvertebrates during plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, which can lead to oxidative stress in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are chemically reactive molecules that can inhibit bacterial growth at high concentrations of nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen that contribute to the degradation of polystyrene (PS) by Zophobas atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecules that can lead to oxidative stress in organisms and are produced in response to microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen, whose levels are measured to assess oxidative stress in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen. Elevated levels indicate oxidative stress within organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive Oxygen Species are indicators of oxidative stress levels in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules involving oxygen, playing a role in oxidative stress in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reactive oxygen species are highly reactive chemicals formed from oxygen, which increase in organisms under oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cbb8ccec16f4756fa7a1fb365886d9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES OF MEALWORM LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The reaction of mealworm larvae including survival rate, weight, and oxidative stress indicators after ingesting microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICRO(NANO)PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Micro(nano)plastics are small plastic particles formed during the biodegradation process and studied for their effects on macroinvertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Small plastic particles that are fragmented during biodegradation processes by organisms such as mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Micro(nano)plastics are tiny plastic particles that, when ingested by organisms like mealworm larvae, can cause oxidative stress and biological damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Micro(nano)plastics refer to micro-sized particles of plastic that can interact with biological systems, presenting potential environmental hazards."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARKLING BEETLE (COLEOPTERA: TENEBRIONIDAE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of beetles that includes species known for their ability to degrade plastic."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEST MOTH (LEPIDOPTERA: PYRALIDAE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of moths that can masticate and degrade plastic, similar to the darkling beetle family."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTARCTIC KRILL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of krill noted in studies of organisms with limited plastic-degrading ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antarctic krill are capable of turning microplastics into nanoplastics through digestive fragmentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antarctic krill is a type of macroinvertebrate previously studied for their interactions with plastics, though they lack significant plastic-degrading abilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Marine organisms that have demonstrated digestive biofragmentation of ingested plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SNAILS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Land-based organisms that have shown biofragmentation capabilities with ingested plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Snails, specifically *Achatina fulica*, are being investigated for their ability to biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Snails are shelled gastropods tested for polystyrene degradation, showing no biodegradation in experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EARTHWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Earthworms are soil-dwelling invertebrates that have been examined for their potential to biodegrade polystyrene, although with limited results."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are studied concerning microplastic effects, showing varied responses in mortality and reproduction under high microplastic concentrations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soil-dwelling organisms capable of biofragmenting ingested plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are soil-dwelling organisms that consume and potentially break down bioplastics, influencing soil health and plastic degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Terrestrial invertebrates commonly studied for their limited role in plastic degradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT OF PLASTIC BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENVIRONMENTAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The study of how plastic-degrading activities by organisms like mealworm larvae affect the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFRAGMENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biofragmentation is the breakdown of complex materials, such as plastics, into smaller fragments through biological processes, including ingestion by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation is the breakdown of large plastic particles into smaller fragments through biological processes, studied in the context of T. molitor's response to MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation refers to the breakdown of plastic particles into smaller fragments in the larvae, indicating a step in the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofragmentation refers to the breakdown of substances by biological means, significant in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The breaking down of larger plastic particles into smaller ones through biological means, performed by organisms like mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process through which biological organisms break down plastic waste into smaller micro- and nano-sized particles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-beb3c133fa4d5851f0feff55507c6874&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MNPS (MICRO(NANO)PLASTICS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Small plastic particles resulting from the fragmentation of larger plastics by biological processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZHONGLIAN PETROCHEMICAL CO.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A company based in Guangdong, China, that supplies PE microplastics for research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zhonglian Petrochemical Co. is the supplier of Expanded PS foam and LDPE foam used in the plastic biodegradation study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GURADO FOOD CO.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A company based in Shanghai, China, providing food-grade jelly used in preparing mealworm larvae feed."&lt;SEP&gt;"The company based in Shanghai, China, that provided the food-grade jelly used in the mealworm biodegradation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19bc661af07a194ef5a3f48c337c8d59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE (1,2,4-TCB)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1,2,4-TCB is a chemical compound used in the study of polymer biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-TCB is a chemical used in experiments to measure degradation products within frass from mealworms after plastic consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"A chemical solvent used in high temperature gel permeation chromatography to analyze polyethylene samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"A solvent used for extracting residual PP from frass samples for HT-GPC analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-TCB is used as a solvent for extracting PE during plastic biodegradation characterization, specifically for LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a chlorinated aromatic hydrocarbon used in a variety of chemical applications and studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-TEMPERATURE GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY (HT-GPC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A method used to quantify the molecular weight of polymers in the plastic degradation study."&lt;SEP&gt;"An analytical method used to determine the molecular weight of polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Analytical technique used to assess the molecular weight change of polyethylene films after treatment by bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a technique used to analyze the depolymerization and biodegradation of PE microplastics by mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography (HT-GPC) is used to analyze polymers at high temperatures to prevent crystallization and achieve improved resolution."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a method used to determine the molecular weights of polypropylene during degradation tests."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is an analytical method used to assess the molecular weight changes in plastics such as PE after degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a chromatographic technique used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polymers at high temperatures."&lt;SEP&gt;"A process used for analyzing the molecular weight distribution of residual PP in frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is a technique used to analyze the molecular weights (M&lt;sub&gt;n&lt;/sub&gt;, M&lt;sub&gt;w&lt;/sub&gt;, M&lt;sub&gt;s&lt;/sub&gt;) of polypropylene foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC is specifically used to identify depolymerization patterns of the ingested PE polymer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6afbb58ebe9108893daf7e65d59fac39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL INDEX (SR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The survival index includes metrics such as survival rate and average weight, used to assess mealworm larvae's physiological performance after biodegradation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C: H: O: N RATIO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The C: H: O: N ratio describes the composition of the mealworm larvae's regular food, indicating its nutritional makeup."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOOD-GRADE JELLY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An adhesive obtained from Gurado Food Co. used to create feedstock for mealworms by mixing with polyethylene (PE)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIGMA-ALDRICH CO.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A supplier of chemicals, including 1,2,4-trichlorobenzene, used in the experiments, based in Shanghai, China."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sigma-Aldrich Co. is a company that provided an assay kit for measuring reactive oxygen species in mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CO-DIET BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Co-diet bran is an exogenous food source added to plastic diets, improving nutrient content and physiological responses in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Co-diet bran serves as an additional nutrient source for larvae, alleviating stress and improving biodegradation efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Regular food for mealworm larvae, serving as a control or part of a mixed diet in biodegradation studies, with a specified C: H: O: N ratio."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM BREEDING CO.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A company based in Shandong, China, from which mealworm larvae were sourced for the biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physiological responses include changes in growth, dehydration, and malnutrition in larvae due to different plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Physiological responses refer to the changes in survival, growth, and development in macroinvertebrates exposed to micro(nano)plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biological reactions of mealworm larvae to different diets and conditions, measured through survival rates and changes in biomass."&lt;SEP&gt;"The change in average weight and other biomass indicators of the larvae following different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Physiological responses observed in larvae include changes in weight, survival rates, and fat content as a result of polyethylene ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION PERFORMANCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The effectiveness of polyethylene microplastics being broken down biologically by mealworms, analyzed in terms of molecular weight reduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"The effectiveness with which mealworm larvae break down and assimilate plastic materials, such as PE, as measured in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE, POLYVINYL CHLORIDE, POLYPROPYLENE, POLYLACTIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A set of plastics that have been shown to be degraded by mealworm larvae in previous studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL EXTRACTION PROCEDURES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methods used to extract and measure different fractions from mealworm frass, indicating degradation stages."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC DEGRADATION EXPERIMENTAL CONDITIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The specific setups in which mealworm larvae were subjected to various diets to study plastic biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a2991bc47bdc7fe9bc0d7c0d3e23f60</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPEROXIDE DISMUTASE (SOD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme that provides a defense against oxidative stress by converting superoxide radicals into oxygen and hydrogen peroxide in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD is an activated antioxidant enzyme that can scavenge excess peroxidative free radicals, reducing oxidative stress in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD is an antioxidant enzyme measured per U/g fresh weight, indicative of the organism's response to oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD is an antioxidant enzyme that helps eliminate superoxide anions by converting them into hydrogen peroxide and oxygen."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase is an antioxidant enzyme that helps break down potentially harmful oxygen molecules in cells, reducing oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD is an antioxidant enzyme that protects cells from superoxide radicals, identified in PS- and CS-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase (SOD) is an enzyme found in DEGs, contributing to oxidative stress management and metabolic regulation."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD is an antioxidant enzyme that protects organisms from oxidative stress by eliminating superoxide anions."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD is an enzyme that helps break down potentially harmful oxygen molecules in cells, used as a marker for oxidative stress in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase is an enzyme measured in mealworms to assess antioxidant capacity and antibiotic performance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GLUTATHIONE-S-TRANSFERASE (GST)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GST is an enzyme measured per U/g fresh weight, playing a role in detoxification processes and against oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"GST is an enzyme that scavenges metabolites of lipid peroxidation, aiding in the protection against oxidative damage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glutathione-S-transferase is an enzyme that catalyzes the conjugation of the molecule glutathione to other substrates, aiding in the detoxification process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CATALASE (CAT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CAT is an enzyme measured in nmol/min/mL, essential for the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide and defense against oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Catalase is an enzyme that converts hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, playing a key role in the body's response to oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Catalase is an enzyme that decomposes hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen, reducing oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYEHTYLENE (PE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyethylene is a synthetic polymer made from ethylene, commonly used in plastic products."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYHYDROXYALKANOATES (PHA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PHA are bioplastics that degrade under natural conditions, providing alternatives to traditional plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyhydroxyalkanoates are microbial polyesters produced by the fermentation of sugars or lipids."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyhydroxyalkanoates are biobased biodegradable polymers that degrade under specific conditions such as elevated temperatures."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8518832c2d3e002c8acf71795e306144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MALONDIALDEHYDE (MDA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOMARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A compound used as an indicator of lipid peroxidation and oxidative stress in organisms exposed to environmental pollutants."&lt;SEP&gt;"MDA content is used to determine oxidative damage to membrane lipids, indicating the level of lipid peroxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"MDA is a biomarker indicating the level of lipid peroxidation and oxidative stress in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"MDA levels are measured in nmol/g fresh weight and serve as an indicator of lipid peroxidation and oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Malondialdehyde is a marker for oxidative stress and is commonly used to measure lipid peroxidation levels."&lt;SEP&gt;"MDA is a compound used to monitor oxidative damage in organisms, especially as a result of lipid peroxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"MDA is a reactive aldehyde and a marker for oxidative stress, measured in the study to understand its levels in mealworms after microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Malondialdehyde is a marker of oxidative stress, measured to evaluate lipid peroxidation levels in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYAMIDE FILTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyamide filter is a type of filter material used to collect residual particles by filtering them based on size."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN PEROXIDE (H2O2)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrogen peroxide is a chemical compound used as a strong oxidizing agent in the digestion process of organic matter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ABBKINE SCIENTIFIC CO., LTD.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Abbkine Scientific Co., Ltd. is a company supplying enzyme assay kits used in the study to measure antioxidant enzyme activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Abbkine Scientific Co., Ltd. is the company providing assay kits for measuring MDA and SOD levels in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
