      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ORIGIN PRO 2021&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Origin Pro 2021 is a software used to perform statistical analyses in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Origin Pro 2021 is statistical analysis software used for data analysis in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a27dff473ed210517e6461ad3e5d783</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TUKEY'S CORRECTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"STATISTICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A post-hoc analysis method for adjusting p-values in multiple comparisons."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical method used to adjust the confidence levels in multiple comparison procedures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tukey's correction is a statistical method used to adjust for multiple comparisons in analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SONG ET AL. (2020)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PUBLICATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Song et al. (2020) is a reference used in the study related to the digestion of organic matters."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRANDON ET AL. (2018)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A study providing empirical measurements for the observed case of mealworm consumption of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"A study that reported consistent rates of polyethylene degradation by larvae, serving as a comparison for current research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Brandon et al. (2018) is a reference indicating the impact of bran as a cofeed for plastic-fed mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Research studies referenced for comparison of experimental results related to substrate degradation and conversion efficiency with different larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Study discussing bacterial groups' involvement in metabolic interference."&lt;SEP&gt;"The study by Brandon et al. provides supporting evidence for LDPE conversion by T. molitor, referenced to validate the findings of the current experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WU AND CRIDDLE (2021)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PUBLICATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wu and Criddle (2021) is a reference related to the digestibility of feedstock in mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f54f1dd7372cfa3dc5f348864eed2261</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRASS YIELD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Frass yield is a measure of the residual waste produced by mealworms after the consumption of PS MPs, expressed as the ratio of frass to consumed PS MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass yield refers to the waste produced by mealworm larvae, used as a measure to assess the effectiveness of polyethylene digestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEIN CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Determined using the Kjeldahl method, the protein content is analyzed in the yellow mealworm samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein Content in larvae is an important measure to determine their condition and effects of various diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein Content refers to the amount of protein present in mealworm larvae, determined using the Bradford method with spectrophotometric analysis at 595 nm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content in mealworm larvae increases due to the need to synthesize more enzymes for PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content is a measurement of the protein levels in mealworm larvae, found to vary with different diet compositions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content refers to the amount of protein present in food types served to mealworms, impacting their nutrition and mass loss."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content measure in the study helps to compare nutritional uptake across different dietary regimes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Protein content is measured through the Kjeldahl method, with adjustments for non-protein nitrogen in insects like mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eed90a978852274ead3771418cd60bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOHYDRATE CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbohydrate content in mealworms decreased after Styrofoam consumption compared to a conventional diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbohydrate content measures the total carbohydrates in mealworm larvae, which decreases with polyethylene ingestion due to potential nutrient absorption issues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbohydrate levels in mealworms were analyzed to determine the mealworms' metabolic response to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"The carbohydrate content is a variable measure in larvae, emphasizing differences under various dietary conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c2e7d253d2108b0ed94be7f2dc7466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GALLERIA MELLONELLA LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of wax moth larvae that can digest certain plastics like PS after force feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria Mellonella larvae, also known as wax moth larvae, have the capacity to degrade polyethylene and polystyrene, contributing to plastic waste management."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria Mellonella, also known as wax moth larvae, are used in biodegradation studies of polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella larvae are recognized for their plastic-degrading capabilities, similar to those studied in mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella larvae are capable of plastic degradation, increasing polymer molecular weights in studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae known as the greater wax moth, capable of complete digestion and biodegradation of polystyrene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae from the species Galleria mellonella, used in studies to isolate polystyrene-degrading bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae are associated with Aspergillus flavus PEDX3, which was isolated from its gut and has a capacity for PE degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella larvae have been observed to degrade PS (polystyrene) by increasing molecular weight indices."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella larvae are wax worms studied for their capacity to degrade PS MPs through biofragmentation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Galleria mellonella larvae, commonly known as waxworms, are investigated for their potential in degrading plastics and experiencing oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b68a8917166a3e45f4bf51e2e04949f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTESTINAL BLOCKAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intestinal blockage is a potential effect of microplastic ingestion in mealworm larvae, affecting their nutrient uptake."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOTAL CARBOHYDRATE CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The total carbohydrate content is a measurement used to assess nutrient levels in mealworm larvae after being fed different diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FAT CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fat Content in larvae provides insights into the effects of various diets on their physiology and metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content in mealworms decreased after Styrofoam consumption compared to a conventional diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content refers to the lipid levels in mealworm larvae, which can vary based on the diet consisting of polyethylene and bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content measures the amount of fat in mealworm food, significantly changing during starvation as fatty acids provide major energy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content indicates the amount of fat present in a substance, evaluated in larvae and excrement to determine nutritional values."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content is a measure of nutritional value, affecting taste and survival rates of the insects during starvation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fat content is determined through extraction and weighing processes, expressed in g/100 g dry matter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf28f07f3e46b29e447aae784667c29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-531058c4540bd4e6a50d141db01d4d11</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A complex protein that catalyzes biochemical reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzyme is a biological catalyst that accelerates the degradation of plant biomass and synthetic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are biological molecules that act as catalysts and help complex reactions occur everywhere in life."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes may play a role in breaking down polyethylene in mealworm larvae, though specific enzymes are not detailed in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes, possibly produced by bacterial or fungal activity, are hypothesized to play a role in breaking down plastics like polystyrene during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"PM S1 is a bacterial isolate from mealworm gut that has shown the ability to degrade polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) by acting as a catalyst in biochemical reactions during its growth and colonization on the surface of plastic beads."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are biological molecules that act as catalysts, accelerating chemical reactions in living organisms, including the degradation of synthetic plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The specific enzyme produced by the bacterium, responsible for catalyzing the degradation of poly(ethylene terephthalate)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes are proteins that act as biological catalysts, speeding up chemical reactions in the gut of mealworms which can contribute to the breakdown of materials such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biological molecules that catalyze the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d7f6907ee7ce47b00537b1458fcc029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa1a88a43b40afd043514c5f6898ee71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE Biodegradation refers to the breakdown of polyethylene plastics by mealworm larvae, which affects their physiological responses and oxidative stress levels."&lt;SEP&gt;"PE biodegradation refers to the microbial breakdown of polyethylene, which is discussed as a research interest to reduce environmental plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC INGESTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic ingestion involves mealworm larvae consuming plastics, which leads to physiological and oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic ingestion is the process by which certain insect larvae consume plastic materials as part of their diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOD, GST, CAT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are antioxidant enzymes (superoxide dismutase, glutathione S-transferase, catalase) whose activity increases in mealworm larvae to combat oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MDA CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MDA (malondialdehyde) content is a marker of lipid peroxidation and oxidative damage in mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE + BRAN DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The PE + Bran diet is a combination of polyethylene and bran, fed to mealworm larvae to study its effects on physiological performance and biodegradation capability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRAN-FED LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bran-fed larvae serve as a control group in the study, used to compare the effects of polyethylene ingestion on mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran-fed larvae serve as control specimens in studies examining the physiological and oxidative stress responses to various diet conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL, GROWTH, AND BIOMASS LEVELS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The survival, growth, and biomass levels in mealworm larvae are measured to assess their physiological response to different diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON RESOURCE RECOVERY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon resource recovery refers to the process through which mealworm larvae assimilate carbon from ingested polyethylene for energy and growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE MICROPLASTICS (PS-MP)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS-MP are type of plastics that can induce physical damage, increased ROS levels, and toxicity in soil invertebrates lacking plastic biodegradation capacity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EISENIA FETIDA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Commonly known as earthworms, used as a model organism demonstrating oxidative damage under microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Eisenia fetida is a species of earthworm known for being affected by gut toxicity from polystyrene microplastics, influencing its microbial community signatures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Eisenia fetida is a species of earthworm, used in the study to observe ROS levels when exposed to polystyrene microparticles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Eisenia fetida is a type of earthworm with limited plastic-degrading abilities, used in studies exposing them to plastic MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Eisenia fetida is a type of soil invertebrate used as a comparison in studies examining the effects of plastic ingestion and oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Eisenia fetida, commonly known as the red worm, is used in vermicomposting and its defense responses to microplastics in soil are of ecological interest."&lt;SEP&gt;"Eisenia fetida is a species of earthworms used in research to study the ecological and toxicological impacts of pollutants, such as microplastics and chemicals."&lt;SEP&gt;"Eisenia fetida, commonly known as earthworms, are studied for their response to environmental toxins."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MULCH FILM-DERIVED PE MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These microplastics are derived from mulch film and are used in studies to explore their effects on organisms like earthworms and mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GENE EXPRESSION AND DNA DAMAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gene expression and DNA damage are affected by oxidative stress in organisms exposed to microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;APOPTOSIS AND ABNORMAL DEVELOPMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Apoptosis and abnormal development in organisms can result from oxidative damage caused by plastic ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e32e296ef016e2403df45f75eedee219</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SOD, or Superoxide Dismutase, is an enzyme measured to evaluate oxidative stress in Tenebrio larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"SOD, or superoxide dismutase, is an enzyme that helps to protect cells by catalyzing the dismutation of the superoxide radical into either ordinary molecular oxygen or hydrogen peroxide, reducing oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Superoxide dismutase, an enzyme that catalyzes the dismutation of superoxide radicals into ordinary oxygen and hydrogen peroxide."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GST, or glutathione S-transferase, is an enzyme that participates in detoxification by catalyzing the reaction between glutathione and a wide variety of substrates, thereby neutralizing toxic compounds including those resulting from lipid peroxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glutathione-S-transferase, an enzyme involved in the detoxification process, by forming conjugates between glutathione and various toxic compounds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CAT, or catalase, is an enzyme that catalyzes the conversion of hydrogen peroxide, a harmful by-product of cellular metabolism, into water and oxygen, thus protecting cells from oxidative damage."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RESIDUAL PLASTIC PARTICLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Residual plastic particles are fragments of plastic materials leftover after partially being biodegraded by organisms like mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The remaining plastic particles in the frass after digestion, categorized by size."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MDA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MDA, or Malondialdehyde, is a biomarker of oxidative stress measured to assess physiological effects on larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"MDA, or malondialdehyde, is a marker of oxidative stress, used to measure the levels of lipid peroxidation in mealworm larvae in response to polyethylene ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastics are small plastic particles less than 5mm in size, often resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic items and posing environmental hazards."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles that pose pollution challenges and are subject to reduction strategies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Small plastic fragments resulting from the biodegradation of larger plastic products such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;Microplastics are small plastic particles, typically less than 5 millimeters in size, that result from the breakdown of larger pieces of plastic and are also manufactured as primary microplastics. They are composed of materials such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and expanded polystyrene (EPS). These particles are prevalent environmental contaminants found in marine, freshwater, terrestrial, and agricultural ecosystems, as well as in landfill leachate and sewage sludge.

Microplastics pose significant ecological, environmental, and health challenges. They are known to pollute both marine and terrestrial environments, serving as vectors for bioaccumulation. This pollution affects a diverse range of organisms, including earthworms and mealworm larvae (Tenebrio molitor), where microplastics can remain undigested, alter feeding behaviors, and impact metabolism. These effects are not confined to smaller organisms; they extend up the food chain, affecting a broad range of organisms, including humans.

In terrestrial ecosystems, microplastics can negatively impact soil properties and plant performance, and they have even been found in animal-based traditional medicinal materials. In addition, microplastics interact with biological entities and elements, such as SARS-CoV-2 RNA in sewage sludge, potentially influencing viral transport and exposure.

Research efforts focus heavily on identifying, quantifying, and mitigating the impact of microplastics due to their widespread presence and associated health risks. Strategies for reducing microplastic pollution include biodegradation through biological agents, such as insect larvae. Despite these efforts, the detrimental effects of microplastics on living organisms and the difficulty of completely eradicating them remain considerable global challenges. This underscores the urgent need for effective and sustainable solutions to address the problem of microplastic pollution.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-207d77d551e893eda6578be68ca3d0db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6464a2f3ca18442f70e254b9341a3e1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00a6288e1109e01eac1ce7ecb35f2eda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76cb64e0aa4e4039b70799a0df8943d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f267b48449a40d614a2f325799752dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Extremely small plastic particles that raise concerns as emerging environmental contaminants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extremely small plastic particles that result from the further breakdown of microplastics, posing significant ecological risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are extremely small particles of plastic, typically below 100 nm, which can interact with biological membranes and molecules, posing significant environmental and health risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny plastic particles that result from further degradation of microplastics, posing environmental and health risks through pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles that, like microplastics, contribute to environmental pollution and pose health risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microscopic plastic particles that interact with proteins in the gut environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extremely small plastic particles which can induce genotoxicity and intestinal damage in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are isolated via a physical method that uses microbial digestion and centrifugation for analysis, ensuring their chemical structure remains unaltered."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are tiny plastic particles that pose environmental and biological risks, often resulting from the degradation of larger plastic items."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tiny plastic particles that are targeted for degradation by certain bacterial strains found in mealworm intestines as part of environmental bioremediation efforts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are extremely small plastic particles, often below 100 nm in size, similar to microplastics in environmental impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are even smaller particles derived from larger plastics that can penetrate biological membranes and potentially disrupt cellular functions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are even smaller plastic particles that pose environmental risks and require management strategies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are tiny plastic particles on a nanometer scale, arising from degradation of larger plastics and contributing to pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are tiny plastic particles with environmental toxicity and risk management concerns."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68c274a59cd5db4cd675bca51f11adb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyethylene Biodegradation is the process by which polyethylene is broken down biologically, involving organisms such as caterpillars and mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene biodegradation is the breakdown of polyethylene by biological means, as performed by organisms such as Galleria Mellonella and Achroia Grisella."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene biodegradation refers to the chemical breakdown of polyethylene plastic facilitated by biological entities such as mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The breakdown of polyethylene by the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, indicating their potential in managing plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"The breakdown of polyethylene plastic by organisms such as Tenebrio molitor and Zophobas atratus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene biodegradation is the process by which polyethylene is broken down by certain bacteria, such as those found in the gut of waxworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene biodegradation is the process of breaking down polyethylene, a common plastic, into simpler compounds through biological means, often involving bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"The breakdown of polyethylene by bacterial strains found in waxworms' guts, a process important for managing plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL MICROORGANISMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Functional microorganisms are microbes with specific roles in biological processes, such as facilitating biodegradation of nanoplastics in gut microcosms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional microorganisms are those involved in the degradation of polypropylene plastic."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE REMOVAL EFFICIENCY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE removal efficiency refers to the percentage of polyethylene degraded and removed from the environment by organisms, used to evaluate biodegradation success rates in studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"The effectiveness of mealworm larvae in removing polyethylene from their environment through biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOMEOSTASIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Homeostasis is the process by which living organisms regulate internal conditions to maintain stable and optimal conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Homeostasis is the process by which organisms maintain a stable internal environment despite external changes, affected in mealworm larvae during PE ingestion and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Homeostasis refers to maintaining internal stability, and its related metabolites were identified in mealworms during polystyrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Homeostasis refers to the self-regulating process by which organisms maintain stability while adjusting to conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Homeostasis pertains to the maintenance of stable internal conditions, which is highlighted in the larvae's ability to manage energy reserves during experimental feeding with PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1175f4bdc5e5c267806fa6c0ff19fcc4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE REMOVAL AND BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The process of breaking down polyethylene (PE) through biological means, particularly involving mealworm larvae that can effectively depolymerize and biodegrade PE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An antioxidant enzyme that plays a role in protecting cells from oxidative damage."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RESIDUAL POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymeric substances left after the process of depolymerization and biodegradation, often composed of partially broken-down polyethylene (PE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Residual Polymers refer to the remaining plastic compounds found in frass after larvae digestion, used to analyze the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Residual polymers are the polymeric materials that remain after biodegradation processes in the larvae's frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Residual polymers refer to the remaining plastic material after partial biodegradation by mealworms, analyzed for chemical modifications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Residual polymers are the remaining polymeric materials within frass samples after consumption and biodegradation by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Residual polymers are the remaining segments of polymer chains after partial depolymerization has occurred during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMAL DECOMPOSITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The process of breaking down compounds by heat, which in this context aids in the depolymerization and biodegradation of polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOMASS LEVELS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The amount of organic material produced by mealworm larvae, which can be influenced by dietary supplements like bran."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROENVIRONMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The gut microenvironment of mealworms supports the biodegradation of plastics, albeit with varying efficiency depending on plastic type."&lt;SEP&gt;"The internal environment within the digestive system of mealworm larvae where interactions such as digestion and biodegradation occur."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL EXPOSURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The contact between organisms, like mealworm larvae, and environmental pollutants such as microplastics and nanoplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIOXIDANT ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Antioxidant enzymes are enzymes that mitigate oxidative stress by neutralizing free radicals in biological systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antioxidant enzymes are involved in mitigating oxidative stress during the biodegradation of plastics in macroinvertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Antioxidant enzymes help protect cells from oxidative damage and were considered as bioactive components in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Proteins such as SOD, GST, and CAD that protect organisms from oxidative damage by neutralizing reactive oxygen species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymes that scavenge free radicals, reducing oxidative stress in organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fccc204d8a48c645da49bd7b3175c262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEXABROMOCYCLODODECANE (HBCD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A brominated flame retardant used in polystyrene foams and other products, posing environmental challenges."&lt;SEP&gt;"A flame retardant used in PS foam not accumulated by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"HBCD is a common flame retardant studied for its fate in environments involving polystyrene-degrading mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hexabromocyclododecane (HBCD) is a flame retardant commonly found in plastics like polystyrene, and its fate in biodegrading organisms like mealworms is of environmental interest."&lt;SEP&gt;"HBCD is a flame retardant found in polystyrene; its fate in polystyrene-degrading organisms shows elevated levels in egested polymers but no bioaccumulation in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"HBCD is a flame retardant that, when ingested by mealworms, is excreted via frass with minimal retention in body tissues."&lt;SEP&gt;"HBCD is a flame retardant whose fate is studied in the context of biodegrading polyethylene by mealworms. It shows high levels in egested polymer but no bioaccumulation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d616d4c3c3199d53c1ae93ebcf3589d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insect larvae that are used in the biodegradation of polyethylene plastics, highlighting their potential in waste management and environmental sustainability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio larvae are insect larvae used in the study for various experiments including biodegradation capacity and physiological assessments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio larvae show oxidative stress responses when exposed to polyethylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio larvae, including mealworms, are significant for research into carbon and nitrogen metabolism during plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio larvae, or mealworms, are studied for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, focusing on changes in their gut microbiome during the process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b693d867918f89c9b28997fe94fc830&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOYBEAN MEAL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Soybean meal is a high-protein ingredient commonly used in animal feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soybean meal is a traditional protein source in broiler diets that mealworm larvae are proposed as an alternative to."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastic particles are small plastic fragments that can cause environmental harm, including intestinal damage in aquatic organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastic refers to tiny plastic particles that accumulate on shorelines worldwide and are an emerging contaminant of concern in marine ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles that have effects on oxidative stress and gene expression in organisms like earthworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are tiny plastic particles that result from the breakdown of larger plastic debris in the environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastic refers to tiny pieces of plastic debris that pose environmental hazards, especially in aquatic ecosystems."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EARTHWORMS (EISENIA FETIDA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species capable of producing NPs from PE MPs, impacting reproductive and cellular viability."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of terrestrial invertebrates affected by phenanthrene and microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms are studied for the combined effects of microplastics and toxic substances like atrazine on their biology."&lt;SEP&gt;"Earthworms (Eisenia fetida) are a species used in ecological studies, known for their interactions with soil components and response to pollutants like microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The earthworm species studied for responses to plastic particles and their interaction with soil components."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6a53d37e7ff6850fc55177f325e49861&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLOMICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolomics is a field investigating the chemical processes involving metabolites, here used to study mitochondrial damage from nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The scientific study of metabolic responses and processes, used in the analysis of PET degradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolomics is the scientific study of chemical processes involving metabolites, and is used in the GC-TOF-MS analysis to investigate polymer-generated organic residues from mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolomics is a scientific analysis performed to study central carbon metabolism metabolites showing insects' response to plastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolomics is the scientific study of chemical processes involving metabolites, setting minimum standards for biological sample reporting."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolomics is the study of chemical processes involving metabolites, used here to understand the impact of polybrominated diphenyl ether-47 on breast cancer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nanoplastic particles are ultra-small plastic bits causing genotoxic effects and physical harm in organisms, such as clams."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are minute plastic particles that induce genotoxicity and intestinal damage in organisms like brine shrimp."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are small plastic particles whose sizes are effectively reduced through microbial degradation in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastic refers to tiny plastic particles which, when ingested, can induce behavioral disorders in organisms, such as terrestrial snails, and have potential effects on human health and ecosystems."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLAME RETARDANT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Flame retardant chemicals are found in PS products and can influence microbial communities and plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Flame retardant chemicals like HBCD are used to prevent fires in materials such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Flame retardants are chemicals used to prevent fires in materials like plastics; their fate during biodegradation is a key environmental concern."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
