    <node id="&quot;GREATER WAX MOTH LARVAE (GALLERIA MELLONELLA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Galleria mellonella larvae, commonly referred to as the greater wax moth larvae, known for interacting with low-density polyethylene (LDPE) by creating holes and chewing the material."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae of the greater wax moth are also studied for their ability to biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Greater Wax Moth Larvae, Galleria mellonella, have shown the potential for biodegradation of different plastics, including polyethylene and beeswax, due to their unique metabolic capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Greater wax moth larvae, scientifically known as Galleria mellonella, were used to isolate Massilia sp. PS1903 for PS degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Greater Wax Moth larvae are insects known for their potential biodegradation capabilities, specifically publicized in breaking down plastic polymers like PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5d8df2e13d3902379597b08eb0e08db</data>
    </node>
