    <node id="&quot;LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08257b9745bccf6dfc5ca860da5a96bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
      <data key="d1">"A thermoplastic made from the monomer ethylene, notable for its use in packaging due to its toughness and flexibility."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of lightweight plastic polymer, which can be broken down through microbial and non-microbial processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a thermoplastic made from the ethylene monomer, widely used for manufacturing containers, dispensing bottles, and plastic bags due to its toughness and flexibility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio obscurus participates in gut microbe-independent biodegradation of low-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-Density Polyethylene is a type of polyethylene studied for biodegradation in superworms and other biological organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of thermoplastic made from the monomer ethylene, known for its toughness and ease of processing, commonly used in packaging, containers, and plastic bags."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene is a type of plastic commonly used in packaging and is subject to degradation by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-Density Polyethylene is a type of plastic more efficiently biodegraded by mealworms compared to high-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene is a form of plastic that can be degraded by certain bacteria like Pantoea present in the gut of T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a thermoplastic made from the monomer ethylene, used for various products including containers, dispensing bottles, and wash bottles."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of polyethylene which can be biodegraded by mealworms, showcasing differences in degradation rates based on density."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-Density Polyethylene is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by certain species, such as the greater wax worm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene (LDPE) is a thermoplastic made from the monomer ethylene, known for its flexibility and toughness, and can be biodegraded by certain organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of polyethylene characterized by its lower density, making it more flexible and often used in plastic bags and films. It is a target for biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-Density Polyethylene is a category of polyethylene, and its biodegradation was confirmed in mealworms through gut microbe-independent mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of plastic degraded by Zophobas atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a thermoplastic made from ethylene, known for its high resilience and versatility in packaging."</data>
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYAMIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-e5caa980be616efa237401a957b69019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3</data>
      <data key="d1">"Photo-oxidative degradation reduces the developmental toxicity of polyamide microplastics, altering their biological impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyamide, often used in textiles and plastics, is studied for its environmental degradation properties."</data>
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEOBACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">Proteobacteria is a major and diverse phylum of Gram-negative bacteria that includes a wide variety of species, some of which are known pathogens causing foodborne illnesses. This phylum is notably prevalent in various environments, including the digestive tracts of insects such as mealworm larvae, where it plays a crucial role in the gut microbiome. Within mealworm larvae, Proteobacteria maintains a significant presence across various diets, particularly those involving plastics like polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU), and ethylene-vinyl acetate (EVA). It is recognized for its role in plastic biodegradation, serving as a dominant phylum in mealworms fed with these substances, contributing to digestion and plastic degradation.

Specifically, Proteobacteria remains a prevalent phylum in PS and PE-fed superworms and yellow mealworms, maintaining its dominance even when relative abundance decreases under certain dietary conditions, such as PS and CS diets, compared to controls. Additionally, in mealworm guts, it is involved in nitrogen fixation processes, contributing to the broader ecosystem functions like those observed in surface ocean metagenomes. A noteworthy species within this phylum, Cronobacter sakazakii, is involved in the degradation of extracellular polymeric substances (EPS). Overall, Proteobacteria's diversity and functional capabilities make it an essential component of microbial communities, especially in environments impacted by varied diets and plastic exposure.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTEROIDES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteroides is a genus of anaerobic, Gram-negative bacteria that are significant in the human intestinal tract."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a genus of anaerobic, Gram-negative bacteria that are significant in the human intestinal tract."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is part of the dominant bacterial community in mealworm larvae and plays a role in degrading different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is part of the dominant bacterial phylum found in the gut of mealworm larvae, linked to dietary changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is one of the dominant genera present in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a phylum observed in the mealworm microbiome, indicating its presence in insects fed with polystyrenes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is a genus of bacteria found in termites, contributing to lignocellulose degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is part of the dominant bacterial community in mealworm larvae and plays a role in degrading different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteroides is part of the dominant bacterial phylum found in the gut of mealworm larvae, linked to dietary changes."</data>
