    <node id="&quot;FIRMICUTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum prevalent in mealworm larvae microbiota, with notable variation in proportions based on the diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Firmicutes is a bacterial phylum, which was the most prevalent in mealworms fed with bran and low MW PS diets."&lt;SEP&gt;Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria characterized by predominantly Gram-positive cell wall structures and is involved in various ecological and biological processes. This phylum is a significant component of the gut microbiome in different insect larvae, particularly in mealworms and superworms. Firmicutes have been identified as a dominant bacterial phylum in the gut microbiome of insects fed various diets, including polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), and other dietary conditions. The presence of Firmicutes is notable for influencing digestion and diet degradation capabilities in these insects.

In mealworms, Firmicutes are prevalent and show variations in their relative abundance based on the diet. They dominate the bacterial community in mealworms fed with polystyrene and represent a major part of the microbes in both bran-fed and expanded polystyrene (EPS)-fed groups. Studies indicate that their abundance tends to increase under specific diets like polyurethane (PU) and during experiments involving certain dietary substrates, such as PET (associated with plastic degraders). However, in yellow mealworms, there was a noted decrease in the abundance of Firmicutes in those fed with plastic baits.

Overall, Firmicutes play a crucial role in the gut microbiome of insect larvae, participating in essential functions such as digestion and nitrogen fixation, thereby adapting to various environmental and dietary conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;"Firmicutes is a phylum of bacteria, most of which have Gram-positive cell wall structures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Firmicutes is another dominant phylum of bacteria in the gut microbiome of mealworms, influencing digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Firmicutes is one of the major bacterial phyla in the mealworm gut, contributing to their diet degradation capability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACTINOBACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">Actinobacteria is a phylum of Gram-positive bacteria characterized by a high guanine and cytosine (G+C) content in their DNA. This group includes various genera, with Kocuria being one among them, and is recognized as a dominant bacterial phylum within the gut microbiota of mealworm larvae. Actinobacteria play a crucial role in regulating the gut microbiome's composition and diversity. They are known for their involvement in breaking down complex organic materials, including bioplastics, thereby facilitating processes like plastic degradation and contributing significantly to the mealworms' dietary adaptation. Furthermore, these bacteria participate in biodiverse microbial communities and are implicated in degrading high-density polyethylene (HDPE). This phylum, influencing the gut microbiome including unclassified-c-Actinobacteria, is essential for the metabolic processes within the larval gut, providing nutrients to insect hosts, and showing notable abundance changes in polystyrene (PS) environments. Overall, Actinobacteria significantly contribute to the ecological dynamics and functional capabilities of the gut microbiome in mealworms.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-304c1b42a6d3f439e83f380d2d64420a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54f22136b514092aeeb2f431170b6ed6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a603837ad5b989bafd614f5eeb8504c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
