    <node id="&quot;ESTERASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymes involved in the breakdown of specific chemical bonds in polyurethane molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes highly active in mealworm larvae, facilitating the degradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that break down ester bonds, potentially involved in the degradation of polystyrene within the microbial community."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases show high activity in mealworm larvae during the degradation of plastics like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are involved in the biodegradation processes of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes evaluated for their potential to break down ester bonds in the context of bacterial growth on NPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes tested for their hydrolytic activity against bacteria on NPS emulsion using Tween 20 as the substrate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases catalyze the hydrolysis of ester bonds, a crucial process in the breakdown of environmental pollutants by Achromobacter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that split esters into acids and alcohols in a chemical reaction with water (a process known as hydrolysis)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes highly active in mealworm larvae, facilitating the degradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases are enzymes that facilitate the breakdown of esters into acids and alcohols, showing involvement in polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Esterases show high activity in mealworm larvae during the degradation of plastics like polyethylene."</data>
