    <node id="&quot;PRINCIPAL COMPONENT ANALYSIS (PCA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A statistical method used to model and analyze differences in metabolic profiles across various treatments."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical method used to transform possibly correlated variables into uncorrelated ones, used for assessing feeding strategy discrimination."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to assess the discrimination between different feeding strategies by analyzing the data from the experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to differentiate and analyze the variation in metabolite profiles between control and PET-fed samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal component analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to relate the substrate to microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical procedure used to observe relationships and patterns within data, based on correlation similarity, helpful in analyzing experimental results."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical tool used to emphasize variation and bring out strong patterns in a dataset, used here to analyze diet effects on microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical procedure that transforms the data of gut metabolites to highlight the differences and similarities among the larvae groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method applied to study the microbial communities of superworms and yellow mealworms fed different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical tool used to emphasize variation and bring out strong patterns in dataset analysis, such as microbiome studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical procedure used to evaluate the relationships between different variables, such as biodegradation, protein, and cations in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"PCA is a statistical method used to simplify the complexity in high-dimensional data while preserving trends and patterns, applied here to microbial community profiles."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical procedure for transforming variables into a set of uncorrelated variables."&lt;SEP&gt;"Principal component analysis (PCA) is a statistical method used to relate the substrate to microbial activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-64b3e57543d6b365fff3ef2b14ddb309&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f8516b10110f884279870c2ef887f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
