    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial genus prevalent in both Tenebrio species, influencing gut microbiome composition across diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Associated with PE diet, playing a significant role in PS degradation in superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of lactic acid bacteria which plays a role in gut health and is known for its ability to survive harsh conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of lactic acid bacteria, commonly found in the intestine, and capable of causing infections."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a bacterial genus found in the gut of mealworms and observed in significant numbers when fed with polystyrenes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus, a predominant genus found in the mealworm microbiome, plays a role in digestive processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus within Firmicutes associated with PU degradation and common in insect gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a dominant genus within the gut microbiome of superworms, contributing to the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is identified as a common gut-associated genus in both species of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus found in the mealworm gut, absent in the foregut and anterior midgut, important for digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria showing significant differences in abundance in mealworm guts depending on dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria found in mealworm guts, playing a role in degrading styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus found in the gut microbiome of three insect species studied, playing a crucial role in polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria found in the gut of all three insect larvae species (Tenebrio molitor, Galleria mellonella, and Zophobas atratus) and might play an important role in the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is identified as a gut microbe similar across different insect larvae species, participating in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of gram-positive bacteria often found in the intestines, with relevance in health and disease, measured for its abundance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a bacterium that produces esterases, facilitating the degradation of indoxacarb insecticide and involved in polymer degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of bacteria prevalent in mealworm larvae, indicative of dietary influences on microbiome composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus is a genus of lactic acid bacteria, commonly found in the intestine, and capable of causing infections."</data>
