    <node id="&quot;BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">Bacteria are single-celled microorganisms widely acknowledged for their significant role in biodegradation and enzyme activity, which can vary depending on environmental conditions. A notable example includes the bacterial strain Bacillus cereus NJDI, which is known for its natural ability to degrade polymer materials, such as low-density polyethylene (LDPE), leading to substantial weight loss over time in polyethylene films. Bacteria are also involved in the degradation of complex materials, including brominated high-impact polystyrene (HIPS) and polyethylene terephthalate (PET).

Through their adhesion capacity, which varies with the number of flagella, bacteria enhance electron transport and adhesion processes, impacting lipid metabolism and the biodegradation process. Various bacterial strains, including those found in the digestive systems of insects like mealworms and T. molitor, have shown significant potential in decomposing plastics such as polystyrene, expanded polystyrene (EPS), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC).

These microorganisms are part of the gut microbiome, with populations fluctuating based on diet and environmental factors. Bacteria in the digestive systems of insects are capable of decomposing urea and facilitating nitrification, as indicated by the presence of amoA and ureC genes. This bacterial activity is not only crucial for breaking down dietary components—including styrofoam—but also for their role in biodegradation processes when dining on unconventional materials such as surgical mask layers. Understanding and utilizing these bacterial capabilities hold promise for innovative solutions in managing polymer waste and environmental sustainability.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-078b6698be699eb7105b7cffadf709af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d57e056cd3b06b37d22117fc75e3a4df&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ce1f7a057da80b206f8354c70d120c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
