    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pseudomonas is a bacterium that produces esterases, which are involved in the degradation of compounds like indoxacarb insecticide."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas is a genus of bacteria used in conjunction with Bacillus to incubate UV-pre-treated PP films."&lt;SEP&gt;Pseudomonas is a diverse and significant genus of Gram-negative bacteria within the Proteobacteria phylum. It is known for its capability to degrade complex compounds, including environmental pollutants and insecticides. Pseudomonas is prominently associated with the degradation of polystyrene (PS), playing a crucial role in its biodegradation process. Species within this genus have been identified in the guts of superworms, yellow mealworms, and wax moths, where they contribute to the breakdown of polystyrene by utilizing it as a sole carbon source. Furthermore, Pseudomonas is involved in the biodegradation of other polymers such as polyurethane and polyolefins and is prominent in the microbiome of PS-fed and polyolefin-fed larvae groups, showing significant differences compared to other feeding conditions.

The genus contributes to the assembly and complexity of the gut microbiome in mealworms and is known for producing various enzymes, including laccase and styrene monoxygenase, which are essential for the degradation of synthetic dyes and polymer materials. Key genes such as alkB, related to alkane biodegradation, and components like membrane-bound Styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) have been identified in Pseudomonas, further highlighting its role in biodegradation processes. The presence of Pseudomonas in these biological systems underscores its importance in environmental and industrial applications due to its ability to transform and degrade synthetic compounds, including plastics and bioplastics, contributing to a diverse and adaptive bacterial community within different ecological niches.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus found in the gut microbiome, related to ecological interactions and nitrogen metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;"A genus related to Prestia megaterium S1 identified in gene sequencing analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria that is often found in soil and is known for producing enzymes such as esterase, which assist in various ecological functions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a ubiquitous genus of bacteria documented for their involvement in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria whose abundance decreased significantly in mealworms consuming PVC compared to those fed on bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of Gram-positive bacteria found in the larval and adult gut of T. molitor, potentially influencing their ability to process PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria characterized by its rod-shaped cells. It is commonly found in various environments and is known for its ability to form spores."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria known for their capacity to transform styrene to phenylacetate and are involved in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus found abundant in larvae fed with PS and CS, recognized for its role in degrading plastic and lignocellulose."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus present in high abundance in the gut of CS-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria associated with the PS diets of greater wax moth larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria identified in the mealworm gut contributing to polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria that was used to incubate UV-pre-treated PP films in an experiment focused on plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a bacterium that can degrade polypropylene effectively by aggregating on polymer surfaces, as revealed by previous studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria known for its ability to efficiently decompose micro-sized polyethylene (PE) in landfill sediments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria involved in the degradation of plastic materials when co-cultured with Acinetobacter species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a bacterium known to produce esterases, which contribute to the degradation of compounds like indoxacarb and other polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus is a genus of bacteria that predominates in bran-fed mealworm larvae, influencing their gut microbiota composition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c4804d2c00d1d5fff2b89eaf6da26da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PANTOEA AGGLOMERANS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pantoea agglomerans is a bacterium known for its association with plants and its ability to produce esterases capable of degrading pesticides like indoxacarb."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pantoea agglomerans is a species of bacteria in the genus Pantoea, known for being a plant epiphyte and opportunistic human pathogen."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pantoea agglomerans is a bacterium producing esterases that participate in compound degradation, particularly indoxacarb."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b1c9087e78bc6ba94c2b63fd88d2ca66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2aeb4d96b02258056c16688ea13a9e90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOGENIC ELEMENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biogenic elements are critical nutrients that initiate numerous biochemical reactions in various environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biogenic elements are natural elements essential for life, typically distributed in environments, initiating numerous biological reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIAL COMMUNITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacterial Community refers to the dynamics of different bacterial populations during the nursery rearing of Pacific white shrimp."&lt;SEP&gt;"The bacterial community refers to the diverse group of bacteria living in a particular environment, such as the digestive tract, involved in numerous activities such as nitrogen fixation and enzymatic reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial communities are groups of bacteria living in specific environments, such as the gut of insects, where they can perform essential processes like nitrogen fixation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The bacterial community comprises various bacteria involved in symbiotic relationships within the digestive tracts of organisms, such as T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9fa884bb7540491e8ab975e29c46d1f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITRIFICATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrification is a biological process within the nitrogen cycle where ammonia is oxidized to nitrites and then nitrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrification is a microbial process that converts ammonia to nitrite and nitrate, significantly observed in PS diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrification is the biological conversion of ammonia into nitrites and nitrates, facilitated by nitrifying bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANAMMOX REACTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anammox reactions involve the anaerobic oxidation of ammonium, critical in nitrogen cycling, and occur in certain bacterial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROSPIRA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrospira species are nitrifying bacteria prevalent in environments with nitrogen cycles, significantly present in T. molitor's gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROSOMONAS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrosomonas species are bacteria that oxidize ammonia into nitrite, contributing to nitrification processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene degradation involves breaking down the polymer chain through processes such as thermal degradation, important for waste management and recycling."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene degradation is the breakdown of polystyrene polymers, possibly facilitated by enzymatic activity within microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene degradation is the breakdown of polystyrene materials into simpler compounds, facilitated by microorganisms and enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene degradation refers to the process of breaking down polystyrene, a significant environmental challenge being targeted by microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene degradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene, a type of plastic, into smaller components, which can occur through various environmental processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOCOCCUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rhodococcus is a genus of bacteria involved in the degradation of styrene and potentially related to polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus sp. is a bacterial genus isolated from mangrove sediment, involved in the degradation of polypropylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus species are Actinobacteria involved in degrading complex compounds and supplying nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus sp. is another bacterium found in the gut microbiota, contributing to the degradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus species are bacteria that participate in the biodegradation of plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus sp. is a bacterium species isolated from mangrove sediment, involved in the biodeterioration of polypropylene microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus sp. is a genus of bacteria known for its diverse metabolic pathways and ability to degrade complex organic compounds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edad11f4e8936fcfb69f8616ad364f18&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584</data>
    </node>
