    <node id="&quot;PROTIST CELL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Protist cells host various microbial entities, emphasizing their role in termite gut ecosystems."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f7a49f1c1e76d6e987e0aa499923d3f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROGEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Essential element measured in frass and biomass for protein content analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is a fundamental nutrient involved in amino acid synthesis in organisms, important in protein conversion studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is an essential nutrient converted into usable forms by nitrogen-fixing communities in ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-purity nitrogen (99.999%) is used as a carrier gas in thermogravimetric analyses of material decomposition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is an element involved in biochemical changes in the digestive system, representing intensive digestive processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen (N2) is converted to amino acids by nitrogen-fixing bacteria in T. molitor larvae to aid PET digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is an essential element provided by the gut microbiome during PET biodegradation as a nutrient source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is essential for protein and nucleic acid synthesis and plays a crucial role in the growth metabolism and microbiota of insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is a critical nutrient, comprising a significant portion of yeast cells, vital for mealworm dietary needs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen content is measured in the larvae's frass and biomass, influenced by different diets, important for determining the metabolic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen in this study is a key component in the analysis of mealworm waste, indicating metabolic changes during polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is mentioned as a component whose concentration is measured in insects' biomass and frass as a function of different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is an essential nutrient required for the long-term survival and development of larvae, which is absent in PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen is an essential nutrient for many organisms and is fixed from the atmosphere by certain microorganisms, including those in mealworm guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6338d56b535220f260826703b0f7229&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e356bad5f8c2dd9188b38c0d9462b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7a49f1c1e76d6e987e0aa499923d3f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TERMITES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insects known for having symbiotic microbiota that produce lignocellulose-degrading enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insects known for their ability to degrade lignocellulose, which is similar to a diet low in nitrogen."&lt;SEP&gt;"Termites host complex gut microbiotas, including nitrogen-fixing communities shaped by ectosymbionts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Termites are insects known for their ability to digest cellulose and are studied for their role in nitrogen fixation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Social insects known for harboring diverse gut microorganisms that coevolve with them, facilitating processes like nitrogen fixation and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Termites are social insects known for their symbiotic microbial communities involved in cellulose degradation and nitrogen cycling."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7a49f1c1e76d6e987e0aa499923d3f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLESTERASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme highly active toward PET oligomers and identified in PET-fed groups, facilitating PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylesterase (CES, EC 3.1.1.1) is an enzyme that hydrolyzes carboxylic esters and is involved in drug metabolism. Its expression was affected by various diets in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylesterase (EC 3.1.1.1) is an enzyme catalyzing the hydrolysis of carboxylic esters, playing a role in the metabolism of various substrates, including drugs like isoniazid."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylesterase is an enzyme detected in the gut bacterial flora, possibly playing a role in the degradation of the insecticide indoxylcarb."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that is found in higher abundance in polypropylene-fed larvae, indicating potential roles in the degradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylesterase is an enzyme involved in breaking ester bonds, contributing to the degradation of complex polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylesterase is an enzyme reported to be very active toward PET oligomers, indicating its role in PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylesterase is an enzyme that cleaves ester bonds and modifies the surface of PET, with high activity in PET-fed larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIAL GUT SYMBIONTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacterial gut symbionts are microbes closely associated with the evolution of herbivory in ants."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIAMONDBACK MOTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Diamondback moth refers to the host organism from which culturable gut bacterial flora, including carboxylesterase activity, has been isolated."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c68f3ddf96d927254424a036cd8b9acf</data>
    </node>
