    <node id="&quot;PRIESTIA MEGATERIUM S1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An isolate that exhibits biodegradation capabilities over PE and PS beads, thriving in specific environmental conditions like pH and temperature."&lt;SEP&gt;"Priestia megaterium S1 is a polymer-degrading gut microbe isolated from the gut of Styrofoam-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae. It is capable of degrading polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) beads, making significant physical and structural changes over 180 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Contributed to microbiome study, isolated from Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYROFOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrofoam is a material containing PS metabolites and impurities, used in the study to test its dietary impact on T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrofoam is a type of polystyrene product that is challenging to biodegrade and was used in the study for larvae consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;Styrofoam, a trade name for expanded polystyrene (EPS), is a synthetic polymer widely recognized for its application in insulation, packaging, and disposable consumer products like coffee cups and coolers, owing to its lightweight and insulating properties. This petroleum-based plastic product is notably challenging to degrade, posing significant environmental challenges as it constitutes a major component of global plastic waste. Its resistance to degradation has resulted in a low recycling rate and contributes to environmental pollution.

However, recent research presents promising methods for managing Styrofoam waste biologically. Studies have demonstrated that Styrofoam can be biodegraded by certain insect larvae, particularly mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) and superworms. These insects have been used in experiments to test the material's biodegradation by feeding on Styrofoam as their sole diet. The larvae can consume and break down the Styrofoam thanks to specific gut microbiota that assist in depolymerizing the material, transforming it into substances like carbon dioxide and lipids.

Through these biological processes, there are observable changes in Styrofoam's molecular weight, ultimately contributing to its degradation. The research has also evaluated the nutritional impacts on the insects, such as potential cytotoxicity, oxidative stress, and nitrogen-fixation abilities, expanding the understanding of Styrofoam's biodegradability potential. These studies highlight a potential avenue for utilizing biological processes to address the environmental challenges associated with Styrofoam waste.&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrofoam is the most popular form of polystyrene, widely used and resistant to degradation, producing significant environmental waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6264a99190316d0aeed63b5d6485999e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-568e2e90ce0d3bbb4e21079f4252f5de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2fb515eae7af2b662d5d600120e7001&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8b7b5a840af17cee78d71347e532d51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a7570e6cac531e94c1680c54f5491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6fbbfcf279533f78737706f5751ee5a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a097963ab8ec0ad6b1d51685fc359027&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e67971bdf45c447f2409fddb80e0d8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d7e47d05442ff6cd0cf042db9974f2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene) is another type of plastic whose biodegradation has been observed and compared with LDPE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic that, despite its different chemical structure, resulted in similar weight changes in mealworms as PVC."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene, a type of plastic studied for mealworm consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene, a type of plastic, causes significant mass reduction in mealworms but leads to less mass loss compared to LDPE due to its structural properties."&lt;SEP&gt;High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of polyethylene (PE) known for its high molecular weight, density, strength, and durability. These characteristics make it a significant contributor to global plastic usage, commonly found in various plastic products. However, these same properties also result in HDPE being resistant to microbial degradation. Despite this, HDPE is extensively utilized in biodegradation studies to understand how it behaves in natural environments and its potential impact on ecological systems.

In particular, HDPE has been investigated for its biodegradation by different microorganisms and insects, such as Tenebrio larvae, including species like T. molitor and T. obscurus. These studies aim to assess HDPE's limited depolymerization and biodegradation, especially in comparison to other polyethylene types like LDPE and LLDPE. HDPE serves as a substrate in these experiments, where its effects on gut microbiomes and physiological processes of mealworms are examined.

The polymer's structure and properties, characterized by its hydrophobicity and high strength-to-density ratio, are known to change under certain microbial and environmental conditions. This aspect is crucial for understanding microbial evenness, oxidative stress response, and the broader ecological and physiological effects, including ecotoxicity in larval subjects. Some microbial strains, such as Priestia megaterium S1, have shown the ability to induce weight loss and surface changes in HDPE, indicating some potential for microbial degradation.

Overall, HDPE's role in biodegradation studies is pivotal not only for examining its degradation dynamics but also for evaluating its ecological impacts and potential for innovation in reducing environmental plastic waste.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-109486cd939a227e73354d0478c59ecf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA CHARACTERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16S rRNA characterization is a molecular technique used to identify and classify microorganisms, employed in the study to determine the identity of the Priestia megaterium S1 isolate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPE (SEM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An instrument used to examine the detailed structure of the biofilm on PS film sheets."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM is a type of electron microscope used to observe very fine detail on the surfaces of materials, used in the study to illustrate the biodegradation of plastic by Priestia megaterium S1."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM is a type of microscope used to characterize morphological changes in materials during biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM is a type of microscope used to analyze surface changes in polystyrene due to bacterial degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOLOGICAL OXYGEN DEMAND (BOD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"BOD is a measure of the amount of oxygen consumed by microorganisms while decomposing organic matter."&lt;SEP&gt;"BOD is a measure of the amount of oxygen required by aerobic microorganisms to break down organic matter in water, used in the study to assess the biodegradation rate of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRICARBOXYLIC ACID (TCA) CYCLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A central metabolic pathway involved in the biochemical reactions within the cell, influencing how bacteria and larvae process PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"The TCA cycle is a series of metabolic reactions used by aerobic organisms to generate energy, involved in the biodegradation process by Priestia megaterium S1."&lt;SEP&gt;"The TCA cycle is a series of chemical reactions used by all aerobic organisms to release stored energy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BARLEY BRAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Barley bran is a component of the diet provided to larvae. It contains protein, starch, fat, soluble fibers, insoluble fibers, and ash."&lt;SEP&gt;"Barley bran is a protein and lipid-rich feed used in mealworm cultivation. It provides essential nutrients necessary for the survival of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Barley bran is one of the feeds used for Tenebrio molitor larvae in the experiment, with and without Styrofoam, to determine their survival rate and biodegradation efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon is a chemical element that, along with hydrogen, forms the backbone of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon is a key element in the composition of polymers like PE and PS, and its utilization and percentage loss are measured during the biodegradation process by Priestia megaterium S1."&lt;SEP&gt;"Element found in LDPE, HDPE, and PS beads analyzed to observe changes post-biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon is another element in PP, which, together with hydrogen, does not suffice for larval nutritional requirements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon is a fundamental element involved in the composition of LDPE and is a key component that is converted during the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Element present in plastic beads, analyzed to detect modifications after microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen is an element in the chemical structure of plastics like PE and PS, with its percentage loss being an indicator of biodegradation by Priestia megaterium S1."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen is a chemical element that, along with carbon, constitutes the backbone of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen is one of the elements in PP, which lacks essential nutrients necessary for larval growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL ADHESION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microbial adhesion describes the initial step in biodegradation, where microorganisms attach themselves to the surface of plastic beads."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT LOSS STUDIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A method to quantify the biodegradation of plastic beads by mealworm gut bacterial isolates through weight measurement."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight loss studies are conducted to quantify the amount of material degraded by Priestia megaterium S1 from plastic beads over time."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CO2 EVOLUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CO2 evolution is a measure of carbon dioxide released during the decomposition of materials by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"CO2 evolution refers to the release of carbon dioxide as a byproduct of the biodegradation process, used as an indicator of microbial activity and efficiency in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANNAMALAI UNIVERSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Annamalai University is the institution where some of the research and authors of this study are based."&lt;SEP&gt;"The academic institution where several analyses, such as BOD and FTIR spectroscopy, were conducted."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOMASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biomass is the accumulated biological material resulting from the activity of Priestia megaterium S1 in degrading plastic and producing CO2, water, and ATP as byproducts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the mass of living organisms, which in mealworms can be affected by microplastic ingestion and nutrient assimilation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Organic material derived from living or recently living organisms, often used in research as a substrate for microbial growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the total mass of mealworms which is used as an indicator of their growth and health when fed varying diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass is the organic material derived from the incorporation of depolymerized metabolites and carbon into the mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass produced by mealworms consuming PS is measured as part of the conversion process to analyze carbon recovery."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the organic material that results from the assimilation of carbon during the degradation process in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the mass of living biological organisms, in this context, related to the mass change in mealworms when fed different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the total mass of living organisms, which in the context of Tenebrio molitor, does not increase significantly when fed polystyrene due to insufficient nutritional content."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the organic material derived from T. molitor larvae after feeding on PET, which retains a small percentage of residual PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to organic material that is produced as a result of biological processes, including the breakdown of substances like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the total mass of organisms in a given area or volume, here representing the growth supported by nitrogen fixation in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biomass refers to the total mass of organisms in a given area or volume; here, it pertains to the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC MATERIALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic materials such as Polystyrene (PS) and Polyethylene (PE) are petroleum-based, widely used due to their low cost, durability, and lightweight nature. They are essential components of daily life but pose environmental challenges due to their slow degradation in natural environments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOW DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE is a form of polyethylene characterized by its flexible plastic structure, commonly processed into pellets."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of polyethylene plastic that can be degraded by insects such as rice moth larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a thermoplastic polymer used broadly in containers and products. It is studied for its melting, crystallinity, and susceptibility to thermal and chemical reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low density polyethylene is a type of plastic subject to processes like pyrolysis for conversion into petrochemical feedstocks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low Density Polyethylene is a type of polyethylene polymer used in employment with mealworms to test plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is one of the plastics examined in mealworm studies, showing varying consumability and degradation potential."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of plastic known for its lower density, tested for depolymerization capabilities in beetle larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low density polyethylene is a type of plastic that is resistant to degradation but can be broken down by microorganisms over time."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-014a80fd61677e73708fa28913d7fc56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HDPE is a plastic type that has been studied for its consumption by mealworms, though it is less favored compared to other plastics like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic commonly used for containers and bottles, involved in plastic degradation analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene with a denser and more rigid structure than LDPE, commonly processed into pellets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;*TENEBRIO MOLITOR* (TM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Also known as the yellow mealworm, TM is a species of insects capable of degrading plastic waste and organic matter through its diverse gut microbiota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;*PRISTIA MEGATERIUM* (PM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A gut bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of plastics like PS and PE, utilizing Styrofoam-fed larvae of TM for its bioactivity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;*GALLERIA MELLONELLA* LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Also known as wax moths, these larvae are organisms that have been discovered to consume and help degrade plastic material."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CIRCULAR ECONOMY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECONOMIC MODEL"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A model of economic development that focuses on sustainable practices, including reusing and recycling materials like plastics through biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Circular Economy is an economic model focused on resource efficiency, waste reduction, and the sustainable management of materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3f0582a5d7e55961a1ce4d971d3e4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNGUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGI"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fungi play a significant role in the biodegradation of plastics due to their ability to decompose organic matter."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPER WORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Super worms, or *Zophobas atratus* larvae, are capable of consuming and aiding the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WAX MOTHS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wax moths are insect larvae that have been reported to degrade polyethylene (PE) within their gut, indicating a potential ability to process plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wax moths, or *Galleria mellonella* larvae, are insects that have been shown to consume plastic materials and assist in their breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89ff8edff56325c22cfed3871f4fbf48</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYROFOAM (SF)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrofoam is a type of expanded polystyrene often used in packaging, targeted for biodegradation by certain bacterial strains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON AND HYDROGEN ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This analysis is conducted to study the changes in molecular composition of plastics as they undergo biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STURM TEST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sturm test is a procedure for determining CO2 evolution from the biodegradation of plastics, using microbial cultures and specific reaction conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Sturm Test is a procedure used to determine the biodegradability of materials by measuring the CO₂ evolution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEPARTMENT OF MICROBIOLOGY, FACULTY OF AGRICULTURE, ANNAMALAI UNIVERSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An institution where biodegradation research and experiments were conducted on plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa2e818c436fabed80b9d3d720ebf491</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE, HDPE, AND PS BEADS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE (Low-Density Polyethylene), HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene), and PS (Polystyrene) beads are plastic polymers used in experiments to test bacterial degradation abilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT BACTERIAL STRAINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut bacterial strains are microbial isolates from mealworm larvae capable of interacting with and potentially degrading plastic polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SISCO CHEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SISCO CHEM is a supplier of laboratory chemicals and reagents, providing materials for scientific research."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL RATE (SR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the proportion of lesser waxworms surviving after feeding on different diets such as WC and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate is a measure of the number of live larvae compared to the initial number, expressed as a percentage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate is calculated to measure the percentage of live larvae in an experimental setup."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate measures the percentage of T. molitor larvae that survive under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate is a measure indicating the percentage of mealworm larvae surviving in controlled experimental conditions over a given time period."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate refers to the percentage of mealworms that continue to live over a specified period, which was measured under different dietary conditions in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate refers to the percentage of T. molitor larvae surviving under different feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate refers to the percentage of mealworms surviving under different feeding conditions, compared in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival rate is the percentage of organisms surviving over a given period under specific conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate (SR) is a measure used to determine the survivability of mealworms under various feeding conditions involving PS, bran, and soy protein."&lt;SEP&gt;"SR is a metric to assess the living percentage of larvae during experimentation and is inversely related to death rate."&lt;SEP&gt;"The percentage of mealworms surviving under different conditions, used to assess the impact of nitrogen fixation ability on their biological performance."&lt;SEP&gt;"The proportion of mealworms surviving under different dietary treatments, used to assess overall health and viability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Survival Rate is measured to assess the health and viability of mealworms under various feeding conditions including different types of PS MPs and starvation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56c8b21b1eaba7633b49cea16714ddc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e97dc712df43805c553423ceb65cd6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c7abab621f928edec1e33d1fe2afd4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8ee8f544a1849c80a5a1524c4f2674c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MATH TEST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The MATH test is a method to evaluate the hydrophobicity of bacterial or fungal cell surfaces towards hydrocarbons."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLEAR ZONE ASSAY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Clear zone assay is a method to assess bacterial degradation of plastic by detecting clearing zones around bacteria colonies on agar plates infused with plastic beads."&lt;SEP&gt;"The clear zone assay is a method used to identify bacteria capable of producing enzymes that solubilize polymers resulting in visible degradation zones."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TM LINNAEUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"*TM Linnaeus* refers to the morphological identification of mealworm larvae used in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYPROPYLENE CONTAINERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Food-grade containers used to incubate the mealworm groups in controlled temperature and humidity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene containers are used to house mealworms during experimental setups, maintaining controlled temperature and humidity conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-838d20fb5f3709d303402ea916d8172f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SF (FEED TEST)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SF refers to a specific feed given to mealworms in experimental setups to test its effects on survival and growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ERLENMEYER FLASK&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of laboratory flask used in the preparation and incubation of microbial cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of laboratory glassware used to cultivate and enrich bacterial cultures in experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"An Erlenmeyer flask is used as a container for growing and testing bacterial cultures from mealworm gut isolates."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of laboratory flask used to incubate PS and mealworm gut suspensions during microbial enrichment experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Erlenmeyer Flask is a laboratory container used to mix and incubate cell suspensions and PS pieces in experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94bf67bf1b585ce1050569a2eb0000cd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRIENT BROTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A medium used for cultivating bacterial isolates in biodegradation studies, indicating utilization of plastics as a carbon source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrient broth is a growth medium used to culture bacteria, providing essential nutrients, though PM S1 can thrive even without supplements when plastics are available as carbon sources."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrient broth is a liquid medium used to cultivate bacteria for isolation and growth studies in the laboratory."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutrient broth is a medium used to culture bacterial isolates for biodegradation testing."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BEEF EXTRACT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Beef extract is an ingredient in nutrient broth used to provide nutrients necessary for bacterial growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEPTONE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An organic compound in the MRD liquid used as a nutrient source in microbial studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peptone is a component of nutrient broth, serving as a source of carbon and nitrogen to support bacterial growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peptone, a nutrient in MRD liquid, is used in processing intestinal substances from T. molitor larvae for microbial analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peptone is a component used in Blood Agar media, providing nitrogenous compounds for bacterial growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88659f9848d30be72c37a99b551a9a0a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NACL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An inorganic chemical compound, sodium chloride, used as a component in both the inorganic salt and LB mediums."&lt;SEP&gt;"NaCl, or sodium chloride, is used in nutrient broth to maintain osmotic balance and provide sodium ions."&lt;SEP&gt;"NaCl, or sodium chloride, is present in the growth environment of Mixta tenebrionis, where the bacterium can tolerate concentrations of up to 7.5% w/v, indicating salt tolerance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride used in LB medium to maintain osmotic balance for microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-146789a250a0dbaae0d8438de692e191&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70bab3579af07d0f99a5f948a90fe1e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOLUENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Toluene is a potentially dangerous compound in PS samples, possibly degraded by insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Toluene is used in MATH tests to assess the hydrophobicity of bacterial cell surfaces."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COOMASSIE BLUE SOLUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A dye used in the experiment to stain bacteria, helping identify zones of clearance indicating bacterial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coomassie blue solution is used in clear zone assays to stain bacterial colonies on agar plates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3d7160b00e54118a03f0273d60bfe0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM GUT BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteria found in the gut of mealworms, capable of biodegradation and actively used in various experiments involving different types of plastic beads."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria residing in the guts of mealworms. Their suppression was assessed through antibiotics and gut suspension assays."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworm Gut Bacteria are diverse microorganisms inhabiting the midgut of mealworms, involved in PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworm gut bacteria are microbes found in the gut of mealworms, known for their ability to break down plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SODIUM DODECYL SULPHITE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A chemical used to remove bacterial cells and other cell debris from treated plastic beads."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BOD (IODIMETRIC METHOD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A method used to measure Biological Oxygen Demand in experiments conducted at Annamalai University."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA GENE SEQUENCING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16S rRNA gene sequencing is a method used to examine the gut microbiome diversity of Tenebrio species larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"A molecular biology technique used to study the genetic sequence of microorganisms to understand microbial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PRESTIA MEGATERIUM S1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial species identified through dendrogram analysis based on 16s rRNA gene sequencing."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABACILLUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another genus related to the Bacillus genus found in the gene sequencing dendrogram analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CYTOBACILLUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus within the Bacillaceae family identified alongside Bacillus and Metabacillus in gene sequencing analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFILM FORMATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A process by which bacterial organisms form protective layers on surfaces, such as plastic beads, in biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process by which bacteria adhere to surfaces in a self-produced matrix, as seen with PM S1 on plastic beads, facilitating degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process involving a layer of microorganisms developing on the surface, such as PS film, facilitating enhanced degradation activity over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process by which microbial communities, such as those formed by Acinetobacter sp. and Bacillus sp., attach to surfaces and facilitate the breakdown of materials like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation refers to the growth of bacteria on surfaces, such as PE mulching films, as part of degradation processes observed using SEM."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm Formation is a process where microorganisms adhere to each other and/or to surfaces, becoming embedded within a self-produced matrix of extracellular polymeric substance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the accumulation of microorganisms on a surface, often leading to enhanced microbial survival and resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process by which microorganisms, like Rhodococcus ruber, adhere to surfaces and produce a protective matrix, associated with partial biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a physiological change seen in Enterobacter hormaechei LG3 during PS degradation under anaerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process by which microorganisms adhere to surfaces and produce a protective matrix, playing a role in environmental resilience and pathogenicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is crucial for microbial communities in microbe-mediated biodegradation of plastics under anaerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is the process in which microorganisms adhere to surfaces and develop organized structures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation by bacteria like Exiguobacterium sp. alters material surfaces and initiates biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation on polystyrene films helps bacteria in surface attachment and steady degradation of the plastic material."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56425bd62513be46b9165bfd84f0dd0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXYGEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Measured to assess changes in plastic beads as a result of biodegradation by mealworm gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxygen is crucial for supporting aerobic metabolic degradation of styrene and the health of the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxygen is essential for the degradation of EPS, as it participates in oxidative reactions that help decompose the polymer in combination with UV light."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxygen levels were kept constant to atmospheric concentration to maintain conditions for microbial activity on LDPE films."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SULFUR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Element analyzed in the plastic beads following biodegradation by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sulfur is a trace element important for the active site of nitrogenase, essential for nitrogen fixation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0855091c2b50520d68971a532789a9a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMAL CONDUCTIVITY DETECTION SYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A system used to monitor nitrogen in the context of elemental analysis during biodegradation assessment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR SPECTROSCOPY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A technique employed to analyze chemical structures of plastic beads, both pre- and post-biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A technique used to identify newly formed functional groups or their removal in polymer molecules during the biodegradation study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy is used to examine chemical alterations at the surface of PVC fragments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy used to analyze low-density polyethylene pre- and post-interaction with larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used to obtain the infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid, or gas, utilized here to analyze gaseous products from the decomposition of fecula and Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, used for identifying chemical changes in polymers during digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"A technique used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid, or gas, useful for analyzing organic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectroscopy is a technique used for obtaining the infrared spectrum of absorption of a substance, applied here for characterizing plastic debris."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15323ef47e29f2927f95274e49f4e5bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CENTRALISED INSTRUMENTATION AND SERVICE LABORATORY (CISL)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The facility within Annamalai University where SEM analyses were performed to study bead surfaces."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEM ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) analysis is a process used to examine the surface structure and texture of materials like plastics after microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscopy used to visualize changes in the surface texture of plastic beads after biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Scanning Electron Microscope (SEM) analysis is a method used to examine the surface morphology of HDPE and observe the structural changes caused by bacterial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM (Scanning Electron Microscopy) analysis is used for detailed imaging of sample surfaces to observe structural changes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZONE OF CLEARANCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An area around bacterial colonies on Petri plates indicating biodegradation capabilities of the bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16a478461d1471ca083f90b3f8e7456e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON SOURCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon sources refer to various concentrations used to observe the survival of PM."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFILM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A biofilm is a collective of microorganisms, such as LG3, whereby they adhere to surfaces and exhibit increased resistance to stress, aiding in the degradation of polystyrene materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm formation is a process where microorganisms adhere to surfaces and produce extracellular polymers that facilitate adhesion and provide a structural matrix."&lt;SEP&gt;BIOFILM refers to a structured community of microorganisms that adhere to various surfaces, encapsulated within a self-produced protective and adhesive polymeric matrix. These communities thrive by forming on both inert and living surfaces, such as beads, polyethylene (PE) mulching films, polystyrene (PS) films, and high-density polyethylene (HDPE) surfaces. The formation of biofilms is a biological process that enhances the survival of these microbial communities while significantly contributing to biodegradation activities. 

Biofilms' ability to encapsulate microorganisms within a self-developed matrix facilitates material degradation, particularly of plastic substances like polyethylene and polystyrene. This process is observable with bacterial strains such as Exiguobacterium sp. strain YT2, Rhodococcus ruber, and others present in environments like the digestive tract of Tenebrio molitor, which aid in the deterioration and decrease the hydrophobicity of the materials they colonize.

The aggregation of microorganisms within biofilms allows them to grow on solid substrates, enhancing microbial degradation processes such as those studied using model organisms like E. coli. These biofilms play a crucial role in biodegradation by providing a stable community structure that allows bacteria to effectively adhere, survive, and utilize various substrates, contributing to the ecological breakdown of synthetic materials.&lt;SEP&gt;"Biofilm is a community of microorganisms that adhere to surfaces and affect metal adsorption behavior on microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc75b7e672a7f7c036713dfa2675d79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b12468cce513394dbebb8b4ce27f62d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-361c5d4ba2e879627f4eb7fc4011ee3b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BEER YEAST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Beer yeast serves as a protein source in mealworms' diet, contributing to their nutritional needs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CASEIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A substrate utilized for testing protease activity on NPS emulsion in bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Casein is a protein used in the diet of mealworms to support their growth and is part of the diet study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-262d7fa1c8f2581943a41cf4897fa253&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOY PROTEIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A dietary supplement added to PS to study its effect on consumption by mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy protein is a plant-based protein derived from soybeans, known for its high nutritional value and used to enhance plastic consumption in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy protein is another protein source for mealworms used to evaluate their effect on biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy protein is used as a nutrition-rich co-diet for T. molitor larvae during plastic degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy protein, when used as a co-diet with PS, enhances the consumption rate and digestion of plastics by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy Protein is a plant-based protein used in this study to feed mealworms in combination with PS, increasing rates of PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy protein is used as a supplemental nutrient in experiments to increase the rate of polystyrene degradation by providing necessary nutrients for enzyme production."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy protein is used as a supplementary feed for mealworms along with PS to support their survival rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Soy protein, purchased from General Nutrition Corporation, is used as a dietary supplement in the study to enhance mealworm PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A process where microorganisms such as gut bacteria in mealworms break down synthetic materials like plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation is the breakdown of substances by microorganisms, such as plastics by microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of materials by microorganisms, leading to changes in the physical and chemical properties of substances such as plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation refers to the use of microorganisms to break down materials such as PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation involves the breakdown of compounds by microorganisms, utilizing enzymes to degrade substances such as plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation is the breakdown of substances through the enzymatic actions of microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of materials by microorganisms, playing a significant role in plastic degradation and recycling."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances by microorganisms, such as the degradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation involves the breakdown of PS by microbial communities in the mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation emphasizes the breakdown of substances through microbial action, significant in efforts to alleviate plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances by microorganisms, including the degradation of plastics like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial degradation refers to the breakdown of substances by microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi."&lt;SEP&gt;"The breakdown of starch-based polypropylene through microbial action."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf641df5c55c7ff41895611514cdd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b50c5a85182344022614ffe10dc9382&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CISL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Centralised Instrumentation and Service Laboratory (CISL) is part of the Department of Physics at Annamalai University, where SEM analyses were conducted."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PM (Presumably a specific type of microorganism) is involved in colonizing and potentially degrading plastic beads such as LDPE, HDPE, and PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KOH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"KOH (Potassium Hydroxide) is used in the absorption stage of the Sturm test to contain CO2 released through biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"KOH (potassium hydroxide) is used to adjust the pH of the polymer emulsion to a neutral level of 7.0."&lt;SEP&gt;"KOH, or potassium hydroxide, is used in the process to digest bacterial growth in the experimental setup."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326aabc25dd967951a7845ea221532ac</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BARIUM CHLORIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Barium chloride is a chemical used to precipitate CO2 during the Sturm test by forming barium carbonate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MSM BROTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MSM (Minimal Salt Medium) broth is used to culture specific bacterial isolates in biodegradation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER FILMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer films refer to the thin plastic materials used in biodegradation experiments to evaluate CO2 evolution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROOM TEMPERATURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Room temperature is the environmental condition at which the Sturm test was conducted over a four-week period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646a65acc7cbdc1d20742888b17613a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;S1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"S1 is a bacterial isolate from mealworm gut that shows effective growth and biodegradation capabilities on nutrient agar medium, particularly in degrading LDPE, HDPE, and PS by utilizing them as carbon sources."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;S2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"S2 is one of the five bacterial isolates from mealworm gut that were screened for their ability to degrade PE and PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;S3&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"S3 is a bacterial isolate from mealworm gut, part of the screening process for potential degradation of PE and PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;S4&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"S4 is one of the mealworm gut bacterial isolates involved in screening for plastic degradation capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;S5&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"S5 is a bacterial isolate identified in the mealworm gut, assessed for its ability to degrade polymers like PE and PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CGK5&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CGK5 is a bacterial strain characterized by high hydrophobicity, affecting its ability to form biofilms and degrade plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOFILM PRODUCTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biofilm production refers to the accumulation of bacteria on surfaces, which can enhance the breakdown of polymers through microbial action."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON-STARVED CONDITIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon-starved conditions are an experimental setup that increases bacterial hydrophobicity, affecting adherence and degradation rates of polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROPHOBICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of how water-repellent a surface is, used to assess changes to the PS film after biofilm removal."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity describes the tendency of bacterial cells to attach to non-polar surfaces, influencing their biodegradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a measure of how a substance repels water, analyzed here with respect to the mealworm gut supernatant."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity refers to the measure of how a substance interacts with water, crucial in the analysis of the mealworm gut supernatant's ability to act as an emulsifying agent."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity refers to the property of being water-repellent, which decreased in polyethylene microplastics after being processed by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A property of being water-repellent, seen in PP due to its hydrocarbon chains, affecting its interaction with enzymes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is a physical property of polymer surfaces that can influence the enzymatic degradation process and is a measure of how water-repellent the surface is."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophobicity is the tendency of a surface to repel water, a property that changes in aged microplastics, affecting biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hydrophobicity of aged PE is reduced due to surface oxidation, affecting microbial adhesion and degradation efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40357516e3f9dd4b2969b73efe444c28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIBEDI AND SIL 2013&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CITATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tribedi and Sil 2013 reference an important study regarding bacterial hydrophobicity's impact on polymer degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KAVITHA AND BHUVANESWARI 2021&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CITATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kavitha and Bhuvaneswari 2021 provide insights into bacterial surface properties like hydrophobicity under different conditions."</data>
