    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA SEQUENCING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16S rRNA Sequencing is a technique used to analyze and identify bacterial communities in the gut microbiome of insect larvae engaged in polypropylene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA sequencing is a method used to identify and classify bacterial strains based on their genetic sequences."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA sequencing is a technique used to identify and analyze the bacterial species present in mixed microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"A molecular biology technique used to identify bacterial species within the mixed microbial enrichment by analyzing sequences from the 16S ribosomal RNA gene."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA sequencing is used to identify and compare bacteria present within a sample, crucial for microbial community analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"A method used to identify and verify the genetic identity of bacterial strains NyZ450 and NyZ451 for their degradation capacity."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA sequencing is used to molecularly identify and compare the sequences of the bacterial isolates with known databases."&lt;SEP&gt;"A method used to analyze the microbial composition in T. molitor larvae's gut to understand changes under different feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"16S rRNA sequencing is a method used for the taxonomic identification of bacterial species in this study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39c4ff9e5812f88117b37503a70b0479&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939fc2ea222a49f84f9bdc11f9801367&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GENUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The experiment represents the gut microbes at the genus level, focusing on their relative abundances and changes in different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"These are genera within the Bacillaceae family, associated with the PM S1 isolate due to their genetic similarity, as determined through 16S rRNA gene sequencing."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FAMILY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACILLACEAE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of bacteria in which the PM S1 isolate falls, known for its application in environmental biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLITE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolites are the intermediates and products of metabolism, which may include by-products of polystyrene degradation by the symbiotic bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"The term metabolite in this context refers to intermediate degradation products formed during the breakdown of nanopolystyrene by gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"These synthetic materials serve as carbon sources for PM S1, which leads to their degradation and further metabolization by the bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLIC MEASURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure related to metabolism, although it is not explicitly detailed in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"BOD is a metric that indicates the amount of dissolved oxygen consumed by PM S1 as it breaks down organic compounds in plastic during aerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic measure refers to the quantitative assessment of metabolic processes, relevant for determining rates of plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Measurements relating to metabolism, or how organisms process substances necessary for maintenance and growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"A quantitative assessment of metabolic processes, such as those linked to the degradation of synthetic polymers by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53ab7dd83bf51674598c6a5bdd146b90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-412d934721b214c2cd0565531172171c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL REACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical reactions involve the transformation of substances, such as the breakdown of polystyrene by symbiotic bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"This is the process by which PM S1 enzymatically breaks down plastics such as LDPE, HDPE, and PS, resulting in a significant reduction of their weight."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE, HDPE, PS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Low-density polyethylene (LDPE), high-density polyethylene (HDPE), and polystyrene (PS) are types of plastics tested for biodegradation by PM S1 in the experimental study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXTRACELLULAR MATRIX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An extracellular matrix is a complex network produced by microorganisms like PM S1, aiding in biofilm formation on the surface of plastic beads during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical moieties in polymer molecules whose presence or alteration are detected during biodegradation analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups like N-H stretching, C-H stretching, etc., are identified in FTIR spectroscopy and change during PM S1's treatment of PS, signifying chemical structure alterations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are modifications of molecular structures assessed using FTIR to understand plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules responsible for characteristic chemical reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups in microplastics undergo oxidation as part of the degradation process observed in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groupings of atoms in molecules that determine chemical reactions, observed with FTIR during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups refer to specific groups of atoms within molecules responsible for the characteristic chemical reactions, whose changes were detected in mealworm frass indicating chemical alterations in PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Groups identified during FTIR and NMR analyses that indicate depolymerization and oxidation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules that have characteristic properties and reactivity, important for the modification of PE to enhance biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups such as ether and carbonyl groups represent specific groupings of atoms within molecules that determine their chemical reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"In the study, FTIR analysis showed changes in the functional groups of PU foam after being processed by mealworms, including bonds such as C-O-C, C-N, and C=O."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups in polymers like PS and PU change during larvae digestion, aiding in the process of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groupings of atoms within molecules that have their own characteristic properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules, newly formed in the frass, indicating chemical alterations of plastics after mealworm digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules responsible for the characteristic chemical reactions of those molecules, identified using FTIR in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules that determine the molecules' chemical reactions. Changes in these groups indicate chemical alteration during PP biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groupings of atoms within molecules that have their own characteristic properties, involved in chemical reactions during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups such as oxygen-containing moieties are observed in aged microplastics, indicating chemical changes during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Specific groups of atoms within molecules that are responsible for characteristic reactions, including those observed in degraded plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d27c41781c64c2b407faecc4bc31466&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39596f0742e3d89f278017df88b8065b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2338bb97657d74ab5d8a444849037692&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262bed73e929944e03b9f72769b8196c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78830eb6f2457838fda4728b21c17bed</data>
    </node>
