    <node id="&quot;MICROCOCCUS LUTEUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Micrococcus luteus strain CGK112 is isolated from cow dung and has demonstrated efficient biofilm-forming ability and degradation potential toward high-density polyethylene (HDPE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Micrococcus luteus strain CGR112 is isolated from cow dung and exhibits biofilm-forming ability and HDPE degradation potential."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS CEREUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of bacterium in the phylum Firmicutes, known for its role in PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus cereus strain CGK5 is associated with cow feces and is utilized in the degradation of commercially available high-density polyethylene (HDPE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus cereus is a bacterium noted for its ability to decompose polyethylene as part of biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOCOCCUS RUBER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">Rhodococcus ruber is an actinomycete bacterium renowned for its ability to colonize diverse surfaces, form robust biofilms, and biodegrade various plastic materials, notably polystyrene and polyethylene. This bacterium plays a significant role in synthetic polymer breakdown, demonstrating its importance in environmental bioremediation efforts. Particularly known for its partial biodegradation capacity, Rhodococcus ruber employs small molecule transport systems that assist in the degradation of polyethylene terephthalate (PET) and other synthetic polymers. Its remarkable plastic-degrading abilities make it a candidate for addressing plastic pollution issues. Despite one contradictory reference to it as a fungus, Rhodococcus ruber is consistently identified as a bacterium across descriptions.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aad5e97885ea1116956513db244b8fb6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d3c25b9affee2f535cb531a09a2384&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a54f7d9e0a43fb94e2fc35f93656fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE bags had holes and scratches caused by mealworms during experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a form of polyethylene characterized by long and short chain branching, resulting in low crystallinity due to hindered close packing."&lt;SEP&gt;Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a widely used thermoplastic made from the monomer ethylene, characterized by its low density, high flexibility, and resilience. It is commonly found in various products such as plastic bags and containers, and is also utilized in agriculture due to its toughness and adaptability. LDPE's polymer structure features lower crystallinity and more branching, which makes it less resistant to biodegradation compared to other forms of polyethylene. As a result, it has become a significant focus of research regarding its environmental impact and biodegradation processes. Studies have shown that LDPE can be biodegraded by various microorganisms, including fungi such as Aspergillus clavatus strain JASK1, bacteria from earthworm guts, and a tri-culture yeast consortium derived from wood-feeding termites. Additionally, LDPE has been tested for biodegradation by mealworms and T. molitor larvae, and can even serve as a carbon source in biodegradation experiments. Despite its widespread use, ongoing research aims to better understand and enhance the biodegradability of LDPE in order to mitigate its environmental footprint.&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of Polyethylene known for its lower density, tensile strength, and other physical properties that affect its digestion by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density Polyethylene (LDPE) is a type of plastic used in the experiment to assess mealworm mass change, indicating its properties and interaction with mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE is a type of polyethylene plastic that can be depolymerized by specific microbes and enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene characterized by its lower density and high flexibility, often used for plastic films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Low-density polyethylene is a type of plastic associated with biodegradation facilitated by gut microbes in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a53fba6106c000acc0dac3484d37a4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (HDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HDPE bags showed marks and holes caused by mealworms during experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a major type of polyethylene with long linear chains, minimal branching, and high crystallinity of 60-80%, used in various plastic applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a plastic material used as a carbon source in biodegradation studies with mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic known for its strength, which can also be degraded by specific bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic that microorganisms like Micrococcus luteus and Bacillus cereus strains have the potential to degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Density Polyethylene is a strong, durable plastic used in many applications, undergoing biodegradation by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene known for its high strength-to-density ratio, which is also subject to various environmental degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene known for its high strength-to-density ratio, used in products like containers and pipes."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene known for high crystallinity and density, making it more resistant to microbial degradation compared to other types."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Density Polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of polyethylene film that is very stable and difficult to degrade in the environment, commonly used in various plastic products."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Density Polyethylene is a type of polyethylene known for its high density, making it sturdy and durable, commonly used in products like plastic bottles and corrosion-resistant piping."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a synthetic polymer known for its strength and density, which is subject to photochemical and microbial degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene with a high degree of crystallinity, composed of linear chains packed closely together with little to no branching."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is another type of Polyethylene distinguished by high density and different physical properties which influence its consumption by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density Polyethylene (HDPE) is a plastic type used in the experiment, affecting mealworm mass change during different time intervals."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic that can be depolymerized by certain insects within their gut environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene (HDPE) is a type of plastic known to form microplastics that can penetrate cells and cause physiological disruptions in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene known for its high strength-to-density ratio, commonly used in manufacturing and packaging."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRI-CULTURE YEAST CONSORTIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A constructed tri-culture yeast consortium from wood-feeding termites is used for the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE) plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bioplastics are biodegradable materials produced from biowastes, playing a crucial role in circular bioeconomy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CIRCULAR BIOECONOMY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Circular bioeconomy is an economic model that focuses on transforming waste materials, primarily of organic origin, into valuable products, emphasizing sustainability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOREFINERY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biorefinery is a process that converts biomass into energy and other beneficial by-products, integrating with the principles of circular bioeconomy."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9a882603f777986db9d8fc65cea91dce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORMS (TENEBRIO MOLITOR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworms (Tenebrio molitor) are insects that can be fed on fresh plant material-supplemented diets and are studied for their growth performance and nutrient composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are insects used in the study of polystyrene biodegradation, hypothesized to degrade plastic through their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, specifically the species Tenebrio molitor, are beetle larvae known for their ability to consume and assist in the biodegradation of polystyrene and other materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are protein-rich agricultural insect pests naturally able to consume and degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, which are known for their ability to degrade plastic materials like EPS through their intestinal microbiota. They undergo a complete life cycle transformation and are used as biological research models."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, also known as Tenebrio molitor, are macro-organisms that can seemingly chew and digest polyethylene, although the exact digestion process is not explicitly confirmed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, or Tenebrio molitor, are larvae which can chew through LDPE but presumably do not chew as efficiently as Galleria mellonella."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, specifically Tenebrio molitor, are larvae used in the study for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, the larval form of the mealworm beetle, are capable of degrading plastic through gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are the larvae of the beetle Tenebrio molitor, known for their ability to degrade plastics such as polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are the larvae of the beetle Tenebrio molitor, used in this study to investigate the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are larval forms of the insect Tenebrio molitor, known for their ability to biodegrade polystyrene and complete their life cycle while feeding on it."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681ece0a8beca8b22f3c6aa11a564f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a7768b61a3d166f2cd01d4e87ac88cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32e10966ec80ea585746639b6be7e04e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOME OF MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The gut microbiome consists of various microorganisms residing in the mealworms' digestive system, playing a significant role in breaking down PS MPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbiome of mealworms contributes to the degradation process of polystyrene and other diets, showing specific responses based on the consumed materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e136b8a35c0d60811b9daf0cbd5729f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOPOLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biopolymers are sustainable products produced by microalgae and cyanobacteria, especially when cultivated in wastewater, providing an alternative to conventional plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROALGAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microalgae are microorganisms capable of producing biopolymers when cultivated under specific conditions, such as in wastewater."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microalgae are studied for bioenergy production and their role in carbon capture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microalgae are microorganisms used in bioenergy production with the assistance of graphene oxide quantum dots for carbon capture."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d8e18d03240e025c60621d616f1de85b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CYANOBACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYLUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cyanobacteria are photosynthetic bacteria that, like microalgae, can produce biopolymers and play a role in ecosystems and biotechnology."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cyanobacteria is a phylum whose relative abundance decreased in the larval gut microbiota due to PS and CS exposure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CORN STRAW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A waste product from corn cultivation, used as a comparative diet material in studies involving mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Corn straw is one of the lignocellulosic materials that T. molitor larvae can degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"Corn straw is used as a dietary component for mealworms, to observe their biological responses and the impact on their gut microbiome during biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WASTEWATER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wastewater is used as a cultivation medium for microorganisms like microalgae and cyanobacteria to produce biopolymers, providing an avenue for biotechnological innovation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wastewater, often untreated, is a byproduct released into waterways, leading to microplastic contamination."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRIENT COMPOSITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrient composition refers to the chemical substances that make up the dietary content of mealworms, affecting their growth performance and biological functions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL ASSISTED HIGH IMPACT POLYSTYRENE (HIPS) DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This process involves microorganisms aiding in the breakdown of high impact polystyrene, a type of plastic, as part of biodegradation research."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEVELOPMENTAL PLASTICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Developmental plasticity describes how mealworms can adjust their development rates and molts in response to different diet conditions, showcasing adaptive flexibility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Developmental plasticity refers to the ability of an organism to change its development in response to environmental conditions, as studied in Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION OF PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A process involving the biological breakdown of plastic materials by organisms such as insect larvae and their associated microflora."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation of plastics involves breaking down plastic polymers like PS through biological processes, such as those mediated by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation of plastics is a process where microorganisms such as bacteria, fungi, and mealworms break down plastic materials into simpler substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation of plastics is the breakdown and assimilation of plastic materials by biological means, often through the activity of microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation of plastics is the process by which microorganisms break down plastic materials into smaller molecules, eventually mineralizing them."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation of plastics is the breakdown of plastic materials by biological means, often involving microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85d18d5d86ed1c061dc44d69e9c09ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC WASTE POLLUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic Waste Pollution is an environmental issue arising from the accumulation of plastics that are difficult to degrade, leading to ecological and health risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic waste pollution is the accumulation of plastic objects and particles in the Earth's environment that adversely affects wildlife, wildlife habitat, and humans."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAE OF TENEBRIO MOLITOR LINNAEUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The larvae of Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, are insects in the family Tenebrionidae that are capable of biodegrading polystyrene and polyethylene foams."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR LINNAEUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus, commonly known as the yellow mealworm or mealworm beetle, is a species of beetle from the family Tenebrionidae and falls within the Tenebrionini tribe. This species is renowned for its larvae, known as yellow mealworms, which have received significant attention in biodegradation studies due to their remarkable ability to consume and break down synthetic polymers such as polystyrene and polyethylene foams. The degradation process occurs in the larval gut, where gut microorganisms play a crucial role. These mealworms are not only capable of biodegrading plastics like Styrofoam but are also involved in the bioconversion of lignocellulosic wastes. Furthermore, Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus is scientifically recognized for its contribution to addressing plastic waste issues through its intrinsic capacity to biodegrade and mineralize various synthetic materials.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1301f6942d17092ef7b22ac40f19f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COLEOPTERA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORDER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Coleoptera is an order of beetles, which includes the family Tenebrionidae to which Tenebrio molitor belongs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects known as beetles, which includes the species used in biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is the largest order of insects, known commonly as beetles, which includes species like Tenebrio molitor that have roles in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects that includes beetles. Tenebrio molitor, being part of this order, is known for its adaptability and ease of industrial breeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects that includes beetles. It is mentioned in the context of insect larvae that can degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects that includes species such as beetles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects known as beetles, which includes the family Tenebrionidae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is the taxonomic order under which Mealworm is classified, commonly known as beetles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is the order to which Tenebrio molitor belongs, including various beetle species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects that includes species such as Zophobas atratus and Tenebrio molitor, whose larvae are involved in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The order of insects to which both superworms and yellow mealworms belong, characterized by hard body cases and diverse feeding habits."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is the order of insects that includes beetles, characterized by their hardened forewings and diverse forms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects within the class Insecta, commonly known as beetles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera is an order of insects that includes beetles, such as darkling beetles, which play roles in biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Coleoptera, commonly known as beetles, includes species that have been observed consuming and decomposing plastics in vivo."&lt;SEP&gt;"The order to which the family Tenebrionidae belongs, includes beetles like darkling beetles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIONIDAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of darkling beetles with species capable of biodegrading plastics such as PS and LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrionidae is the beetle family to which organisms like Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms and superworms, belong."&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrionidae is a large, cosmopolitan family of beetles commonly referred to as darkling beetles. This family comprises over 20,000 species, including economically and ecologically significant species like Tenebrio molitor, Zophobas atratus, and Zophobas morio. Noted for their resilience in harsh environments, these beetles exhibit considerable adaptability, thriving in diverse habitats and on varied diets, which can include polystyrene. Tenebrionidae species are renowned for their larvae's ability to biodegrade various plastic polymers, contributing to biodegradation processes. This family belongs to the order Coleoptera and is recognized for its role in both ecological and biodegradation contexts, with the mealworm (Tenebrio molitor) being one of its most well-known members.&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrionidae is the family classification for darkling beetles, whose larvae have shown capabilities in degrading plastics like PS and LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrionidae is the biological family to which Tenebrio molitor belongs, commonly known as darkling beetles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-db672d5836dfe372ee7c47ee4dd6fbd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a221e0c62ecd5a2502ec0a4473ab0999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35eed7e10fb1cc08baaecab1b1cffad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-302543e7a2fdb3596cc8a10e7072ec01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4304c39ef3427872131caf77e7cdb438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38283df790b03806f99ca90272a1000f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7b499dc783c21f764eb35989a449f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cff93a2e1d68c6ed1fcd9f720197238f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMOSPHERE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A scientific journal in which studies related to the biodegradation of plastics were published."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemosphere is a publication in which results regarding degradation of plastic waste by microbial strains are discussed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemosphere is a scientific journal that publishes research papers on chemicals in the environment, including studies on biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A scientific journal where the study on polystyrene digestion by yellow mealworms was published."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemosphere is a scientific journal that publishes research related to environmental science and chemistry."&lt;SEP&gt;"A scientific journal that publishes research on the chemistry and impact of environmental pollutants."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21bee96879472a1c6df454d862dc9e90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPRINGER NATURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Springer Nature is a global publishing company that disseminates scientific, technical, and medical content, such as the study on biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91df729b04e3d570337e02872cf1c38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TCA CYCLE&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-970b4f5f0c494fbc90aab36156ecc044&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a708f09b97ac93da05bd1d20bdfe174&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae</data>
      <data key="d1">"The TCA Cycle, also known as the Krebs cycle, is a key metabolic process involved in energy production through the oxidation of acetyl-CoA."&lt;SEP&gt;"The TCA cycle is a key metabolic pathway that processes phenylacetyl-CoA and other metabolites for energy production in microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The TCA cycle is involved in the energy production and metabolic activities enhancing the biodegradation process by Priestia megaterium S1."&lt;SEP&gt;"The TCA cycle, also known as the Krebs cycle, is a key energy-producing process in mitochondria."</data>
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE (PE), POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
      <data key="d1">"PM S1 utilizes PE and PS as sole carbon sources, leading to significant degradation of these polymers during growth, confirmed by experimentation with weight loss and media changes."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOCHEMICAL OXYGEN DEMAND (BOD)&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
      <data key="d1">"The mealworm gut bacteria, through the action of PM S1, increase the biochemical oxygen demand as they degrade plastics, indicating their metabolic processes."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS CEREUS NJDI&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillus cereus NJDI is notable for its efficient biodegradation abilities of LDPE, setting a comparative standard for the activities of other bacterial isolates like PM S1."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS STRAINS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-2fd075a78abe1dca64ddea928d3f94b7</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillus strains have been evaluated for their efficacy in degrading HIPS, confirming the broad potential of this genus for environmental biodegradation tasks."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOTORULA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rhodotorula is a fungal genus involved in the biodegradation of plastics and is found in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodotorula is a genus of yeast-like fungi implicated in the degradation of polyethylene (PE) in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodotorula shows higher relative abundance in PS-fed and PE-fed yellow mealworms, indicating a specific functional group associated with PS and PE degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodotorula is a specific fungal genus associated with plastic degradation, showing significant increases in plastic-fed groups compared to bran-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CRYPTOCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cryptococcus exhibits high relative abundance in PS-fed and PE-fed yellow mealworms, suggesting its role as a functional group in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cryptococcus is a genus of fungi significantly involved in the degradation of polystyrene (PS) in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cryptococcus is a genus within the Tremellaceae family, including species like Cryptococcus neoformans, which can cause infections in humans."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cryptococcus is a fungal genus that increased significantly in abundance in plastics-fed groups, particularly associated with PE and PS degradation in yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cryptococcus is a genus of fungi that are strongly associated with plastic degradation in yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISSATCHENKIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Issatchenkia is a genus of yeast associated with the degradation of both polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GPC ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) Analysis is a technique used to determine molecular weights of polymers, illustrating changes due to degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) analysis is used to determine the molecular weights and molecular weight distribution (MWD) of additives in the foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC (Gel Permeation Chromatography) analysis measures the molecular size distribution of polymers like PET, indicating structural changes during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) analysis is a method used to determine the molecular weights and the degree of polymer degradation in plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel permeation chromatography, a technique used for separating and characterizing polymers based on their size."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC, or Gel Permeation Chromatography, analysis provides information on depolymerization and degradation indicators like molecular weights of PS residues."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC Analysis (Gel Permeation Chromatography) is used to evaluate the molecular weight distribution of polymers in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC analysis, or gel permeation chromatography, is used to analyze polymer degradation in frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"GPC Analysis (Gel Permeation Chromatography) is employed to measure the molecular weight distribution of polymers in the biodegradation study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) Analysis is employed to determine the molecular weight distribution (MWD) and depolymerization pattern of polystyrene residues after mealworm consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT BACTERIAL-MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The gut bacterial-microbiome includes the population of bacteria residing in the digestive tract of insects, impacting functions such as digestion and plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT FUNGAL-MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The gut fungal-microbiome encompasses the fungal species in the insect digestive tract, contributing to the degradation process of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACHROIA GRISELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A moth species known for larvae that may play a role in breaking down plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of lesser waxworm larvae that can ingest and biodegrade plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia Grisella is an organism known as the lesser waxworm, which has shown an ability to degrade high-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia Grisella, known as the lesser waxworm, is capable of efficiently biodegrading polyethylene waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella is an insect reported to have the ability to break down plastics like PS, PE, PVC, and PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella is commonly known as the lesser waxworm and is capable of biodegrading polyethylene (HDPE) waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, the lesser waxworm, is another insect species that has shown ability to ingest and degrade plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, the lesser waxworm, is known for its ability to degrade polyethylene waste efficiently."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, known as the lesser waxworm, is a species capable of biodegrading polyethylene, a common plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, or lesser waxworm, has been observed participating in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, known as lesser waxworm larvae, have been shown to process and degrade plastics like high-density polyethylene under varying conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella is a plastic-eating lesser wax moth species documented for efficiently biodegrading polyethylene waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella larvae showed potential for polyethylene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The genus of the lesser waxworm which belongs to the Pyralidae family, known for its ability to consume and degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella, also known as the lesser waxmoth, can efficiently biodegrade polyethylene (HDPE) waste, presenting a natural solution for plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achroia grisella is a lesser waxworm known for efficiently biodegrading polyethylene waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ee82f655853704e6653d8146973278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLODIA INTERPUNCTELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Known as the Indian mealmoth, capable of ingesting and biodegrading certain plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia Interpunctella, commonly known as the Indian mealmoth, can consume polyethylene films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella is known for its larvae's ability to degrade plastics like PS, PE, PVC, and PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, commonly known as the Indian-meal moth, is an insect that has demonstrated capabilities in plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indian mealmoth, is another insect studied for its potential in plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indianmeal moth, is an insect used in research studies for isolating plastic-degrading bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, commonly known as the Indian meal moth, is a species of pantry pest whose larvae can damage polyethylene films by chewing, potentially indicating a role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella is the scientific name for the Indian mealmoth, the larval stage of which is known as waxworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indianmeal moth, is a common pest known for infesting stored food products."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella is a genus of moth known for midgut bacteria capable of biodegrading organic wastes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella is a species of moth larva known for its ability to penetrate packaging films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, also known as the Indianmeal moth, is a species of moth larvae that affects packaging materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, or Indian-meal moths, were studied for their potential in degrading polyethylene plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4da90774e2e5cb1fea30fd5aa390760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSECT LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insect larvae are involved in the biodegradation of hydrocarbon-based plastics, emphasizing their potential role in addressing plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae are the immature stage of insects involved in the study of plastic biodegradation and its impact at this developmental stage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae are the juvenile forms of insects, many of which have been identified as capable of ingesting and degrading plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae harbor plastic-degrading microbes in their guts, aiding in plastics degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insects that secrete emulsifiers to aid in plastic biodegradation, potentially accelerating the process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae like superworms and yellow mealworms are studied for their ability to consume and degrade plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insect larvae are studied for their potential to degrade plastic materials, thus contributing to discussions on plastic waste management."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d642e5476221b3ea202c68d7e6070b96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRAN DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bran diet was used as a control in experiments to compare the effects of plastic ingestion in insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran Diet is the nutritional component fed to control groups to compare against plastic diets regarding the growth and survival of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran diet is used as a control diet in experiments to compare the effects of plastic diets on the gut bacterial community structure in superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran Diet refers to mealworms fed with bran as a control and comparison to those fed with polystyrene in experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"A diet made of bran given to mealworms, contrasting with the PU diet to measure enzyme activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The bran diet refers to a natural diet used in the experiment to compare microbiome diversity and functionality against the PU diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"The bran diet represents one of the feeding options for mealworms, affecting their gut microbiome composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran Diet is a non-plastic diet used as a control to study gut microbial differences among the test larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran diet is the natural feedstock used as a control in experiments with Tenebrio molitor larvae to assess the impact of plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"A diet given to control samples in experiments, compared against test samples feeding on LDPE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6746a90a5be233f46211ddf406c02e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATR-FTIR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A technique used to analyze changes in nanoplastic samples by assessing their functional group peaks and indices such as carbonyl index."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance - Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a technology used to analyze materials like PET for degradation potential in marine environments."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is a technique used to characterize major functional groups in frass, indicating plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is a technology used to analyze frass samples and confirm the degradation of plastics in the guts of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Attenuated Total Reflectance-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, a method to analyze chemical changes and degradation in plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Attenuated Total Reflection Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) is a technique used to study molecular characteristics and changes in plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Attenuated total reflection Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (ATR-FTIR) is a method to detect new functional groups in samples during microbial plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is utilized for detailed surface analysis of substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a technique used to analyze changes in functional groups in PS and PU foam before and after being consumed by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR, or Attenuated Total Reflectance Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, is used to identify chemical structures and bonds in PS films, identifying new peaks associated with microbial treatment."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is a spectroscopic technique used to characterize the oxidation and degradation of mask materials after being processed by yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Attenuated Total Reflectance-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy, a technique used to analyze the chemical properties of mask residues."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is an analytical technique used to confirm foam plastic biodegradation and depolymerization after mealworm digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR is an analytical technique used to identify functional groups in the plastics and frass of yellow mealworms, indicating chemical changes from plastic consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Attenuated Total Reflection Fourier Transformed Infrared Spectroscopy used to evaluate oxidation and biodegradation of ingested polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16700b183fc641abfa74ad7fbb0917fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CITROBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus detected in PE-fed groups, participating in gut microbiome dynamics and nitrogen metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter is a bacterium found to be associated with plastic diets, specifically PE and PP, in superworms and mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria associated with the degradation of polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP) in insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter is a genus of gram-negative coliform bacteria, citrobacter species can cause infections like gastroenteritis, measured in feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria known to cause infections, particularly in the urinary tract and intestines."&lt;SEP&gt;"A genus of nitrogen-fixing bacteria identified in the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter is another genus of bacteria associated with nitrogen fixation in the insect gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter is a genus of bacteria that colonizes the digestive tracts of organisms, such as mealworms, especially when they are fed with plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPHINGOBACTERIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus associated with gut microbiome composition, found in PE-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sphingobacterium is a bacterium associated with PS diets in superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sphingobacterium is a genus of bacteria associated with the biodegradation of polystyrene (PS) in superworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DYSGONOMONAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of bacteria that plays a role in the degradation of PET polymers as part of the mealworm's gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Associated with PS diet in superworms, indicating a significant role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dysgonomonas is a bacterium also associated with PS diet in superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Dysgonomonas is a genus of bacteria found in the gut microbiome, associated with PS degradation in superworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KLEBSIELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria found in the mealworms' gut, involved in nitrogen fixation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a bacterium associated with mealworms in the context of plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria linked with polystyrene (PS) biodegradation in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a type of bacteria that does not support gut health and is associated with higher protein content and frass nitrogen of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria capable of using bioplastic as a sole carbon source, found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is another genus of bacteria identified in mealworms, highlighting variation in microbiome composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria with which the bacterium WJ2020 is associated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus with which strain WJ2020 is phylogenetically related, showing high similarity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria identified as significant nitrogen-fixing symbionts in the gut of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Klebsiella is a genus of bacteria known to have representatives that are nitrogen-fixing symbionts in Styrofoam-eating mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterobacter genus is associated with bacteria capable of degrading polystyrene, particularly from partially degraded plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a bacterial genus detected in the gut microbiomes of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a bacterial genus identified as predominant in the gut of Tenebrio molitor, involved in potential degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of bacteria associated with the degradation of polypropylene (PP) in superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is associated with PP diets in superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a predominant genus within the larval gut microbiota in all diet groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus in the Enterobacteriaceae family found in mealworm gut that may play a role in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of common gram-negative bacteria involved in opportunistic infections, analyzed for its abundance in feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of common Gram-negative, facultatively anaerobic, rod-shaped bacteria of the family Enterobacteriaceae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter is a genus of facultative PS-degrading gut bacteria isolated from mealworms, functioning under oxygen-limited conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter represents a genus of bacteria capable of nitrogen fixation, found in the guts of some insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d4e1982efefa47339205e972562b51f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KLUYVERA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria found to be part of the nitrogen-fixing gut microbiota in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a bacterium species present in the superworm gut microbiome and involved in polystyrene digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a genus of bacteria associated with polypropylene (PP) degradation in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is associated with plastic diets in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a genus of bacteria identified among the gut microbiota of larvae across all dietary groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kluyvera is a predominant bacterium in the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor larvae, associated with polypropylene depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e127a4930b6154498b93c2e94f2b7c20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MANGROVIBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mangrovibacter is a genus dominant in the gut microbiome, playing a role in the biodegradation processes of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mangrovibacter is related to PU diet in superworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ASPERGILLUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fungal genus noted to be significantly overrepresented in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a fungal genus with plastic biodegradation capabilities, observed in the mealworm study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a fungus involved in PS plastic degradation in mealworm guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is reported for the degradation of PE plastic in wax moth larvae and increases in PS-fed lesser mealworms, highlighting its potential as a plastic degrader."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus within the Trichocomaceae family, known for several important species including pathogenic ones like Aspergillus versicolor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a fungus with high presence in PS and CS-fed mealworms, known for its ability to degrade plastic and lignocellulose."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi known for its biodegradation capacities, including degrading plastics like PE and PU. It appeared in all mask diets with a high relative abundance, indicating its potential role in mask degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi found in the gut microbiota of polystyrene-fed insects and is capable of participating in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi known to degrade several plastic materials, including LDPE and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a genus of fungi, consistently found in PS samples with notable differential abundance compared to CT samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus is a type of fungi reported to have plastic degradation ability and was observed in PU-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYPODERMAELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hypodermaella is another fungus involved in PS plastic degradation in mealworm guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRICHODERMA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fungal genus that appeared to be overrepresented in PS samples as per DESeq2 analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a fungus known to participate in PS plastic degradation in mealworm guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is noted to increase in PS-fed lesser mealworms, suggesting a role in the gut fungi community associated with plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a genus of fungi implicated in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a genus of fungi known for its ability to degrade polystyrene by producing enzymes, involved in the breakdown process in the insect gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichoderma is a genus of fungi that was found to be overrepresented in PS samples compared to CT samples, although rarely recovered."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS PLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS Plastic (Polystyrene) is identified as a suitable material for transformation via yellow mealworms, illustrating the potential for plastic waste reduction."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS stands for polystyrene, a type of foam plastic used in the diet experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE PLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE refers to polyethylene, a type of foam plastic used in the sole diet experiments for larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW MEALWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">The Yellow Mealworm, scientifically known as Tenebrio molitor, refers to the larval form of a beetle and is recognized for its exceptional ability to biodegrade various types of plastics. These plastics include polystyrene (PS), polyethylene (PE), polyurethane (PU) foams, and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). This biodegradation capability is primarily due to the diverse microbiome within the yellow mealworm's gut, which houses bacteria such as Enterococcus, Enterobacteriaceae, Escherichia-Shigella, and Lactococcus. These microbes play a significant role in breaking down the chemical structures of these plastics, specifically the benzene ring in polystyrene, showing unique chemical signatures identified through FTIR and ¹H-NMR analyses.

Research has extensively focused on the yellow mealworm's interaction with different plastics, examining their ability to adapt to changes in diet, growth, nutritional content, and degradation efficiency. These larvae are not only nutrient-rich and protein-dense, making them economically beneficial, but they have also been deemed safe for human consumption by the EU Food Safety Agency. Studies involving yellow mealworms examine their potential for practical applications in plastic waste management and biodegradation. Additionally, experiments investigate how plastic ingestion affects their gut microbiome, nutritional quality, and developmental stages, such as pupation.

In conclusion, the yellow mealworm is a valuable model organism in scientific studies due to its dual role in nutrition and environmental sustainability. Its involvement in plastic degradation, driven by an adaptive gut microbiome, makes it a key entity in ongoing research related to environmental preservation and waste management.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-896c5c0e4d1805ded38b335876a02f78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce7cf71b094ef4329d3da3b628438bec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb235a6eddd0a0e69be356e83362fc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-779ca6b59945309c6bf5040c9fb474ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3667703f7416ebab666e65b375dd8582&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Z. ATRATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zophobas atratus, or superworms, have been noted to degrade PS in a manner similar to mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;Zophobas atratus, commonly known as the superworm or mini mealworm beetle, is a species of darkling beetle larvae that has garnered significant attention in scientific research due to its capacity to biodegrade various types of plastics. In studies, Z. atratus has been specifically used to investigate the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and polypropylene (PP). These research initiatives focus on the plastic degradation capabilities of the larvae, particularly the associated gut bacteria that facilitate this process. The larvae exhibit distinct patterns of depolymerization and surface modification of these synthetic materials.

Z. atratus is often compared to Tenebrio molitor larvae for its potential in polymer biodegradation, showing similar susceptibility to nutritional limitations and unique microbial communities that enhance their biodegradation abilities. Experiments involving Z. atratus also examine how plastic diets influence larval gut microbiomes, PS consumption, and how various diets, such as those containing wheat bran, affect their metabolism. The species is valuable in understanding the mechanisms of polymer depolymerization and the broader impact of plastic consumption on ecological and microbiome studies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df045e015825dac8935c9c6d8a402cda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7106a16c81492914e4ba58e248689714&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC DIETS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic Diets, including PU, EVA, and PP, influence gut microbial composition, leading to distinct microbial community structures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic diets refer to feeding larvae with various types of plastics, such as PS and PE, to analyze their degradation in the larval gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TGA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"TGA (Thermogravimetric Analysis) is a method used to measure the amount and rate of weight change in a material as a function of temperature, indicating thermal stability and composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA, or Thermogravimetric Analysis, is a method used to assess plastic degradation by measuring weight changes in frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermal Gravimetric Analysis (TGA) is performed to characterize the thermal decomposition and stability of PS feedstock and frass samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is employed to assess change in weight in relation to change in temperature, often used to study degradation kinetics."&lt;SEP&gt;"TGA stands for thermogravimetric analysis, a technique applied to demonstrate changes during PU biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) assesses pyrolysis reactions and mass-loss in mealworm fecula to understand polystyrene breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric Analysis, a technique used to determine the physical and chemical properties of the mask residues by measuring weight changes under controlled temperature."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) detects the thermal modification and degradation of Styrofoam to frass, illustrating differences in PS content and degradation efficiency among larvae species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric analysis, employed to measure changes connected with biodegradation in terms of polymer composition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a76f613b36454134940f78fba1c0f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ITS GENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Internal Transcribed Spacer (ITS) gene is sequenced to analyze fungal community diversities in larvae with plastic diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OTUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"OTUs (Operational Taxonomic Units) are clusters of organisms grouped by DNA sequence similarity, used in the analysis of microbial diversity in samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTUs (Operational Taxonomic Units) are used to analyze the diversity and community structure of gut microbiomes using 16S rRNA gene sequencing."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are clusters of organisms used to study biodiversity and their associations with plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units, indicates the number of distinct bacterial entities detected in gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units (OTUs) are a method used to categorize bacteria based on sequence similarity, providing insights into microbial diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units used to measure changes in bacterial community diversity and richness during polystyrene feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units, used to categorize bacteria and fungi based on sequence similarity, serving as a proxy for species in diversity studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Operational Taxonomic Units used for assessing species richness and fungal diversity within plastic-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ec1a5b2913dd6782ff1e0533dd57325f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRAN-FED GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bran-fed Group refers to the control group of larvae fed with bran to compare their survival and growth against those on a plastic diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran-fed Group refers to the larvae fed exclusively on bran, providing a control group to compare the effects of plastic diet on microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Control dietary group where larvae feed on bran, used as a baseline for comparison with plastic-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"The group of superworms or yellow mealworms that were fed a diet of bran, providing essential nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bran-fed group refers to the control group of mealworms fed with bran, used for comparison in microbial studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS-FED GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS-fed Group refers to the group of larvae fed with Polystyrene plastic to assess its impact on survival and development."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS-fed group refers to mealworms fed with polystyrene, resulting in specific microbial and genetic adaptations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The group of superworms or yellow mealworms that were fed a diet of polystyrene foam exclusively during the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE-FED GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE-fed Group refers to the group of larvae fed with Polyethylene plastic to assess its impact on survival and development."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL GROUPS IN FRASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Functional Groups in Frass refers to chemical structures identified in frass using ATR-FTIR spectroscopy to assess degradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMAL GRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermal Gravimetric Analysis is a method used to characterize thermal stability changes in samples such as frass and plastic feedstock."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermal Gravimetric Analysis is a technique used to study thermal changes in plastic polymers in frass to assess plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NET WEIGHT CHANGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Net Weight Changes refer to the measure of weight variation in larvae during the study, used to assess the impact of different diets on growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL SURVIVAL RATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larval Survival Rates indicate the percentage of larvae that survived under different dietary conditions, observing the impact of plastic diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PUPATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A developmental stage in insects that can be adversely affected by a deficient diet and buildup of toxic substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is a developmental stage in insects where larvae transform into adult form, influenced by factors like diet and microplastic exposure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is a developmental stage in insects where they transform from larvae to pupae, which can be delayed by insufficient nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the process of larval transformation, affected by diet, notably the plastic diet impacts pupation speed but not successful adulthood."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the process of transformation from larva to pupa, used in the study to assess the developmental impacts of plastic diets on larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the process of transformation of mealworms into pupae, which is an indicator of their developmental progress."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of mealworms developing into pupae, which can be delayed or advanced by microplastic feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"The developmental process during which larvae transform into a pupae. It temporarily halts plastic consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the process of larvae transforming into pupae, used to assess the health of T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the process indicating development in larvae, used as a health and development marker for the species in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the biological process during which larvae transform into a pupal stage before becoming adults, occurring when certain energy thresholds in reproduction buffers are met."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the phase in the life cycle of T. molitor where larvae transform into pupae under certain conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pupation is the process by which larvae transform into the pupal stage, initiated when certain energy density thresholds are met."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACCUMULATED FOAM CONSUMPTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Accumulated Foam Consumption refers to the amount of foam consumed by larvae, used to measure dietary intake."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VORTEXED AND CENTRIFUGED PROCESS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Vortexed and Centrifuged Process is a method used to separate plastic remnants in frass for analysis in degradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-16810fc8beb169dfca32904d41bd992b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORM Z. ATRATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworm Z. atratus is a larval species studied for its ability to consume plastic materials, specifically polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), and is known for its higher rate of plastic consumption compared to T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW MEALWORM T. MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Yellow mealworm T. molitor is another larval species involved in the study, consuming PS and PE but with lower plastic consumption rates compared to superworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-FED DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The plastic-fed diet consists of solely plastic materials, which proved insufficient to support significant growth or development in either larvae species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRAN-FED CONTROL GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The bran-fed control group serves as a comparison group in the study to assess the effects of a more nutritionally complete diet on larvae growth and development."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS) FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene (PS) foam is a type of plastic material consumed by the larvae, analyzed for its degradation and oxidation by their digestive systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a form of plastic that can be degraded by Tenebrio molitor larvae through microbial and enzymatic processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE (PE) FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyethylene (PE) foam is another type of plastic material investigated in the study, with its consumption patterns and degradation also observed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene foam is another plastic type that yellow mealworms are reported to consume and biodegrade."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidation is a chemical reaction involving the increase in oxidation state, providing evidence of polymer degradation in LDPE foams."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidation refers to the chemical reaction through which polypropylene undergoes structural changes, complementing biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Oxidation is a multifaceted chemical process that plays a significant role in the degradation and structural transformation of various polymers, particularly plastics such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polyurethane (PU), and polyethylene terephthalate (PET). It primarily involves the reaction with or addition of oxygen to a substance, often leading to the loss of electrons, which is a critical factor in altering the chemical structure of these materials. This process has been observed in various contexts, such as the enzymatic activity within the larval gut and mealworm digestion, where it results in the formation of carbonyl groups and other structural changes in microplastics.

In practical terms, oxidation contributes to the deterioration of materials, as seen in the degradation of mask layers and polyethylene microplastics, highlighted by changes in specific low-intensity bands in FTIR spectra. During studies, it has been noted that oxidation results in the incorporation of oxygen or nitrogen into the polymer structure, which affects the functionality and integrity of the material. This process is further observed in the frass of larvae, indicating the breakdown of plastic functional groups in their digestive system.

Moreover, oxidation is a pivotal reaction in the biodegradation processes facilitated by bacterial strains such as WJ2020 and WJ2025, which cause oxygen atoms to be immobilized on the surfaces of PS films, aiding in their degradation. This oxidative process not only occurs under biological conditions but is also evident when polyethylene films are incubated with bacterial strains, indicating microbial involvement in material degradation.

Overall, oxidation is a fundamental chemical reaction that significantly impacts the environmental fate of synthetic polymers and their processing, signaling increased degradation under environmental and biological influences.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DE-POLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"De-polymerization is the breakdown of polymer structures, like PS and PE, facilitated by biological processes in the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATR-FTIR ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ATR-FTIR Analysis investigates changes in chemical properties and functional groups during nanoplastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR analysis is a technique used to confirm the chemical changes in PE and PS, indicating degradation processes in the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3196d2d200ebf060e966a565b5eb26de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOAM PLASTICS CONSUMPTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The process of larvae consuming plastic foams, specifically polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE), to study their ability to degrade plastic materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRITIONAL AMENDMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The addition of nutrients, such as bran, to the diet of larvae to improve their ability to sustain plastic consumption and support growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL GROWTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larval Growth refers to the developmental changes in mealworms over the experimental period, measured through weight changes and survival rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larval growth refers to the development and maturation process of the yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The development and increase in size of larvae over time, influenced by their diet and ability to consume plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larval growth refers to the developmental process of larvae, influenced by nutrition, temperature, and other environmental conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2417ca11674320d88eecdf4cb66f9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL PUPATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The stage in the insect life cycle where larvae transition to pupae, a phase that can be adversely affected by inadequate nutrition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FINAL WEIGHT CHANGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The net change in body weight of the larvae at the end of the experiment, serving as an indicator of growth and health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STUDENT'S T-TEST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"STATISTICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A statistical test used to compare means between groups, applied in the study with Tukey's correction."&lt;SEP&gt;"A statistical test used to determine the significance of differences in plastic consumption rates between different groups of larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Student's t-test is a statistical test used to compare the means of two groups to determine the significance of differences."&lt;SEP&gt;"Student's t-test is a statistical test used to evaluate variations due to diet and species, with adjustments for multiple comparisons using Tukey's correction."&lt;SEP&gt;"Student's t-test is used for pairwise comparisons among different datasets within the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Student's t-test is a statistical test used to compare the means of two groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE FOAM DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The process of polyethylene foam undergoing chemical breakdown in the presence of enzymes and conditions within larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE FOAM DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The process by which polystyrene foam is chemically broken down during digestion within larval guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIFE CYCLE COMPLETION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Life cycle completion refers to the ability of mealworms fed on polystyrene to reach maturity and complete their reproductive cycle."&lt;SEP&gt;"The full progression of an organism's growth from birth to maturity, which can be compromised by inadequate diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRASS SAMPLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Frass samples are collected excretions from larvae after digesting polymer mixtures, analyzed for residual polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Residues primarily consisting of mealworm waste material used in various analyses, including thermo-gravimetric analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"The waste material produced by larvae after digesting plastics, analyzed to assess the extent of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waste products of larvae, used in enrichment cultures to isolate bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Frass samples are the waste material excreted by mealworms, collected to determine plastic consumption and mass reduction during the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92b1e432ea4bee0f332d7303188e6de7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30a7209d626a6319ba5600bea7f27299&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdb13eda4d3cf5385a0a8284c7e213b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BODY SIZE DIFFERENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The variation in body size between different larval species, necessitating adjustments in consumption rate calculations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a1fbf409e74e27aa2b223e54f2e0def</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL OR CARBOXYLIC ACID GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Functional groups such as hydroxyl or carboxylic acid groups formed on the surface of degrading plastics, indicating a chemical change from hydrophobic to hydrophilic."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DSC-TGA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"DSC-TGA are techniques used to analyze the thermal stability and composition changes in plastics after passage through the mealworms' gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"DSC-TGA is a thermal stability test used to assay weight loss and decomposition temperature ranges of plastics and frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Differential Scanning Calorimetry-Thermogravimetric Analysis (DSC-TGA) used to study the thermal stability and degradation patterns of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"DSC-TGA is an analytical technique that measures the thermal properties and weight changes to assess the degradation state of materials consumed by yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"DSC-TGA is used to analyze the plastic biodegradation process and the thermal properties of residual polymers after digestion by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BENZENE RING SKELETON&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A structural component of polystyrene (PS) that undergoes cleavage during degradation in larval guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER CHAIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A polymer chain is a series of chemically bonded repeated units in a polymer, whose size affects its biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The sequence of repeating units in plastics like PE and PS that can be modified by the addition of functional groups such as C=O."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL GUTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The digestive tract of superworms and yellow mealworms where degradation of PE and PS plastics occurs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=O STRETCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C=O stretch is a functional group identified in the frass, showing incorporation of oxygen into the polymer during mealworm biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon-oxygen double bond stretch, indicative of oxidation occurring in PE and PS during the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"C=O stretch refers to a carbonyl bond and its presence in frass samples indicates oxidation and potential incorporation of oxygen into the polymer chains during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f370c2f246a6c8a776ae90277a5aacc8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROPHILIC SURFACE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The surface characteristic of plastics altered by functional group additions, leading to increased affinity for water."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CENTRIFUGATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A laboratory process used to separate frass components by density, aiding in the analysis of partially degraded plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A process by which mixtures are separated using centrifugal force, used here to remove heavy debris from samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OPTICAL MICROSCOPE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An optical microscope is used to observe polystyrene degradation at the micro-level during the experimental processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Used to observe changes in the morphology of frass and plastics, aiding in visual analysis of degradation patterns."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARGER DEBRIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larger pieces of plastic present in superworm frass, influenced by the size difference in larval mouthparts and bodies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLUFFIER FRASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The texture difference in PS-fed frass compared to PE-fed frass, linked to the polymeric structure differences between PE and PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c9a869d17d470cb9ca00328986147a3f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT-BACTERIAL MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The gut-bacterial microbiome in superworms and yellow mealworms undergoes significant community changes when fed with plastic diets. These changes include alterations in species richness and diversity, which are crucial for the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut-bacterial microbiome represents the bacterial communities residing in the gastrointestinal tract of yellow mealworms, affected by ingestion of different mask layers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DSC (DIFFERENTIAL SCANNING CALORIMETRY)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"DSC analyzes changes in the thermal properties of HDPE, such as its melting point."&lt;SEP&gt;"DSC is a technique used to evaluate the thermal properties of plastics, indicating the extent of modification and degradation after passing through the larval gut of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OTU (OPERATIONAL TAXONOMIC UNIT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"OTU represents a measure of bacterial diversity, assessing community structure changes in the gut microbiome of mealworms when fed different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTUs are used in microbiology to classify groups of closely related individuals, indicating diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"OTUs are units used to classify groups of closely related individuals, often used in microbial ecology to categorize bacteria associated with plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHAO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chao is an estimator used to measure species richness in a community, applied here to gauge the bacterial diversity in the gut microbiome of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACE (ABUNDANCE-BASED COVERAGE ESTIMATOR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ACE estimates species richness and diversity, used to assess the bacterial community in superworms and yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c1f0f043d44950fae05e8207a298d4a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE-FED SUPERWORM GUT BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The gut bacteria in superworms fed with Polyethylene (PE) show significant changes in diversity, particularly sensitive to a plastic-only diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS-FED SUPERWORM GUT BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut bacteria in superworms fed with Polystyrene (PS) with notable changes in community diversity, involving genera such as Pseudomonas and Enterococcus."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YELLOW MEALWORM GUT BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The yellow mealworm gut bacterial community changes according to their plastic diet, with noted decreases in diversity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ASCOMYCOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fungal phylum predominant in both PS and CT samples, accounting for a considerable portion of taxa."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ascomycota is a fungal phylum observed with increased abundance in middle-fed mealworms, part of the fungal shifts due to diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ascomycota is an unclassified fungus that showed a significant increase in abundance in the bran-fed group of yellow mealworms and is associated with biodegradation of mask plastics, particularly the middle layer."&lt;SEP&gt;"The predominant fungal phylum in bran-fed and PS-fed superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ascomycota is a predominant fungal phylum involved in the degradation of various polymers within gut fungal communities of plastics-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d33654bada6ddaf4917586dd5fd41e9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BASIDIOMYCOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fungal phylum well-represented in PS samples but less abundant in CT samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Basidiomycota is a phylum of fungi frequently encountered in polystyrene samples and less common in control samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Basidiomycota is a phylum of fungi observed at high frequency in PS samples, contrasting with its near absence in CT samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Its abundance increases in yellow mealworms' guts fed on plastics, suggesting an association with plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Basidiomycota is a predominant fungal phylum whose relative abundance increased in all plastics-fed groups, suggesting a strong association with plastics diets in yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NMDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Non-metric multidimensional scaling, a method used to cluster bacterial communities based on diet and species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UNWEIGHTED UNIFRAC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A distance metric used to analyze microbial community similarity based on phylogenetic information."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRAY-CURTIS DISTANCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of dissimilarity between two sets of data, used here to analyze gut bacterial communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALPHITOBIUS DIAPERINUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alphitobius diaperinus is a beetle species studied for temperature effects on growth and metabolic rate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alphitobius diaperinus is an insect evaluated for its nitrogen-protein conversion factors, suggesting its edible potential."&lt;SEP&gt;"Known as lesser mealworms, associated with PS feeding and a part of studies on microbial involvement in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alphitobius diaperinus is known for its bacterial and fungal diversity in the gut, especially when fed on polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alphitobius diaperinus, also known as the lesser mealworm, is an edible insect species used for protein conversion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alphitobius diaperinus is the lesser mealworm beetle, studied for its role in polystyrene consumption and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Alphitobius diaperinus, commonly known as the lesser mealworm, is an insect capable of feeding on and degrading polystyrene, primarily through the metabolic activity of its gut microbiota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-97444337eddb4e9e3d88bde93ce02aef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HELICOVERPA ARMIGERA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of moth, mentioned in context with Issatchenka, a fungus found in plastic-fed superworm guts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYPHOPICHIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A predominant genus of fungi found in both bran-fed and plastic-fed superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hyphopichia is a predominant fungus in the guts of bran-fed superworms and yellow mealworms, associated with high carbohydrate metabolism efficiency and decreased presence in plastic-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEBARYOMYCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fungal genus prominent in bran-fed groups but reduced in abundance under plastic-fed conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Debaryomyces is a predominant fungus in the guts of bran-fed superworms and yellow mealworms, with high carbohydrate metabolism efficiency, but decreases or disappears in plastic-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the predominant fungi in the guts of both bran-fed superworms and yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Debaryomyces is a yeast genus predominantly found in the guts of bran-fed yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a91a3c11281861b559c31c3107c72e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISSATCHENKA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fungal genus with increased abundance in PE-fed superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Issatchenka is a dominant genus in PE-fed superworms, accounting for a significant percentage of gut fungi, and is associated with plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WICKERHAMOMYCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of fungi referred to in the context of changes in the fungal community of PE-fed superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wickerhamomyces is a dominant genus in PE-fed superworms, contributing significantly to the fungal composition compared to bran-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-93d5b1c190fdca6b5e456b1513515be4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHIZOMUCOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rhizomucor is a dominant genus in PE-fed superworms and appears to be associated with plastic degradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PENICILLIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another fungal genus found in greater abundance in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Penicillium is a genus of fungi identified in PS samples with a high Log2-fold change, indicating potential overrepresentation compared to CT samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Penicillium is a genus of fungi with species capable of breaking down polyethylene and mineralizing polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Penicillium is one of the fungi recorded to increase in abundance in PS-fed lesser mealworms and is considered for its potential in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYPHODERMA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hyphoderma shows an increase in PS-fed lesser mealworms and is considered as part of the gut fungal compositions potentially involved in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PE-FED SUPERWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PE-fed Superworms exhibit certain dominant fungal genera associated with plastic diets, showing adaptation to consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS-FED SUPERWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS-fed Superworms have specific fungal communities that adapt to the plastic diet, with certain genera increasing in abundance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbb6f13dbe3de759712fa3a0c9780470</data>
    </node>
