      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Citrobacter amonalaticus Y19 is a microorganism involved in the hydrogen production pathway as studied in metabolic-flux analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN PRODUCTION PATHWAY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The hydrogen production pathway is a biological process involving metabolic-flux analysis in Citrobacter amonalaticus Y19."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL COMMUNITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Assemblages of interacting populations of microorganisms, influenced by host factors and environmental variables."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities are collections of microorganisms that function together in specific environments, such as those involved in the degradation of hydrocarbons and plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities include diverse microorganisms in environments like soils, seawater, which attempt to biodegrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities refer to diverse groups of microorganisms involved in dark fermentation of enzymatic hydrolysates, impacting hydrogen production."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities in insect guts comprise complex ecosystems of bacteria, archaea, fungi, and protozoa influenced by diet and environmental factors."&lt;SEP&gt;"The complex networks of microorganisms in the gut of yellow mealworms that play a role in plastic degradation and exhibit structural and functional adaptations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities, such as those in the gut microbiomes of mealworms, participate in complex interactions to facilitate biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities in the guts of superworms and yellow mealworms aid in the degradation of specific plastics, such as PS and PU."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial Communities refers to the diverse groups of microorganisms that inhabit the gut, affected by diet type."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial communities are groups of microorganisms that work synergistically to degrade PS under anaerobic conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79e833cbc3a0a6e2c80e1b69da50317e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8076792f39f6c801b538312a7b45fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMATIC HYDROLYSATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymatic hydrolysates result from breaking down materials by enzymes, used in studies of microbial communities and hydrogen production."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZOPHOBAS ARATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zophobas aratus larvae are studied for their gut enzymes and microbiome's role in the biodegradation of foam plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus larvae are used in biodegradation studies to assess changes in gut digestive enzymes and microbiome related to foam plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, also known as the superworm, is a species of beetle larvae involved in the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, known as the superworm, is recognized for ingesting different plastics and exhibiting an associated change in gut microbiome that aids in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, commonly referred to as superworms, are larvae of a darkling beetle species and have been studied for their ability to degrade plastics such as polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, commonly known as the superworm, is a species of larvae that can biodegrade polypropylene through gut microbe-dependent processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, referred to as superworms, is studied for its ability to biodegrade plastics with associated microbiome changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zophobas aratus, a type of superworm, has shown potential for use in polystyrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARINE ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Marine enzymes are derived from marine organisms such as algae, bacteria, fungi, and sponges and have diverse biotechnological applications."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC WASTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic wastes are discarded plastic materials that pose environmental challenges and are subject to biodegradation and valorization studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARK FERMENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dark fermentation is a biological process used to generate hydrogen and other products from organic material without light."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN PRODUCTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrogen production is a process influenced by microbial community shifts in microbial electrolysis cells involving L-cysteine."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen production refers to various processes generating hydrogen gas, with methods including biological production like dark fermentation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLIC-FLUX ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolic-flux analysis is a technique used to analyze the rates of metabolic reactions within a cell."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMATIC PROFILE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzymatic profile refers to the spectrum of enzymes expressed or active within a given microorganism or microbial community, often related to their biodegradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymatic profile refers to the spectrum of enzyme activities present in a biological sample, such as the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile refers to the range and activity levels of enzymes present in organisms or environments, influencing biochemical processes like biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The enzymatic profile refers to the collection of enzymes expressed in Tenebrio molitor, which contributes to its ability to biodegrade various substances such as cellulose and synthetic polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bd2f7b94f62fa18de9e02426577d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOTECHNOLOGICAL APPLICATIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biotechnological applications refer to the use of biological processes, organisms, or systems to manufacture products intended to improve the quality of human life."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene biodegradation involves the breakdown of polystyrene by organisms, including bacteria from soil and specific microbial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene biodegradation is a process where polystyrene is broken down by biological agents, such as mealworms, for conversion into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene biodegradation is the process of breaking down polystyrene plastic using biological organisms or systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene biodegradation refers to the biological process of breaking down polystyrene, a common plastic, by microbes or other means to address plastic waste pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which yellow mealworms, larvae of Tenebrio molitor, digest and break down polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process involving the breakdown and mineralization of polystyrene by plastic-eating mealworms, indicating a potential solution to plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene biodegradation is a biological process where specific organisms break down polystyrene material into simpler substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene biodegradation refers to the process where certain insects like mealworms consume polystyrene and degrade it internally, potentially involving microbial gut flora."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene biodegradation involves the breakdown of polystyrene, a common plastic, by biological means through various organisms and microbial activities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene biodegradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene plastic material into simpler compounds by biological agents, such as the mealworms described in the studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biological process by which polystyrene is broken down by insects or their microbiomes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d85ab9c6af7915311ccbb4804160be5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5ea4395a1eb1886507c72cdf33db169&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e77298fbeb95f87269261b74c2158d5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e3058d086d89f1ce9baa29cbc29bb48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-825c2784fa66ce67114e4173ed0702f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc744f6fa5a9ea876a0423d37eedfc87</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE WASTE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene waste is a type of plastic waste that can be consumed by Tenebrio molitor, leading to microbiome diversity and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene waste refers to discarded polystyrene that is subjected to biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene waste refers to discarded polystyrene materials, which are the focus of biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene waste refers to discarded polystyrene products subjected to biodegradation studies to evaluate environmental impacts and degradation efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f830cfd93570c7a9762518aa356ab69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-678bd592bfdda3f6dd8f2a6659de7fde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIAL STRAINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacterial strains are subtypes of bacteria characterized by their specific genetic makeup."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains from insect guts participate in the biodegradation of materials like polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains from the guts of waxworms have shown evidence of polyethylene biodegradation, important for reducing plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains known for degrading hydrocarbon xenobiotics and potentially involved in PS (polystyrene) biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Specific strains of bacteria identified in the study as capable of degrading bioplastics like PBSA, PBS, and PCL in the mealworm gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains from the guts of waxworms that contribute to the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains refer to specific types of bacteria identified for their capabilities in degrading polystyrene nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains are used in research for their ability to biodegrade materials such as polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacterial strains involved in the biodegradation of polyethylene originating from the guts of plastic-eating waxworms."</data>
