    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS SP. YP1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial species that aids in the degradation of commercial LDPE film in tandem with Enterobacter asburiae YTI."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another bacterial strain located in waxworm guts, playing a role in the degradation of polystyrene films."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain also isolated from waxworm gut, facilitating the degradation of polyethylene in laboratory conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterium isolated from insects that can degrade LDPE as a sole carbon source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is an LDPE-degrading bacterium isolated from the gut of darkling beetles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a gut bacterium capable of degrading about 10.7% of LDPE films in 60 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another biodegradable PE strain isolated from the intestine of Indian meal moth larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain isolated from the gut of waxworms, capable of degrading polyethylene (PE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterium from the gut of plastic-eating waxworms, known for biodegrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterial strain involved in the degradation of polyethylene, specifically LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a bacterium from the gut of plastic-eating waxworms, with the capability to degrade polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a polyethylene (PE)-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the gut of a plastic-eating waxworm."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillus sp. YP1 is a polyethylene-degrading bacterial strain isolated from the gut of PE plastic-eating waxworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b77d05458a2050e3057c427cf81e24ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10004ab6a85cecf4effb74c306df57cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c39e41f4f36c9c6854fa71508c35a08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086890e8567c60d8cfa7c03b0cc61f9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-IMPACT POLYSTYRENE (HIPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High-Impact Polystyrene (HIPS) is a type of polystyrene that has been modified to be more impact-resistant."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-impact polystyrene is a type of polystyrene modified with rubber to improve its impact resistance, often used in products requiring stronger plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-impact polystyrene is a variant of polystyrene used in making products like liquid containers and toys."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE (EPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Expanded polystyrene is a form of atactic polystyrene used in building insulation and packaging, noted for its lack of crystallinity."&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded Polystyrene (EPS), commonly referred to as Styrofoam, is a lightweight, rigid, and closed-cell foam made primarily from polystyrene. This material is extensively used in building insulation and packaging due to its excellent thermal properties, compressive strength, and low density. Often found in packaging materials for electronic equipment, EPS is known for its utility in providing insulation and protective capabilities in packaging solutions. Despite its widespread use, EPS is a significant environmental pollutant due to its resistance to natural degradation, attributed to its high molecular weight, hydrophobic characteristics, and robust structural stability. This results in EPS persisting in landfills and its potential release of microplastics and toxic substances during incineration, posing significant environmental challenges.

Research has investigated potential solutions for EPS biodegradation by exploring its consumption by organisms such as Tenebrio molitor mealworms. Studies have shown that when EPS is included in the diet of these mealworms, it can lead to biodegradation, offering a promising avenue for reducing the environmental footprint of EPS. These studies have considered various factors, including the health and microbial distribution in the gut of mealworms, as well as the impacts on larvae's growth and nutritional composition. Experiments have been conducted to compare how different species, including rats, process EPS, especially when integrated with mealworm powder in their diets.

Despite its environmental impact, EPS remains an important material in numerous industrial applications. Scientific research continues to seek innovative management and mitigation strategies to curb its negative environmental effects. Suppliers like Woosung Resin Co., Ltd. provide EPS for its various applications, although the material's large volume relative to its mass significantly contributes to waste. The potential for organisms to break down EPS provides a hopeful path forward for addressing the challenges posed by this prevalent polymer.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-188daf7716cd1d683a79562bf4d6ab90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c1e1694d44433fcf53dc8120c479ca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e88a3caade44f7fbc493dfc1c3ca8f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af801199dfd68205bddcbe0bcda26eef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65434948dabde813d99972f40a04d8de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3235cc6102d988875f1dda974a56038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e755eeac14110dcfdcfc32e51f30b89&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5911f995d46a77458be83382b8c67f9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36b964b1825429cad2c7d13c1ea8827e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81a3bc3c202af119c80775d5bf23d8b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8b79a6f4c112b0b62ada6756140e3e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2934b88523093a8bba74cc29a0c775e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ed48dc66abf9c27721101b1adac15e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LINEAR LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (LLDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LLDPE is a type of low-density polyethylene plastic that was reported as difficult to degrade by the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"LLDPE is a type of polyethylene composed of linear molecules with no long chains, possessing higher levels of short chain branching than HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"LLDPE is a type of polyethylene with medium crystallinity (20-60%) and more short chain branching than HDPE, affecting its use and degradability."&lt;SEP&gt;"LLDPE is a type of polyethylene with significant flexibility, used in plastics like stretch film and bags."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linear Low-Density Polyethylene (LLDPE) is a linear polymer with a significant number of short branches, offering flexibility and strength."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linear low-density polyethylene is a type of polyethylene with a significant degree of short-chain branching, offering greater tensile strength and puncture resistance compared to LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Linear low-density polyethylene is a variant of polyethylene that has a linear structure with low-density properties, often used in agricultural mulching films."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYACTIC ACID (PLA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bio-based plastic that can be broken down into smaller particles by organisms such as mealworms and earthworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A biodegradable plastic also subjected to degradation studies by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A biodegradable, bioactive thermoplastic derived from renewable resources, such as corn starch or sugarcane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyactic acid is a biodegradable thermoplastic derived from renewable resources like corn starch or sugarcane, used as an alternative to petrochemical-derived plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA is a biodegradable plastic used as a comparison in biodegradation research."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA is a biodegradable thermoplastic derived from renewable resources, hydrolyzable under certain conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA is a biodegradable plastic that undergoes fragmentation and partial depolymerization by earthworms in their digestive system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdbea1d06006b95d57c8b628d9e3f8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe381daaa91bbff20758798e5baf82b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a228b2d8328bdce2419d34f8f59bc3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLESIOPHTHALIMUS DAVIDIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plesiophthalimus davidis is an insect involved in breaking down and modifying plastics to some extent."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LLDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">Linear Low-Density Polyethylene (LLDPE) is a type of polyethylene polymer that is characterized by its lesser degree of branching compared to Low-Density Polyethylene (LDPE). It is notable for its resistance to degradation, although it is still subject to biodegradation processes, particularly when subjected to treatment by mealworms, specifically the larvae of Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus. LLDPE is widely used in studies to evaluate the biodegradation potential and effects on the gut microbiomes of these mealworms, influencing microbial community diversity, structure, and stress response. Despite its relatively lower consumption rates by mealworms compared to other plastic types, LLDPE's biodegradation and depolymerization are areas of interest due to its impact on physiological functions and potential to cause impairment when ingested. These studies contribute to understanding LLDPE's role in global plastic usage and its environmental implications, as well as its comparative biodegradation rates alongside other polyethylene variants such as HDPE and LDPE.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d8c6850a83a1c6efd4f1a3a37a0a8181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a193cc62c03cb6460b0434df42d8c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-988ef111808996ddbe6e483d6505ab45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd73d3cca5434181ee9dd1ad1b8f8ab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9528beb2e85a7fb89449a9233eb979bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3380732c060363504db5f6e4b8610b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPS (Expanded Polystyrene Foam) is another type of styrofoam product used to feed mealworms in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS refers to expanded polystyrene, a material that the Yellow mealworm larvae feed on, leaving holes in the material."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a polymer material that T. molitor larvae can feed on, demonstrating potential degradation activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene, a type of plastic known for its lightweight and insulating properties, that some insects feed on."&lt;SEP&gt;Expanded Polystyrene (EPS) is a lightweight, rigid foam plastic made from solid beads of polystyrene. This material, known for its low hardness and glossy surface, is predominantly used in packaging and insulation. Available in both white and grey variants, EPS is a foamy, amorphous structured plastic that has been a focal point in various scientific studies to assess its interaction with biological entities and its biodegradation potential. One primary area of interest is its use as a diet component for Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms. 

These studies focus on the depolymerization and biodegradation capabilities of mealworms, facilitated by certain microorganisms in their gut. When fed EPS, mealworms experience alterations in metabolism and changes in the expression of specific genes, affecting their intestinal microbial diversity and overall growth. Despite these changes, mealworms have demonstrated efficient biodegradation capacity, showing significant mass loss of EPS, which indicates its high bioavailability and potential for biodegradation.

Researchers have noted that EPS consumption affects mealworm's lipid and carbohydrate levels, while different pre-treatments and bedding types can influence the rate and efficiency of EPS consumption and degradation. The material's resilience and structural characteristics have made EPS a valuable experimental substrate in studies exploring innovative and environmentally friendly waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS (Expanded Polystyrene) is another form of polystyrene not typically broken down by Cronobacter."&lt;SEP&gt;"Expanded polystyrene (EPS) is widely used due to its properties such as lightness and insulation, and is known to contribute to environmental pollution as microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS refers to expanded polystyrene, which is used as an energy source for mealworms in the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32f340b7166025be3c56178a94ae480d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6fd56772ef66691cf6f1c613ba42e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe29ef34ad1e5ab11c4ec7f5270fa94d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9859524b7a6851b786570d2a704e34f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-449dceb5649c6baa34ecc2218cd3e9e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7a14229bceb339a4ca1baf0772b47ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HIPS (High Impact Polystyrene) is a type of plastic that can be degraded by some bacterial species."&lt;SEP&gt;"HIPS (High Impact Polystyrene) is an amorphous polymer with higher cumulative consumption rates compared to other plastics when fed to mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"HIPS (High Impact Polystyrene) is one of the experimental diets fed to mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Impact Polystyrene (HIPS) is a rigid plastic product evaluated for its degradation by mealworms, incorporating rubber as an additive."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PVC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PVC, or polyvinyl chloride, is a plastic material characterized by a smooth surface, making it difficult for mealworms to consume."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) is one of the types of microplastics studied for its biodegradation and impact on mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) is a widely used synthetic plastic polymer known for its durability and resistance to environmental degradation due to its non-hydrolyzable chemical structure. As a petroleum-based plastic, PVC is recognized for its resilience, yet, it is also the subject of extensive research regarding its potential biodegradation by specific microorganisms and insects, including the larvae of mealworms like Tenebrio molitor. Studies into this process have shown that while PVC is highly durable, mealworms can consume it, leading to depolymerization and partial biodegradation. However, this does not provide the larvae with adequate nutrition and can result in physiological stress such as dehydration, malnutrition, and significant growth inhibition.

The complex molecular structure and elemental composition of PVC, particularly its chloride groups, contribute to its lower degradability compared to other plastics like polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS). When ingested by mealworms, PVC releases chlorinated intermediates that can cause oxidative stress and shifts in the microbial communities within the larvae's gut. Moreover, the combination of PVC consumption with bran can affect reactive oxygen species (ROS) levels in mealworms. Despite its resistance to breakdown, research into PVC's degradation by insects offers valuable insights into potential strategies for mitigating its environmental impact and highlights the broader discussions on pollution reduction and management in the context of synthetic plastics.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddb3edd44829125161ad624ea793ae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f61cd3a131593ce6b058802713d43ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b36e69e51416db099951b925beff9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb00229bf45a3bd9b8a17e10a1c93d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RNA SEQUENCING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"RNA sequencing is a process used to analyze the transcriptome of mealworms to understand genetic expressions related to plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIFFERENTIAL SCANNING CALORIMETRY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Differential Scanning Calorimetry is a technique used to determine the crystallinity of plastics, providing insights into polymer properties that affect degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Differential scanning calorimetry is a technique used to study the crystallization temperature and melting process of materials, such as PS in this context."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-TEMPERATURE GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography (HT-GPC) is employed to measure molecular weights and polydispersity of plastics, such as PP and LDPE, in degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-temperature gel permeation chromatography is a technique used to measure the molecular weights of polyethylene during its degradation process by bacterial strains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GEL PERMEATION CHROMATOGRAPHY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is a technique used to determine the molecular weight distribution of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel Permeation Chromatography (GPC) is used to evaluate the molecular structure of plastics like EPS and HIPS during mealworm degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel permeation chromatography (GPC) is a type of size exclusion chromatography that separates analytes by size, often used to characterize polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel Permeation Chromatography is an analytical method used to measure molecular weights of materials like polypropylene in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gel permeation chromatography is a technique used for analyzing the molecular weight distribution of polymers like plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEGS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"DEGs (Differentially Expressed Genes) indicate changes in gene expression in response to various conditions or treatments."&lt;SEP&gt;"DEGs are differentially expressed genes showing significant changes in expression in response to plastic feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"DEGs, or differentially expressed genes, are genes that show differing expression patterns between control and experimental conditions, such as in mealworms fed bran versus plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Differentially Expressed Genes (DEGs) are analyzed in mealworms to identify metabolic pathways and functional enzymes involved in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) which are analyzed for function and pathway annotations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac5b96e63e6f5e34fe841f690d9f78ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YANG ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"RESEARCH GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Yang et al. are authors who conducted studies on the degradation capabilities of plastics like PS and PE by mealworms, contributing to understanding plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. refer to a group of researchers contributing to studies on plastic degradation by bacterial strains isolated from insect guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. is a research group responsible for conducting multiple studies on the biodegradation of polystyrene using mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. refers to the group of researchers who conducted related studies previously, relevant for methodological context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. are researchers who contributed to the identification of superworms’ abilities to process polystyrene and polyethylene efficiently."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. refers to a group of researchers who have conducted studies on polystyrene degradation using mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. is a research group whose methodology was adapted for PS film preparation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. is a referenced study that supports findings about the incorporation of polystyrene into lipids in larval intestines."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. refers to the authors of studies that analyzed biomass changes and CO2 conversion in larvae fed with polystyrene as an energy source."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRANDON ET AL.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Brandon et al. contributed to research that explored the degradation of various plastics by insects, providing insights into the complexity of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Brandon et al. presented similar research on degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene, yet their findings faced scrutiny regarding incomplete mass balances."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-566d464a7713ef7ca266f3d5f24a1a59&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b08485bef3ee3db21e59e159ad4c3e20</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CONTROLLED-ENVIRONMENT CHAMBER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The controlled-environment chamber is used to maintain stable conditions for rearing mealworms during the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SHORE HARDNESS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Shore hardness is a measure of the hardness of the plastics used in the experiment, indicating material properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shore hardness is a measure of the resistance of a material to indentation, used to test the robustness of plastic samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shore hardness is a measure used to determine the hardness of materials, significant in assessing physical properties of polystyrene variants."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-06a44b308af148551c5b7feef324d88b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d47d39d364c5c66ef3fc32ef741ca63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DENSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Density is a property of the plastics studied, representing the mass per unit volume."&lt;SEP&gt;"Density refers to the mass per unit volume of the Expanded PS (EPS) Foam, characterized at 0.014 g/cm³ in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Density refers to the mass per unit volume of materials, affecting how mealworms consume polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CRYSTALLINITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Crystallinity describes the degree of structural order in a solid, influencing the biodegradation potential of polymers like PP and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity indicates the degree of structural order in the plastic materials used in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a plastic, impacting its biodegradability and consumption by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer that impacts hydrophobicity and degradation rates of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer. In PET, crystallinity impacts its physical characteristics and susceptibility to degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer, which can decrease due to microbial action on HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the degree of structural order in polymers, reduced during biodegradation by certain bacterial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer, influencing its physical properties and degradability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer, affecting its mechanical properties and degradation rate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a polymer, relevant to its biodegradability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of structural order in a solid, affecting its susceptibility to microbial and enzymatic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the structural order in polymers like PET, analyzed to understand how its biodegradation affects its physical characteristics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity is a physical property describing the degree to which materials like polyethylene have a structured, ordered arrangement of molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity refers to the degree of long-range order in a material; in polymers, it affects hardness, density, and resistance to biodegradation and enzymatic reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystallinity describes the degree of structural order in a solid, which affects the biodegradation potential of polyethylene plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eb7955dc484d5f656e1be7b406abb3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the mass of molecules in a sample, used to assess the changes in PS during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A metric that decreases as a result of the degradation of HDPE and other plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight (Mn and Mw) quantifies the mass of molecules in the plastics LDPE and EPS, determined through HT-GPC and GPC analyses."&lt;SEP&gt;"The sum of the atomic weights of all atoms in a molecule, influencing the properties of polymers like polyols in polyurethane."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight changes in PE films are measured to evaluate the extent of degradation, providing insight into structural modifications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight measures the mass of a molecule, used to compare pristine Styrofoam and frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"The molecular weight of HDPE can decrease due to the breakdown of long polymer chains during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of a molecule, influencing the degradability and digestibility of plastic polymers by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of a molecule, used to assess the degradation of Styrofoam by mealworms and gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of molecules and is used to analyze changes in PS pieces before and after biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of a molecule, a significant factor in determining the extent of PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight influences the biodegradability of polymers, with lower weights generally more susceptible to microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A measure of the mass of molecules in a substance, which decreases in plastics like PE after biodegradation by the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of a molecule of LDPE, which changes as the polymer is depolymerized by the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the mass of a molecule, which changes during the biodegradation and depolymerization processes of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular Weight (MW) is a measure of the mass of molecules, critical in the characterization of polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-639bb2429f20c4fe3994c4a0d15f3295&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06ba9ca61fb4f42582bbf7bd00ff0005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PDI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PDI (Polydispersity Index) is a measure of the distribution of molecular mass in a given polymer sample."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polydispersity index (PDI) is a measure of the distribution of molecular mass in a given polymer sample, indicating changes in chain length distribution after biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-67028993c2650049216602d03fb3f786&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HT-GPC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOOL/TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HT-GPC (High Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography) is a technique used for determining the molecular weight distribution of polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"HT-GPC (High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography) is used to analyze molecular weight distribution of PE particles."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography (HT-GPC) is used to determine the molecular weight of the polyethylene films for assessing degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-Temperature Gel Permeation Chromatography used to analyze polymer residue and assess depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-temperature gel permeation chromatography used for analyzing molecular weights in frass samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6f28f1c36076d1f249dbc4cd67b16f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AGILENT CARY 630 FTIR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Agilent Cary 630 FTIR is an instrument used to conduct FTIR analysis on plastic samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-718900b924b6910a58e8ead81c01c723</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRIZOL REAGENT&quot;">
