      <data key="d2">chunk-f545e57b1a2b3da8601b4d23e1260c94</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEPOLYMERASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme that is potentially involved in breaking down polymer chains of PET in the gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Depolymerase is an enzyme responsible for the degradation of polymers, favoring the amorphous regions over crystalline in plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5238515b2316265fb5cb6c6295c54e8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NAHCO3&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"NaHCO3, or sodium bicarbonate, is an additive involved in the production of expanded polypropylene (EPP)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AZODICARBONAMIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Azodicarbonamide is a chemical additive used in the production of expanded polypropylene (EPP) due to its specific foaming properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STARVATION CONTROL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CONTROL CONDITION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Starvation control is a reference condition used to evaluate the effects of microplastic ingestion on mealworm mortality and growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Starvation control refers to a group monitored for survival without feeding, serving as a baseline for mortality comparison in mealworm experiments with plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPP (Expanded Polypropylene) is a type of polypropylene that requires strict manufacturing conditions due to its high crystalline structure, affecting its consumption by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96aac7c9638ee245b9db5df3ec1d2a1f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIPS, EPS, LDPE, LLDPE, PP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are types of plastics involved in the experiment, impacting the growth and weight of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"These are types of plastics with varying crystallinity and degradation potential as studied in the context of mealworm consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROLASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrolases are enzymes that have the greatest activity changes during the enzyme-catalyzed reactions in plastic-fed mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FATTY ACID DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fatty acid degradation breaks down fatty acids to provide energy and building blocks under stress conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acid degradation involves breaking down fatty acids into acetyl-CoA, significantly noted in the degradation process in plastic-fed mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acid degradation refers to the breakdown of fatty acids to generate energy. This process is observed in mealworms fed with bran and plastic, indicating different gene expression patterns depending on the diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0efd7a9e4443dc5d3dfe588c7f7efc13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHOSPHATIDYLINOSITOL PHOSPHATASE SAC2, ESTERASE FE4, PHOSPHOLIPASE D2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are specific enzymes upregulated in mealworms when lacking nutrition, particularly in those fed with LDPE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CYTOCHROME P450 6A1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme involved in fatty acid degradation and potentially in isoniazid metabolism during plastic breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KEGG PATHWAY &quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A pathway analysis indicating the enrichment of specific pathways related to plastic degradation in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHOSPHATIDYLINOSITOL PHOSPHATASE SAC2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This enzyme is involved in hydrolysis during the degradation of LDPE by mealworms and shows upregulation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTERASE FE4&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme that is upregulated in mealworms during nutritional deficiency, specifically when fed LDPE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHOSPHOLIPASE D2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Involved in the enzymatic activity during nutrient deficiency in mealworms fed on LDPE, showing significant upregulation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACYL-COA OXIDASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Key enzyme in fatty acid degradation, involved in the degradation of plastics in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENOYL-COA HYDRATASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme important in the process of fatty acid degradation and plastic breakdown in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3-HYDROXYACYL-COA DEHYDROGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This enzyme plays a critical role in the degradation of fatty acids, crucial to breaking down plastics in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACETYL-COA ACYLTRANSFERASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Acetyl-CoA acyltransferase (EC 2.3.1.16, 2.3.1.9) is an enzyme that catalyzes the transfer of acetyl groups from acetyl-CoA to acyl groups, playing a role in fatty acid metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Involved in the final step of fatty acid degradation, aiding in plastic degradation by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RT-PCR ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A molecular biology technique used to validate the RNA-Seq data related to DEGs in plastic- and bran-fed mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PLA (Polyactic Acid) is a biodegradable plastic that undergoes hydrolytic degradation, providing insights into sustainable materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA (Polylactic Acid) is a biodegradable polymer known for its environmentally friendly properties and ability to decompose faster than most conventional plastics like PET, PE, and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA (Polylactic Acid) is a type of plastic used in the experiment that accelerated the pupation in mealworms but did not lead to adulthood."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA stands for polylactic acid, a biodegradable plastic that can be consumed by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA, or Polylactic Acid, is mentioned in the context of PS removal efficiency calculations, indicating a component of the polymer matrix."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA (Polylactic Acid) is a biodegradable plastic tested for its impact on oxidative stress and mass reduction in degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA (Polylactic acid) is a biodegradable polymer broken down via enzymatic hydrolysis by specific enzymes like polyester hydrolases."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA (polylactic acid) is a type of biodegradable plastic that was used in the study to feed larvae, showing higher degradability and supporting larval growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA is a biodegradable polymer tested for degradation by macroinvertebrates like Tenebrio molitor in plastic biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA, or polylactic acid, is a type of biodegradable thermoplastic derived from renewable resources such as corn starch or sugarcane, used in the study as a material for plastic degradation experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polylactic Acid (PLA) is a type of biodegradable plastic that when combined with bran, affects the ROS levels in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable polymer tested in the study for degradation and consumption by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA, a biodegradable plastic, is used in experiments to evaluate its interaction with MW and WM model insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polylactic acid (PLA) is a biodegradable plastic substrate tested for metabolic impacts on insect models."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA, or polylactic acid, is a biodegradable plastic with low molecular weight that has been linked to the genus Lactococcus's degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"PLA, or polylactic acid, is a biodegradable plastic material used in the studies, observed for its mass loss when consumed by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54049db5a67a6464f459ccc427765140&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c6ced866009d759592c16009bc619b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f38f2f3673b813b3a817c014229c9565&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7a93f0b3dbebad30b6aeabec429a40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae2c294948460ef392ceeec82d5e81f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACYL-COA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Acyl-CoA refers to a group of coenzyme A derivatives involved in the metabolism of fatty acids. Specific enzymes like acyl-CoA oxidases catalyze reactions involving acyl-CoA."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CYTOCHROME P450 6A1 (CYP6A1)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cytochrome P450 6A1 (EC 1.14.14.1) is an enzyme involved in the insertion of oxygen into fatty acids, indicating its role in the metabolism of medium to long-chain fatty acids."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GLUTATHIONE S-TRANSFERASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Glutathione S-transferases (EC 2.5.1.18) are enzymes involved in detoxification processes by conjugating glutathione to various substrates, including drugs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Glutathione S-transferases (GSTs) catalyze the conjugation of glutathione to make compounds more water-soluble, aiding in metabolism during PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea2d94de229e98ea99b0d40b7b077ad</data>
