    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene is a compound related to polystyrene, with debates on its formation during polystyrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a metabolite that is part of the degradation pathway in some microorganisms and shares a core structure with malachite green."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is an organic compound used as a precursor to polystyrene, a widely used plastic material."&lt;SEP&gt;Styrene is a volatile organic compound and a monomer of polystyrene, extensively used in the production of plastics, resins, and foams. It is identified as a key component in the polymerization process that creates polystyrene and recognized as an integral part of various industrial applications. Styrene also plays a significant role in waste management due to its potential for recovery from polystyrene and its implications for environmental contexts. During studies, styrene is often observed as a chemical by-product or decomposition product of polystyrene, particularly during thermal breakdown or biodegradation processes. It is detected using analytical methods such as GC-MS and FTIR, where it serves as the main decomposition product in such analyses.

Biodegradation of styrene involves various microbial and enzymatic processes, with certain bacteria and fungi capable of breaking it down through metabolic pathways that include oxidation and cleavage. This degradation can result in the formation of phenylacetyl-CoA and other intermediate compounds. While beneficial in managing plastic waste, styrene also poses environmental and health risks, particularly when metabolized into styrene oxide, a compound with potentially harmful effects. Therefore, understanding the degradation pathways of styrene remains a critical aspect of research concerning the environmental impact of polystyrene and its derivatives.&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is a precursor to PS and follows two biodegradation routes that produce intermediary compounds like 2-phenylethanol."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene is an organic compound that can be degraded by various microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-497648a1cf8e41fac896250ae1eebbb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cefffe48b3d2ec347f880c77300fc041&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa27760a0a889fe7b58360dbb150cdf3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ceb4368e146509caa737d9534ce3c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be41a05c5e519995f6622e2258511b70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37f447b9d6a8b38f80be0854c9c294af&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
