    <node id="&quot;GENE EXPRESSION&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fb491e686c96e54f8ea43a9f1ba97eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720</data>
      <data key="d1">"Beeswax consumption by G. mellonella larvae affected gene expression related to carboxylesterase and lipase families."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gene Expression refers to the changes in transcriptional activity in Tenebrio molitor's gut bacteria during polypropylene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gene expression refers to how the host organism's genetic instructions are used to synthesize proteins during polymer degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gene expression refers to the process by which the instructions in a gene are used to synthesize gene products, such as proteins, which is altered in larvae fed different MW PPs."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which information from a gene is used in the synthesis of functional gene products like proteins, observed in context with PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gene expression is the process by which information from a gene is used to synthesize functional gene products, often explored in studies of stress responses in organisms like Eisenia fetida."</data>
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FATTY ACIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-014b72d0a2657cbc89c4dee5f2b32bba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df24ea37743c236d369911024fc20bfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-246606838d14d58d03d733b6250a0460&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a52fc2936b4d2a771dbc2d6a37eeb710&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
      <data key="d1">"CES2 activity is affected by fatty acids, serving as inhibitors, especially unsaturated types in mealworms during plastic ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids stored in the form of triglycerides are oxidized to provide energy during mealworm starvation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty Acids are components involved in various biological processes, including those transformed by the CYP52 family in Candida tropicalis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids are bioactive molecules identified in the polar fraction of frass, indicating enzymatic activity related to the biodegradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Different types of fatty acids are present in larvae, affected by their diet, including polyunsaturated fatty acids."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids are carboxylic acids with long hydrocarbon chains, important for energy storage and cellular function."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids are compounds extracted from mealworm larvae and their composition is analyzed using gas chromatography to determine the relative content."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids in mealworms are analyzed for their content and quality, with the study noting lower levels in insects fed on polymer diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids are used by insects during starvation, oxidized to provide energy when lipids are depleted, as observed in mealworms fed PS and PSr."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids such as C&lt;sub&gt;12:0&lt;/sub&gt;, C&lt;sub&gt;14:0&lt;/sub&gt;, and C&lt;sub&gt;16:0&lt;/sub&gt; were measured for bacterial characterization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids are a significant component of human nutrition, discussed in FAO reports and scientific studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty acids are an essential component of mealworms, contributing to their nutritional value as a food source."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fatty Acids, such as oleic acid, hexadecanoic acid, and octadecanoic acid, are identified as metabolic intermediates in PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d0">"FATTY ACID"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC CONSUMPTION&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff201df58dec460c49cd31920c29f11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31f2ee9a779791fef2da810debc12720&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworms are capable of ingesting certain types of plastics, influencing their growth and metabolic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the physiological ability of mealworms to consume plastics, specifically micro-polystyrene, as part of their diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic consumption refers to the behavior exhibited by mealworms in consuming and subsequently biodegrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic Consumption refers to the ingestion of plastic materials like EPS, LDPE, and PS by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which mealworms consume and alter various types of plastics involved in the experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which mealworms ingest and metabolize various types of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
    </node>
