      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Refers to the different ages of mealworm larvae, which impacts their ability to consume and degrade microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PARTICLE SIZE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Describes the size of microplastic fragments, affecting their consumption and degradation by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3-MONTH-OLD MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworm larvae at three months old, identified as having the highest rate of plastic consumption and degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MORTALITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of death rates in mealworms when exposed to microplastic feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mortality rates provide information on the survival outcomes of mealworms under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mortality of Tenebrio molitor larvae refers to the rate at which the larvae perish during the experiment, influenced by the type of EPS consumed."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mortality in yellow mealworms is used to evaluate the survival rate and the impact of plastic ingestion on their lifespan."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mortality is a measure of death rate in larvae, which increases with exposure to non-degradable plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mortality is the rate of death observed in mealworm populations, significant in assessing the impact of various feeds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL CONTAMINATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The presence of undesired microplastics in ecosystems, resulting in pollution and potential harm to organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-02a57c5d6ee92292f1b1bd3dbe76e612</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RIGID MICRO-PS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rigid micro-PS (MPS) is a type of microplastic material with a density of 1.114 g cm⁻³ and a shore hardness of 25.6 HD, used in various sizes for experimentation purposes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;URBAN PROTEIN ENGINEERING TECHNOLOGY RESEARCH CENTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An organization that provides mealworm stocks for scientific research related to microplastic consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOUTH CHINA AGRICULTURAL UNIVERSITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An academic institution from which the original insect colony was obtained for the mealworm experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YOUNG LARVAL STAGE (YLS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Young Larval Stage (YLS) refers to one-month-old mealworms with a body weight of approximately -3 mg ind⁻¹."&lt;SEP&gt;"Young Larval Stage is the early developmental phase of mealworms during which MPS consumption and degradation abilities are assessed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MIDDLE LARVAL STAGE (MLS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Middle Larval Stage (MLS) refers to two-month-old mealworms with a body weight of approximately -20 mg ind⁻¹."&lt;SEP&gt;"Middle Larval Stage is the mid-developmental phase for mealworms analyzed for MPS consumption and degradation capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OLD LARVAL STAGE (OLS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Old Larval Stage (OLS) refers to three-month-old mealworms with a body weight of approximately -80 mg ind⁻¹."&lt;SEP&gt;"Old Larval Stage is the mature stage of mealworms, showing the greatest MPS consumption and exhibiting significant degradation effects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MPS CONSUMPTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MPS consumption measures the amount of microplastic ingested by mealworms, affected by particle size and larval age."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KUMHO TIRE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kumho Tire is a company located in Korea that supplied the Rigid micro-PS used in the experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KOREA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Korea is the country where Kumho Tire, the supplier of Rigid micro-PS, is based."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MGC-350HP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MGC-350HP is a model of controlled-environment chamber used for maintaining stable conditions in mealworm experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YI HENG&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Yi Heng is the manufacturer of the MGC-350HP chamber used in mealworm research for controlling environmental variables."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SHANGHAI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Shanghai in China is a source location for mealworms studied for their plastic consumption rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shanghai is the location where the Yi Heng company, manufacturer of the MGC-350HP, is based."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTIC PARTICLE SIZE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Refers to the size range of microplastic particles tested in mealworm consumption experiments, which affect ingestion rate and larval survivorship."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1283355f048671359adcde7d3137af9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTIC PARTICLES (MPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastic Particles (MPS) are small plastic particles used in this study to analyze consumption, degradation, and impact on mealworms at various larval stages."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHYLETHYNE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methylethyne is involved in the chemical structure of polymers, relevant to the oxidation process observed in MPS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydroxyl Groups (3700–3100 cm⁻¹ in FTIR spectra) are involved in oxidation reactions during MPS digestion by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are chemical groups consisting of an oxygen atom bonded to a hydrogen atom, contributing to hydrophilic properties in compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups are functional groups identified in the degraded PS residues, indicating increased hydrophilicity and chemical transformations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups consist of an oxygen bonded to a hydrogen atom, contributing to the hydrophilic nature of biodegraded materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxyl Groups were assessed in the study, though their presence was less noticeable in the FTIR spectra of the degraded MPS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C—N STRETCH VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The C—N stretch vibration appears in the FTIR spectrum, indicating chemical modifications in the MPS during mealworm digestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM SURVIVAL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworm survival rates are impacted by feeding on MPS alone, with varying resistance across different larval stages."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR (Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy) spectra are used to characterize chemical bonds in polymers such as PET, providing insights into changes during degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectra are used to analyze chemical modifications in the MPS during mealworm digestion, showing shifts in functional group peaks."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra is used to analyze the chemical composition of MPS feedstock and frass from mealworms to determine the effects on larval diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra is a process involving Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid, or gas."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is an analytical technique employed to identify organic, polymeric, and in some cases, inorganic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectra stands for Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, a technique used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption of a solid, liquid, or gas, showing changes before and after biodegradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectra used to analyze changes in chemical properties of PS films after microbial degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared Spectra (FTIR) is used for analyzing chemical bonds, essential in studies of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) identifies functional groups and structural changes within polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR Spectra show absorbance peaks attributed to chemical decomposition products like carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide from fecula."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are used to analyze chemical structures and assess changes in PS/CS samples when treated with enzymes."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are used to analyze chemical changes in mealworm fecula samples to assess polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy data indicating functional groups in degraded polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR spectra are used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption of a solid, liquid, or gas, providing detailed information about the chemical bonding and molecular structure of the samples analyzed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98f748ef0b9e55c370617ad91bcf219c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fc215d3abb38f76b4ee0d22c4f1eb11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5e8cdca8afb1d7153fba5e3c11b1f669&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59defb995f5b0409c31d1922916c2f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ada834fc8d4bea41c0ce1770d6f57e4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MPS is a component fed to mealworms, affecting their survival and growth rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"MPs, or microplastics, are small plastic particles greater than 1 μm, often present in environmental samples and of concern due to their ecological impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"MPs stands for microplastics, which are environmental contaminants that have been observed in various invertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"MPs are microplastics, small plastic particles whose biodegradation is being studied in mealworms and other larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YLS, MLS, OLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"YLS (Youth Larval Stage), MLS (Middle Larval Stage), and OLS (Old Larval Stage) represent different developmental life stages of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL PERIODS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Survival periods refer to the duration for which mealworms survive under various feeding conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larval weight is a measure of growth and development in mealworms, affected by diet and environmental conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larval weight is a measure of the growth status of mealworms under different feeding regimes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FITTED DEATH RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fitted death rate is a calculated measure used to compare mortality trends of mealworms under different dietary conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STANDARD ERRORS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Standard errors indicate the variability of measurements, providing information on the reliability of mortality data in mealworm studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHYLEN ROCKING VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methylen rocking vibration is a specific region observed in FTIR spectra analysis to study the chemical structure of mealworm feed and frass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GROWTH TRENDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Growth trends describe the observable patterns of change in mealworm development based on diet, including periods of increase and decline in weight."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8a522a615bbcac2e06f97f2e4e61975b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTICS (MPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MPs are small plastic pieces less than five millimeters long which can be harmful to our ocean and aquatic life."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles that can be ingested by organisms, causing harmful effects such as weight loss and increased mortality."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics, including PS MPs, are used in experiments to assess their consumption rates and biodegradation by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"MPs, particularly PVC, PS, and PLA, are used in studies to evaluate the degradation effects and physiological stress responses in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles, less than 300 μm, that pose environmental and biological risks due to their persistence and potential for bioaccumulation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Small plastic particles, including PVC, PS, and PLA, which are ingested by Tenebrio molitor larvae in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that result from the degradation of larger plastic waste, such as the PE polymers studied."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles including high-density polyethylene (HDPE), low-density polyethylene (LDPE), and linear low-density polyethylene (LLDPE), used to examine biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles resulting from environmental contamination and subject to ingestion by invertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic pollutants that arise from the breakdown of larger plastic wastes, including PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles less than 5mm in size, which can be ingested by organisms, affecting their health and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic pieces less than 5 mm in size, resulting from the decomposition of larger plastics, posing significant threats to ecosystems and health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles, ranging from 10 μm to 5 mm, generated from the degradation of larger plastic products like PS and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles, ranging from 100 nm to 5 mm, originating from the breakdown of larger plastic debris and posing environmental and health risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles resulting from the breakdown of larger plastic debris, posing risks to environmental health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are small plastic particles used in this context to refer to polystyrene particles consumed by mealworms for biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are tiny plastic particles of various polymers that can cause environmental and biological damage."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5540fc6fe4d3ef0fd986ec36a589c77f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdc8398efba587929eebd0a0842f6e69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HORMONAL SYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The hormonal system regulates growth and development in organisms, with nutrient deficiencies affecting hormone expression and pupation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRIENT UPTAKE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrient uptake is the process by which organisms absorb nutrients from their diet, impacted by the presence of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Community of microorganisms living in the mealworm gut, adapting to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota in the insect digestive tract, particularly in Tenebrio molitor larvae, contributes significantly to the biodegradation processes, including PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms within the gut, which can be altered by exposure to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota refers to the community of microbial species residing in the digestive tract of T. molitor, playing a role in PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota refers to the composition of microbial communities that live in an organism's gut and influence metabolism, as studied in some of these texts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms, including bacteria, living in the digestive tract of organisms like Tenebrio molitor, responsible for the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The group of microorganisms in a particular environment, analyzed in the study for their metabolic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms, such as bacteria and fungi, living in a specific environment like the mealworm gut, playing a synergic role in the degradation of PU."&lt;SEP&gt;"The community of microorganisms in the mealworms' gut, essential for understanding the metabolism of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms, such as bacteria, present in the gut of organisms like mealworms, important for plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms in the gut of larvae involved in the plastic biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0e81871d41a32fbdcbbd4f699efae3d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dd9a0647afad70038c8052c24eb5a67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENERGY RESERVE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Energy reserve is the stored energy in organisms, necessary for survival and successful pupation, influenced by nutrient availability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MLS indicates a specific developmental stage of mealworms used in studies to assess growth and nutrient uptake under different dietary conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"OLS is a developmental stage in mealworms that shows their response to dietary changes and microplastic exposure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"YLS represents an early developmental stage in mealworms where they show varying mortality rates when exposed to dietary changes, such as microplastic ingestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-DEGRADING LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic-degrading larvae are capable of breaking down plastic materials within their digestive systems, offering potential solutions for waste management."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REPRODUCTION BUFFER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reproduction buffer is an energy reserve specifically allocated for reproductive processes in organisms as described in the DEB model."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reproduction buffer is an energy reserve used to support reproductive processes, which can be affected by changes in diet and energy availability in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reproduction buffer is the accumulation of energy reserves used for reproduction, primarily contributing to egg production in later stages of development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Reproduction buffer represents an energy reserve critical for reaching reproductive thresholds in T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reproduction buffer is a concept within the DEB model representing energy reserves used for reproduction, reaching a threshold during pupation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reproduction buffer is a concept within the dynamic energy budget model, necessary for successful pupation in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reproduction buffer is an energy storage threshold necessary for reproduction in organisms, particularly referring to energy density threshold values like [E_R] for pupation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e819d53f76bcab4b74eba714efd32163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eee7e44e4c15567a057d7d295d240211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-998b63e5349034d477c2087c139669ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DYNAMIC ENERGY BUDGET MODEL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The dynamic energy budget model describes how organisms assimilate energy from food and utilize it throughout their life stages, particularly in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TROPHIC TRANSFER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Trophic transfer describes the movement of substances, such as microplastics, through different levels of the food chain, influencing ecological dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trophic transfer refers to the movement of substances across different levels of a food chain."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trophic transfer is the process by which substances like nanoplastics move through different levels of the food chain and ecosystem."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ARENICOLA MARINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Arenicola marina, commonly known as the lugworm, is part of studies on microplastic uptake and weight loss in marine ecosystems."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOLSOMIA CANDIDA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Folsomia candida is a springtail species used in studies to gauge the impact of microplastics on survival and reproduction."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TERRESTRIAL WORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Terrestrial worms encompass a variety of species affected by microplastic ingestion, with commonalities in gut response mechanisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AQUATIC WORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aquatic worms, like lugworms, are used in studies to understand the environmental impact of microplastic exposure in water ecosystems."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84558838faaf585c61e883356c52c0e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESSENTIAL NUTRIENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Essential nutrients are crucial for the proper development of organisms, and their insufficiency leads to postponed pupation in worms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POPS (PERSISTENT ORGANIC POLLUTANTS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"POPs are hazardous chemicals that can accumulate along with microplastics in the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MPS (MICROPLASTICS IN SMALLER SIZES)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MPS refers to microplastics of smaller sizes preferred by mealworms for ingestion and studied for their impact on mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OLD LARVAL STAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Old larval stage refers to the late developmental phase of mealworms, where significant microplastics intake and metabolic changes occur."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MLS (MINERALIZED TO BIOMASS IN MEALWORMS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MLS refers to the process where ingested microplastics are converted into biomass by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YANG ET AL. (2015A)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"REFERENCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A reference to a study or paper by Yang et al. concerning biodegradation rates relevant to the experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"This is a reference to a study that provided estimates regarding EPS consumption and degradation by mealworms and beetles."&lt;SEP&gt;"This study demonstrated the mineralization of polystyrene to biomass in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. (2015a) is a reference cited in the study that provided data on carbon mass balance and EPS consumption by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. (2015a) refers to a scientific study focused on the biodegradation of polystyrene by microorganisms such as mealworms, detailing specific findings about carbon balance and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. (2015a) is a cited study that analyzed the conversion of PS into biomass and CO2 in larvae, providing foundational data on how PS affects growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. (2015a) is a study reference, providing past data on larvae consumption of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yang et al. (2015a) provides earlier research reference on the growth percentage of T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b33a3915745323f1bd29bd2febf60b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a97d5b092508f2e837932274d6b3282&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09d03c0a2dce3f57876d209a83a2e82a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5740f0973ff804cae61f6fc12f3e3753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c11c77665793ac79aecfb19a89dd1f52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LWANGA ET AL. (2017)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SCIENTIFIC STUDY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This study confirmed that high concentrations of microplastics collected from chicken feces were due to ingestion by microplastics-fed earthworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZHU ET AL. (2018B)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SCIENTIFIC STUDY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This study provided a model for predator-prey relationships, showing the role of co-occurrence in microplastics transport in soil."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PREDATOR-PREY RELATIONSHIP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An ecological interaction where predators feed on prey, which can enhance microplastics transport in the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC MANAGEMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENVIRONMENTAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic management refers to the strategies and actions taken to manage plastic waste, considering the environmental impact and potential hazards."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-640f0655e17fab473cca3b460c2795de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbiomes are microbial communities within mealworms that synergistically biodegrade PET intermediates and provide nitrogen sources during the process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The community of microorganisms that reside in the digestive tract, significant for PET degradation in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"The diverse communities of microorganisms residing in the digestive tract, which respond to and participate in the biodegradation of PET polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes refer to the microbial communities present in larvae, influencing the plastic degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbiota within the larvae guts that play a significant role in the degradation of plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbiomes consist of the community of microorganisms living in the digestive tracts of organisms like insect larvae, influencing biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-705bf68703e5d236b2ffabb7ecb3de5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0014171c7ef07a60c6be3b57e791688c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER POLYMER BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The breakdown of polyester polymers, specifically PET, by biological means, involving both physical and enzymatic actions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE (PET)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A commonly used plastic that is challenging to biodegrade, often found in fibers for clothing and liquid containers."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET is a type of plastic that has been the focus of significant biodegradation research."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET jars were used to house mealworms during the experiments and were denser, preventing mealworms from biting through."&lt;SEP&gt;Polyethylene Terephthalate (PET) is a widely utilized fossil-based plastic polymer that is predominantly used in the production of fibers for clothing, containers for liquids and foods, and for thermoforming applications. In the textile industry, PET is commonly referred to as polyester. It is a common thermoplastic material that makes up approximately 6.9% of plastic products, contributing notably to microplastic and overall plastic pollution. Despite its durability and resistance to biodegradation, PET has become a focal point in environmental studies due to its significant role in plastic waste. Research has shown that PET can undergo biodegradation through fungal enzymes, certain microbial processes, and specialized enzymes engineered for this purpose. Additionally, biodegradation studies have also explored the potential of PET being broken down by insect larvae such as Tenebrio molitor and bacterial communities. PET's recyclability further enhances its appeal in consumer products, especially in packaging and beverage containers. Due to its extensive use and environmental impact, PET is frequently targeted in biodegradation studies, making it an important subject of research concerning biodegradation and plastic pollution mitigation.&lt;SEP&gt;"PET is another form of plastic being considered for biodegradation evaluation, similar to PVC and PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of plastic detected as microplastics in animal-based traditional medicinal materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of plastic that requires further study to understand its biodegradability by organisms like mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dad389953d78e790a40a9267c5b591c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2359b10600f30376fe077f5e942cc999&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-394b3365ffb36d05dca2ef06bf74e462&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbdeaa1a437ba819412de45788aa119c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d575c206eb616c4c970fd48fe3349247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f1aa99e321e9e3b94552e6c6f8e00f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5a7fb8d6a76ecfbfc810f952bfba090&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HAFNIA-OBESUMBACTERIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial genus involved in the biodegradation process of PET, contributing to the biochemical breakdown in the gut of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hafnia-Obesumbacterium refers to bacteria of a genus which plays a role in human gut flora. Its abundance is investigated in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5982537ede5118840b65596ee2a04896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16S RRNA ANALYSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16S rRNA analyses are used to identify and quantify bacterial species involved in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A molecular analysis method used to identify and understand the bacterial community structure and function related to PET biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b759b81206d0e33e320f2d61e540ae47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLOME ANALYSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The study of metabolites involved in the biodegradation process of PET, providing insights into the metabolic pathways utilized."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYME REPERTOIRE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A collection of enzymes contributed by both host larvae and their gut microbiota, which are involved in the biodegradation of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OMICS ANALYSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Comprehensive analytical techniques that study various molecular biology fields, including genomics and metabolomics, providing insights into biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOCHEMICAL MECHANISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The proposed way in which mealworms and their gut microbiota collaborate to break down PET, involving various metabolic pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL IMPACT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Environmental impact refers to the effect that biodegradation processes have on the environment, particularly in terms of reducing plastic waste and pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"The effect of pollutants like PET on natural ecosystems, including the accumulation of hazardous microplastics and nanoplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The potential positive effects on the environment through the reduction of plastic waste via mealworm consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-475b22fe6100b14cced604f739663fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM DIGESTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The biological process by which mealworms consume and break down materials, such as PET, contributing to mass reduction of the polymer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-25f3365d0b08e5e77183d813ce6a1049</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET (POLYETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of plastic associated with degradation by certain bacteria found in T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"PET is a plastic that could also be biodegraded by mealworms, like PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyethylene terephthalate, commonly abbreviated as PET, is a type of plastic widely used for packaging and containers. It belongs to hydrolysable polymers and is susceptible to microbial degradation and enzymatic attack."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VAN DER WAALS FORCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Van der Waals forces are weak attractions between molecules that contribute to the physical properties of polymers like PET. These forces play a role in the crystallinity and structural stability of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolome refers to the comprehensive profile of metabolites, explored in the study by analyzing mealworm tissues to understand PS biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the complete set of metabolites present in the gut microbiota and is indicative of the metabolic pathways active in PET biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The metabolome encompasses the complete set of small-molecule metabolites found within a biological sample, in this case, the larvae of T. molitor, during the biodegradation of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"The metabolome refers to the complete set of metabolites in mealworms, which is reprogrammed during the biodegradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9af0d95fd5f52d02b9cb33387951963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GLYCOLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Glycolysis is a metabolic pathway that breaks down glucose to extract energy. It was mentioned as a chemical method for treating waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMINOLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aminolysis is a chemical reaction involving the replacement of a functional group by an amine. It is used in the chemical treatment of polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MECHANICAL TREATMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mechanical treatment involves the physical handling or processing of materials, such as collecting plastic for pelleting, as a means to manage waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL TREATMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical treatment refers to the application of chemical processes, such as methanalysis, hydrolysis, glycolysis, aminolysis, and ammonolysis, to manage and recycle plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSICAL TREATMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physical treatment involves non-chemical and non-biological processes to manage materials or waste, including methods that influence physical properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methanalysis is a chemical process used in recycling plastic, involving the breakdown of polymers using methanol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMMONOLYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ammonolysis is a chemical reaction involving the breaking of bonds in a polymer using ammonia, contributing to the chemical treatment of waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTIC (MP)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastics are tiny plastic particles less than 5mm in size. In this context, they refer to commercial PET resin microplastics used in degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microplastics are tiny plastic particles with a size &lt;0.1 mm, which T. molitor larvae can degrade, particularly in combination with other dietary components."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2cfbb4289b0cc4c8b69884f6d57c447</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HONGYUAN PLASTIC FACTORY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hongyuan Plastic Factory, located in Shanghai, China, is a manufacturer of one of the commercial PET resin microplastics tested in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ZHONGXIN PLASTIC FACTORY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Zhongxin Plastic Factory is the supplier of commercial PET microplastics used in this research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Zhongxin Plastic Factory, based in Guangdong, China, supplied a commercial PET microplastic resin with specific molecular characteristics for comparative analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-96c76e51cdc8235f685cdc9efc87b6a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WHEAT BRAN (WB)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"WB serves as a co-diet in the feeding tests of T. molitor and Z. atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat Bran diet provides nitrogen nutrition, crucial for larvae's metabolism compared to a PE diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat Bran is used as one of the diets for mealworms in the study to analyze their consumption and digestion metrics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is used as a feeding substrate for T. molitor larvae in biodegradation experiments, providing necessary nutrients."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a dietary fiber component used as feedstock for T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is used as a control diet in the study to compare the effects of PE polymers on Tenebrio larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is used as a control diet in the study, providing a baseline for comparing the effects of different polymers on gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat Bran is the outer layer of the wheat grain and is rich in fiber. It is used in the study as a feed component."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran is a by-product of wheat milling, used as a co-diet in experiments due to its nutritional benefits for mealworms, enhancing their ability to consume PP."</data>
