    </node>
    <node id="&quot;IDEONELLA SAKAIENSIS 201-F6&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium within the phylum Proteobacteria known specifically for PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium known for its ability to degrade PET, having one of the highest PET degrading capacities in vitro. Its activity is significantly slower compared to T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a bacterium noted for its ability to degrade PET in vitro, used as a comparison in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6 is a known PET-degrading bacterium that can reduce PET by 75% in 75 days."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1H NMR SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1H NMR (Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) spectra assess the chemical environment of hydrogen atoms in polymers, providing evidence for chemical changes during PET biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of the mass of one molecule of a substance, significant in defining the structure and properties of polymers like polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight (MW) refers to the mass of a molecule of a substance, used to describe polymers such as PET, PE, and PS in terms of their chain lengths and distribution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is the weight of a molecule that affects the degradation rate of PET by influencing polymer chain availability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular Weight (MW) measures the size of polymer chains in plastics like polypropylene, influencing their degradation by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight (MW) refers to the mass distribution of polymer chains in polypropylene, influencing its biodegradability when consumed by Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular Weight (MW) is a key property of polymers, indicating the mass of a mole of a polymer, and influencing its degradation rate and efficiency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight determines polymer properties and its rate of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight is a measure of the mass of a molecule, affecting the depolymerization and biodegradation capability of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular Weight indicates the size of the polystyrene polymers, affecting the rate of degradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular weight refers to the weight of polystyrene polymers, which influences the biodegradation process in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-566afea8a2a89bd188f68d0afa1bb173&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-326a8dda1cdd196236b9c836192c76f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b673e7590aa1d03b7721694ed73cd74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEGRADATION RATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Degradation rates refer to the speed at which polymer materials like PET, PE, and PS break down under specific conditions, important for evaluating environmental impact."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HALF-LIFE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Half-life is the time required for a substance to reduce to half its original amount, used here to describe the rate of PET degradation by T. molitor larvae and Ideonella sakaiensis 201-F6."&lt;SEP&gt;"The time required for half the amount of PET to be biodegraded by T. molitor, shorter than that of other non-hydrolysable polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-846559469c70e081884fea2f9a4b7317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
