    <node id="&quot;LIPID METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An essential metabolic process correlated with Dysgonomonas sp. and Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp., involved in the biodegradation of PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid metabolism involves the breakdown, synthesis, and transformation of lipids in organisms, which is reprogrammed during the PET degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid metabolism encompasses the processes of synthesizing, breaking down, and regulating lipids in organisms, significant in responding to PET ingestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid metabolism involves the breakdown and conversion of lipids for energy, especially active in PET-fed larvae using fatty acid pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET Biodegradation refers to the breakdown of polyethylene terephthalate by biological means, including microbial and host involvement."&lt;SEP&gt;"The decomposition process of PET in Tenebrio molitor larvae, assisted by gut microorganisms and particularly bacteria like Dysgonomonas sp. and Hafnia-Obesumbacterium sp."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AEROBIC BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aerobic bacteria are a classification of bacteria that can utilize oxygen for growth, relevant to the function of polystyrene-degrading bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aerobic bacteria contribute to the oxidation and depolymerization of PET during its biodegradation process in the larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria that require oxygen for survival and contribute to the biodegradation of PET in the gut microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FACULTATIVE ANAEROBIC BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These bacteria play a role in the hydrolysis, fermentation, and mineralization of PET during its degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANAEROBIC BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anaerobic bacteria mainly contribute to PET hydrolysis, fermentation, and mineralization in the degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria that do not require oxygen for survival and play a role in the PET degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXTRACELLULAR PET HYDROLASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymes secreted by microbes that aid in the breakdown of PET by initiating its degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMOTAXIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemotaxis refers to the ability of gut microorganisms to migrate towards nutrient sources like carbon during PET-fed conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;4-HYDROXYPHENYLACETATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A compound formed as a major intermediate during aerobic decomposition in the PET biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3,4-DIHYDROXYPHENYLACETATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An intermediate compound identified during the aerobic decomposition of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3-PHENYLPROPANOATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intermediate compound formed during the aerobic decomposition of PET, involved in further metabolic processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3-HYDROXYANTHRANILATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A major intermediate metabolite during aerobic PET decomposition which further participates in metabolic pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FORMIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A metabolite generated through anaerobic decomposition during PET degradation which can be utilized by certain bacteria for mineralization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Formic acid is the simplest carboxylic acid used in lab chemistry for various applications."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLOME ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Analysis of metabolites which revealed the synergistic metabolic pathways involved in bio-degradation, mineralization and assimilation by gut microbiota and larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolome Analysis refers to the process of studying metabolites during PET biodegradation to understand metabolic pathways and interactions between host and microbiomes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b19ac5bdc755bb8e9deb8e317ce365dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION ENZYME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymes involved in the biodegradation of PET, including hydrolases, oxidases, decarboxylase, depolymerase, oxidoreductase, and dehydrogenase."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df5e2448d1e9703b511b85725469467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET POLYMER DEGRADING ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These enzymes, including hydrolases, oxidases, decarboxylase, depolymerase, oxidoreductase, and dehydrogenase, are involved in the degradation of PET polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMOTAXIS PROTEINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These proteins assist in the movement of organisms in response to chemical stimuli and are involved in the PET degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRANSPORT PROTEINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These proteins facilitate the movement of substances across cells and play a role in the PET degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLAGELLA FORMATION-RELATED PROTEINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These proteins are involved in the formation of flagella, which are essential for motility in microorganisms during PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FACULTATIVE BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteria that can switch between aerobic and anaerobic metabolism and are involved in the PET degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Facultative bacteria are microbes that can survive with or without oxygen, pertinent to the polystyrene degradation process in mealworm gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Facultative bacteria are capable of fermenting, found in the gut of T. molitor, affecting its digestive processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Facultative bacteria can switch between aerobic and anaerobic metabolic pathways to adapt to varying oxygen levels in environments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET DEGRADATION PROCESS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The sequence of biochemical reactions involved in breaking down PET polymers in the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MINERALIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mineralization involves the conversion of organic substances into inorganic ones, often occurring alongside biodegradation in mealworm digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization is a biological process where organic matter is converted to inorganic compounds, occurring in partial degradation of PVC in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The conversion of organic plastic compounds into inorganic substances by invertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;Mineralization is a biological and chemical process through which organic material is converted into inorganic substances. This transformation is often part of the biodegradation of complex materials like plastics. In the context of polystyrene biodegradation, organisms like mealworms and Zophobas Atratus play a crucial role by facilitating this process. Mineralization involves various biochemical reactions, including the conversion of organic compounds into minerals such as carbon dioxide (CO2) and water (H2O). This step may also involve the potential formation of chloride ions (Cl-) in the case of polyvinyl chloride (PVC). Additionally, the process reduces the weight of plastic inputs as these are transformed into gases like CO2 within the mealworm gut, serving as an energy source for their growth. Overall, mineralization is integral to the final stages of plastic degradation, including polymers like PET, by breaking down and incorporating substances into living systems.&lt;SEP&gt;"Mineralization refers to the conversion of organic plastic compounds into inorganic substances through biological processes, often by microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd1ab39969c669dba42f162e8497c23f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81b6237c84f002117fbfe7575d9cdd6e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba22196a59b67cbf67af7d76a039af1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ASSIMILATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Assimilation in this context refers to the absorption and incorporation of the breakdown products of PET into biological systems for further use or growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Assimilation refers to how mealworms incorporate mass from consumed plastic, affecting their overall mass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTERMEDIATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intermediates are the compounds formed during the middle steps of a biochemical pathway, such as those produced in the degradation of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTERSPECIFIC INTERACTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Interspecific interactions refer to the relationships and interactions between different species within an ecosystem, suggested to occur in the larvae's gut during PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2b715dc0bf578795b387fe1255d07f22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNGAL ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fungal enzymes are involved in the biodegradation of plastics, highlighting the role of fungi in plastic decomposition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fungal enzymes are used as catalytic tools for the degradation of polyethylene terephthalate (PET)."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH SALINITY WASTEWATER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High salinity wastewater contains high levels of salts, which can accelerate the removal of micropollutants with the help of light-driven catalysts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPOLLUTANTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Micropollutants are contaminants that exist at low concentrations, which can be removed by specific catalytic processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOSTABLE PETASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermostable PETase is an engineered enzyme variant designed for the degradation of PET at higher temperatures."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOMONOSPORA FUSCA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermomonospora fusca is a thermophilic bacterium capable of biodegrading certain polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE DIGESTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene digestion refers to the biological breakdown of polystyrene by organisms, such as certain larvae, resulting in its biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene digestion refers to the breakdown of polystyrene within certain organisms such as mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of digesting polystyrene, a type of plastic, by organisms such as the yellow mealworm, leading to its breakdown and potential biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process by which specific insects like Tenebrio molitor degrade polystyrene, a type of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene digestion refers to the process by which organisms like yellow mealworms break down and biodegrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4b74f16813537103549cdcb6770abaea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c0c246e98a503a16ace92f28394b584</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHLORIDE IONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chloride ions present in high salinity wastewater accelerate the process of micropollutant removal using light-driven catalysts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIGHT-DRIVEN CATALYSTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Light-driven catalysts are used to accelerate the removal of micropollutants in water treatment processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL RADICALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydroxyl radicals are reactive species generated in electrochemical processes for water purification."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ELECTROGENERATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Electrogeneration refers to the generation of reactive species such as hydroxyl radicals for water purification."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMODYNAMIC INVESTIGATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermodynamic investigation involves studying energy transformations in chemical processes like electrogeneration."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KINETIC INVESTIGATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kinetic investigation involves analyzing the rates of chemical reactions, such as those in electrogeneration for water purification."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET degradation is a process where polyethylene terephthalate is broken down by various enzymes and microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOUPCYCLING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bioupcycling refers to transforming waste materials into new, useful products through biological processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78f3828bbe45c2c38004a29d55dcbf40</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolism encompasses the chemical reactions that maintain the living state of the cells and organism, which may be disrupted by poorly degradable plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism in this context refers to the process by which mealworms break down microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism is the set of life-sustaining chemical reactions in organisms, involved in the biodegradation process of plastics by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The metabolic processes within insects that are affected by the introduction of polystyrene, leading to metabolic stress and degradation of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism is the set of life-sustaining chemical reactions in organisms, influenced by factors like the presence of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism refers to the chemical processes that occur within a living organism to maintain life, and in this study, it is affected by different feeding regimes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism refers to the chemical reactions in mealworms and their gut microbiota that assimilate nutrients and degrade substances like PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism refers to the chemical reactions in living organisms that maintain life, including metabolism of hydrocarbons in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism of mealworm larvae is influenced by their diet and is studied to assess polymer degradation and nutrient assimilation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism refers to biochemical processes for energy production and growth in insects, which varies across different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism refers to the biochemical processes within larvae, influenced by the type of diet they consume."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the biological processes in mealworms that process and break down PU foam or wheat bran."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism refers to the biochemical processes occurring within the larvae that facilitate the biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism in mealworms is affected by low nutritional value from plastic, impacting their mass and viability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolism in mealworms refers to how they process plastics, impacting their weight and viability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d8f385513fcff2125529486530ad5a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64a060d0c9650ae8bed2ca9b44eef10c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a38885f67ed46b97bb84086f575fd43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acbe3a2cb3a17985d07d15bed638bd90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a24fcfbc571e8620a1fc59b215b42e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ead6f17ad517015b638608c5bfe6852&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb16cd27b6682965375050748ee77862&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3d2baf02fdfbafa95ded13de61aaaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1769c4becf46c99994cb7ced2bffda1d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CRASSOSTREA GIGAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Crassostrea gigas is a species of oyster studied for its response to microplastic exposure, including oxidative stress and proteomic changes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEOMIC PROFILING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Proteomic profiling is used to analyze the protein expression changes in organisms exposed to environmental stressors like microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOPHILIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermophilic degradation refers to the breakdown of materials, such as plastics, at high temperatures by heat-loving organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIUM THERMOCELLUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Clostridium thermocellum is an engineered bacterium used for the thermophilic degradation of polystyrene terephthalate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CENTRAL CARBON METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Central carbon metabolism is a critical metabolic process in insects, evaluated for changes when exposed to plastic substrates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLY(ETHYLENE TEREPHTHALATE) (PET)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Poly(ethylene terephthalate) is a type of polyester plastic commonly used in packaging and bottles, subject to environmental degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC FEED SUBSTRATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic feed substrate is used as a carbon source for insect models to study their metabolic responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic feed substrate refers to plastic material used as a dietary component in experimental settings to study its impact on organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-74e2601f950d62c093ceb86612b2374f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYME ACTIVITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
