      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LCFBM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LCFBM is a medium used for growing bacterial strains NyZ450 and NyZ451 with polyethylene to evaluate their degradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"LCFBM is the medium used as the sole carbon source for growing the bacterial strains in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"LCFBM stands for Low-Carbon-Footprint Biological Medium, which is used for incubating bacterial cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"LCFBM is a medium used for incubating PS and cell suspension during biodegradation experiments with Strain YT2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Liquid culture designed to support the growth of PS-degrading microorganisms during enrichment."&lt;SEP&gt;"LCFBM presumably refers to a lab context or medium regime used for bacterial growth to degrade nanoplastic particles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68041096d198a27b4ed526281746b7f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaffdadca6e8a3e9721b2dcb031b93f7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS MICRO POWDERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS micro powders are tiny particles of polystyrene used as a carbon source in incubation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS FILM PIECES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS film pieces are larger fragments of polystyrene used as a carbon source in incubation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT INCREASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Weight increase is a measure of growth observed in bran-fed larvae, unlike PS-fed larvae, indicating nutritional impact."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-92ea4ca7a83af5c086ae157fb801b326</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAE OF TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The larvae of Tenebrio molitor, also known as yellow mealworms, are used to biodegrade PS by chewing and ingesting the material."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larval stage of the beetle Tenebrio molitor, often used in research on biodegradation and as an alternative protein source."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLITES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Compounds produced during metabolism, analyzed to understand PS and CS degradation in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolites are the intermediate or end products of metabolism, identified in the frass fractions being analyzed through chromatography and mass spectrometry."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolites are the smaller molecules resulting from the breakdown of polystyrene during the digestion process in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolites are the substances produced during metabolism, observed in mealworms after digesting microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolites are smaller molecules formed from the breakdown of long-chain PS molecules in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolites are intermediate or end products of metabolism, with varying expression levels depending on the mealworms' diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20395efa1299dc8b777774e765ad1159&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c810740a381d4d9e829e28c01e62564&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FIG. 1B AND D&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Figures 1B and D in the document illustrate the survival rates and PS consumption over a 32-day test period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Manganese (Mn) is listed as a minor component in the frass, showcasing the breakdown products of EPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is a parameter representing the lowest molecular weight portion of a polymer sample, used in assessing plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is the number average molecular weight measured for polymers using gel permeation chromatography in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn, or number average molecular weight, is an indicator of the average molecular weight of PS molecules in a sample."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn represents the number-average molecular weight, a key measure in assessing the degree of depolymerization of PVC."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn is the number-average molecular weight of a polymer, analyzed post-consumption of PS by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Number-average molecular weight (Mn) is a property measured in the study, indicating depolymerization of PS feedstocks by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn refers to the number average molecular weight of a polymer, indicating the average size of polymer chains in LDPE samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-110013d54a5fd492446b1fe049a60fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molecular Weight (MW) is a physical property of PET affecting its biodegradation rate, with different MW levels tested in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Molecular Weight (MW) is a significant factor in understanding the degradation rate and patterns of polymers like PP and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mw is the average molecular weight in the middle of the distribution curve of a polymer, crucial in evaluating depolymerization and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mw is the weight average molecular weight, another parameter measured for polymers with gel permeation chromatography in this context."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mw, or weight average molecular weight, is an indicator of the weighted average molecular weight of PS molecules in a sample."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mw represents the weight-average molecular weight, used alongside Mn to evaluate changes in the plastic polymer structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mw refers to the weight-average molecular weight of a polymer, measured for PS residues to observe changes due to digestion by larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight-average molecular weight (Mw) is a property measured in the PS residues extracted from larvae frass, showing changes due to depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mw stands for the weight average molecular weight, used to evaluate changes in polymer size during biodegradation of LDPE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c426315f0f412528b9c6fb2a82dff2c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b95a108327e39d37d20bdd99db541164&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c84ac252cb9ce906354bda2ad1113aba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW DISTRIBUTION (MWD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MWD refers to the distribution of molecular weights in polymer samples, indicating the range and frequency of different sizes present."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBONYL GROUPS (C=O)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbonyl groups appear during PS degradation as a sign of chemical alteration and breakdown of PS molecules."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-89344d5b5d9debdca45ab8c7c963a585</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN BOND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrogen Bond is the attractive force between the hydrogen atom of a molecule and an electronegative atom, often oxygen or nitrogen, present in another molecule or within the same molecule."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen bonds are intermolecular interactions influencing the hydrophilic or hydrophobic properties of materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydroxyl Group is a functional group consisting of an oxygen atom bonded to a hydrogen atom (–OH), commonly found in alcohols and contributing to the hydrophilic properties of compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hydroxyl (-OH) functional group appeared in the chemical analysis of degraded PVC, indicating oxidative degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroxyl groups (-OH) are functional groups introduced into the polymer during the degradation of PS, contributing to increased hydrophilicity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLIC ACID GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxylic Acid Group is a functional group consisting of a carbonyl group (C=O) and a hydroxyl group attached to the same carbon atom, contributing to acidic properties in organic molecules."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(^{1}H\) NMR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNIQUE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\(^{1}H\) NMR (Proton Nuclear Magnetic Resonance) is a technique used to glean information on the structure, dynamics, reaction state, and chemical environment of molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"\({ }^{1} \mathrm{H}\) NMR is a type of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy used to determine the structure of organic compounds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d6c72a17160a2bc126220798c799a6b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL SHIFT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical Shift refers to the change in the position of NMR resonance, reflective of the electronic environment surrounding a nucleus, used to determine molecular structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBONYL GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbonyl Group is a functional group composed of a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom (C=O), commonly occurring in aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids, and their derivatives."&lt;SEP&gt;"The carbonyl group is a functional group that was observed in polymers, indicating oxidation after being metabolized by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The carbonyl group is another integral component of the oxidized structure of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are functional groups characterized by a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom, newly formed in PS as part of its degradation process."&lt;SEP&gt;"The carbonyl group is a functional group involving a carbon double-bonded to an oxygen, appearing during biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d4ea946a931acf7e5a8292c30ea4c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(T. MOLITOR\) LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larvae of the Tenebrio molitor species, used in experiments for biodegrading various polyethylene types."&lt;SEP&gt;"\(T. molitor\) Larvae, commonly known as mealworms, are the larval form of the mealworm beetle, Tenebrio molitor, known for their ability to degrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VOLATILE ORGANIC COMPOUNDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Compounds released during decomposition that evaporate easily and contribute to weight loss at certain temperatures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Volatile Organic Compounds (VOCs) are organic chemicals that easily vaporize at room temperature, contributing to air pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Volatile Organic Compounds are compounds that easily become vapors or gases, consisting of organic chemicals such as those found as emissions from certain solids or liquids."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RING CLEAVAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ring Cleavage is a chemical reaction in which the ring structure within a molecule is broken, indicating transformation in compound structure during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBONYL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbonyl Groups are functional groups characterized by a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom, important in organic reactions and evidenced in PS transformation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are functional groups composed of a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom, often found in various chemical processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups (-C=O) are functional groups whose formation is a key step in the biodegradation of plastics like HDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical groups formed as a result of the biodegradation process involving Bacillus sp."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are chemical groups that increase in presence within the mealworm fecula compared to polystyrene feedstock."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are chemical groups consisting of a carbon atom double-bonded to an oxygen atom, identified as part of the new functional groups formed in polyethylene after biodegradation by lesser waxworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical groups containing a carbon-oxygen double bond, forming as a result of polyethylene biodegradation in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbonyl groups are functional groups observed in the PS frass residues as a result of chemical transformations during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6675480dfd01dcb533166d01b8e5482e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-069f8447676509ffdd0028e9c05fb9ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eecad183fc5700f3d7e0a266e293de1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eca628f2d85c664191a5205194ec475</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROPHILIC SURFACE PROPERTIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrophilic Surface Properties refer to the affinity of a surface to attract and interact with water molecules, increasing the solubility of compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophilic surface properties describe surfaces that attract water due to the presence of polar groups like hydroxyls, facilitating water adhesion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrophilic surface properties refer to the increased affinity for water observed in degraded PS, due to chemical changes such as bond cleavages."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b858a4eb57553f5b1ae041e1c7160&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(^{1}H\) NMR SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\(^{1}H\) NMR Spectra provide insights into molecular structure by illustrating the chemical environment of hydrogen atoms in a compound."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(CH_2\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\(CH_2\) is a methylene group consisting of one carbon atom bonded to two hydrogen atoms, common in hydrocarbon chains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDIZED FUNCTIONAL GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidized Functional Groups are chemical groups that have been altered through oxidation, suggesting a chemical change in composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidized functional groups are chemical groups within polymers that have undergone oxidation, which can affect the material's properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ORGANIC WASTE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Organic Waste is natural waste material derived from living organisms, which can be biodegraded through natural processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-edd352a71a8bc6e66ece9484f8b59118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROORGANISMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microorganisms are part of the digestive system of mealworms and play a role in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;Gut Microorganisms are a diverse and complex community of bacteria and other microbes residing in the gastrointestinal tract of various organisms, notably including mealworms such as Tenebrio molitor and larvae like Alphitobius diaperinus. These microorganisms play a crucial role in digestion and overall health, as well as in the biodegradation and mineralization of plastics, particularly polystyrene. In mealworms, gut microorganisms assist in breaking down and mineralizing polystyrene into simpler compounds, a process that highlights a symbiotic relationship between the host and its gut flora. This microbial population has also been linked to the decomposition of other plastics such as polyethylene (both LDPE and HDPE) and polypropylene. The microbes present in these systems, including those in mealworms and other larvae like Plesiophthalmus davidis, are essential for understanding the metabolic processes involved in biodegradation, making them a vital component of research into environmentally friendly waste management solutions.&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microorganisms are the diverse microbes residing in the digestive system of organisms, playing a crucial role in the biodegradation of substances like polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-be9cf34196820cc6cfcf8a91406db608&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24ba7d6b1b5275089cbabe56bc33b92e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33f35f87c2ff7792eb41bdd8debdb5f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0627e3f1b0fb440795f73c4fa33093c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab754d1dc0a02ee3f869d2b082a710e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85c9587b55f24a8ae5ba6b478dbb98e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a69982e6abe36c2a0d4a70b78720ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0e22d97687eaef3d31a60f7da6e668&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b8cc3aa81b03290b589ccf0a1548ca0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b3c26a06ad850bb97d0ca2d9217be1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7232abef4870641de7945afeb6a4bf93</data>
    </node>
