    <node id="&quot;HDPE (HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of polyethylene composed of linear chains with very low levels of short chain branching, resulting in high crystallinity and stability."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of polyethylene known for its strength and high density, used in various applications, and subject to biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic that has been shown to be effectively biodegraded by the lesser wax moth Achroia grisella."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of polyethylene known for its high strength-density ratio, subject to biodegradation by certain waxworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"HDPE is a type of plastic included in the study, typically produced as plastic bags, to analyze its consumption by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"High-density Polyethylene is another type of plastic consumed by mealworms, but at lower rates compared to PS and PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22e4b8000531766cffd145ef32353af2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b387da8298c55673284dca93ea82fc9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95142a3f585446bd848eeedadd1ab713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21d8b17fc7993bee31f7e07e4db97cfa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f75b58db5211fc7348ccca8206b9bbae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-EATING WAXWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic-eating waxworms are known for harboring bacteria in their guts that can biodegrade certain types of plastics such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms have the ability to consume and degrade plastics such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"These are worms that have been shown to biodegrade polyethylene through bacterial strains in their guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Waxworms are larvae that have been found to eat and possibly degrade plastics, providing potential avenues for bioremediation research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae known for their ability to consume and break down plastic materials, such as polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms have gut bacteria capable of degrading polyethylene and polystyrene, showing potential for bioremediation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms are larvae known for their ability to consume plastic, potentially assisting in biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms are used in environmental science research for their capability to consume and break down plastics, offering potential solutions to plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating waxworms are organisms capable of biodegrading and mineralizing polystyrene through their gut microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d908d878ed4e7fc36f3af71a5be7f2ad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d068dbad68e3ecd909a551f43f82b893&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc31a0931fceb1446bc6ec4d0c587f7c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f4c282fe2323dc6e310501af888c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-EATING MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworms that participate in the biodegradation and mineralization of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic-eating mealworms degrade polystyrene through gut microbe activity."&lt;SEP&gt;Plastic-eating mealworms, specifically the larvae of the species Tenebrio molitor and Tenebrio obscurus, are known for their remarkable ability to consume, degrade, and mineralize plastic materials, particularly polystyrene. This process is primarily facilitated by the microorganisms present in their guts, which enable the mealworms to biodegrade and metabolize these otherwise non-biodegradable materials. The mealworms' capacity to break down polystyrene and potentially other plastics like Styrofoam not only showcases a unique adaptation but also presents significant potential for addressing environmental plastic waste challenges. By utilizing both chemical and physical means, these organisms play an important ecological role, contributing to the study of biodegradation and offering innovative solutions in terms of natural recycling resources and waste management strategies.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db0003a7244c51697d0129eb84b8e28e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5279ebea7d4d5e22eebe3106de3ba1c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d97be338c51b4c4354e2d0f8de9c9dc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cede90fd3b3eee0bd12d582eaa7d54c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54215690a971d0637c3a6fa61d10f685&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-937d9d4e1671351e8e90f0bd2647f321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bce368b44455642f4b9a1dacc4cdf0f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89c37eb3e3a5b6ab731f5dbe280aa054&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
