      <data key="d2">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;IUPUI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Indiana University Purdue University Indianapolis (IUPUI) contributed to the biology research within the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrition refers to the dietary components provided to mealworms, which enhance their consumption rate and ability to degrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-10474cc69c90eb0c245c8f55d1ec2834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GENTAMYCIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ANTIBIOTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gentamycin is a bactericidal antibiotic that inhibits depolymerization of PS in mealworms, highlighting the role of the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gentamycin is an antibiotic that, when fed to mealworms, disrupts their ability to degrade polystyrene by affecting gut bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARKLING BEETLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Darkling beetle is the common name for species in the family Tenebrionidae, including Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetles belong to the family Coleoptera: Tenebrionidae and are involved in the biodegradation of plastics through their larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"The darkling beetle refers to a genus of beetles, which includes species like Tenebrio molitor that can metabolize expanded polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetle refers to Tenebrio molitor, known for its use as a model in edible insect research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetle refers to the adult form of mealworms, which can accelerate development under improved rearing conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The common term for beetles of the family Tenebrionidae, involved in plastic degradation such as PE and PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d01d291012bd548fc067e250ec39fba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d76ad321e1c68d5f139cdb94d5bf42</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PETROLEUM-BASED PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Petroleum-based Plastics are synthetic materials made from fossil fuels, of which Styrofoam is an example, that are being studied for biodegradation by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Petroleum-based plastics are synthetic materials resistant to biodegradation, major contributors to pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Petroleum-based plastics are synthetic polymers derived from petroleum, including polystyrene, polyethylene, and polypropylene. They are widely used due to their durability and low production costs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21c07a838ff0a489c73c8319e5011134</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\MATHRM{CO}_{2}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CO2 is a product of polystyrene biodegradation by mealworms, resulting from their metabolic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"CO2 is monitored and trapped during the experiments to measure its conversion from Styrofoam ingestion by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon dioxide collected as part of the stable isotopic test to assess PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-70735757eafccc0f762f21f5d3ac8556&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81552b76a273b31cc2bd9ed21ed7a919&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIPID INCORPORATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lipid incorporation refers to the integration of carbon from polystyrene into lipids by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISOTOPIC STUDIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SCIENTIFIC METHOD"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Isotopic studies use \({ }^{13} \mathrm{C}\)-labeled materials to track the transformation of substances like PS during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYNTHETIC PLASTIC WASTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Synthetic plastic wastes are human-made polymers contributing to environmental pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL CONCERN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Environmental Concern refers to the issues arising from plastic waste impacting ecosystems and human health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental concern relates to the issues surrounding the accumulation and impact of synthetic plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-530244abdca5889849fcf86f017ebd77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET STORE MEALWORM STRAIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A particular strain of mealworm from a pet store known to biodegrade PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7c343b12adf5bbce5bf80b96909fe66d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS MATERIALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS materials refer to polystyrene, a synthetic polymer commonly used in products like Styrofoam, that is subject to biodegradation in this study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\({ }^{13} \MATHRM{CO}_{2}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This refers to carbon dioxide enriched with the carbon-13 isotope, a product of polystyrene mineralization during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIPIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A class of biochemical substances whose levels were analyzed in mealworms fed with various types of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipids are a class of organic compounds that can incorporate carbon from polystyrene during biodegradation, as shown by isotopic studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"The study observed a decline in lipid content in mealworms due to starvation or specific feeding, indicating their role in energy storage."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipids in sewage sludge and scum from wastewater treatment facilities can be converted into biodiesel, a value-added product."&lt;SEP&gt;"Organic compounds including fats, oils, and waxes, found in organisms and a potential precursor for biodiesel production in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipids are a class of nutrients that are formed in part by the incorporation of carbon from biodegraded polystyrene in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipids are fat molecules whose levels significantly decrease in mealworms fed PS and PSr, indicating a metabolic response to the unavailability of these materials and potential starvation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipid content in mealworms was quantified using the sulfo-phospho-vanillin assay, which helped in evaluating changes due to plastic consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipids are stored fats in insects, including mealworms, playing a key role in surviving starvation and environmental stress by providing energy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipids, including triglycerides, serve as key energy reserves during starvation, significant in mealworms' physiological response to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipids serve as energy reserves within the insect's body, crucial for energy during starvation, and their oxidation is integral to metabolic processes in insects."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f71fddc259d51158d5c79f38bf41523f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0ec64a7c629b14bfb6652f1960a337c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a11793261251544a542146bc9bf0c5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6d515521d6d2b72e82154cc544d26cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f7fa795762aa89809d8221dc9dda92fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8624d79db8ed1c4488f890a1d0c085a5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2281f98bcfd555a703fd86232dfa42fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM STRAIN FROM BEIJING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This strain of mealworms demonstrated a loss in the ability to degrade polystyrene after antibiotic treatment, suggesting a role for gut microbes in the process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS Biodegradation refers to the process through which polystyrene polymers are broken down by mealworms, involving changes in gut microbiome and metabolic pathways."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS biodegradation refers to the biological breakdown of polystyrene by organisms such as mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS biodegradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene into simpler substances through biological means, including mineralization and depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS biodegradation refers to the microbial breakdown of polystyrene (PS) into simpler compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of breaking down polystyrene, often involving microorganisms and enzymatic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biodegradation of polystyrene by Tenebrio molitor larvae involves the breakdown of this plastic material into less harmful substances through the larvae's digestive processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"The process of plastics being broken down by biological means, specifically by mealworms in this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS biodegradation refers to the breakdown of polystyrene polymers by microorganisms, facilitated by depolymerization and potentially resulting in chemical transformations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70eb294c60e73338ed59a2eddf057c1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a8b07ee5dc7a7af59a93d03311efe60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\({ }^{13} \MATHRM{C}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ISOTOPE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This refers to the isotopic form of carbon used to trace the process of polystyrene mineralization into carbon dioxide."&lt;SEP&gt;"\({ }^{13} \mathrm{C}\) is used as a marker in PS to trace the origin of the carbon in the CO₂ produced by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33a0a8b02ff930ea552c8902e4d0defd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYROFOAM PLATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are plates made from polystyrene, used as feedstock A for biodegradation studies indicating different molecular weights for comparative analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSULFOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insulfoam refers to the brand producing Styrofoam plates, which serve as samples for testing polystyrene biodegradation capacities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL MODIFICATIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical modifications occur in plastics as a result of degradation and partial oxidation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"These are the changes in the chemical structure of polystyrene as identified using spectroscopy methods like FTIR, resulting from digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical modifications refer to the molecular changes observed in plastics, such as degradation and oxidation, caused by environmental or biological processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a8521d10cd6a04d8ba536be9f9dec8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACADEMIC AND NON-ACADEMIC INSTITUTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These institutions collaborated globally to provide data and resources for the study of mealworm polystyrene consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WORLDWIDE SURVEY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A survey organized to collect global data on mealworm behavior and PS consumption, led by one of the co-authors."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8f6621e8191c2fc4d28a63e80e5d467e</data>
    </node>
