      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARSHALL SCHOOL, DULUTH MN, USA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Marshall School in Duluth, Minnesota, USA, conducted studies on mealworm plastic consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UNIVERSITY OF QUEENSLAND, ST. LUCIA, AUSTRALIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The University of Queensland in Australia conducted research on mealworm consumption of PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BANDAR LAMPUNG, INDONESIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bandar Lampung in Indonesia is one of the locations where mealworm plastic consumption was studied."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLAND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Poland is a country where mealworms consuming various plastics, including PVC and PLA, were documented."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YANG ET AL., 2015A, YANG ET AL., 2018&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"REFERENCES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These studies reported formation of beetles from larvae when fed PS plus bran and demonstrated that mealworms can assimilate labeled PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRANDON ET AL., 2018&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A peer-reviewed study documenting the consumption of PE by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Brandon et al.'s 2018 studies also offer methodological references that are utilized in the material analysis described."&lt;SEP&gt;"A referenced study that explores depolymerization in the context of polymer consumption by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BOZEK ET AL., 2017&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"REFERENCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A study from Poland documenting mealworms consuming plastics such as PVC and PLA."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOWARD, 1955&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"REFERENCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A reference describing mealworms' natural behavior of feeding on decaying forest vegetation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8b0b175969d87b3be377f65b988d0a52</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROGEN SOURCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nitrogen sources are essential nutrients for growth and survival that were lacking in the mealworms' diet when fed PS alone."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen sources are provided by gut microbiomes during the degradation of PET, supporting biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC PS CONSUMPTION RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific PS Consumption Rate (SPCR) refers to the rate at which polystyrene is consumed by an organism or process."&lt;SEP&gt;"This measure quantifies the rate at which mealworms consume polystyrene, highlighting differences based on dietary conditions and mealworm sources."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FTIR (FOURIER-TRANSFORM INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An analytical technique used to obtain infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid or gas, used here to study microplastic and larval frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a technique used to identify chemical bonds and structures in materials like polypropylene during degradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is a technique used to obtain an infrared spectrum of absorption or emission of a solid, liquid, or gas, and here it analyzed the chemical changes due to PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical method employed to detect chemical changes in polymers during degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical technique used to detect chemical modifications and degradation of polymers in mealworm larvae frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"FTIR is an analytical technique used to identify chemical changes and new functional groups in mealworm frass after consuming PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-728d6b0b96814c5c3c7b93042e9b9856&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e340b8704554cdc28486ac37e78ab0fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN AND CARBON COMPOSITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This refers to the molecular structure of polystyrene consisting primarily of hydrogen and carbon atoms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\MATHRM{M}_{\MATHRM{N}}\) AND \(\MATHRM{M}_{\MATHRM{W}}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\(\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{n}}\) (number average molecular weight) and \(\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{w}}\) (weight average molecular weight) are measures used to assess the degree of polystyrene depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRACE ELEMENTS AND AMINO ACIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are essential nutrients not provided by PS alone, necessitating the addition of bran to the mealworm diet for balanced nutrition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c818a01113d8c07554629cc5edfd28f9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of bacteria under the order Bacillales, which includes Bacillus alkalinitrilicus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a family of Gram-positive spore-forming bacteria, which includes the genus Bacillus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another bacterial family found to be more abundant in PS samples than in CT samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a family of bacteria comprising genera such as Virgibacillus and Pseudogracilbacillus, which display differential abundance in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacillaceae is a family of bacteria that can be part of the gastrointestinal tract's commensal microbiota and are involved in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOBACILLACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of bacteria that includes Lactobacillus species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactobacillaceae is a family of bacteria whose growth in intestines can be promoted by including T. molitor in diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactobacillaceae is a family of lactic acid bacteria, which includes beneficial gut microbes such as Lactobacillus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactobacillaceae is a family of bacteria with a high relative abundance in the bran-fed control group of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactobacillaceae is a family of bacteria with decreased abundance potentially due to harmful polymer additives and components."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactobacillaceae is another dominant bacterial family affected by microplastics; their abundance is reduced when larvae feed on microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5251cc2ed910c3785b2c7242b7cbc11e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e663286500e382f6d296e2b683bf542&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEUCONOSTOCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of bacteria including Weissella found in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leuconostocaceae is a family of bacteria that includes Weissella, a genus found in larval gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Leuconostocaceae is a family of lactic acid bacteria that are often involved in the fermentation of dairy and vegetables."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ERWINIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Erwinia is a genus of Enterobacteriaceae, known for plant pathogenic species that cause diseases like fire blight in apples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Erwinia is another bacterial genus observed within the mealworm gut in the study, contributing to its microbiome diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Erwinia is a genus of bacteria isolated from the adult T. molitor gut, possibly linked to the adult's altered capacity to process PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Erwinia is a genus of bacteria that includes plant pathogens, typically Gram-negative and rod-shaped."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14f9f0280098bb86719394a4bd53e5bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5318125e74217718b470b7721d1d6365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2665d67f816d2da20a065d708c370210&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLIC ACID GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxylic acid groups are organic functional groups containing a carbon–oxygen double bond and an OH group, often participating in hydrogen bonding."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carboxylic acid groups contain a carbon-oxygen double bond and play a role in defining the hydrophilic characteristics of the biodegraded material."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5a94048d4a1499a73a50e74a59245e6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=C STRETCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C=C stretch refers to the type of vibrational mode within molecules such as benzene rings, characterized by the stretching of carbon-carbon double bonds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROPHOBIC SURFACE PROPERTIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrophobic surface properties describe surfaces that repel water, often due to non-polar, water-repelling functional groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYROFOAM DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrofoam depolymerization is the chemical process of breaking down the polymer chains in styrofoam into smaller molecules."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-04683827a8d88bc2fa28ae73ccc0714f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIAL COMMUNITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbial communities are in mealworms and Indianmeal moth larvae, responding differently to plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbial communities are the diverse microbial populations found in the digestive tracts of organisms, such as wild bumble bees, and their variation is associated with pathogen infection."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbial communities in mealworms play a significant role in the oxidative digestive process that contributes to the biodegradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbial communities refer to the combined populations of bacteria and fungi in the gut of insect larvae, crucial for plastics degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Gut microbial communities consist of various microorganisms residing in the gut, playing roles in digestion, immunity, and potentially interacting with microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3fe22bd6c91507d8d1e60d24886e0eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf7769e197cb664b8d9e41a4c93eec50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER FILMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyester films are flexible, plastic-based films that can be damaged by the chewing activity of insect larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE DIGESTIVE MACHINERY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidative digestive machinery in mealworms facilitates the breakdown of polystyrene within their gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b27c93b39534612a0492f52ce1f3cd8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradability is the ability of substances, like PLA, PS, and PVC, to be decomposed by biological organisms, influencing their environmental impact and toxicological profile."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the capability of a material to be decomposed by biological processes involving bacteria or other living organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the capacity of a substance to be broken down by biological organisms, such as the biodegradation of polymers like polystyrene and polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the capacity of materials to be broken down by the metabolic actions of microorganisms, an important aspect in reducing plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradability refers to the capability of being decomposed by biological agents, such as bacteria or fungi, discussed here in relation to photodegraded polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eada60ee606520104bb515c4896d6b0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLY(METHYL METHACRYLATE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Poly(methyl methacrylate) is a synthetic polymer that is the subject of biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Poly(methyl methacrylate) is a type of plastic known for its resistance to biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Poly(methyl methacrylate), often abbreviated as PMMA, is a type of plastic that can be biologically degraded under certain conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENOL FORMALDEHYDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenol Formaldehyde is a synthetic polymer produced by the reaction of phenol with formaldehyde, used commonly as a thermosetting plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phenol formaldehyde is a synthetic polymer known for its durability, examined in biodegradation contexts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phenol formaldehyde is a synthetic polymer known for its insulating and adhesive properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phenol formaldehyde is a synthetic polymer obtained by the reaction of phenol or substituted phenol with formaldehyde."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phenol formaldehyde is a polymer known for its resistance to biodegradation but has been studied for potential degradation solutions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL CHARACTERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical characterization involves analyzing chemical properties and reactions, often applied in biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Chemical characterization involves analyzing the chemical composition and properties of a substance, such as polystyrene, to understand its behavior and interactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISOTOPIC TESTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Isotopic tests are analyses that use isotopic labeling to trace biochemical pathways and determine the extent of biodegradation or chemical transformations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Isotopic tests are laboratory methods used to study chemical changes during biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Isotopic tests are techniques used to investigate chemical compositions and processes, such as those involved in the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Isotopic tests are used to assess the biodegradation process of plastics by tracking isotopic changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Isotopic tests are used to trace and understand chemical processes by studying isotopic variations, often applied in biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-550dde13ffd2abcf536dd87f7d4c1b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422e833d3381bd5fd0b5a3d63d9047f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sina is a source of news reporting on scientific topics, such as the digestion of plastics by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0a3d3cf8ea423f7e0f637dddade1bbf1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEGRADATION COMPOUNDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Compounds such as monomers and oligomers that form during the biodegradation of polystyrene, analyzed in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FREE FATTY ACIDS (FFAS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FATTY ACID"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Long chain fatty acids released during plastic biodegradation, contributing to the understanding of metabolic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Long-chain free fatty acids that accumulate in insects' biomass during polystyrene biodegradation, indicating enzymatic degradation and metabolism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4f26c2702cadbaabe34c0d0e5cd2ec9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ddf4b13293dd3d8d0887af10fc52c98</data>
