      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium within the Enterobacteriaceae family present in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Erwinia oleae is a bacterium listed under the DAPP-PG 531 group with GU accession numbers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-014b72d0a2657cbc89c4dee5f2b32bba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCUS FAECIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species within the Enterococcaceae family found in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus faecium is a species of bacteria that may enhance gut health and was identified as prevalent in specific diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOCOCCUS LACTIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium that was more abundant in T. molitor gut samples fed with PS, suggesting its potential role in biodegradation of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species within the Streptococcaceae family present in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus lactis is a species of bacteria known for its role in dairy production, showing prevalence in certain diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus lactis is a species of lactic acid bacteria belonging to the family Streptococcaceae, known for its potential role in PS biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOCOCCUS GARVIAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium present in higher concentration in T. molitor gut samples with PS feeding, indicating a role in PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another species within the Streptococcaceae family found in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lactococcus garviae is a species of lactic acid bacteria that has been identified as a possible candidate for polystyrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c095cc3175666d5093fe411f9a39cb66&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
