      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species within the Leuconostocaceae family present in the gut of mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROCOCCUS MUNDTII&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species within the Enterococcaceae family observed in mealworm's gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterococcus mundtii is part of the family Enterococcaceae and is identified in the larval gut microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f7eff62476fb3de27969b584ac3e605</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIUM SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Clostridium species are anaerobic bacteria within Clostridiaceae-1, potentially involved in styrene degradation. They have been identified in larval gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridium species are bacteria with potential probiotic benefits, although they present certain challenges in their application."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c038f908eb10a4a5715fb9cff3e05527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria noted for their capability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas species are a group of bacteria known for their diverse metabolic capabilities, including the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene (LDPE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas species are bacteria capable of biodegrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE), a type of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas species, belonging to the family Pseudomonadaceae, are known for their capability to degrade environmental contaminants such as styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas species are one of the bacterial genera found in the gut of T. molitor, known for their ecological roles and biotechnological applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas species are bacteria known for their capacity to biodegrade various environmental pollutants including low-density polyethylene (LDPE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas species are bacteria known for their ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), a type of plastic."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d9491734042920c5911740665415835&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecf2c478d08c7bc8e491f630aa289881&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21a8e638d3972014f836efe8aa62e3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FAECALIBACTERIUM PRAUSNITZII&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of bacteria with minor population density, considered beneficial for gut health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Facalibacterium prausnitzii belongs to the family Kuminococcaceae and is part of the larval gut microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEUS SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Proteus species are part of the family Enterobacteriaceae and have potential involvement in the biodegradation of polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STENOTROPHOMONAS RHIZOPHILA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stenotrophomonas rhizophila is of the family Xanthomonadaceae, involved in diverse ecological functions including potential degradation activities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMA SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Spiroplasma species are generally considered pathogenic in insects but were the most abundant genus found in T. molitor microbiomes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALTERNARIA ALTERNATA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alternaria alternata, part of the family Pleosporaceae, is previously reported to grow during polyethylene degradation and potentially involved in PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ASPERGILLUS VERSICOLOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aspergillus Versicolor is a fungal species known for its ability to biodegrade Poly(Butylene Succinate-Co-Butylene Adipate)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus versicolor, within the family Trichocomaceae, has been associated with the biodegradation of other polymers and may be active in PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7e3ac1a351ece6e7f29ea15d8b1c721d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
