    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONADACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial family that plays a key role in the degradation of polystyrene as observed in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"A family of bacteria found to be significantly more abundant in PS samples than in CT samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonadaceae is a family known for genera like Pseudomonas, which have roles in biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonadaceae is a family of bacteria known to play a significant role in the gut of insects, potentially involved in polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonadaceae increases significantly in PP-fed groups, indicating its association with PP degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonadaceae plays an important role in the degradation of PP, as indicated by its significant presence in PP-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonadaceae is a family within the class Gammaproteobacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonadaceae is one of the bacterial families classified under Gammaproteobacteria in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KUMINOCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kuminococcaceae is a family of bacteria including Faecalibacterium, which are part of the gut flora."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XANTHOMONADACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Unclassified Xanthomonadaceae is part of the microbial community involved in PS degradation in mealworm guts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Xanthomonadaceae is a family including Stenotrophomonas, involved in ecological and biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ERWINIA OLEA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Erwinia olea is a bacterium belonging to the family Enterobacteriaceae, found in gut samples of PS-fed T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MYCOBACTERIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mycobacterium is a genus within Actinobacteria involved in styrene and other hydrocarbon degradations."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rhodococcus is a genus of Actinobacteria known for providing nutrients and colonizing the digestive tracts of insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus is a genus of bacteria that shows significant variation in the mealworm gut microbiome under different feeding conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus is a genus of bacteria where Styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) enzymes have been located."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus is a genus within Actinobacteria, known for its diverse metabolic pathways, including styrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus is a genus that shows increased abundance in both PS and CS-fed larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus is another bacterium that has demonstrated the ability to degrade polypropylene in a mangrove environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0d13e8c770c847882a94a6b78b0979cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81f09d7c1a5d91a7a0b4766ca0e47f56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPHINGOMONAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus associated with gut microbiomes, influenced by the host diet and environmental conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sphingomonas is a genus of bacteria that increased in abundance in the PVC-fed mealworms, associated with microbial degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sphingomonas is a genus within α-proteobacteria, recognized for its environmental and biodegradation capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XANTHOBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Xanthobacter is a genus within α-proteobacteria, involved in hydrocarbon degradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GOORDNIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Goordnia is a genus within α-proteobacteria, noted for its ecological roles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROMONOSPORACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Micromonosporaceae is a family within Actinobacteria, involved in the degradation of natural and synthetic compounds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPHINGOMONADACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sphingomonadaceae is a family of α-proteobacteria, known for members involved in pollutant degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPORIDIOBOLACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sporidiobolaceae is a family that includes Sporobolomyces, a genus found in fungal communities within PS diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sporidiobolaceae is one of the dominant families in plastics-fed groups, displaying considerable variation compared to bran-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLEOSPORACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pleosporaceae encompasses fungi such as Alternaria and Pithomyces, which participate in organic decomposition and potential polymer degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pleosporaceae is a large family of ascomycetous fungi."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRICHOCOMACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Trichocomaceae is a family of fungi that includes several important genera such as Aspergillus and Penicillium."&lt;SEP&gt;"Trichocomaceae is a fungal family including Aspergillus, known for active roles in biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PITHOMYCES CHARTARUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pithomyces chartarum is a species from the family Pleosporaceae, with limited activity in the presence of PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIET 1&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diet with a bran-to-PS ratio of 4:1 used in the study to evaluate the degradation of PS in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 consists of a 4:1 bran to PS (polystyrene) mass ratio, without the inclusion of water, which results in a higher excretion of PS oligomers in the frass compared to Diet 2."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 is a feeding regime for Tenebrio molitor larvae combining bran and polystyrene in a 4:1 weight ratio."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 is a specific dietary condition under which primary amides increased and fatty acid excretion was affected."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 is an experiment diet used in the study with a bran:PS ratio of 4:1, used to compare to control and experimental conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 refers to a low bran:PS ratio (4:1) possibly affecting microbiome composition and PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 refers to the diet consisting of a lower amount of bran compared to other diets, influencing chemical degradation product concentration."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 represents a feeding regimen of 4:1 bran to PS ratio, showing specific bacterial and fungal presence changes in T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 1 is characterized by a 4:1 polystyrene ratio without water in the study of mealworm biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIET 2&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diet containing a bran-to-PS ratio of 20:1 and water, used to evaluate the impact on PS degradation in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2 elaborated the excretion of fatty acids, and cholesterol levels were affected, due to the dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2 includes a higher ratio of bran and water (Bran:PS 20:1 + H2O), affecting microbial growth and chemical properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2 is an experimental diet with a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 and additional water, analyzed for impact on larvae eating behavior and PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2 is characterized by a high bran:PS ratio (20:1) with added H2O, promoting better hydration and PS consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2, a 20:1 bran to PS ratio with water, influences microbial decrease particularly in fungal communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2 includes a bran to PS (polystyrene) mass ratio of 20:1 with the addition of water, leading to a lower excretion of PS oligomers in the frass and a different distribution of PS monomers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2 includes the presence of water and shows the highest polystyrene consumption with higher mealworm growth and survival rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 2 is a feeding regime for larvae with bran and polystyrene in a 20:1 ratio, including water every fourth day."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIET 3&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A diet with a bran-to-PS ratio of 20:1 without water, used in studying the degradation of PS in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 denotes a 20:1 bran to PS feeding protocol, with notable increases in certain fungal species."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 features a high ratio of bran to polystyrene (Bran:PS 20:1), affecting the composition of chemical degradation products."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 involves a bran:PS ratio of 20:1 without water, leading to the development of anaerobic bacteria like Clostridium."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 is a variant of Diet 2 but lacks additional water, aimed at investigating the effect of water absence on larvae feeding behavior and PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 showed similar effects to Diet 2 regarding fatty acid excretion and increased cholesterol, influenced by PS ratio and water content."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 has a 20:1 bran to PS (polystyrene) mass ratio without water, leading to results similar to Diet 1 in terms of PS oligomer excretion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 has a 20:1 polystyrene ratio without water in the study of mealworm biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Diet 3 is the same as Diet 2 but without hydration, used to analyze the metabolic response of larvae to polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e4030ea35bee29093c1a8f19bc90774&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395b01f15fd97b70e2368941120e9a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3eb3742a8be739a1146cbf15e6b7ae5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROFLORA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microflora are microorganisms residing in mealworm intestines, contributing to the biodegradation process of polystyrene through enzymatic actions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microflora collected from the intestines of T. molitor larvae is studied to assess microbial interaction and activity related to PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microflora refers to the community of microorganisms inhabiting T. molitor's digestive tract and their interaction with diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYCAPROLACTONE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polycaprolactone is a biodegradable polyester, mentioned as a compound similar to PS in biodegradation contexts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-681a7143730d41e569d0d76a926eb069</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;IDIOBOLACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Idiobolaceae is a family of fungi which includes Sporobolomyces."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPOROBOLOMYCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sporobolomyces is a genus within the family Idiobolaceae, known for its pink yeast species such as Sporobolomyces roseus."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALTERNARIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alternaria is a genus within the Pleosporaceae family, known for common saprophytic and plant-pathogenic fungi."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLADOSPORIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cladosporium is a genus within the Davidiellaceae family, comprising many ubiquitous environmental fungi like Cladosporium herbarum."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MALASSEZIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Malassezia is a genus of fungi within the Malasseziaceae family, often associated with skin conditions in humans and animals."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACETOPHENONE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Acetophenone is an organic compound used in the synthesis of other chemicals and was identified in samples related to PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"An organic compound identified in frass after PS biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1,1-DIPHENYL ETHYLENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1,1-Diphenyl Ethylene is an organic compound found as a byproduct in the analysis of digested insect biomass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRANS-1,2-DIPHENYLCYCLOBUTANE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A compound that results from the PS degradation in larvae frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"trans-1,2-Diphenylcyclobutane is an organic compound identified among the degradation products studied."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1A-TETRALIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1a-Tetralin is an aromatic compound found during the analysis of biomass degradation products."&lt;SEP&gt;"1a-tetralin is noted with chemical composition and its standard deviation in data, involved in chemical reactions and degradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f2122660fcfebecf752000198a16f222&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1E-TETRALIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1e-Tetralin is an aromatic compound identified in the study of biomass-related degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PITHOMYCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pithomyces is a genus within the Pleosporaceae family, known for mold species that can be plant pathogens or saprophytes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANDIDA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Candida is a genus within the Saccharomycetaceae family, containing species such as Candida albicans, a common yeast that can be pathogenic in humans."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1,3,5-TRIPHENYLCYCLOHEXANE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1,3,5-Triphenylcyclohexane is a chemical compound identified among PS degradation products in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"A product of PS biodegradation found in larvae frass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;2,4,6-TRIPHENYL-1-HEXENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"2,4,6-Triphenyl-1-hexene is a compound discovered as a byproduct of plastic packaging degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A compound formed during the degradation of PS in larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLEOSPORACEAE UNCLASSIFIED SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An unclassified genus within the Pleosporaceae family observed during the fungal analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRICHOCOMACEAE UNCLASSIFIED SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An unclassified genus within the Trichocomaceae family, representing unidentified fungal species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DOTHIDEOMYCETES UNCLASSIFIED SPECIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CLASS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An unclassified species within the Dothideomycetes class noted in the fungal family analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS COMPOUNDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS Compounds are degradation products related to polystyrene identified in the study's insect biomass analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NIAS COMPOUNDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Non-Intentionally Added Substances (NIAS) are byproducts found in materials such as plastics, identified in the chemical analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de9904e6f38706256e15c7957f8f0527</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;S STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE (SMO)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene monooxygenase (SMO) is an enzyme that catalyzes the oxidation of the sidechain of styrene to styrene oxide in an aerobic environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE OXIDE ISOMERASE (SOI)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) catalyzes the isomerization of styrene oxide to phenylacetaldehyde."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene oxide isomerase (SOI) participates in the aerobic metabolic degradation of styrene, converting styrene oxide into phenylacetaldehyde."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE-2,3-DIOXYGENASE (SDO)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene-2,3-dioxygenase (SDO) introduces two oxygen atoms into styrene, forming styrene cis-glycol during anaerobic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VINYLCATECHOL 1,2-DIOXYGENASE (VC12O)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Vinylcatechol 1,2-dioxygenase (VC12O) catalyzes the meta-cleavage of 3-vinylcatechol during anaerobic degradation of styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vinylcatechol 1,2-dioxygenase (VC12O) is involved in the breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol, producing further metabolites."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VINYLCATECHOL 2,3-DIOXYGENASE (VC23O)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Vinylcatechol 2,3-dioxygenase (VC23O) catalyzes the breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol into various products."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vinylcatechol 2,3-dioxygenase (VC23O) is part of the anaerobic styrene degradation pathway, leading to the breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLACETALDEHYDE DEHYDROGENASE (PAD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenylacetaldehyde dehydrogenase (PAD) converts phenylacetaldehyde to phenylacetic acid."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phenylacetaldehyde dehydrogenase (PAD) is involved in the conversion of phenylacetaldehyde to phenylacetic acid in styrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SHORT-CHAIN FATTY ACIDS (SCFAS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FATTY ACID"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Short-chain fatty acids are produced during the fermentation of nutrients and contribute to intestinal homeostasis in the gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANAEROBIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anaerobic degradation is a process by which microorganisms break down substances like styrene in the absence of oxygen."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic degradation occurs without oxygen and contributes to the breakdown of compounds like styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AEROBIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aerobic degradation involves microorganisms using oxygen to degrade substances like styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aerobic degradation involves using oxygen to break down compounds like styrene with the help of microbial actions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3-VINYLCATECHOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"3-Vinylcatechol is a compound that might undergo further breakdown by specific dioxygenases."&lt;SEP&gt;"3-Vinylcatechol is a metabolite formed during the anaerobic degradation of styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE OXIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An epoxide derived from styrene that can be metabolized by microorganisms during styrene degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene oxide is an intermediate formed from styrene during microbial metabolic degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene oxide is a reactive metabolite of styrene linked to carcinogenic effects in oral exposure bioassays."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene oxide is a chemical compound derived from styrene and serves as an intermediate in several styrene degradation pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FRASS NITROGEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Frass nitrogen indicates the nitrogen content in the fecal matter of the larvae, related to dietary intake and microbial activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLIC PATHWAYS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolic pathways are a series of chemical reactions occurring within a cell that lead to the conversion of one or more substances into others, crucial for maintaining life."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways refer to the series of chemical reactions occurring within a cell to maintain life, such as styrene degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways are the biochemical routes involved in the degradation of plastics in insect larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways in Tenebrio molitor involve processes through which the larvae's microbiome degrades polystyrene and lignocellulose."&lt;SEP&gt;"Series of chemical reactions occurring within a cell, contributing to catabolism, anabolism, and regulation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways are series of chemical reactions within a cell that lead to biosynthesis or breakdown of specific substances like nitrogen or carbon compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways refer to the series of chemical reactions in larvae that are influenced by diet type and plastic composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways consist of a series of chemical reactions within a cell, critical for the understanding of polystyrene degradation mechanisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways refer to the series of chemical reactions within the mealworm gut that facilitate the degradation of plastics and transformation of compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Metabolic pathways involve sequences of chemical reactions in cells, often modified by environmental factors like Mi-PS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630e33352368a0c7f34d56ad45a38e56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydration refers to the presence of water in the diet, which enhances the biodegradation process of polystyrene by improving microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydration refers to the process of providing adequate water to organisms, impacting their metabolism and survival rate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e415042ae4079ee5fe96c7105eb534c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d2f43eebe2309fba46c5335d76df56</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGENASE (SDD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrogenase (SDD) catalyzes the re-aromatization of compounds, notably 3-vinylcatechol in this context."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CORYNEBACTERIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Corynebacterium is a genus of bacteria identified for containing membrane-bound styrene oxide isomerase (SOI)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Found in the gut microbiome of larvae, associated with nitrogen metabolism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2e1faf524e6fabeb512a07b13f37e51e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLACETALDEHYDE REDUCTASE (PAR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenylacetaldehyde reductase (PAR) is involved in equilibrium reactions converting phenylacetaldehyde and phenylethanol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE OXIDE REDUCTASE (SOR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene oxide reductase (SOR) catalyzes the conversion of styrene oxide into phenylacetaldehyde."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPOXIDE HYDROLASE (EH)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Epoxide hydrolase (EH) catalyzes the biochemical hydrolysis of styrene oxide to phenylethanol-1,2-diol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;2-ETHYLPHENOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"2-Ethylphenol is a product of the anaerobic degradation of styrene, leading to further metabolic reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSECTS' FRASS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Insects' frass is waste material excreted by insects, which can contain degraded monomers and oligomers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly known as mealworms, are a type of larvae that consume foams and are used in studies to understand plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;Tenebrio molitor larvae, commonly referred to as mealworms or yellow mealworms, are the larval stage of the mealworm beetle. These larvae are notable for their unique ability to biodegrade various materials, including both natural substances like cellulose and synthetic polymers such as polyethylene (PE), polystyrene (PS), polypropylene (PP), and polyvinyl chloride (PVC). They possess a specialized gut microbiome that, along with specific enzymes, facilitates the decomposition and transformation of these materials, including difficult-to-degrade microplastics such as expanded polystyrene (EPS) and low-density polyethylene foams. 

These larvae have been extensively involved in studies focused on the biodegradation of plastic foams and polymeric substances, where they transform materials into waste aggregates. They contribute to the environmental biodegradation efforts by enhancing the depolymerization process through mastication and ingestion. Their gut microbiota not only aids in digestion and glucoside detoxification but also plays a role in analyzing physiological responses such as oxidative stress and survival rates during biodegradation processes.

Research involving Tenebrio molitor larvae has included experiments to enrich PE-degrading bacterial strains, assess PS degradation, and isolate PS-degrading bacteria, showcasing their versatile applications in biodegradation research. These larvae are frequently used to study biodegradation processes under controlled dietary conditions, specifically examining the biodegradation of aged microplastics through gut microbial activity. The presence of this sophisticated biological system in Tenebrio molitor larvae makes them an important subject for studies concerning their gut microbiome and its response to various diets, purchased for research purposes from companies like Tianjin Huiyude Biotechnology Co.&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae, the mealworms used in the study to examine biodegradation of common microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c6dfb08bb454e8ff642e7975ba7af10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af682f78e3e8970d7d70b027a8f79116&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17052974ec1c317689bf07f0684f130f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-383865d9c2a2b8a7cabd754f1193b8f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4df787efdb36123e3f2a76b5402278ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-567c36cf69b624a7ec0d891d3bd15163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3c4d027474e8769f550ce290c9391ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-032b3b0fe0366e6e76e86abf8c48d200&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7367fc6115c08ae427a6e9e91eaa1de3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56f83fc7c1ea25f2ead74aceccfe4cf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7df3b1ec42b9ac5c08513c3b568a399&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb14281b4a095b6c531146d40b5110a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6035f4b0686837c8c4319bab80d70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-31ad3152e855d5b8085e1e61af57515d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3351564dbd6a0ee6a8b97f6687378ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c02a8ea5e75c58dcb9cfbbcd64f37d82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f141d484320c21ec01b892a9b7d56486</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;3-VINYLCATECHOL META-CLEAVAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"3-Vinylcatechol meta-cleavage refers to the breakdown process that involves the enzymes VC12O and VC23O."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;2-VINYL-CIS,CIS-MUCONIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"2-Vinyl-cis,cis-muconic acid is a product of metabolic breakdown of 3-vinylcatechol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;2-HYDROXY-6-OXOOCTA-2,4,7-TRIENOIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"2-Hydroxy-6-oxoocta-2,4,7-trienoic acid is an intermediate compound in the degradation of 3-vinylcatechol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;2-HYDROXYPHENYL- ACETIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"2-Hydroxyphenyl- acetic acid is a metabolic product that arises during anaerobic degradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLACETALDEHYDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenylacetaldehyde is an intermediate compound formed by the action of SOI and further reacted by PAD to form phenylacetic acid."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLACETIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Observed in PE diets, phenylacetic acid consumption reflects distinct metabolic adaptations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Phenylacetic acid is a metabolic product formed from phenylacetaldehyde by the action of PAD, which enters the TCA cycle."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLETHANOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenylethanol is a compound involved in equilibrium reactions catalyzed by PAR."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYLETHANOL-1,2-DIOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenylethanol-1,2-diol is a compound formed during the hydrolysis of styrene oxide by EH."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MANDELIC ACID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mandelic acid is a product of the oxidation of phenylethanol-1,2-diol by a dehydrogenase."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;2-ETHYLHEXANOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"2-Ethylhexanol is a metabolic product which indicates ring cleavage during anaerobic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OLIGOMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oligomers are a degradation product of styrene polymers, often found in lower quantities during efficient microbial metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oligomers are chemical compounds released from plastic waste which can contaminate soils and groundwater."&lt;SEP&gt;"Short chains of monomers identified in the decomposition of PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oligomers refer to short chains of monomers similar to styrene, also susceptible to biodegradation, contributing to the degradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57edc76f6646a41c4845d94baa519950&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MONOMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Monomers are foundational units of polymers that can be degraded by microbiological processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3815960770483b9c0a1579ca8442e3a6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXPANDED PS (EPS) FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Expanded PS foam packaging, characterized by a density of 0.014 g/cm³, is the primary substrate being assessed for biodegradability by T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AARHUS UNIVERSITY, DEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL SCIENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aarhus University, Department of Animal Science is the institution where T. molitor larvae were reared."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROLLED BARLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rolled barley is part of the diet provided to T. molitor larvae, acting as a control diet for the biodegradation experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rolled barley is used as part of the diet for larvae in the study to assess its impact on polystyrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATR-FTIR SPECTROSCOPY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ATR-FTIR (Attenuated Total Reflectance-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy) is a technique used to identify functional group changes in materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"ATR-FTIR spectroscopy is a technique used to confirm the PS nature of samples by assessing chemical and physical properties through infrared spectra."&lt;SEP&gt;"Attenuated Total Reflection-Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy is an analytical technique used to characterize materials, such as confirming the PS nature of samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a38c714e7f1f701ec1149e25910e16e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIFFERENTIAL SCANNING CALORIMETRY (DSC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"DSC is a thermoanalytical technique used to measure the thermal properties of materials, confirming the nature of materials like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"DSC is a thermodynamic analysis method used in the study to further analyze the properties of PS, confirmed by matching FTIR spectra and glass transition data."&lt;SEP&gt;"Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC) is used to analyze the thermal properties and change in crystallinity of HDPE film."&lt;SEP&gt;"Differential Scanning Calorimetry is a thermoanalytical technique used to determine the glass transition temperature of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6b4ab01931acbc7aa9c77cd03531c57e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c4244cae75095bda7fb526c3d0c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WATER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A universal solvent used in various chemical and biological processes, including phase separation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water is a critical component tested in different diets to assess its influence on T. molitor larvae growth and PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water is provided as a component of the diet for mealworm larvae and plays a role in their growth and PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Water is supplied to all groups of T. molitor larvae to maintain necessary hydration conditions during the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e25fe1af3bede4605e7b94d457c9634&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6dcd4cd232a018bcab118acc67b3f0f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLIMATE-CONTROLLED ROOM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A climate-controlled room is used to maintain consistent temperature and humidity for larvae during biodegradation experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEAD LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dead larvae are monitored and removed throughout the experiments to assess survival rates and health during PS biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTESTINAL MICROFLORA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intestinal microflora are beneficial microorganisms extracted from T. molitor larvae, relevant in studying biological interactions during PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MRD LIQUID&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A dilution medium used in the experiment, contains peptone and sodium chloride."&lt;SEP&gt;"MRD liquid is a dilution solution composed of peptone and sodium chloride used in preparing intestinal contents for analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SODIUM CHLORIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An inorganic compound in the MRD liquid used to maintain osmotic balance in cellular processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride is a component of MRD liquid used to dilute the intestinal microflora during sample preparation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sodium chloride, commonly known as salt, is used to create saline solutions for experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a32ea55d79e87933e26b4165db47b1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BARLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Barley is used as a base diet for larvae, providing nutritional support during experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"Barley refers to a nutritional component given as part of the feeding diet to larvae in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5134944e002326fa5ca136c470d99e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAE SURVIVAL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larvae survival is measured to evaluate the health and viability under various feeding conditions during experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84f576487c8d473c1524eb1141730a12</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAE INTESTINES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Part of the larvae that is used for extraction and analysis in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c83f95e0134b38f78f2624da01ab0f79</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERNATANT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Supernatant is the liquid remaining after solids are removed through centrifugation, containing factors secreted by the gut microbiome that enhance PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Supernatant refers to the liquid fraction remaining after centrifugation, containing various metabolic products and secreted factors from microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The liquid remaining after centrifugation, used for further analysis in the experiment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The supernatant is a liquid byproduct collected during experiments, specifically stored for subsequent analyses and supplementation in microbial cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"The supernatant is a liquid product containing secreted factors from both mealworms and their gut bacteria, used to study degradation processes."</data>
