    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR (T. MOLITOR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of darkling beetle, commonly known as the mealworm, studied for its growth and feed type effects."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of mealworm, commonly used in studies for their capacity to consume and break down various plastics such as polystyrene (PS) and polyethylene (PE)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor is an organism from whose digestive tract certain bacteria have been isolated, relevant to this study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as mealworms, are larvae capable of biodegrading various plastics including polyurethane. They adapt their gut microbial community to assist in this biodegradation process, providing a natural approach for plastic decomposition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as yellow mealworm, is a microorganism capable of biodegrading expanded polystyrene (EPS) due to the presence of specific gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is a species of larvae known for their role in biodegradation experiments, specifically when fed Styrofoam and wheat bran."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2a131a2ef26575f9f4b81bb622ef823e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a3e42eecd3967a9e53cfadc926363ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e276f74ca596eed69175a31dab0bf51b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-512840a413df7ae3d881389c5cf1feb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1</data>
    </node>
