      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another species of mealworm studied for its ability to consume and degrade plastics. It is compared with T. molitor in the context of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio obscurus is another microorganism studied for its ability to biodegrade polystyrene, similar to Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TAI'AN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A location specified as the origin for T. molitor larvae used in plastic degradation experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"A region where mealworms exhibit unique metabolic effects on microplastics such as PVC and PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tai'an is a city in China where mealworms were used in experiments examining changes in microplastic decomposition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tai'an is a city where mealworms were collected for the study and demonstrated different capabilities in metabolizing polymers compared to other locations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tai'an is one of the regions involved in studying the effects of mealworms ingesting microplastics."</data>
