      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A region where mealworms can metabolize PVC and other microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Another location listed as the origin for T. molitor larvae used for analyzing plastic consumption rates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shenzhen is a city where mealworms were collected, and these mealworms showed distinct changes in the crystalline structure of PS and LDPE after metabolism."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shenzhen is another city in China contributing mealworms for research into the breakdown and metabolism of microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Shenzhen is one of the regions where mealworms' interaction with microplastics was examined."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXPANDED PS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A specific form of the polymer Polystyrene that exhibited an increase in mealworm weight during certain studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a54e547b0e77432a65a8a9804c44c5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(T\). MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as the mealworm beetle, is a species of darkling beetle whose larvae are used in the study to test polymer digestibility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as the mealworm, is used in experiments to determine the effects of different types of plastics on larval weight."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22d348f5320f96e129128362b6e0407c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRICLOSAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An antimicrobial agent potentially added to plastics to inhibit microbial growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Triclosan is an antibacterial and antifungal agent found in consumer products, and its interaction with microplastics may enhance toxicity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b5cbd81fc528c18f42514a2a74d4921e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;P. AERUGINOSA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a bacterium isolated from the gut of mealworms responsible for altered polymer surfaces."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METALLOOCENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of catalyst compound based on Hf, used in the production of polyolefins."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYOLEFINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of polymer used widely in various applications; its production involves catalysts like metalloocene compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyolefins are a group of polymers including polyethylene and polypropylene, characterized by carbon-carbon backbone structures and resistance to biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-119b3f144b4ec234392cca17031bd7a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium isolated from the gut of the Zophobas morio insect contributing to the biodegradation process by altering the surface of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of bacteria isolated from the gut extract of superworms, known for its ability to biodegrade various types of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria known for producing rhamnolipid biosurfactants used in various industrial applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is known for degrading crude oil and producing biosurfactants aiding hydrocarbon degradation, also isolated from PS-degrading superworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Ki2y) is a bacterial strain with the ability to biodegrade plastics, producing clear zones in specific media assays."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterial species identified in the study, with several strains being analyzed for plastic degradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium capable of breaking down polystyrene and previously isolated from gut of mealworm larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium involved in the degradation of various forms of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterium found in soil, capable of degrading plastic waste including PS foam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a bacterial species associated with the biodegradation of polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-01bf9080cbf55e62c0b26d0e054bd1fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-41635122b30ee45048cc885b38fff6ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda80cb027c8107defa00011d491b002&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd104d06a1ede06701ecfa96bccc2c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-043ac4479b542b2eb2620db8d408349b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ADULT INSECTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Adult insects have the ability to feed on and degrade plastics, impacting biodegradation efficiency, although their lifespan is shorter when not on optimal feed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEM (SCANNING ELECTRON MICROSCOPY)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SEM is a technique used to visualize surface alterations in polymers, contributing to the study of biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM is a technology used to observe cell morphology and ultrastructure in bacterial studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"SEM is used to observe the surface of degraded PS films, providing visual confirmation of erosion by bacterial action."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-576a8e18f3d6ea8c7d4a5f2cd554423c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE CHANGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemical changes in plastics characterized by increased oxygen content, suggesting oxidative degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TIME-LAPSE MOVIE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A visual recording documenting the process of plastic waste entomoremediation during the experiment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ELEMENT CONTENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The measurement of various elements present in T. molitor larvae after experimentation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NATIONAL SCIENCE CENTRE, POLAND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The funding organization that supported this research through a grant."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-98d7ae4d155085781614411da5564816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE (LDPE AND HDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyethylene, including its low-density (LDPE) and high-density (HDPE) forms, is a common plastic material subject to biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLY(L-LACTIC ACID)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Poly(L-lactic acid) is a biodegradable polyester that can be degraded by specific thermophiles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMATIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A biological process where enzymes are used to break down synthetic plastics, aiding in the reduction of plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymatic Degradation is a biological process involving enzymes breaking down plant biomass and synthetic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymatic degradation involves the breakdown of polymers like polyesters through enzyme activity, often by microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enzymatic degradation is a biological process involving enzymes breaking down plant biomasses and synthetic polymers, offering potential solutions for plastic waste management."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40407fe311340b08da856202f38b459c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIPASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of lipids, active within the mealworm gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipase is an enzyme that catalyzes the breakdown of fats and can degrade polyesters, like those present in some plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipase is an enzyme that catalyzes the breakdown of fats and is encoded by the PueA gene in Pseudomonas chlororaphis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipase is an enzyme whose activity was determined through the hydrolysis of p-nitrophenyl laurate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipase assists in the breakdown and hydrolysis of lipids to release fatty acids."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipase is part of CAZymes found in PS- and CS-fed larvae, contributing to lipid and plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lipase is an enzyme activated in PET-fed samples, involved in the breakdown of fats and acting on ester bonds in PET biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a4c43611ebe237c15c5f9201a9f8dbee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2c62bcfb28547d6b2336f142d27a326&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67425e341a5b2c8102281581fa148554&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5f4bda412174e8b173f180d9b4311ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microbial enzymes are enzymes produced by microorganisms that are involved in various biochemical processes, including the recycling of recalcitrant petroleum-based plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial enzymes are produced by microorganisms and are utilized in the recycling of petroleum-based plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial enzymes are proteins produced by microorganisms that facilitate the biodegradation of plastics, e.g., through enzymatic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial enzymes are used for recycling petroleum-based plastics, facilitating the breakdown of persistent polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Microbial enzymes are explored for their potential in recycling recalcitrant petroleum-based plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81610bdb5fdb95d9a333b42668532b9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59dab99372af01b60a073d8eac17870f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYETHYLENE GLYCOLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyethylene Glycols are polymers that can be biodegraded by certain bacteria such as Pseudomonas stutzeri."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOPHILIC BACTERIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of bacteria that thrives at relatively high temperatures and is capable of degrading certain plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of bacterium capable of thriving at higher temperatures and involved in the biodegradation process of polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-648afbc8ee82935d4026f5915152491c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABLE PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradable plastics are designed to be broken down by microorganisms and other natural processes to reduce pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastics are designed to break down more easily under environmental conditions, potentially reducing long-term pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastics are materials that can be broken down by microbes over time, potentially including PET through enhanced methods studied here."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastics are those that can be broken down by microorganisms, considered as a potential solution to global plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastics designed to degrade through biological processes, potentially reducing environmental impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastics that can be broken down by natural processes, often involving microbial action or insect activity, to reduce environmental impact."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-276c82b9935756a3e668c788458ff922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADING MICROORGANISMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microorganisms, typically bacteria and fungi, that can degrade pollutants like plastics through biological processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOCHEMICAL PROCESSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Processes that involve chemical reactions within and relating to living organisms, often used in the degradation of materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOPHILES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microorganisms that can survive and thrive at high temperatures, some of which are used in the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION EFFICIENCY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure of how effectively a material, such as certain plastics, is broken down by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation efficiency refers to the extent and effectiveness with which organisms like mealworms biodegrade substances such as plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb7b0638c9c59e134df0491b542efdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SBR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SBR, or styrene-butadiene rubber, is a material whose biodegradation is being studied in insects."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vulcanized styrene-butadiene rubber that can be biodegraded by larvae such as Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TIRE CRUMB&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Material composed of shredded tires targeted for biodegradation by certain larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tire Crumb refers to small granules of vulcanized rubber studied for biodegradation by insect larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3d5261cecf34dd62f3f6ee6f6c3e7834&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GREATER WAX MOTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Greater Wax Moth is one of the species observed, with a gut microbiome that includes Enterococcus, Enterobacteriaceae, Serratia, and Enterobacter, which are organisms helping in polystyrene digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Greater Wax Moth refers to the larvae of Galleria mellonella, capable of biodegrading polystyrene and affecting their gut microbiome diversity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Greater Wax Moth, or WM, is an insect species that can degrade plastics, benefiting from its consumption of bee wax, which contains various chemical compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Greater Wax Moth, Galleria mellonella, its larvae are known for biodegrading polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The greater wax moth is a type of larva used in experiments to observe its effects on LDPE film mass loss through consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"The greater wax moth is a species known for its potential ability to biodegrade low-density polyethylene, aiding in plastic waste management."&lt;SEP&gt;"Greater wax moth larvae have microorganisms in their guts that assist in degrading PS, showing certain similarities with other larvae, except a unique FTIR spectra peak distinct from yellow mealworm."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-013f364e92a266c0e922cb0582f7a997&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6e5407936cdb4f49bd5363f782dab563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91ffc7c76e8e0a1757d91f7cf62f568&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a214d2b1c90aeb78e6e8562679d4963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CONFUSED FLOUR BEETLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Confused Flour Beetle, Tribolium confusum, whose larvae are involved in biodegrading plastic wastes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RICE MOTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Rice Moth, Corcyra cephalonica, its larvae are used in the biodegradation of low-density polyethylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd8d6b8105d9868a4ffadf5779d0df5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOGAS GENERATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biogas generation refers to the production of biogas through the breakdown of organic matter, in this case, post-production wastes from insect breeding."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BLACK SOLDIER FLY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Black Soldier Fly is another insect used to manage food waste, compared to mealworms and superworms in terms of biocontainment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The black soldier fly, known for its antimicrobial effects and impact on the gut microbiota of animals, offers benefits in waste biodegradation and animal nutrition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Black Soldier Fly larvae are used as an insect model to test the metabolic impact of plastics and are found to have unique metabolic pathways when exposed to different plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Black Soldier Fly, or BSF, is an insect species recognized for its ability to upcycle organic substrates and is notable in studies of plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8c8dab62f56f2bfabf08bfe99049386a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44565f26e7290c5eb0f26935608a5f16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRACHYSTOLA MAGNA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Brachystola magna is another insect species from which chitosan is derived for comparative studies with Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARK MEALWORM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"INSECT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dark mealworm (Tenebrio obscurus larvae) is related to the study of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The dark mealworm, scientifically known as Tenebrio obscurus, is capable of biodegrading polystyrene, showcasing an important ecological role."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIMICROBIAL EFFECTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Antimicrobial effects refer to the ability of substances, such as those from black soldier fly and yellow mealworm fats, to inhibit microbial growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2c3165b5b483903ac54f813f43b4076d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR LINNAEUS (COLEOPTERA: TENEBRIONIDAE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tenebrio molitor Linnaeus, known as yellow mealworms, are larvae of a beetle species that are studied for their potential to biodegrade expanded polystyrene and low-density polyethylene foams."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOW-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of lightweight plastic material used in a variety of applications, studied for its susceptibility to biodegradation processes by certain microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBE-DEPENDENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The reliance on microorganisms for the degradation of materials, highlighting the biological interactions necessary for processes like biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEAVY METALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Heavy Metals are a group of metals studied for their potential toxicity and need for bioremediation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are elements that can be toxic to organisms and are found in marine plastic litter, posing environmental and health risks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Heavy metals are pollutants that can be released into the air during plastic waste combustion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87427955fe47fc204be31f4d99036055&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METALLOIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Elements that have properties in between metals and non-metals and are present in marine plastic litter, contributing to environmental pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YANG, S.S.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A researcher involved in the study of polystyrene digestion and biodegradation within yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WU, W.M.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A researcher contributing to the understanding of biodegradation in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BRANDON, A.M.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A scientist participating in research on the biodegradation of polystyrene by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-af9ad6e808e93b61ec40d6ba4131fda7</data>
    </node>
