      <data key="d1">"Mealworms from the Shenzhen region are tested for their ability to metabolize and degrade microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM DIET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mealworm diet refers to the specific diet meals consumed by mealworms, including PS and bran, influencing the study of plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The diet composed solely of microplastics to test their metabolism and degradation by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;X-RAY DIFFRACTION (XRD)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"X-Ray Diffraction (XRD) analysis is employed to reveal changes in the relative crystallinity of HDPE film post-degradation by Bacillus sp. PELW2042."&lt;SEP&gt;"X-ray Diffraction is used to determine crystal structures in samples, such as microplastics and frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"XRD is a technique used to study the crystalline structure of microplastics before and after being processed by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02761f0aee6116a25ce05444f6ce8242</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYZER (TGA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"TGA is used to perform thermal characterization of Styrofoam and frass, assessing the degradation rate and composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric Analyzer is used to analyze the thermal properties of samples during heating."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c651f9305c4265540ed7deeb891eae71</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIMETHYLFORMAMIDE (DMF)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"DMF is a solvent used in the extraction of samples for GPC analysis, characterized by its compatibility with certain polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;1,2,4-TRICHLOROBENZENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene (TCB) is a chemical compound utilized as a solvent in chemical reactions and known for its application in studies relating to the degradation of plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a solvent used for the extraction of LDPE samples and known for its high-temperature stability."&lt;SEP&gt;"1,2,4-Trichlorobenzene is a chemical compound used in polymer studies for its solvent properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3aaf626f7d7f62d0cee4a0e5efafeb69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa3bd7c611ad935dba56fd3d7300cbc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CRYSTALLINE STRUCTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Crystalline structure refers to the ordered arrangement of atoms in a crystalline polymer, which changes upon metabolism by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"The crystalline structure of polypropylene affects the biodegradation process of mask layers by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Crystalline structure refers to the ordered arrangement of molecules in a solid, impacting the degradation rate of polymers ingested by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f79f3fee16e3f983c9f04a66e4d50760&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AVERAGE WEIGHT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Average weight measures the mean weight change in mealworms when fed different diets, including bran and microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BHONE ET AL. 2012, YANG ET AL. 2015A, ZHANG ET AL. 2017&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESEARCH"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These references support the findings that microplastics, as a diet for mealworms, do not support adequate growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1e45f9527e7b07e945435dae572dc387</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XRD SPECTRA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"XRD Spectra is an analysis technique used to determine changes in the crystalline structure of plastics before and after metabolism by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOGRAVIMETRIC ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA) measures changes in a material’s weight in relation to changes in temperature, used for studying thermal stability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermogravimetric Analysis is an analytical technique to measure changes in physical and chemical characteristics of materials as a function of temperature."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d76783f53d38e8c7423dff747f0413d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;O–H STRETCHING VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"O–H stretching vibration is a characteristic band observed in FTIR analysis, indicating the presence of hydrogen-bonded hydroxyl groups in the polymer after degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"O–H stretching vibration refers to the vibrational motion of an oxygen-hydrogen bond, seen in various spectroscopic analyses to identify chemical structures."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=O STRETCHING VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C=O stretching vibration is a characteristic absorption band seen in FTIR, indicative of carbonyl group presence, often resulting from oxidation reactions."&lt;SEP&gt;"C=O stretching vibration is an essential feature in the analysis of carbonyl compounds, indicating the presence of functional groups like ketones and aldehydes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–O STRETCHING VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–O stretching vibration is an absorption band in FTIR, associated with carbon-oxygen bonds in degraded polymer samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"C–O stretching vibration refers to the vibrational motion of a carbon-oxygen bond, essential for analyzing alcohols and ethers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CH₂ SHEAR VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CH₂ shear vibration refers to a type of molecular motion found in polymers and used to infer changes in molecular structure during analyses."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR STRUCTURE CHANGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molecular structure change refers to alterations in the arrangement of atoms in polymers as a result of biological metabolism and chemical reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer metabolism is the process by which mealworms digest and chemically transform synthetic polymers into simpler substances."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7ed721221326657b60aadb09faeeac92</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CH\(_2\) AND CH STRETCHING VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CH\(_2\) and CH stretching vibration involves the vibrational motions of methylene (CH\(_2\)) and methyl (CH) groups, important in analyzing hydrocarbons and polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–CL STRETCHING VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–Cl stretching vibration involves the vibrational motion of carbon-chlorine bonds, crucial for identifying organochlorine compounds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DECHLORINATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dechlorination is a chemical reaction involving the removal of chlorine atoms from a compound, significant in the breakdown of PVC."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHAIN SCISSION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chain scission refers to the breaking of chemical bonds in a polymer chain, leading to degradation and depolymerization during pyrolysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PVC MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PVC microplastics are small fragments of polyvinyl chloride, a common plastic, analyzed for their decomposition properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyvinyl chloride (PVC) microplastics, when ingested by mealworms, undergo depolymerization and produce toxic byproducts adversely affecting the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE microplastics are small fragments of low-density polyethylene, a widely used plastic, known for undergoing pyrolysis and chemical changes upon decomposition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALKENES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alkenes are hydrocarbons containing carbon-carbon double bonds, produced as main products during the pyrolysis of LDPE microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;O–H PLANE VARIABLE ANGLE VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"O–H plane variable angle vibration is a type of vibrational motion affecting the angle of an O–H bond within a plane, important in spectroscopic analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CH\(_2\) SHEAR VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CH\(_2\) shear vibration involves lateral motion within methylene groups, significant in identifying material properties in polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CH VARIABLE ANGLE VIBRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CH variable angle vibration refers to changes in the angle within methyl (CH) groups, helping in the analysis of molecular structures."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS MICROPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PS Microplastics refer to small particles of polystyrene used in degradation experiments with mealworm gut microbiomes."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics are small particles of polystyrene used as a carbon source for microbial growth and biodegradation studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics are small plastic particles used as a sole carbon source to study microbial growth and degradation in mealworm gut microbiome studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS microplastics are tiny remnants of polystyrene, analyzed for their structural changes during degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene (PS) microplastics, ingested by mealworms, are notably depolymerized into shorter chains during digestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-902ed6a67c601276c5dbf43c35dd824f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-668212da3bf356132cadb63df6ce09b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eba752cb179633f5dd054ee7c9ee2cba</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MN VALUE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mn value indicates the number-average molecular weight, reflecting the polymer's average mass being degraded or depolymerized."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mn value reflects the number-average molecular weight, which indicates changes in molecular weight distribution in frass from mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW VALUE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mw value is the weight-average molecular weight, representing the distribution of molecular weights in a polymer, critical in depolymerization studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Mw value represents the weight-average molecular weight, used to assess how frass differs in molecular weight distribution due to polymer cross-linking reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CROSS-LINKING REACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A reaction that occurs in polymers, affecting the molecular weight distribution of mealworm frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cross-linking reaction involves the formation of chemical bonds linking polymer chains, affecting the molecular weight distribution of degraded materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DECOMPOSITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Decomposition is a chemical process where complex materials break down into simpler substances, observed in the degradation of microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-4e50d86c5a0ab8e4188ede9ae519d241</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR STRUCTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The molecular structure influences the digestion and metabolism of microplastics by mealworms, particularly with PVC."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENERGY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Energy in mealworms is influenced by their diet, impacting mass, nutrient metabolism, and survival during starvation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Energy refers to the caloric value that microplastics inadequately provide for mealworm growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b0491f3e96c1d355b8431b9d74868f63&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-236f4db8df75799fa6a0a07921158cf0</data>
