      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIALES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORDER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An order of bacteria noted for both PE-reg and OAT diets, varying between aerobic and anaerobic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"An order of bacteria under the class Clostridia."&lt;SEP&gt;"Clostridiales is an order of anaerobic, spore-forming bacteria involved in the fermentation of organic compounds in the gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"An order of bacteria potentially involved, albeit in low abundance, in the degradation activities in T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family under Clostridiales, including Clostridium species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIUM ORYZAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species from the Clostridiaceae family examined in the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTERIALES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORDER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An order of bacteria under the class Gammaproteobacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales are involved in the degradation of pesticides, lignocellulose, and metabolism of aromatic compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales are part of the gut microbiome regardless of diet, playing a consistent role in digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of gram-negative bacteria, many of which are found in the intestines of mammals, including pathogens like E. coli."&lt;SEP&gt;"Partly characteristic of the PE-oxo diet, this order is involved in gut microbial dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria within Gammaproteobacteria, which was divided into seven families including Erwiniaeae in 2016."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacteriales is an order of bacteria that includes the genus Klebsiella, which has nitrogen-fixing capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"The order of bacteria to which the nitrogen-fixing genera found in mealworm guts belong."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fa10245aaff722ac7d69e73bed8afe74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-706b5d3a2fa68c3f52b58132d05b7165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0e30f29e9016acae216566f53e60cea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bf827d2b1d02488e17469a22da9e252&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a4712059883b356b4aea23cb7ef1514</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMA VELICICRESCENS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species with high population density observed under both diet conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOBACILLUS CRISPATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of Lactobacillus identified in the bran-fed group with low population density."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOBACILLUS GRAMINIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species found in both diet groups with varying population densities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOBACILLUS JOHNSONII&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of Lactobacillus present only in the bran-fed group."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOBACILLUS SALIVARIUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species present in the bran-fed group, absent in the EPS-fed group."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIODES DIFFICILE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species known for its role in intestinal conditions, found minimally in the bran-fed group."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEPTOSTREPTOCOCCACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial family including Clostridiodes difficile, identified with low population density."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-73db2a18ccd822d0da3ef61e68d3a6f6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrene degradation is a metabolic pathway observed in PS and CS-fed mealworms, suggesting adaptive microbial processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene degradation is a process through which the chemical compound styrene is broken down into simpler substances, often facilitated by specific metabolic pathways in microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eaf1f7b81bc271c665d7776cc3114a92&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LYSINIBACILLUS TAIWANENSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lysinibacillus taiwanensis is a species of bacteria involved in styrene degradation according to the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KEGG PATHWAY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A collection of pathway maps representing molecular interactions and reactions like those enriched in response to PS and CS."&lt;SEP&gt;"KEGG pathway refers to a set of defined pathways that represent molecular interaction, reaction and relation networks in organisms, helping in understanding high-level functions and utilities of the biological system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROORGANISMS IN NATURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microorganisms in nature are independent living entities that need to prepare for various environmental stresses to survive."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYMBIOTIC MICROBES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Symbiotic microbes are associated with invertebrates' ability to digest polymeric substances like wood and plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Symbiotic microbes are those which live in association with another organism, here involved in the degradation of polystyrene within the host's body."&lt;SEP&gt;"Symbiotic microbes residing in invertebrates' intestines can degrade complex substances like wood and plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7734a37a076c4f934de059042daf10cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9424d3ce58d3ff2e0adc795d24fe84f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6</data>
    </node>
