      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACTIVATED SLUDGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Activated sludge contains mixed microbial populations used in wastewater treatment, capable of degrading organic pollutants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Activated sludge is a complex biomass used in wastewater treatment and biodegradation studies of plastics like polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Activated sludge is a process in which aerated mixtures of microorganisms are used to treat industrial and domestic wastewater, facilitating biodegradation of organic pollutants."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET BOTTLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET bottles are a type of plastic container commonly used for beverages, contributing to plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HDPE NATURAL BOTTLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"HDPE natural bottles are a type of durable plastic container used in packaging, contributing to municipal waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MUNICIPAL SOLID WASTE (MSW)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MSW refers to the waste materials produced by households and businesses, including significant amounts of plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMOPLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Thermoplastics are a type of plastic material that become pliable or moldable at a specific elevated temperature and solidify upon cooling. They are used globally with production expected to change from 2020 to 2050."&lt;SEP&gt;"Thermoplastics are a type of plastic that becomes moldable upon heating and solidifies upon cooling, commonly used in various applications."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6aab9d327fcd7d10b5d4fbb22c03292a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df896f6505a6c800d1edd6c75fce9b4f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYCARBONATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polycarbonate is a type of plastic that is hazardous to the environment and health, part of the 26% most hazardous plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VOLATILE ORGANIC COMPOUNDS (VOCS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"VOCs are air pollutants potentially released during the incineration of plastic wastes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYCYCLIC AROMATIC HYDROCARBONS (PAHS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PAHs are carcinogenic pollutants released during the incineration of plastic wastes."&lt;SEP&gt;"PAHs are complex hydrocarbons that certain Achromobacter strains can effectively decompose."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYCHLORINATED DIBENZO-FURANS (PCDFS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PCDFs are harmful chemicals that may be emitted from the incineration of plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MUSSELS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mussels are ecologically important species harmed by chemicals leached from plastic debris, serving as an indicator of pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SALT-MARSH GRASSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Salt-marsh grasses are ecologically sensitive to microplastic pollution, indicating environmental health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CORALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Corals are ecologically important marine species affected by microplastic pollution and chemical infiltration."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABLE MATERIALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradable materials are alternatives to traditional plastics and are emphasized in pollution control strategies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOOD WEBS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Food webs can be disrupted by hazardous chemicals from microplastics, affecting multiple species and ecological balance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CATALYST REMNANTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Catalyst remnants are residues from the production of plastics that can leach out and contaminate the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMERIZATION SOLVENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymerization solvents are used in plastic production and may leach into the environment, causing potential pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC ADDITIVES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic additives are chemical substances used to enhance the properties of plastics but can become environmental pollutants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plastic additives are substances added to polymers like polystyrene to enhance physical properties but can affect biodegradability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GROUNDWATER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Groundwater is a vital water resource that can be contaminated by chemicals released from plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURFACE WATER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Surface water bodies like rivers and lakes can be polluted by chemicals and microplastics from plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARINE WATER BODIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Marine water bodies include oceans and seas that are increasingly polluted by large amounts of plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARCINOGENIC SUBSTANCES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carcinogenic substances are chemicals that can cause cancer and are emitted during the incineration of plastic wastes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PRECONSUMER PLASTIC WASTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Preconsumer plastic wastes are generated during production processes and can often be recycled and reused."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POSTCONSUMER PLASTIC WASTES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Postconsumer plastic wastes are generated after consumer use and contribute significantly to environmental pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REUSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reuse involves using a product or material again for the same or new purposes, reducing the need for new plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RECOVERY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Recovery refers to the process of retrieving materials or energy from waste plastics, aiding in waste management."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEPARATION EFFICIENCY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Separation efficiency is crucial for effective recycling processes to separate plastics from waste streams."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COST-EFFECTIVE CLEAN-UP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cost-effective clean-up refers to the economical strategies used to remove and recycle plastic waste from the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL AESTHETICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Environmental aesthetics involve the visual quality of landscapes that can be compromised by improper plastic waste management."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LANDFILL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Landfills are sites for disposal of waste materials, including plastics, and can cause long-term environmental issues."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ABIOTIC DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Abiotic degradation involves non-living factors like sunlight and wind breaking down plastics, leading to chemical leaching."&lt;SEP&gt;"Abiotic degradation involves the breakdown of substances through non-living chemical processes, like oxidation or hydrolysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"The breakdown of materials due to non-biological processes such as UV light, thermal oxidation, or chemical reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-602bba09eb2c308c400b412f39f4adb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOXIC BY-PRODUCTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TOXIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Toxic by-products are hazardous substances formed during the breakdown of plastics through unclean processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS FLUORESCENS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial strain involved in the catabolism of styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas fluorescens is a bacterial strain capable of growing on styrene as the sole carbon source, isolated from enrichment cultures."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas fluorescens is a versatile bacterium known for its role in soil health and plant growth promotion, also involved in lignin oxidation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas fluorescens is a bacterial species involved in the solubilization and biodegradation of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas fluorescens is a strain of bacteria known for its ability to catabolize styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfa5755e6c9a5eeae3f595d901dff422&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-933de951613fc7eadfd14dde9bd18b2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XANTHOBACTER SP. STRAIN 124X&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Xanthobacter sp. strain 124X is a styrene-degrading bacterium isolated from a sewage culture."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLAVIN ADENINE DINUCLEOTIDE-REQUIRING STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This enzyme is involved in oxidizing styrene to styrene oxide (phenyloxirane) and is a key component in the metabolic pathway of styrene biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTER CLOACAE DG-6 (ATCC 35929)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enterobacter cloacae DG-6 (ATCC 35929) is capable of almost stoichiometric conversion of styrene to CO2 and low concentrations of aromatic and alicyclic compounds under anaerobic conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOCOCCUS RUBER STRAIN C208&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rhodococcus ruber strain C208 is a biofilm-producing actinomycete that can degrade polystyrene flakes when incubated with them as the sole carbon source."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PAENIBACILLUS URINALIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Paenibacillus urinalis is a bacterium mentioned in the context of polystyrene biodegradation research."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACTINOMYCETE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Actinomycete refers to a group of bacteria known for forming complex multicellular structures and in the context of polystyrene degradation research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinomycetes are a group of bacteria known for their ability to decompose organic materials, including some synthetic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinomycetes are a class of bacteria known for their role in decomposing organic matter and producing antibiotics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Actinomycetes are a group of Gram-positive bacteria known for forming branching filaments and their role in decomposing organic material."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0462cccaa75cf13e553e16026939880b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c4652e08c4ac6887914d0deebe761b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STYRENE MONOOXYGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A novel flavin adenine dinucleotide-dependent enzyme involved in the bacterial degradation of styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Styrene monooxygenase is an enzyme that catalyzes the oxidation of styrene to styrene oxide, a crucial step in styrene degradation by microorganisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MIXED MICROBIAL CULTURES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mixed microbial cultures are complex communities of microbes that participate in the biodegradation of plant-based materials, such as polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEWAGE CULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sewage culture is an artificial ecological environment used to isolate and study microorganisms that degrade compounds like styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYL ACETIC ACIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenyl acetic acids are compounds involved in the metabolic pathways of microorganisms capable of degrading styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHENYL OXIRANE (STYRENE OXIDE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phenyl oxirane, also known as styrene oxide, is a metabolite resulting from the oxidation of styrene and has a role in its biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANAEROBIC CONDITIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anaerobic conditions are environments lacking oxygen where novel bacteria capable of degrading plastics might be investigated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic conditions are environments lacking oxygen, where certain anaerobic microorganisms can metabolize substrates like polystyrene through fermentation or other metabolic pathways."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic conditions are environments that lack oxygen, under which certain bacteria can degrade compounds like styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic conditions refer to an environment devoid of oxygen, allowing specific microbial activities such as biofilm formation and PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic conditions refer to environments lacking oxygen, which facilitate specific microbial metabolic processes in PS biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic conditions refer to environments that lack oxygen, under which Enterobacter hormaechei effectively degrades polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic conditions refer to environments that lack oxygen, under which certain bacteria like Enterobacter sp. LG3 can degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Anaerobic conditions refer to environments without oxygen where specific microbial metabolic activities occur."&lt;SEP&gt;"Environmental conditions lacking oxygen, under which certain microbes, like strain LG3, perform plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d80979bb7e58b10bb558705b5d96fa99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345c72af5aa26d5c4e06d7df5d8a1c85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eafea2357c59ac6712f4d9ab41ac5d14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;14C STYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"14C styrene is a radioactively labeled form of styrene used in laboratory studies to measure biodegradation by tracking 14CO2 release."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7432cb0d6c74fd2dd80b065fbad26b4b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PAENIBACILLUS URINALIS NA26&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial strain isolated from buried PS film in soil, used EPS as carbon source but showed limited biodegradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS SP NB6&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial strain found in buried PS, utilized EPS as carbon source, no notable polystyrene weight reduction observed."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA NB26&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial strain isolated from soil, used EPS as carbon source, intermediates detected with limited polystyrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AZOTOBACTER BEIJERINCKII HM21&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A lignin-decolorizing bacterium with hydroquinone peroxidase that degraded polystyrene in a dichloromethane-water system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOCOCCUS RUBER C208&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial strain with reported polystyrene weight reduction over 4 to 8 weeks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus ruber C208 is a bacterium that removes 4% of branched LDPE films treated with UV irradiation over 30 days."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rhodococcus ruber C208 is a bacterial strain previously reported to degrade polystyrene (PS) with a lower capacity compared to other strains over a certain period."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dfcb17715cb4e5148b33061c1b6cbf1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa9972450967f344c19cb747d7ce649e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROQUINONE PEROXIDASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme from Azotobacter beijerinckii HM21 involved in the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"An enzyme produced by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121, capable of degrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroquinone peroxidase is an enzyme produced by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 that aids in the degradation of polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroquinone peroxidase is an enzyme secreted by Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 that degrades polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydroquinone Peroxidase is an enzyme produced by Azotobacter beijerinckii, capable of degrading polystyrene in a dichloromethane-water system."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-PERFORMANCE LIQUID CHROMATOGRAPHY (HPLC)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An analytical technique used to identify compounds such as intermediates produced during polystyrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ATIQ ET AL. (2010)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A study that reported the isolation of bacterial strains from PS film, focusing on their biodegradation capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-df4c8c66bfbbfc37ed842f53d2b8796c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUCLEAR MAGNETIC RESONANCE (NMR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"NMR is a spectroscopic technique to observe local magnetic fields around atomic nuclei, used in this context to study biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"NMR is employed to analyze the native compositions of Styrofoam and frass, indicating potential chemical structure changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"NMR is used to analyze the structure and composition of polymers during depolymerization and oxidation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d1498160e7767dd16008724d09b2ac64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-79c11bccb4ef9bd152bdae9b5c2c5c8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS) CONSUMPTION RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The rate at which mealworms consume polystyrene, impacted by factors like temperature and co-feeding with bran."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TEMPERATURE (25 °C)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Temperature was maintained at 25 °C during the experimental cultivation of mealworms for polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The temperature at which experiments with mealworm biodegradation of PS were conducted, affecting the degradation rate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-220952ca1d34f3ffef65c5d71e22015e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HARBIN, CHINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A location in China from where PS-degrading mealworms were collected for analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COMPTON, CALIFORNIA, USA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A region in the USA contributing mealworm samples for the study of PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PUR (POLYURETHANE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyurethane is a versatile plastic material that might be degraded by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL AND PHYSICAL PROPERTIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Refers to the properties of plastics like PS that are altered during the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-DEPOLYMERIZING ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hypothetical enzymes that could degrade plastics to solve pollution problems, suggested for separation and identification."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5d3b3c7bbc61318a9728caff13ac5b04</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARINE POLLUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Marine pollution concerns the introduction of harmful substances such as plastics into the ocean, which affects marine life and ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Marine pollution refers to the harmful effects on marine environments due to pollutants such as plastic waste, which can be transported from land into the ocean, affecting marine life and habitats."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-827853316f7304c444884dc82f757802&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Large molecules composed of repeating subunits, relevant in the study of T. molitor's digestive system."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymers are large molecules used in the production of plastics, which can contribute to environmental pollution when not managed properly."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymers refer to large molecules, such as plastic, composed of repeated subunits, which are targets for biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymers refer to large, complex molecules composed of repeating structural units, which include plastics studied for environmental degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymers are large molecules composed of many repeated subunits, studied here in terms of photodegradation and biodegradability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bdf2753a9a78b282c1ca2c0539e3203d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf8041a3cdcd6091f6c96b8b67229df2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fd138e047a780efd715a5ab4701b58c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84297b844ec01d2f58756b669806fd62</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABLE POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradable polymers are a type of polymer that can be broken down by natural processes, particularly by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable polymers are materials that can be broken down by natural biological processes, often used in environmental studies to evaluate synthetic polymer degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable polymers are types of plastics that can be broken down by microorganisms over time, reducing their environmental impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"Materials designed to undergo biodegradation, often used in environmentally-friendly applications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Materials studied for their properties, including the capacity to be decomposed by microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"These are polymers that can decompose under natural conditions, including PHA, PLA, and PCL, but typically require specific conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable polymers like PLA and PBAT are plastics that can be broken down by biological processes, including those by macroinvertebrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable polymers are plastics designed to break down more easily in the environment through natural processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymers like lignin and starch are being tested to improve the biodegradability of polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable polymers are plastics designed to break down more rapidly in the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff933f404b1e6da13d6c1319bc7432c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a31a12c930b59b872c94dda484d0db4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MYTILUS EDULIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mytilus edulis, commonly known as the blue mussel, is a species of marine bivalve mollusk that can ingest microscopic plastics, leading to their translocation within the organism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is a U.S. government agency responsible for protecting human health and the environment by regulating pollutants such as plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CANCER EPIDEMIOLOGY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cancer epidemiology is the study of the patterns, causes, and effects of cancer in specific populations, and can provide insights into environmental factors like pollution."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYNTHETIC POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Synthetic Polymers are man-made materials consisting of long chains of molecules, subjected to enzymatic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Synthetic Polymers, such as polystyrene and polyethylene, are used as a diet for larvae to study their impact on survival and energy loss."&lt;SEP&gt;"Synthetic polymers are man-made materials composed of long, repeating chains of molecules, often examined in studies of environmental degradation and biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Synthetic polymers are man-made materials used extensively in the production of plastics and other goods. They are not biodegradable and are a significant source of plastic pollution."&lt;SEP&gt;"Synthetic polymers are man-made materials studied for degradation by microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi, particularly in ecological and experimental settings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Synthetic polymers, such as polystyrene, are used in studies for potential degradation by microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ee46611b24ea3848df979ded841f06b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59453e4c878e72ac6985a08043cdf126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c31fc195067eeb8acb7f98417bd6744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MARINE DEBRIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Marine debris includes various waste materials, such as plastics, which interact with toxic chemicals in the ocean."&lt;SEP&gt;"Marine debris refers to human-made objects discarded in marine environments, often composed of plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Marine debris refers to human-made objects that are deliberately or accidentally disposed of in marine environments, contributing to pollution and harming wildlife."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a72a9944bcd114b6a3be55ad9b596c77&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1684575ed5f73a2ebe1adc7fbbb3b942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRANSPORT AND RELEASE OF CHEMICALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This refers to the movement and liberation of chemical substances from plastics into the environment, potentially affecting the health of wildlife and ecosystems."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL AND HEALTH HAZARD RANKING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This concept involves evaluating the potential risks posed by various substances, including plastic polymers, to environmental and human health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-71372e333b39cee7eea0c53f89bec5c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;XANTHOBACTER STRAIN 124X&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A strain known for its ability to metabolize styrene oxide and 2-phenylethanol, with involvement in styrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Xanthobacter strain 124X is known for its metabolism of styrene oxide and 2-phenylethanol."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS 305-STR-1-4&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A strain of Pseudomonas capable of producing phenethyl alcohol from styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AZOTOBACTER BEIJERINCKII HM121&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A strain capable of enzymatic degradation of polystyrene using hydroquinone peroxidase."&lt;SEP&gt;"Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a bacterium known for the enzymatic degradation of polystyrene using hydroquinone peroxidase."&lt;SEP&gt;"Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a lignin-degrading bacterium known to secrete hydroquinone peroxidase, an enzyme that can degrade polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Azotobacter beijerinckii HM121 is a bacterium that produces hydroquinone peroxidase, an enzyme that can degrade polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-effe1e87d51e126ff4ec5cf9e49d6524&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-630d06d8ab372b4cadd7e3772d8fa1fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;2-PHENYLETHANOL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"2-Phenylethanol is a secondary product produced by strain LG3 during anaerobic PS degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"2-phenylethanol is an intermediate compound in one of the anaerobic styrene biodegradation pathways."&lt;SEP&gt;"An aromatic alcohol formed as a metabolite during the microbial degradation of styrene by certain bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"2-Phenylethanol is an aromatic alcohol that can be metabolized by certain bacterial strains in the context of styrene degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44e006d8af33633a06dac6789334329d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL TRANSFORMATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The alteration of chemical structures by microbial action, which includes the conversion of compounds such as styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANAEROBIC CONSORTIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anaerobic consortia refer to groups of microorganisms that thrive in environments devoid of oxygen, often involved in degradation processes such as that of styrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Groups of microbes that work together in oxygen-free environments to transform compounds like styrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-20bc31ca22e33a7b244779745e9eeb73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38a07d29ecfa06f82d97e69f5cd2c801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION RATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biodegradation rates measure how quickly materials like polystyrene are broken down by organisms such as mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation rates refer to the speed at which microorganisms can convert materials into simpler substances."&lt;SEP&gt;"The speed at which microorganisms can break down substances, important for assessing the efficiency of biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"This refers to the speed at which biodegradation occurs, important for assessing the efficiency of biological waste processing methods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradation rates refer to the speed or extent to which plastics are biologically degraded, varying based on factors such as organism type and environmental conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff228c7711f94fb27152b187b1fa3eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56604c68c2f6221f63a4afa5d457e8ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c7bcc9530f9dc72038f774f6f084ab6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMICAL CHARACTERISTICS OF POLYSTYRENE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The chemical structure and properties of polystyrene that make it resistant to biodegradation and widely used in various products."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;US NATIONAL SCIENCE FOUNDATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A government agency that funds scientific research across various domains, including biodegradation and environmental studies."&lt;SEP&gt;"The US National Science Foundation provides partial funding for the study, promoting scientific research and discovery."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b7c2ef16f6809388c0c1a87c26bd5c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NATIONAL NATURE SCIENCE FOUNDATION OF CHINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An organization in China that provides grants for scientific research, supporting investigations into biodegradation and ecological processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STATE KEY LABORATORY OF URBAN WATER RESOURCE AND ENVIRONMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A laboratory that offers support for research into urban environmental issues, including biodegradation projects."&lt;SEP&gt;"A research facility in China focused on water resources and environmental studies, providing support for biodegradation research."&lt;SEP&gt;"A research laboratory that funds studies related to biodegradation and environmental sciences."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FINANCIAL SUPPORT GRANTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Various financial supports and grants for scientific research and development in biodegradation and environmental technology."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-39598f698094d1663ca3438bc143bc90</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VOCS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-1b0664a95aeeb896dcab096b098c3b54</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic waste incineration can release VOCs into the air, contributing to air pollution."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON DIOXIDE (CO2)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLITE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A gaseous byproduct of bacterial metabolism, whose concentration is measured alongside methane."&lt;SEP&gt;"CO2 is a significant metabolic product formed during the mineralization of polystyrene by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon dioxide is a colorless gas produced during the breakdown of organic materials, such as the biodegradation of EPS by mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon dioxide is a gaseous end product of the mineralization of plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4443da1fe36ec7133cd5add18d1957a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-237dcdbb783e27ec19d2ebc6b854b744</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEDIUM DENSITY POLYETHYLENE (MDPE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MDPE is a type of polyethylene used in gas pipes, carrier bags, and other products requiring toughness and resistance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTICS (NPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"NPS refers to nano-sized plastic particles that Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 can degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs are extremely small plastic particles that result from the degradation of larger plastic debris."&lt;SEP&gt;"Smaller plastic particles that are less than 1 μm in size and considered emerging pollutants with environmental and biological implications."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are even smaller than microplastics, contributing to environmental pollution and derived from plastic materials like PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics (NPs) are extremely small plastic particles, ranging from 1 to 1000 nm, potentially formed during the biodegradation of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nanoplastics are ultrafine plastic particles with significant environmental implications, emerging from polymer degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules that are responsible for the characteristic chemical reactions of those molecules, such as those involved in the degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules that are responsible for the characteristic chemical reactions of those molecules."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups containing oxygen confirmed the biodegradation and oxidation of LDPE and PS via FTIR analyses."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups refer to specific groups of atoms within molecules that are responsible for the characteristic chemical reactions of those molecules, such as those found on microplastics affecting levofloxacin hydrochloride adsorption."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional groups are specific groups of atoms within molecules that determine the characteristics and chemical reactions of those molecules."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5050676818c53a0ef6d691d3049e923c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e6ccf223c8c62d59ba994731f546ad9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c42631c88da011b836666dae04cfa11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9578db0f9ed0398705827be5d4ee450</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EPS (EXPANDED POLYSTYRENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of polystyrene foam known for its low density, used in packaging and insulating materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a form of PS with a specific molecular weight, used in experiments to test biodegradation in contexts like T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a type of polymer feedstock used in biodegradation experiments to study its breakdown by T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a type of plastic used for insulation, available as ordinary white EPS and grey EPS with graphite. It is characterized by different thermal properties and apparent densities."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a type of plastic that can be biodegraded by mealworms under certain conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a type of polystyrene used in the experiment for mealworms to chew and burrow into, being evaluated for biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"EPS is a form of polystyrene; mealworms' ability to digest it is under study regarding its degradation by intestinal microbes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-11de08c24a626abb11d37d71e9567e2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53dd546535ef4247004faab1fa475889&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a5710c6b3eb755779f706360ec72260e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3b2f11b2dff6f9e39c409f2bbd26c9d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0638e0fe93dc27e678672a164e6088c2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIETARY CONDITIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dietary conditions refer to the feeding environment provided to T. molitor larvae that affects their intestinal microbial flora."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEDIMENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sediments are natural materials, like sand and silt, that can accumulate microplastics, affecting aquatic ecosystems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sediments refer to materials at the bottom of water bodies which host microbial communities involved in biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ffec9fc63ae323ee0c22c612bb89755</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SEAWATER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Seawater is an aquatic environment that contains microbial communities that participate in the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SLUDGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sludge, often found in wastewater, contains diverse microorganisms that contribute to the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SOILS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Soils host a variety of microbial communities that play a role in the biodegradation of polyethylene and polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COMPOST&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Compost is a medium rich in microorganisms used for biodegrading plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW (WEIGHT-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mw refers to the molecular weight measurement of polymers like EPS and LDPE, used in biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MN (NUMBER-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mn is a measure of the average molecular weight of a polymer, relevant in biodegradation analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9aa972c0f1c2838103f61cd8ae101a8f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BEIJING&quot;">
