    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE foam is a form of low-density polyethylene used in packaging, subject to microbial degradation and depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE foam is a type of non-hydrolyzable plastic used as a feedstock for larvae to study biodegradation and consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE foam is another plastic product tested in the study for biodegradation. It has a density of 0.0255 g/cm³ and, like PS foam, is free from major additives."&lt;SEP&gt;"LDPE foam, or low-density polyethylene foam, is another type of plastic material ingested by mealworms to study its biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1740bb657b0f90c48ee5e6d75945ce</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PUR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PUR (polyurethane) is a polymer that has been studied for biodegradation by a landfill microbial culture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a polymer used widely in products, subject to microbial degradation in landfill conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR), a plastic material subject to biodegradation, especially through insect activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane, a hydrolyzable polymer that can be biodegraded more readily than non-hydrolyzable plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyurethane (PUR) is a type of petroleum-based plastic product that yellow mealworms can biodegrade efficiently through their digestive processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d2169963c4d6dae9b98da331d39eaa72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1006283f24aba2aa605f9e719f36cf9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-02e6f7bbb2c1ef92c57b423020b0cc5c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INDIAN MEAL MOTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Indian meal moth (Plodia interpunctella) is known to consume and potentially degrade various plastic materials."&lt;SEP&gt;"Larvae of the Indian meal moth reportedly house PE-degrading bacteria in their digestive tract."&lt;SEP&gt;"Plodia interpunctella, commonly known as the Indian meal moth, involved in studies for biodegrading polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Indian meal moth is a species known to harbor PE- and PS-degrading bacterial strains."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6e05bc34fccfca5e177064daf8346866&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffb4e34985ec90215629dc19750c8bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49ebafd73759fc2bf3d4a642ccad96e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </node>
