    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEPTOTHRIX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Leptothrix is a genus of bacteria significantly associated with the microbiome fed with polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CITROBACTER SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Citrobacter sp. is a bacterium associated with the degradation of polypropylene in the gut microbiome of Zophobas atratus larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a bacterium significantly related to PS and PE diets in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is a bacterium strongly associated with both PE and PS plastic diets in mealworms, involved in aerobic plastic degradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is associated with both polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) degradation, identified in the microbiome of mealworms fed different plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is associated with the biodegradation of PE within the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is associated with the degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene in T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter species are bacteria found within the mealworm gut microbiome, strongly associated with the degradation of both polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is linked with PE and PS degradation in T. molitor and belongs to the Enterobacteriaceae family."&lt;SEP&gt;"Citrobacter sp. is identified as a significant microbial component of the gut microbiome in Z. atratus larvae that are fed polypropylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2efbc0287662aa64684e4db6a131c3de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KOSAKONIA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Kosakonia sp. is a facultative anaerobic bacterium linked with PE and PS biodegradation, playing a role in oxygen incorporation during plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is another bacterium involved in the biodegradation of PE within the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is noted for its association with the degradation of polyethylene and polystyrene in T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. shows a strong association with both polyethylene (PE) and polystyrene (PS) degradation in the gut microbiome of mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia species are bacteria found within the mealworm gut microbiome, strongly associated with the degradation of both polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is associated with both PS and PE diets in mealworms and plays a role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kosakonia sp. is associated with PE and PS degradation in T. molitor, part of the Enterobacteriaceae family."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b42ef7860e1f056d47955d0d13a15cd8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c28d5d503e85e3e6ad15cee35d50e98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-323c02b97d9365a25761a155d358b17a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIGNIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A complex organic polymer in plant cell walls that was biodegraded by a consortium of white rot fungi."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignin is a complex organic polymer found in plant cell walls, often degraded naturally by certain microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignin is a complex organic polymer present in bran, which supports microbial communities involved in depolymerization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignin is a complex organic polymer in plant cell walls consumed by larvae, studied for its digestion in CS feedstocks."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignin is a complex organic polymer in plant cell walls, subject to degradation by enzymes such as laccase and manganese superoxide dismutase."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignin is a complex organic polymer, part of lignocellulose, undergoing degradation through oxidation and enzymatic activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignin is a natural polymer found in plants that is also degraded by Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lignin is an organic polymer often co-processed with plastic polymers during thermal degradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-85fe36521680f0d09a603eab04706561&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6c2706bd609f920aa61d5ef2310db6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca3745cb01a4209e2eae28914dde3b52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53d382cbd1392dbcf9530428c2eb8584&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEMICELLULOSE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hemicellulose is a component of lignocellulosic materials that T. molitor larvae can degrade."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hemicellulose is another structural component of plant cell walls, digested by larvae as part of the CS feedstock."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hemicellulose, part of lignocellulose, is broken down into sugars such as xylose during microbial degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d39fc863538a777920c4eafb37bcf8a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WHEAT STRAW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A fibrous plant material fed to yellow mealworms for biomass production and subsequent biochar generation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat straw contains lignocellulosic materials degraded by microbes in T. molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat straw is an agricultural byproduct used as feed for yellow mealworms for insect biomass production."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05fb1b56e288f49324a636e1365b5406&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db579d338d8f90574e3ac67a3ae9938d</data>
    </node>
