      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rice straw is another source of lignocellulosic material degraded by T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d5dc0ba5d8a1abfb746af0dedb7d575b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORMS (LARVAE OF TENEBRIO MOLITOR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larvae of Tenebrio molitor, also known as mealworms, can biodegrade polyethylene and plastic mixtures and affect the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are larvae known for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene, which impacts their gut microbiome and overall health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, the larvae of the beetle species Tenebrio molitor, are used in studies for their ability to biodegrade polyethylene and plastic mixtures, affecting the gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"These larvae are capable of biodegrading polyethylene and plastic mixtures, which affects their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms, particularly the larvae of Tenebrio molitor, are recognized for their ability to biodegrade plastics like polyethylene and polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are studied for their role in biodegrading polyethylene through their gut microbiome."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are the larvae of the beetle Tenebrio molitor, known for their ability to biodegrade various types of plastic, including PE, PS, and PVC."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5338aa2127367c868a3e7331e1f66225&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19868f1234aac10de61c710c8352c076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03980ce7209ce3f1b1c62c6ed9961e27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33180d5b7e1ebfedf8de59ba1a2a2d04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ee089593160e720e507848fa8c917a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-59879670a8110deb87c1843ffdb71c75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4</data>
    </node>
