    <node id="&quot;WIGGLESWORTHIA GLOSSINIDIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Wigglesworthia glossinidia is an endocellular obligate symbiont of tsetse flies, referenced in studies about genome sequencing and symbiotic bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ASPERGILLUS CLAVATUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aspergillus clavatus is a fungal strain known for its capability to degrade low-density polyethylene (LDPE), isolated from landfill soil."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus clavatus is a fungus identified as being capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE), found in landfill soil."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aspergillus clavatus is a strain capable of degrading low-density polyethylene (LDPE), isolated from landfill soil."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-503c588468d284ba03a34235957f9b02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30b68116bde3b0e31c29adbd05cadaaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRITIONAL INTERACTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutritional interactions refer to the mutually beneficial relationships between organisms and microorganisms in digesting nutrients, as seen in insect-microbial symbioses."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GENOME SEQUENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The genome sequence refers to the complete set of DNA, including all of its genes, within an organism, used for understanding genetic makeup, such as in the research on Wigglesworthia glossinidia."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TSETSE FLIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"INSECT SPECIES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tsetse flies are insects that rely on obligate symbiotic bacteria like Wigglesworthia glossinidia for their survival and nutritional needs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3891caf14683493be3d8709ced467492</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHIZOPHORA DOMINICA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Rhizophora dominica is a species that belongs to the order Coleoptera and family Tenebrionidae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYLLOSPHERE YEASTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGI"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phyllosphere yeasts are fungi that inhabit the leaf surface and can degrade biodegradable plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOTA OF TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are the collective microbial communities present in the gut of the mealworm, Tenebrio molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADABLE PLASTIC-DEGRADING ENZYME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme characterized for its ability to degrade biodegradable plastic, isolated from a yeast from the larval midgut of a stag beetle, Aegus laevicollis."&lt;SEP&gt;"An enzyme characterized from a yeast isolated from the larval midgut of Aegus laevicollis that can degrade biodegradable plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Biodegradable plastic-degrading enzymes are proteins that can break down plastic into smaller, non-toxic components."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5f99668e767a56e2e64ece791653b952&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AEGUS LAEVICOLLIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of stag beetle from whose larval midgut an enzyme capable of degrading biodegradable plastics was isolated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Aegus laevicollis is a species of stag beetle from which a plastic-degrading enzyme was identified."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ff1127258b5df378d8b32fff2a93eb84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-56af93e805daf3987d95ff99ab8e891d</data>
    </node>
