    <node id="&quot;ENTEROBACTER HORMAECHEI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A gut bacterium from mealworms involved in polystyrene microplastics biodegradation under anaerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterium identified as part of the mealworm's gut flora related to polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterium involved in microbiome studies with insects like Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Subject of microbiome study, involved in biodegradation processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterial species with subspecies identified through genome sequencing, highlighting its taxonomic classification."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is a bacterium performing heterotrophic denitrification, often found in wastewater treatment contexts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is a facultative anaerobic bacterial strain isolated from mealworms, capable of oxidizing and depolymerizing polystyrene under anaerobic conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is a species of the genus Enterobacter that is known for its facultative anaerobic capability, exhibiting significant polystyrene degradation activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is an opportunistic pathogen that produces Lipid A, potentially enhancing toxicity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enterobacter hormaechei is the species to which the strain LG3 belongs. It is involved in the degradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a7cbc8be34e0de8aebc6fd547bacc627&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3934e2c5488a209e54e10ba4c0335a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f91980be083a0bf5b52d43a640e9d787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e81a4fde1bdef8287df342fc27193de6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4af956dbe737dee68ab6b9834fe2fcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82390f5824b5ef543e1f258b1a47e167</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER CHAINS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Large molecules composed of repeated subunits that are broken down during the depolymerization process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer Chains refer to the series of linked polystyrene molecules that can be broken down by oxidative enzymes secreted by gut bacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer chains refer to the sequence of repeating monomer units that mealworms break down during depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cbedc06292e5cf07fbca1958d8587e6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE ENZYMES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidative Enzymes are secreted by bacteria to degrade polystyrene polymer chains by breaking them down."&lt;SEP&gt;"Oxidative enzymes are crucial in initiating the breakdown of polystyrene by introducing oxygen into the polymer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9fcb421212175a148a0a1858582cfb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-652bf0615ec39fba0cedd113ec87a78a</data>
    </node>
