    <node id="&quot;TRANSCRIPTOME PROFILING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Transcriptome profiling is a molecular biology technique used to study gene expression in organisms like bighead carp under different dietary conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transcriptome profiling is the measurement of the activity of all RNA molecules within a cell, providing insights into gene expression responses during polymer degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfbd751517e11b1b4052961a01008cbc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRIENT-LIMITED PP POLYMER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrient-limited PP polymer refers to polypropylene materials that are low in essential nutrients, used to study the microbial-mediated degradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-31b2c5f608adabba1cc6986356e96598</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MEALWORM REARING AND BIODEGRADATION OF PP POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This process involves rearing larvae under controlled conditions and assessing their ability to biodegrade polypropylene polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;13C STABLE ISOTOPE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ISOTOPE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"13C Stable Isotope analysis is used to assess changes in the chemical signature of polypropylene residues."&lt;SEP&gt;"Stable isotope of carbon used to track changes in isotope signatures for analysis of biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b04ae62930e9961f964284418f1848b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRANSCRIPTOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The complete set of RNA transcripts produced by the genome, whose sequencing provides insights into gene expression changes following Mi-PS treatment."&lt;SEP&gt;"The transcriptome includes all RNA transcripts present in the mealworms, examined to analyze expression changes in response to different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Transcriptome refers to the complete set of RNA transcripts produced by the genome, for which the profile would provide deeper insights into the function of each isolate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-fcc545cfe4d6e8e04c3698a149d4101a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SECOND-GENERATION SEQUENCING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Second-generation sequencing is a high-throughput method used to analyze the genetic material from the gut microbiome and transcriptome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-152e743d95b64fab136afadf0304d459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ISOTOPIC FRACTIONATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Isotopic fractionation is a methodology used to study biodegradation of polypropylene by analyzing changes in stable isotope signatures before and after it's processed by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIAL COMMUNITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbial community refers to the microorganisms present in the digestive systems of Tenebrio larvae, which were analyzed for diversity and structure changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the collection of microorganisms residing in the gut of T. molitor larvae, which show changes under different conditions and play a role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community in Tenebrio molitor larvae plays a crucial role in assisting the biodegradation process of polypropylene by manipulating microbial dynamics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community refers to the microbiome compositions within T. molitor and T. obscurus, influenced by diet and species."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community of Tenebrio larvae consists of diverse bacterial families and shifts in response to PS feeding."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community refers to microorganisms living in the gut of larvae that are studied for their role in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community includes various bacteria that interact with microplastics, facilitating biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The gut microbial community refers to the diverse population of microorganisms inhabiting the mealworm guts, affected by dietary changes and contributing to biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f23046f647dab4d4caebd663db9aecde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6dce72fccb3df94ec407aba3d4df8eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ee6535da22915c7094098e016b19d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cb5478046e8b80ff49694ccdb8f92fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PRISTINE PP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pristine PP refers to the original form of polypropylene before undergoing biodegradation, having specific molecular characteristics such as molecular weight and polydispersity index."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP RESIDUES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PP residues are the remaining portions of polypropylene after Tenebrio molitor larvae have ingested and partially degraded it, exhibiting changes in molecular composition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STABLE ISOTOPE SIGNATURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stable isotope signature is a compositional characteristic of polypropylene that can be altered through biodegradation, used to track changes during processing by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC PP CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific PP consumption rate (SPCR) is a calculated metric indicating the consumption efficiency of polypropylene by larvae over a specific time period relative to their population."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COMMERCIAL PP MPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Commercial PP MPs are market-ready polypropylene microparticles subjected to biodegradation studies by mealworms, with documented molecular characteristics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\DELTA^{13} \MATHRM{C}\) VALUES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\(\delta^{13} \mathrm{C}\) values are a type of isotopic measurement used to analyze the changes in carbon isotopes during the biodegradation of polypropylene by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GPC RESULTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"GPC results refer to the data obtained from Gel Permeation Chromatography used to assess changes in the molecular weight of polypropylene after biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dbc20d6948e6ca1157ecd7f80a183952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STABLE ISOTOPE ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stable isotope analysis is a technique used to assess the biodegradation of petroleum-derived plastics, offering insights into environmental impacts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACHNATALEA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A dominant bacterium found in the gut of T. molitor larvae, associated with the degradation of natural organic polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"A genus of bacteria found to be abundant in the PET-fed groups and identified as a potential natural organic polymer-degrader."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HAFNIA-OBSUMBACTER SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium found in the gut of T. molitor larvae, reported to be associated with PET and PS degradation in other species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KLEBSIELLA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria, including strains like Klebsiella pneumoniae Mk-1, known for its plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation capabilities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MIXTA SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteria reported in T. molitor as involved in plastic degradation and nitrogen fixation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CRONOBACTER SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria, noted for its potential role in plastic degradation within the gut microbiome of T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cronobacter sp. was one of the dominant genera in the control group of the study, relevant for its presence rather than direct degradation activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cronobacter sp. is a bacterium identified in the mealworm gut microbiome, which may play a role in microbiome stability and plastic degradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-17b1e554820493d86eb8bc96ba8bef37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CORRELATION-BASED NETWORK ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A method analyzing co-occurrence patterns of genera to understand relationships in microbial communities under different conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLOSTRIDIALES FAMILY XIV.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of bacteria found in the gut of PP-L-fed T. molitor larvae, possibly associated with PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACHNOSPIRACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial family thought to play a role in PP degradation in T. molitor larvae, despite low abundance."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEDIOCOCCUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria associated with high MW PP degradation and fermentation in other contexts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pediococcus sp. is a genus of bacteria associated with the production of organic acids from water-soluble carbohydrates during the fermentation of whole-plant corn silage and is suggested to utilize intermediates of polypropylene (PP) degradation as substrates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MW (MOLECULAR WEIGHT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mw represents the averaged molecular weight of polymers, affecting microbial community variations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Refers to the molecular weight of different polypropylene (PP) polymers, influencing the gut microbiota composition and effectiveness of biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-582f48080aab075de76c9c590558639f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP-L, PP-M, AND PP-H&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Categories of polypropylene (PP) with different molecular weights used in feeding T. molitor larvae to study effects on gut microbiome and degradation potential."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYOLEFIN-DEGRADING BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microbial species capable of breaking down polyolefin plastics such as polypropylene, identified in the gut of T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIRGIBACILLUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria possibly involved in the degradation of PP in the gut microbiome of T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KLUYVERA PNEUMONIAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A specific strain of bacteria identified in T. molitor studies associated with high MW PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FERMENTATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A metabolic process used by some bacteria like Pediococcus sp., potentially linked to plastic degradation pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HEATMAP ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A method using Euclidean distances to reveal similarities and differences in gut microbial communities based on feeding conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9c310f4f7adc3c5b8340e973a5af45e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PP FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PP foam is a product with high molecular weight used in this study, compared to PP-H."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene foam, a type of polymer that is subject to biodegradation by T. molitor larvae and Stenotrophomonas strain."&lt;SEP&gt;"PP foam is the polypropylene material used in degradation studies with larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene (PP) foam is a type of polymer material used in the experiment as a control and reference sample during spectroscopic and microbial analysis."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polypropylene foam fed to larvae to observe the effects of microbial suppression and biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc908e62eec4d1d05265c6e451cca84c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4265fd894f71345b4ef09c26e6adbda5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACINETOBACTER NYZ450&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Acinetobacter NyZ450 is a bacterium that cooperates with Bacillus spp. NyZ451 in the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) mulching film."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS SPP. NYZ451&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacillus spp. NyZ451 is a bacterium that cooperates with Acinetobacter NyZ450 for the biodegradation of PE mulching film."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CUTINASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme used in the enzymatic hydrolysis of PET, limited in processing high-crystallinity PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Cutinase is an enzyme found in a higher abundance in polypropylene-fed larvae, playing a potential role in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ABC TRANSPORTER PATHWAYS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ATP-binding cassette (ABC) transporter pathways are highly expressed in larvae fed distinct molecular weights of PP, facilitating intracellular importation of intermediates produced from the extracellular depolymerization of PP."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHEMOTAXIS-RELATED PATHWAYS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chemotaxis-related pathways, including two-component systems, quorum sensing, bacterial chemotaxis, and flagellar assembly, are enriched in PP-fed microbiota, aiding bacterial community colonization and motility."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROGEN METABOLISM PATHWAY (KO00910)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The nitrogen metabolism pathway (KO00910) is enriched in PP-fed groups, indicating nitrogen fixation by intestinal bacteria under nitrogen-deficient diet conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PICRUST ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Picrust analysis is a method used to predict the functional composition of metagenomics samples based on marker gene data, revealing a higher abundance of specific enzymes in PP-fed larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SECONDARY METABOLITES BIOSYNTHESIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Secondary metabolites biosynthesis is a process in which organisms produce complex organic compounds not directly involved in the normal growth, development, or reproduction, noted to be up-regulated in PP-fed larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL METABOLISM IN DIVERSE ENVIRONMENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microbial metabolism in diverse environments encompasses the various biochemical reactions occurring in microbial cells situated in different environmental conditions, essential for PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMINO ACID METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Amino acid metabolism involves the synthesis and degradation of amino acids, correlated with augmented pathways when larvae are fed with different PP types."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBON METABOLISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carbon metabolism includes processes by which organisms convert carbon-based compounds into cellular energy, enriched within PP-fed gut microbiota."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon metabolism involves biochemical processes that convert carbon sources into energy and biomass, essential for cell functioning and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon metabolism involves the biochemical processes responsible for the formation, breakdown, and interconversion of carbon compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon metabolism is involved in the larvae's ability to utilize carbon sources for energy and growth, impacted by their diet of PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon metabolism refers to the metabolic pathways insects utilize to assimilate carbon from plastic substrates."&lt;SEP&gt;"Carbon metabolism refers to the biochemical processes that manage the transformation and use of carbon compounds in organisms, related to plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-90367ecb21e0f14b2e548acf307a2628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e478d12101aa862a23f9dfc7ac8896b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-813b71242f366f10218c10631cd7dee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ce341034979daadf1f9dc7037397d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9a81144d30653377d6d90e26c4497eb0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUORUM SENSING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Quorum sensing is a cell-to-cell communication process that bacteria use to regulate gene expression in response to population density."&lt;SEP&gt;"Quorum sensing is a regulatory system used by bacteria for coordinating behavior based on the local density of the bacterial population, highlighted in plastic degradation by microbiota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb2c1ef38dbbafa838f87d5f4d6fd6a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLAGELLAR ASSEMBLY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Flagellar assembly refers to the process of building the flagellum, a whip-like appendage that provides motility in bacteria, activated in PP-fed microbiota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TWO-COMPONENT SYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A system within microbes activated by diet-derived signals, helping respond and possibly degrade composites like PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Two-component systems are a type of signal transduction pathway commonly found in bacteria, involved in the chemotactic navigation of bacteria toward PP during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5b30daa90b5c91314e1d65e87f191166&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIGNOCELLULOSE DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lignocellulose degradation involves breaking down complex plant materials into simpler sugars, analogous with mechanisms used in PP degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer depolymerization is a chemical reaction process in which polymers are broken down into monomers or oligomers, observed in the degradation of PP."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer depolymerization refers to the process of breaking down large polymer chains into simpler monomers, often facilitated by enzymes and microbial activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymer depolymerization is the breaking down of polymer chains into smaller fragments, a key phenomenon in mealworm-mediated plastic digestion."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfe58fb1bbaa5c35e9c6c837d0b1050f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0817d79fa27f2f5b078e09086250c6e0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MONOXYGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of enzyme identified among the DEGs in T. molitor larvae, contributing to the degradation and oxidation of PP polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEROXIDASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme detected as part of the PP degradation process in larvae, contributing to PP oxidation and polymer breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peroxidase is an enzyme involved in oxidation-reduction processes, detected in degradation pathways of lignocellulose and PS in larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peroxidase plays a role in the oxidative degradation of aromatic compounds in PS and lignin."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peroxidase is a degradative enzyme upregulated during the biodegradation process, aiding the breakdown of oxidized PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Peroxidase is an enzyme that participates in oxidative reactions, contributing to the degradation of aromatic and alkyl compounds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3a889b716cac361e6e04405777075076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca86254d65e47c4049e193cf2098b964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-158317f8eba5f6198135df044d385935&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91523cdd8f510a966e9b301a0adb96b9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MRPS (MULTIDRUG RESISTANCE PROTEINS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROTEIN"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Proteins within T. molitor larvae that are involved in the efflux of plastics, reducing metabolic pressure and aiding in survival under nutrient restriction."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TRANSCRIPTOMIC ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The study of changes in gene expression in T. molitor larvae fed with PP polymers, identifying DEGs related to adaptive and degradative responses."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIODEGRADATION OF PP POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The process by which T. molitor larvae and their gut microbiota work together to break down polypropylene polymers through enzymatic action and microbial activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-181e2c8b4d78916fefcf87c119f11d02</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOEMULSIFYING FACTORS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Substances secreted by T. molitor larvae and their gut microbes to enhance the biodegradation of PP by facilitating enzyme access to polymer surfaces."</data>
