    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR (YELLOW MEALWORM)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tenebrio molitor, commonly known as the yellow mealworm, is a species of darkling beetle used in experiments to study plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC CONVERSION RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic Conversion Rate refers to the efficiency with which yellow mealworms convert ingested plastic into biomass or waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT CHANGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The cumulative net weight change of superworms and yellow mealworms over 35 days, used as an indicator of growth and health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight change in yellow mealworms is measured during plastic degradation experiments to indicate growth and health."&lt;SEP&gt;"The weight change in mealworms from consuming plastics is critical to their viability and reflects their nutritional adaptation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Weight Change refers to the decrease in weight of mealworms after being fed with EPS for 15 days, with an average reduction of 10.8%."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6cc0afd02f25b584d6e3a755fc7272b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53f87a406d4812c07e783e6cbfc09606&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c16dd402d0a926c1a975280e2b32e84e</data>
    </node>
