      <data key="d1">"This refers to the changes in growth and gene expression in organisms like mealworms when they are fed plastic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yellow Mealworms' growth and gene expression can be influenced by the presence of plastics in their diet."</data>
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYBETHYLENE ADIPATE-CO-TEREPHTHALATE (PBAT)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PBAT is a biodegradable plastic developed as an alternative to petro-derived plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYHYDROXYALKONATES (PHA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PHA is a biodegradable plastic used as an alternative to conventional plastics and is known for its biocompatibility."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MACROINVERTEBRATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Large invertebrate organisms like Tenebrio molitor, used in studies to degrade plastics and assess physiological responses to microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Macroinvertebrates are larger invertebrate organisms that play a role in the biodegradation of plastics, generating microplastics and potentially nanoplastics in the process."&lt;SEP&gt;"Macroinvertebrates are organisms without a backbone that are large enough to be seen with the naked eye, involved here in the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84a8f1c256b1141b6bd2b404e817dfb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INFLAMMATORY RESPONSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Inflammatory responses are physical reactions arising from the ingestion of microplastics by organisms without the ability to degrade plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The body's reaction to harmful stimuli such as microplastic ingestion, potentially resulting in damage and stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1536c14bdcd58ae9dabf3a49f6724cd8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL POLLUTANTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Substances like microplastics and nanoplastics considered to have adverse effects on the environment and living organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTARCTIC KRILL (EUPHAUSIA SUPERBA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species known to trituration of PE MPs into NPs, increasing biological barrier crossing capacity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WOODLICE (PORCELLIO SCABER)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Species known to ingest and biofragment plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENCHYTRAEIDS (ENCHYTRAEUS CRYPTICUS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Species involved in ingesting and biofragmenting plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARKLING BEETLE FAMILY (TENEBRIONIDAE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The family to which Tenebrio molitor, a plastic-degrading macroinvertebrate, belongs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PYRALID MOTH FAMILY (PYRALIDAE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family known for species capable of biodegrading plastics, similar to darkling beetles."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIGNOCELLULOSIC MATERIALS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lignocellulosic materials comprise plant biomass made of cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin, and can serve as indicators or substrates in biodegradation research."&lt;SEP&gt;"Materials that are similar to the plastics that some insect larvae can digest."&lt;SEP&gt;"Substances found in plant biomass, known to be consumed by certain larvae like those of T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-694aaaa7c2839ea048d46b6a2bb13748&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44bfda02e6c219cb097ec88543433798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PYRALID MOTH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pyralid moths belong to the family Lepidoptera: Pyralidae and are associated with plastic degradation activities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROINVERTEBRATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microinvertebrates are small animals involved in various ecological roles, including the degradation of plastic materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-104d99248e84b2776ff5ca17c285a7bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIFE STAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Life stage pertains to various stages in an organism's life cycle, here examining how bacteria at different growth phases respond to polystyrene degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae were in their 3-4 larval instars, indicating a rapid growth phase necessary for the study's outcomes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-07a91e5cf8b623163be09e14d1f4978e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C: H: O: N ELEMENT RATIO&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The element ratio of Carbon, Hydrogen, Oxygen, and Nitrogen in wheat bran, indicating its composition as a co-diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ELEMENTAL ANALYZER (VARIO EL CUBE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An advanced analytical instrument used to determine the element ratio of carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, and nitrogen in wheat bran."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHLORINATED INTERMEDIATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intermediate compounds formed during PVC degradation that may be toxic."&lt;SEP&gt;"These are potentially harmful products generated from the biodegradation of PVC, which negatively influence larval survival and health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHTHALIC ACIDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Additives measured in plastic materials that are less than 0.5 ppm, indicating a high purity of materials."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Analytical method used to determine statistical significance of survival rates among larvae under different dieting conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KAPLAN-MEIER SURVIVAL ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A statistical technique employed to analyze survival rates and differences among larval groups receiving varied diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STATISTICAL SIGNIFICANCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METHODOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure used to determine the reliability of constructs such as survival and weight difference results in this study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXTERNAL ABIOTIC DISTURBANCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Factors outside of biological activity that could influence larval sensitivity and responses during experiments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MACROINVERTEBRATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A classification for organisms such as Tenebrio molitor larvae, which are large enough to be seen with the naked eye and are significant in biodegradation studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC PLASTIC CONSUMPTION RATE (SPCR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The rate at which plastics are consumed by larvae, measured in mg plastics per 100 larvae per day, indicating metabolic activity associated with microplastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RAPID GROWTH PERIOD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A developmental phase of Tenebrio molitor larvae during which they exhibit significant weight gain and heightened metabolic activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BINZHOU MEALWORM CO.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Binzhou Mealworm Co. is the company from which the yellow mealworms, used in the study, were purchased."&lt;SEP&gt;"The supplier of Tenebrio molitor larvae and wheat bran utilized in the study, located in Shandong, China."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-0cba3b06dd82e87d3b582bc21c475868&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SIX-WEEK PERIOD&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TIME FRAME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The duration over which larval physiological responses such as survival rates and average weight changes were monitored during the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c64d0f1648a87497fbcae56f3089b417</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOMASS NUTRIENT CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biomass nutrient content refers to the level of key nutrients such as sugar, fat, and crude fat in larvae, impacting their health and growth."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DIGESTIVE PROCESSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Digestive processes involve the breakdown of plastics within the larval gut through enzymes and hydrolysis reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRIENT CONTENT NORMALIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrient content normalization is used to evaluate physiological responses of larvae by assessing nutrient levels under different dietary conditions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TOTAL SUGAR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Total sugar is one of the components assessed in the larvae to evaluate changes in biomass nutrient contents based on different diet conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Total sugar refers to the carbohydrate content in larvae, which is reduced when plastic is the sole diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER DEGRADABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymer degradability indicates the ease with which plastic breaks down, affecting larval growth and nutrient absorption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROLYSIS REACTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydrolysis reactions are processes in which water molecules break down polymers into monomers, aiding in the digestion of plastics by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROPLASTIC (MPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastics (MPs) are small plastic particles that can be ingested by organisms such as T. molitor larvae, affecting their physiological responses."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHLORINATED ORGANICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Chlorinated organics are compounds formed during the incomplete biodegradation of PVC in larvae, associated with toxicity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GROWTH INHIBITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Growth inhibition refers to the reduced development and growth in larvae caused by plastic diets with low degradability."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEHYDRATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dehydration in larvae results from consuming hydrophobic plastics like PS and PVC, leading to higher mortality."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTERMEDIATE TOXICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intermediate toxicity refers to the potential harmful effects of partially degraded plastic on organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymerization is the chemical process by which monomers are combined to form polymer chains, affecting the structure and degradability of plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polymerization is the chemical process that creates polymerized plastics such as PP, PU, and EVA, which are subject to biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-8ee541583aebc7a78ac49285860e4da0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An abbreviation used for Nanoplastic Surfaces, which are analyzed through ATR-FTIR graphs to study spectral changes and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS refers to Novel Polystyrene Substance, targeted in degradation studies involving various bacterial strains."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPS refers to nanoplastic particles, which are being studied for their degradation using various analysis methods."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs, or nanoparticles, refer to plastic particles smaller than 1 μm, which can induce serious physiological stress and toxicity in organisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs are nanoplastics, which are smaller than microplastics and also studied for their biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"NPs, or nanoplastics, are extremely tiny plastic particles of concern due to potential environmental risks."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e557113f1cfd2472fa9c51d1cdc3069a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47e7fe379fcd6b17740bc4d953cd3b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bdcf1b4859e82ab15050837ab539dc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0881f766e34a5d97ee711cd2edd793f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"ROS (Reactive Oxygen Species) levels are used to assess oxidative stress and damage in organisms, represented by fluorescence intensity per mg protein."&lt;SEP&gt;"ROS, or reactive oxygen species, are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen that can cause oxidative stress in organisms. Their levels were measured as indicators of stress in larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORMS (Z. ATRATUS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworms, or Zophobas atratus, are a type of beetle larva known to feed on and degrade plastic materials, similar to T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf697c73d05470fb4a143aa0e4700787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ROS (REACTIVE OXYGEN SPECIES)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Reactive Oxygen Species (ROS) are chemically reactive molecules containing oxygen. In this context, their levels are measured in larvae and are used as an indicator of oxidative stress."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-DEGRADING ABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Refers to the capability of an organism to break down plastics. Eisenia fetida is noted to have poor plastic-degrading ability in the context of the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLUORESCENCE INTENSITY/MG PROTEIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A metric used to quantify the intensity of fluorescence per milligram of protein in the samples studied, indicating the level of ROS."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PS MPS (POLYSTYRENE MICROPARTICLES)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene microparticles are small plastic particles used in the study to measure their effect on ROS levels in organisms like Eisenia fetida."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-dcf2bd0dbdce4d7299bfc35487eaf0f5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROGEN CHLORIDE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL REACTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A degradation product from PVC that contributes to oxidative stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hydrogen chloride is released during PVC biodegradation adding to oxidative stress in organisms exposed to it."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f44f9d05edb3efaf7fdb8f0f1517549</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER HYDROLASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyester hydrolases are enzymes that catalyze the hydrolysis of polyesters such as PLA, facilitating their biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLESTERASES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxylesterases are a group of enzymes involved in breaking down esters, including those found in certain plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;OXIDATIVE DEPOLYMERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Oxidative depolymerization is a chemical reaction involving the breakdown of polymers by reactive oxygen species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e98fb757cb68ae45626058d9c3a15cb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSIOLOGICAL STRESS RESPONSES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These responses refer to the overall reactions of organisms, like T. molitor larvae, to stressors such as microplastics, involving changes in oxidative stress and survival metrics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPARTICLES (NPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"NPs are extremely small plastic particles that can form through the degradation of MPs, posing potential health and environmental risks."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC-DEGRADING CAPABILITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic-degrading capabilities refer to the ability of organisms like T. molitor larvae to break down plastic materials, reducing them into smaller particles or assimilating them."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BIOMASS DEPLETION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Biomass depletion is the reduction in biological mass, usually as a result of negative stress factors like those induced by microplastics exposure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIOXIDANT ENZYMATIC ACTIVITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These activities involve enzymes like SOD, GST, and CAT that protect against oxidative stress by neutralizing reactive oxygen species."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRIENT CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutrient content refers to the composition of essential nutrients within an organism or diet impacting growth and physiological health."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-22ae90a77be18cbac99e5441d47ce2d7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL BREEDING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Larval Breeding refers to the process of raising and growing larvae, a stage in the lifecycle of certain invertebrates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;B.-Y.P.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"B.-Y.P. performed most of the experiments and analyses and helped draft the manuscript."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Y.S.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Y.S. performed some data analysis, provided key technical guidance, and co-wrote the manuscript."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cf54b3c2c01c935a178261a31b8ed897</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;P.L.&quot;">
