      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A polymer type that affects the diversity and composition of gut microbiota in the context of plastic-fed yellow mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyester is a category of polymers that is subject to biodegradation by fungi and other microorganisms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyester is another term for polyethylene terephthalate (PET), often used interchangeably in discussions about plastic waste."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyester refers to textiles made from polyethylene terephthalate (PET), widely used in the industry due to its durability and versatility."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyester, represented by polyurethane (PU) in the study, is a category of polymers assessed for its impact on larval growth and microbiome."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH-CRYSTALLINITY PET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High-crystallinity PET refers to a specific form of PET with a higher degree of orderly arrangement, making it a subject of this biodegradation study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INTERMEDIATE PRODUCTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Intermediate products are byproducts formed during the partial biodegradation of PET before its complete breakdown by mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UPREGULATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Upregulation refers to the increase in gene expression, such as host genes enhancing their activity for PET breakdown in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMERIC WASTE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymeric waste refers to discarded polymer materials like PET, which contribute significantly to environmental waste concerns."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYNERGISTIC ROLE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A synergistic role involves different entities working together to enhance each other's effects, such as gut microbiomes working with mealworm processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-c638e6375f0847be0d1116f4846e5e1b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MONOHYDROXYETHYL TEREPHTHALATE (MHET)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"MHET is a component of PET, consisting of polymerized units."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACTINOMYCETOTA PHYLUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial phylum involved in the enzymatic hydrolysis of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONADOTA PHYLUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial phylum known to participate in PET hydrolysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACTERIODOTA PHYLUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial phylum tested for its ability to enzymatically degrade PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PETASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of PET, capable of processing monomers and low-crystallinity PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LEAF COMPOST-CUTINASE (LCC-ICCG)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme used in treating high-crystallinity PET, with a significant impact on biodegradation time."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR LINNAEUS 1758&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of beetle contributing to plastic biodegradation, notably the yellow mealworms used for PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AROMATIC POLYESTERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aromatic polyesters are a category of plastics including PET, characterized by a repeating aromatic or benzene ring structure within their polymeric chain."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMERIZED UNITS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymerized units refer to the repeated sequence of monomers that form the long-chain structure of polymers like PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT (MW)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Average molecular weight refers to the mean of the molecular weights of all molecules in a given polymer sample, influencing properties such as strength and melting temperature."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT-AVERAGE MOLECULAR WEIGHT (M_W)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Weight-average molecular weight is calculated based on the weight fraction of each polymer chain, providing insight into the distribution of chain sizes in a polymer."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYLUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENETIC CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Phylum is a level of classification or taxonomic rank below Kingdom and above Class, used in the biological taxonomy of organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIGH CRYSTALLINITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"High crystallinity in polymers like PET refers to greater structural order, often resulting in increased stability but slower degradation rates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOW-CRYSTALLINITY/AMORPHOUS PET&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Low-crystallinity or amorphous PET lacks a well-defined crystalline structure, making it generally more susceptible to enzymatic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARKLING BEETLES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A family of beetles to which Tenebrio molitor and species like Zophobas atratus belong, noted for their potential in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetles belong to the family Tenebrionidae, some members of which have displayed the ability to biodegrade plastic materials like PET."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling Beetles refer to beetles of the family Tenebrionidae, whose larvae were used in this study to degrade polypropylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"Darkling beetles, belonging to the family Tenebrionidae, are studied for their larvae's ability to consume and potentially biodegrade various plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5613b129e38cb777855c04f228fe7e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0caad5c42a4b470a8c41ac3fb68c1f64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-411368ddb6a73777c1dd4c683bd389c0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PYRALID MOTHS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pyralid moths belong to the family Pyralidae, with some species contributing to the biodegradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMERIC CHAIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The polymeric chain is the backbone of polymer materials, consisting of repeated monomer units, the structure of which defines many of the material's properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANIMAL FEED&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Animal feed includes food products specifically formulated and produced for consumption by livestock and other animals."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUSTAINABLE FOOD PROTEIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sustainable food protein refers to alternatives to traditional protein sources produced with minimal environmental impact, such as mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BROAD EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION (BD) PATTERN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Broad extent depolymerization refers to the extensive breakdown of polymer chains into smaller molecules, enhancing biodegradation rate."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIMITED EXTENT DEPOLYMERIZATION (LD) PATTERN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Limited extent depolymerization involves a minimal reduction in polymer chain length, often occurring with high molecular weight polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURFACE HYDROPHOBICITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Surface hydrophobicity describes a material's tendency to repel water, affecting its interaction with enzymes and microorganisms in biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THREE-DIMENSIONAL STRUCTURE OF POLYMERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The three-dimensional structure of polymers, including aspects like crystallinity, influences how a polymer behaves and degrades."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1679027e80b766e0469f7323db15b497</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMER TYPE&quot;">
