    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\({}^{13}C\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"\({}^{13}C\) is a stable carbon isotope whose enrichment in residual PET indicates biodegradation in the larval gut."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MN, MW, MZ&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mn (number average molecular weight), Mw (weight average molecular weight), and Mz (z-average molecular weight) are measures used to understand the molecular weight distribution of polymers, providing insight into their degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mn, Mw, and Mz are measurements (average molecular weight, weight-average molecular weight, and z-average molecular weight, respectively) used to assess the polymerization and depolymerization of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b21eb0db725b38c3e0b5c01526ce90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC CONSUMPTION RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific consumption rate measures the amount of polystyrene consumed by mealworms per unit of time relative to their weight."&lt;SEP&gt;"Specific consumption rate measures the rate at which mealworms consume PS under different conditions, such as varying temperatures and nutrient supplements."&lt;SEP&gt;"Specific consumption rate refers to the amount of PET consumed by the larvae per unit time, providing an insight into their biodegradation efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5fb9111bd088e1990cde867c6d85cf28&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc7f923e7edaa7bcfa79c0f7a9896e00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPECIFIC REMOVAL RATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"METABOLIC MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific removal rate indicates the rate at which PET is reduced in the larvae, showcasing the removal of PET material after consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;STABLE CARBON ISOTOPE \(\DELTA^{13} \MATHRM{C}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Stable carbon isotope \(\delta^{13} \mathrm{C}\) is used to trace the enrichment of carbon isotopes in residual PET after degradation, reflecting changes in isotope ratios due to metabolism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WATER-SOLUBLE FRACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The water-soluble fraction refers to the part of the frass containing hydrophilic digested organic substances and salts, relevant to the biodegradation process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ETHANOL-EXTRACTED FRACTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The ethanol-extracted fraction represents the lipophilic substances in the frass, which account for a portion of the biodegraded PET output."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RESIDUAL SOLID CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Residual solid content indicates the portion of frass that consists of non-soluble materials, including residual enzymes and microorganisms, post-PET consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METABOLIZED COMPONENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metabolized components are the end products of PET degradation, contributing to the compostition of the frass."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INORGANIC RESIDUES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Inorganic residues are non-organic compounds found in frass, which result from the breakdown of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a88f3ed3eec20cee61421cc094dde89f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTIBIOTIC TREATMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ANTIBIOTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A mixture of antibiotics used in the study to suppress gut microbes in T. molitor larvae to observe the effects on PET depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MICROBIAL INHIBITION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The inhibition of gut microbes in T. molitor to demonstrate that PET depolymerization is independent of microbial action."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WB-FED GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Groups of T. molitor larvae fed with wheat bran (WB), which provide abundant nutrients for larval development."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET1100, PET27100, PET63500&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Different molecular weight PET polymers (1.10, 27.10, and 63.50 kDa respectively) used in the study to test the effect on biodegradation by T. molitor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\MATHRM{M}_{\MATHRM{N}}, \MATHRM{M}_{\MATHRM{W}}, \MATHRM{M}_{\MATHRM{Z}}\)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molecular weight-related measures used to characterize PET polymers before and after depolymerization."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;\(\DELTA^{13} \MATHRM{C}\) STABLE ISOTOPE SIGNATURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A stable isotope signature used to confirm PET biodegradation by comparing residual to virgin PET MPs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RESIDUAL PET CONTENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Represents the percentage of PET remaining in the frass after consumption by larvae, indicating the level of biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WB&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"WB (wheat bran) is used as the control diet for the larvae in the biodegradation experiments."&lt;SEP&gt;"WB refers to Wheat Bran, used as a control diet for comparison against polyethylene diets in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"WB, or Wheat Bran, is a diet provided to control groups, known for its nutritional richness used to enhance larval growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"Wheat bran, used as a nutrient-rich diet for T. molitor larvae to support development and compare growth against PET-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"WB is a normal feedstock for yellow mealworms and superworms, providing all necessary nutrients for mealworms to complete their life cycle."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6ac1b1db6ffab6f4cfea7a525785a2fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4d84d6de496440fcabc6616d1c96ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa33fb34ba06610c290cdfd8a5b4e916&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AGAR GELS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A medium used to feed PET MPs to T. molitor larvae in the study, facilitating controlled experimentation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-69679184959639a1ff81c7392cd5cef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET1100&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET1100 is a type of polyethylene terephthalate with a low molecular weight, exhibiting a rapid and significant biodegradation in the larval intestinal tract."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET27100&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET27100 represents a medium molecular weight polyethylene terephthalate, common in commercial products, showing moderate biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET63500&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET63500 is a high molecular weight polyethylene terephthalate, showing less biodegradation efficiency compared to lower molecular weights."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–H GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–H Group represents carbon-hydrogen bonds, observed to decrease during the oxidation of PET in FTIR analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–O STRETCHING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–O Stretching involves the ether aromatic group in PET, which is degraded during the biodegradation process by T. molitor larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=O FUNCTIONAL GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C=O Functional Group is a carbonyl group whose intensities and positions shift during PET oxidation during degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL FUNCTIONAL GROUP&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydroxyl Functional Group is introduced into PET structure as a part of the biodegradation process, increasing polymer hydrophilicity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METHYLENE PROTONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Methylene Protons arise from main chain fragmentation in PET, detected during 1H NMR analysis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e24cc3865ed97850c3f25795ff514e8d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;16 S RRNA GENE AMPLICONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MARKER"</data>
      <data key="d1">"16 S rRNA gene amplicons are used to assess the gut microbial community responses to high purity PET MPs exposure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS GOTHETIILI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A strain of bacterium within the phylum Firmicutes known for PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BACILLUS MURALIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Another species within Firmicutes capable of degrading PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SERRATIA PROTEAMACULANS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterium in the Proteobacteria phylum associated with PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VIBRIO SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacteria species within the phylum Proteobacteria involved in PET degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Vibrio sp. is part of the microbial culture used in the biodegradation of polypropylene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a8c5ce99d234a2ac272a8b4b8135e53</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTERASE/LIPASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"An enzyme found in the PET-fed groups, responsible for initiating the biodegradation of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALKANE 1-MONOXYGENASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alkane 1-monoxygenase is an enzyme that can introduce oxygen to the alkane substrate, breaking down hydrocarbons by acting on C-C bonds and transforming them to alcohols."&lt;SEP&gt;"An enzyme that can introduce oxygen to the alkane substrate, contributing to the breakdown of hydrocarbons, including PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LACTOCOCCUS SP.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lactococcus sp. is a genus that was dominant in the control group, known more for its presence than degradation activity."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6c5b5ab19cbfa62cb38c185cba114ed3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET-DEGRADING METABOLIC FUNCTIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"PET-degrading metabolic functions refer to the metabolic pathways and activities in organisms, particularly in the gut microbes of T. molitor, that lead to the breakdown of PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBES IN T. MOLITOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut microbes in T. molitor are microorganisms residing in the gut of T. molitor larvae, playing a significant role in degrading PET and processing its metabolites."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KEGG GENES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"KEGG genes are genes involved in various metabolic pathways and biological functions that were significantly enriched in the gut microbiota of PET-fed groups compared to the control group."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAZY FUNCTION CLASSIFICATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CAZy function classification refers to the categorization of carbohydrate-active enzymes involved in PET degradation based on their expression levels in different PET-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;N-FIXING BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"N-fixing bacteria are symbiotic bacteria present in the gut microbiota that can fix nitrogen, providing supplementary nutritional benefits for T. molitor larvae fed on PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;METAGENOME SEQUENCING ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Metagenome sequencing analysis is a method used to study the genes and metabolic functions of microbial communities, such as those involved in PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ESTERASE/LIPASE (K14731)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Esterase/Lipase is an enzyme involved in degrading PET, with its related genes showing higher expression levels in PET-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CARBOXYLESTERASE (K002170)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Carboxylesterase (K002170) is a specific enzyme variant involved in PET degradation, highlighted in the heatmap plot of the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALKANE-1 MONOOXYGENASE (K00496)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alkane-1 monooxygenase (K00496) is an enzyme variant crucial to breaking down hydrocarbons, including those derived from degraded PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CAZY DATABASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"CAZy database classifies carbohydrate-active enzymes, helping identify those involved in PET degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PET-FED GROUPS: PET100, PET7100, PET63500&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"EXPERIMENTAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are groups of T. molitor larvae fed with different molecular weights of PET in the study, showing differential enzymatic and metabolic responses."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;BENZOATE, AMINO BENZOATE, FLUOROBENZOATE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are aromatic compounds involved as intermediates during PET degradation, to which gut microbes display chemotaxis."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROTEIN OF 8.60 TO 23.00 ΜG PER DAY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This refers to the limited amount of protein provided by nitrogen-fixing bacteria in the gut of T. molitor larvae fed with PET."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-41b8c7f0b790b0289a7683fde0eca126</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NITROGEN-FIXING BACTERIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Bacteria capable of converting atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia, playing a crucial role in the nitrogen cycle."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria in the gut of Styrofoam-eating mealworms capable of fixing atmospheric nitrogen and supplying it to the host."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria isolated from mealworm guts capable of fixing nitrogen, important for nitrogen cycles and studied through genomic and biochemical methods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bacteria present in the gut of mealworms responsible for nitrogen-fixation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nitrogen-fixing bacteria are microorganisms capable of converting atmospheric nitrogen into a form usable by living organisms, supporting nitrogen supply in specific environments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-77817419b966e501f1106866cbea7350&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b12451ba2a22e6f74c543f982a0a7b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9aac53ba8b358857c4dcd3b6cd9e8991&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d290a6a81802d3048c35d2a2fb1252c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e37da2fbe64f85c50c36da45006f4c23</data>
    </node>
