    <node id="&quot;EPS BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EPS biodegradation refers to the breakdown of expanded polystyrene into simpler compounds such as biomass and carbon dioxide, primarily facilitated by mealworms and their gut bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FOURIER-TRANSFORMED INFRARED SPECTROSCOPY (FTIR)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"FTIR is used to gather spectral data, providing evidence of polystyrene breakdown in fecula samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MASS BALANCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mass Balance refers to the accounting of ingested and egested mass of polystyrene to evaluate degradation and conversion to frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass balance is a chemical accounting method used to track the mass of inputs and outputs in the mealworm experiments to measure degradation and mineralization."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass balance is a concept used to measure the input and output of mass in any system, particularly in chemical processes like biodegradation, to ensure conservation of mass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass balance is an analytical calculation determining the conversion efficiency of plastics into gaseous or other forms during biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mass balance is an analytical measurement indicating the total input and output of biodegradable material, such as polyethylene in lesser waxworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-05b7bf66b11c66f02e73a07fb823b8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29fa50fac7d523f43c540627750c823&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6bd3778dedc6a0463017df9f8d6396eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE FEEDSTOCK&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene feedstock refers to the original material provided to mealworms for the purpose of studying their ability to biodegrade this specific form of plastic."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-260d8c268067de32e5ac98d66ab70a6c</data>
    </node>
