    <node id="&quot;ACHROMOBACTER XYLOSOXIDANS M9&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial strain isolated from yellow mealworm intestines, exhibiting potential for degrading polystyrene."&lt;SEP&gt;"A bacterium isolated from Tenebrio molitor, showing 7% biodegradation over 30 days in PS film."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a bacterium capable of degrading polystyrene nanoplastics effectively, showing significant potential for bioremediation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter xylosoxidans M9 is a strain isolated from mealworm intestines known for degrading petroleum hydrocarbons and different types of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-26138366e1d9310732edc569ba338bfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cbf098ec253f99187f3c98d5d0589f7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NANOPLASTIC POLLUTION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nanoplastic pollution refers to the contamination of ecosystems, particularly marine environments, by tiny plastic particles derived from larger plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ACHROMOBACTER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A genus of bacteria known for its potential in biodegradation, including species such as Achromobacter denitrificans involved in breaking down pollutants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter is a genus of Gram-negative bacteria known for its adaptability and potential in pollutant degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter is a genus of bacteria known for its robust enzymatic activity and historical efficacy in xenobiotic degradation, making it a subject of interest in studies focused on polystyrene biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter is another bacterial genus found in the T. molitor larval gut."&lt;SEP&gt;"Achromobacter is a genus that exhibits fluctuating relative abundance in mealworm gut microbiome depending on the diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-aa64e5cd2c08637934509262e56ab363&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64e35cb810117768309e5f4db5d7e6b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2961e2aad708e7b78a585260097b4d2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f2fd4b2e978e6745e0dde602114a826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA DSM 50071&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bacterial strain from the intestine of the superworm Zophobas atratus, capable of degrading polystyrene and affecting gene expression levels for digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Involved in a microbiome study, associated with Zophobas atratus."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterial strain involved in the decomposition of polystyrene through physical and chemical processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pseudomonas aeruginosa DSM 50071 is a bacterium isolated from the intestine of the superworm (Zophobas atratus) and capable of PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-939f85d52d9301668deab282264e5fd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dafca19c0156f11127587dba3673503&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c808bc1867233246c6ad41186de9452</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MASSILIA SP. FS1903&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Massilia sp. FS1903 is a bacterial strain contributing to the breakdown of polystyrene, aiding bioremediation efforts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5c6c5d405260e0a6f512a9504195dc0d</data>
    </node>
