    <node id="&quot;ECOTOXICOLOGICAL EFFECTS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-5073433ee060ce75e140a6626619108b</data>
      <data key="d1">"Microplastics are known to have adverse ecotoxicological effects on aquatic food webs, impacting organisms from primary producers to humans."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HUMANS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-918d0788c95399a6714bd40c688594de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3569d74f8a4c9d0851777a5a66284a00</data>
      <data key="d1">"Humans are affected by plastic pollution through indirect consumption of plastics via the food chain and exposure to environmental pollutants."&lt;SEP&gt;"Humans are affected by the presence of microplastics in the food web, raising concerns about health and safety."</data>
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EXTRUDED POLYSTYRENE (XPS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is a type of Styrofoam used in the study that exhibits higher density and compressive strength compared to EPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is another type of polystyrene foam used in the study, noted for its higher density and compressive strength compared to EPS, and sourced from food-trays."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is used for manufacturing items like coffee cups and food trays."&lt;SEP&gt;"XPS is a type of polystyrene foam often used in food packaging and insulation, distinguishable by its density and properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extruded Polystyrene (XPS) is another form of polystyrene tested as a diet type, influencing larvae's growth and nutritional metrics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Extruded polystyrene is a type of atactic polystyrene used for products like food containers and cups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a170da66288c73388872b8a43fa51e33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a00d67f63d22d1959c8a565b6a86d22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-457d054c08a16428ed81526fc2ddce41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38e212e8bab88c4d7b6d63a157e27c00</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;YEASTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRIENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Yeasts are single-celled fungi that can exist within insects as endosymbionts, facilitating nutritional and metabolic processes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yeasts are used as the standard diet for mealworms in the experiment, serving as a baseline for comparing the effects of Styrofoam feeds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Yeasts here refer to fermented cassava yeasts used as a standard diet for feeding the mealworm larvae in the control group of the study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE FOAM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene Foam is a plastic material, typically used for packaging, that presents environmental challenges due to its persistence and low recycling rate."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam refers to the general category of materials including both EPS and XPS, utilized in this study to explore their effects on mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is a type of feed with almost zero nutritional value, leading to high mortality in mealworms."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polystyrene foam is used as feed, resulting in lower nutritional yields of fatty acids in mealworm larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a660790845c6a68ed38b428a7756fc84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5b6629e8c030ab3162553d83aa6eca4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ECOSYSTEM IMPACT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ECOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Styrofoam degradation by mealworms is considered for its potential ecological impact, reducing plastic waste and protecting wildlife from ingestion hazards."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SURVIVAL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSIOLOGICAL RESPONSE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Survival in mealworms is linked to their ability to utilize stored energy from lipids and carbohydrates during starvation and when processing plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"The survival of mealworm larvae feeding solely on Styrofoam was examined to assess its viability as a diet alternative."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cc88563632a3abf926bf4e4ac16973fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;REPRODUCTIVE PARAMETERS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The reproductive parameters of mealworms, such as egg-laying, were measured to determine the impact of Styrofoam as a feed source."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GROWTH AND DEVELOPMENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Growth and development refer to the changes in size and maturity of mealworms under various feeding conditions, including Styrofoam."&lt;SEP&gt;"Growth and development refer to the changes in size, weight, and form experienced by mealworm larvae through various life stages."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FECUNDITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fecundity in the context of this study refers to the reproductive capacity of adult beetles, assessed by the number of eggs produced."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fecundity is the ability to produce offspring, assessed in mealworms to evaluate the impact of different diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fecundity refers to the ability to produce an abundance of offspring or new growth, important in assessing the reproductive success of Tenebrio molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"Fecundity refers to the reproductive capacity of an organism or how many offspring it can produce."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d6a80044bea074bdbcb68f489aa8af0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3cec3addc1bee202783dff1583ba54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697bf99ecf19f99243f3b5a4ef5b334c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e540abd667582c77f4d2222836147ac</data>
    </node>
