      <data key="d2">chunk-af5eb6a8326b4977801e2f7694a2c750</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;A. GRISELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A. grisella larvae are known for their ability to eat and digest beeswax, with potential to degrade polyethylene due to the presence of PE-degrading gut bacteria."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WC (WAXCOMB)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Waxcomb is the normal feed for lesser waxworms, composed of beeswax with esters, long-chain alcohols, and fatty acids."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-ab6b219720db5a6e3384c884c280984c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LESSER WAXWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Lesser waxworms are larvae that can consume polyethylene (PE) and potentially degrade it. They are used in research to study PE biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Lesser waxworms are used in this study as a model organism to examine the biodegradation of polyethylene (PE) through enzyme synthesis and nutrient assimilation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b25215becbc007a60f24b86a2d6aaf26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bd8cf0d5102bbca724e0773be0eeae</data>
