      <data key="d1">"Fungal isolates are single colony or strain of fungi isolated for research purposes, such as studying PVC degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9bc3253e8d41785f994d1c6fcc2b6c47</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLESIOPHTHALMUS DAVIDIS LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These larvae utilize their gut microbial flora to facilitate rapid degradation of polystyrene."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MOLECULAR CHARACTERIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molecular characterization involves identifying molecular components and mechanisms, especially relevant in studying organisms that degrade plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYVINYL CHLORIDE DEGRADING FUNGAL ISOLATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Certain fungi capable of breaking down polyvinyl chloride, contributing to research on reducing plastic waste."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-f15cc70e99a3287ced1a6cbb6c8d76a4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYNTHETIC POLYMER FILMS&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-63813cc5cb4812528045bd8f1ced4313</data>
      <data key="d1">"Trichoderma hamatum attacks petroleum-based synthetic polymer films, facilitating their degradation."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;KOCURIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A Gram-positive bacterial genus over-represented in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kocuria is a bacterial genus identified as part of the gut microbiota in polystyrene-fed Alphitobius diaperinus, potentially involved in plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kocuria is a genus of bacteria in the Actinobacteria phylum, found to have notable differential abundance in PS samples."&lt;SEP&gt;"Kocuria is a genus of bacteria significantly overrepresented in the gut of polystyrene-fed larvae, although not previously associated with plastic consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a1070f8c33eb8afcfcffac95df9012d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b4b45d826a6a7956015e680f5fb8ee6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYPHODERMELLA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hyphodermella is a fungal genus identified in the gut microbiota of polystyrene-fed Alphitobius diaperinus, potentially linked to plastic degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hyphodermella is a genus found within Agaricomycetes, present in the PS samples with significant differential abundance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hyphodermella is a genus included in the studies, although there is no current association with plastic breakdown."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-d9095195e9cf06b9887c03abde6284d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6ddf9dd30d7258964408f9a915d3478&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYNTHETIC DEBRIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Synthetic debris includes synthetic materials like plastics which accumulate in the environment, causing pollution and health risks to living organisms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-001e8f54368c9a236f702fd09cddf4a8</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;INSECT GUT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL FEATURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The insect gut serves as a habitat for diverse microbial communities involved in processes such as biodegradation of synthetic polymers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALPHITOBIUS DIAPERINUS LARVAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LIFE STAGE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"This refers to the last instar larval stage of Alphitobius diaperinus, specifically studied for polystyrene consumption."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AGRIPET GARDEN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Agripet Garden is the source from which Alphitobius diaperinus larvae were obtained for experimental studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYSTYRENE FRAGMENTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL CHARACTERISTIC"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polystyrene fragments are residues left post-consumption, analyzed to assess the degree of biodegradation by larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COMMERCIAL ORGANIC CARROTS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Commercial organic carrots serve as a combined source of energy and water for the control group of Alphitobius diaperinus larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-56af57b5211422531c03b0cbcfded1b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;UNIVERSITY OF SIENA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"University of Siena is an academic institution that provided partial support for the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"University of Siena's Department of Life Sciences provided the controlled environment for rearing the larvae during the experiment."</data>
