      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
      <data key="d1">"Molecular markers are used to identify species of gregarious isolates from mealworms and super worms."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SYMBIOTES&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
      <data key="d1">"These genes are part of the sterol biosynthesis pathway in symbiotic yeast-like organisms found in certain insects."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CELLULOSE, POLYETHYLENE AND POLYSTYRENE WASTE&quot;">
      <data key="d2">chunk-767a2e5a2bde762f9c1b3dbbf7746d09</data>
      <data key="d1">"The enzymes and organisms within the gut microbiome of Tenebrio molitor aid in the degradation of these waste materials."</data>
      <data key="d0">"UNKNOWN"</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT FUNGI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROORGANISMS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut fungi refer to the fungal communities present in the larvae's gut. These fungi, such as Rhodotorula and Cryptococcus, are involved in the degradation of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ETHYLENE VINYL ACETATE (EVA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EVA is another type of plastic being studied, noted for its distinctive biodegradation profile in the larval gut and associated with specific changes in gut microbial community."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA) is a copolymer used in the study to observe its adverse effects on larval survival rates and gut microbiome shifts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIROPLASMA, ACINETOBACTER, PSEUDOMONAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are bacterial genera found in the gut of Tenebrio molitor larvae, with varying abundances depending on the type of plastic diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOTORULA, CRYPTOCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These fungal genera are dominant in the gut of plastics-fed Tenebrio molitor larvae and have been previously associated with plastics degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ASPERGILLUS, CLADOSPORIUM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"These are fungi found in plastics-fed groups of larvae, known for their ability to degrade various types of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC POLYMERIZATION TYPES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic polymerization types refer to the various structural forms of plastics, such as PP, PU, and EVA, which influence biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYOLEFIN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of polymer that influences the composition of gut bacterial communities, particularly distinguished from polyester and copolymer-fed groups."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyolefin is a type of polymer, represented by polypropylene (PP) in the study, used to evaluate its effects on microbial gut communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Polyolefin refers to a category of polymers including PP that undergo biodegradation in scientific studies."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COPOLYMER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Complex polymers impacting the bacterial and fungal community structure of yellow mealworms compared to other plastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Copolymer is a polymer derived from two different monomer species, represented by ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA) in this study."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9d7500fe4985cb2b885f9eb244ed3c2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ETHYLENE-VINYL ACETATE (EVA)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Ethylene-Vinyl Acetate is a type of foam plastic tested as a diet for yellow mealworms, noted for its potential toxicity and adverse effects on survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ethylene-vinyl acetate is a complex structured plastic biodegraded by yellow mealworms, significantly affecting their larval weight and survival."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT MICROBIOME ANALYSIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Gut Microbiome Analysis involves assessing the microbial community in the mealworm's gut to understand its role in plastic biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WEIGHT CHANGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Weight Changes refer to the difference in larval weight recorded throughout the experiment, highlighting the nutritional impact of various diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRITIONAL CONDITIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NUTRITIONAL COMPONENT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nutritional Conditions describe the dietary and environmental factors affecting mealworm growth, such as plastic or bran diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-444b8acd02a76616457012e1ac0db23b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LARVAL WEIGHT CHANGE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"HEALTH MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The change in larval weight observed in yellow mealworms when consuming different diets, including plastics, indicating the nutritional impact of their diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LIFE ACTIVITIES OF YELLOW MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The daily biological processes and actions of yellow mealworms, which are influenced by their diet, including the consumption of plastics."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CHAO, SHANNON, AND SIMPSON INDICES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Statistical measures used to estimate bacterial species richness and diversity in the gut microbiome of plastic-fed yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COMPLEX STRUCTURAL PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastics with intricate chemical structures requiring a diverse microbial community for effective biodegradation in mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;THERMAL STABILITY CHANGES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PHYSICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alterations in the thermal stability of plastics observed in DSC-TGA analysis after passing through yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7d0e4cb4324bc684ebb2ca6136c04ad0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MORAXELLACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Moraxellaceae is a family within the class Gammaproteobacteria."&lt;SEP&gt;"Moraxellaceae is observed to increase significantly in PP-fed groups, indicating a specialized role in PP degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Moraxellaceae sees a notable increase in PP-fed groups, suggesting its specific relationship with that diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Moraxellaceae is one of the bacterial families classified under Gammaproteobacteria in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"One of the dominant families in mealworm intestines during aerobic EPS enrichment culture, contributing to degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-eadba0dcfe8eff9c8040ec5f17e1adb3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bfd05eac1abc28ac416e6a56ccd6a9d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10f59d5d10f08e8107f36bff5a4c93ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PORPHYROMONADACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Porphyromonadaceae increases notably in EVA-fed groups, suggesting a significant role in the microbial degradation of EVA."&lt;SEP&gt;"Porphyromonadaceae's abundance increases in EVA-fed groups, suggesting a role in EVA foam degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PEDICOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Pedicoccus disappears from groups other than the bran-fed, indicating a specific association with bran diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"Pedicoccus is a genus noted for its presence in bran-fed groups, indicating a potential preference for non-plastic substrates."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PLASTIC SOLE DIETS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"DIET"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Plastic Sole Diets in the study include PU, EVA, and PP, used to observe their effects on gut microbiota."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPIRULINA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Spirulina is a notable genus dominant in PP-fed groups, highlighting a specialized adaptation to PP environments."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-476e13b69587aa4e81395e2fd00a9d5a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EVA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"EVA is presumably a product mentioned alongside PP and PU, although no additional context is provided."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7043eb8aa4221f396a5c65fb6493bd41</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DYGONOMONAS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dygonomonas is a dominant genus in EVA-fed groups, indicating a specific role in EVA degradation, with increased dominance compared to the bran-fed group."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MORTIERELLOMYCOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mortierellomycota is one of the three predominant fungal phyla obtained in the gut-fungal microbiome studies of yellow mealworms with plastics diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DEBAROMYCESACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Debaromycesaceae is the dominant family in bran-fed groups but has less than 1% abundance in plastics-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TREMELLACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tremellaceae is a dominant family in plastics-fed groups, with significant differences in its abundance among the various dietary groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AUREOBASIDIACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aureobasidiaceae is dominant in PU-fed groups, indicating its role in the fungal community associated with this diet."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPOROBACTERIACEAE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FAMILY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Sporobacteriaceae is dominant in EVA-fed groups, increasing significantly in abundance compared to bran-fed groups."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PU DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The decomposition of polyurethane (PU) by specific gut bacteria in yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;EVA DEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The process by which ethylene vinyl acetate (EVA) is degraded in the gut microbiome of yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT BACTERIAL COMMUNITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The populations of bacteria residing in the intestines of yellow mealworms, which vary with different plastic diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;GUT FUNGAL COMMUNITIES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The populations of fungi in the intestines of yellow mealworms that change significantly with different plastic-based diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNGAL DIVERSITY ESTIMATORS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Tools like OTUs, Chao, Shannon, and Simpson indices used to evaluate diversity within gut fungal communities."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COVERAGE INDEX&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A measure in ecology used to estimate what proportion of the total species in a community have been sampled."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYPHOPICHA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"GENUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A dominant fungal genus in bran-fed yellow mealworms, associated with high carbohydrate metabolism efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;RHODOTORULA AND CRYPTOCOCCUS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specific groups of fungi associated with plastic degradation and prevalent in plastic-fed groups, especially effective in PE and PS degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-a3f07da33b55bf8a25fe06beee23e90e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYESTER PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyester plastics are composed of polyisocyanates and polyols, containing C=O bonds and nitrogen, leading to increased fungal enrichment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYMERIZED PLASTICS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polymerized plastics refer to plastics like PP, PU, and EVA, which can be biodegraded by organisms like yellow mealworms."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYISOCYANATES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyisocyanates are components of polyester plastics composed of nitrogen and C=O bonds."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYOLS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL COMPOUND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyols are chemical components of polyester plastics involved in their structure and composition."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNGAL COMMUNITY RICHNESS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Fungal community richness refers to the abundance and diversity of fungi present in the gut microbiome under different diets."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CORE GUT FUNCTIONAL MICROBIOME&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBIOME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The core gut functional microbiome refers to the essential microorganisms in the gut of yellow mealworms involved in breaking down various plastic types."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3e34d58a20a707b35e8fcd5658ba2924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;CLADOSPORIUM SP. P7&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNGUS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Cladosporium sp. P7 is a fungal species involved in the biodegradation of polyester polyurethane, altering metabolic pathways."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AMIDEASE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ENZYME"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Amidease, an enzyme used in combination with esterase for the enzymatic recycling of thermoplastic polyurethanes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ALCYCLIPHILUS SP.BQB&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BACTERIUM"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Alcycliphilus sp.BQB is a bacterial strain involved in the biodegradation of commercial-type polyester polyurethanes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;J. FUNGI&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"J. Fungi refers to a publication that includes reviews and studies about fungi involved in plastic degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-03f2fc4d98412efb8a792474cc47e558</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SUPERWORMS (ZOPHOBAS ARATUS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Superworms are a species of larvae known to biodegrade polypropylene through a gut-microbe-dependent depolymerization process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DARK MEALWORMS (LARVAE OF TENEBRIO OBSCURUS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"MICROBES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Dark mealworms are larvae that can biodegrade low-density polyethylene through a gut microbe-independent depolymerization process."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PHYSICAL-CHEMICAL PROPERTY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Physical-chemical properties refer to the characteristics of substances like polyethylene that can influence its degradation process in various environmental contexts."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-1ac276660dd76d0ef97a8a0b1a59664c</data>
    </node>
