      <data key="d2">chunk-d7f79551ab9793788cd662ab66bca5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NUTRITIONAL LIMITATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A condition leading to increased cannibalism behavior in larvae, impacting their feeding patterns."&lt;SEP&gt;"Nutritional limitation refers to the lack of essential nutrients in the diet, resulting in decreased larval survival and increased cannibalism."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENERGY SOURCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Energy source refers to the means by which larvae sustain their life functions, inadequately provided by PP as a sole diet."&lt;SEP&gt;"Energy source refers to the origin of metabolic energy for organisms, such as mealworms, vital for their growth and survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Energy sources for insects during starvation include triglycerides and proteins, which are metabolized for survival."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-91831340731171413dc4c412a325e034&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a9d70651c9f13362e4c1e3ce10eab0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd3092744be0c66dad5833977dedd913</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPPC-W&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOCHEMICAL MEASURE"</data>
      <data key="d1">"SPPC-W, or Specific Polypropylene Consumption per Weight, measures larvae's consumption of PP relative to their weight."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PENG ET AL., 2020A&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL CONCEPT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Research focused on depolymerization patterns for plastics such as PP and LDPE in Z. atratus larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b2d6bcd4d2cc81a978694e417e76747e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LDPE BIODEGRADATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL PROCESS"</data>
      <data key="d1">"LDPE biodegradation refers to the process by which microorganisms such as larvae of T. molitor break down low-density polyethylene polymers through depolymerization, resulting in chemical changes to the polymer's structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ENZYMATIC ATTACK ON C-C BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"BIOLOGICAL FUNCTION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Enzymatic attack on C-C bonds involves enzymes breaking down carbon-carbon bonds within polymer chains, a crucial step in the biodegradation process of polymers such as PP and LDPE."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;MN AND MW&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL PROPERTY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Mn (number average molecular weight) and Mw (weight average molecular weight) are measures of molecular weight changes in PP, indicative of polymer degradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C-C BONDS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C-C bonds are carbon-carbon bonds in polymer chains, which are targets for enzymatic attack during biodegradation processes."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-51f749234fed052c982d79f81b93cc29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–H ALKYL BEND&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–H alkyl bend is a type of chemical bond identified in PP foam, which disappears in frass samples due to reactions."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;AROMATIC STRUCTURES C–H RING&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Aromatic structures C–H ring refers to chemical bond structures identified in PP foam and are affected during biodegradation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-C=O CARBONYL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-C=O carbonyl is a functional group formed in frass samples indicating oxidation."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;-C-O STRETCH&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"-C-O stretch is a functional group formed in frass samples, contributing to changes in chemical properties."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HYDROXYL BAND O–H&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Hydroxyl band O–H suggests a change from hydrophobic to hydrophilic properties in the frass samples."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;POLYOLEFIN PLASTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Polyolefin plastic is the type of plastic undergoing biodegradation by the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C–O GROUPS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"FUNCTIONAL GROUP"</data>
      <data key="d1">"C–O groups are functional groups formed during biodegradation, indicating chemical changes in polymer structure."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-cd90c8f9754a78aabdbd7053e1adf90b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;C=C - H (ALKENE BONDS)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"CHEMICAL BOND"</data>
