    <edge source="&quot;TENEBRIO MOLITOR&quot;" target="&quot;POLYVINYL CHLORIDE (PVC)&quot;">
      <data key="d3">72.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms have been found to degrade polyvinyl chloride, though less is known about the mechanisms compared to PS and PE."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor employs its larvae to biodegrade polyvinyl chloride (PVC), highlighting its potential in waste reduction strategies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor is involved in the biodegradation of PVC, fostering environmental sustainability."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae are being studied for their potential to biodegrade PVC."&lt;SEP&gt;"PVC shows higher toxicity as compared to PLA and PS, impacting the physiological health of Tenebrio molitor larvae."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae have been shown to biodegrade PVC, highlighting their role in addressing plastic waste problems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Tenebrio molitor larvae have been shown to biodegrade PVC, offering potential for bioremediation strategies."&lt;SEP&gt;"Research on Tenebrio molitor demonstrates its capability to biodegrade PVC, contributing to sustainable waste management approaches."&lt;SEP&gt;"The larvae ingested PVC, facilitating research into biodegradation and biochemical impact of this synthetic polymer."</data>
      <data key="d5">"biodegradation, alternative waste management"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, consumption"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, environmental impact"&lt;SEP&gt;"biodegradation, plastic interaction"&lt;SEP&gt;"insect biodegradation, waste management"&lt;SEP&gt;"sustainability, environmental innovation"&lt;SEP&gt;"toxicity, physiological impact"</data>
