    <edge source="&quot;POLYSTYRENE (PS)&quot;" target="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">97.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Mealworms are capable of degrading polystyrene, a process that challenges PS's non-biodegradable status due to high molecular weight and stable structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and biodegrade polystyrene, converting it into CO2, depolymerized metabolites, and biomass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms have the ability to digest and metabolize polystyrene, transforming it into CO2 and biomass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms were fed with raw polystyrene to observe mass loss and biochemical changes, indicating that PS does not support development."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of degrading polystyrene, evidenced by changes in their gut microbial communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of ingesting and biodegrading polystyrene within their gut, converting it into CO2 and other compounds."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume significant amounts of polystyrene, indicating their potential role in the degradation and reduction of this plastic's environmental impact."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and degrade various forms of polystyrene, showing different rates depending on the density of the product."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms were fed with PS foams to study the degradation activity of their gut bacteria on PS."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms ingest and biodegrade polystyrene (PS) foams, leading to fragmentation into microplastics."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and degrade polystyrene, transforming a certain percentage into CO2 and excreting the rest."</data>
