    <edge source="&quot;T. MOLITOR&quot;" target="&quot;LDPE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">130.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"LDPE is a diet fed to T. molitor larvae affecting their gut microbiome diversity and composition."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is demonstrated to be effective at biodegrading LDPE, showing its importance in plastic breakdown solutions."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is fed LDPE to study its oxidative degradation capability as part of their physiological response."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is involved in biodegrading LDPE, utilizing alterations in its gut microbiome to survive on plastic diets."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are fed with LDPE to test the biodegradation of this type of polyethylene."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are used to test biodegradation of LDPE plastic foam, similar to their use with EPS."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae consume and biodegrade LDPE as one of the studied polyethylene polymers."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is involved in the biodegradation of LDPE, contributing to its breakdown."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor is shown to biodegrade LDPE more efficiently than HDPE, indicating a higher microbial degradation capability."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae consumed LDPE microplastics during the study to evaluate their degradation ability."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae significantly depolymerize and biodegrade LDPE, reducing its molecular weights across all chains."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are capable of degrading LDPE polymers, demonstrating their potential in plastic waste management."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are capable of depolymerizing LDPE, showing that this process can occur independently of gut microbes."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae are capable of digesting LDPE, converting a portion into CO2, and assimilating it into biomass, demonstrating biodegradation capabilities."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. molitor larvae have been shown to biodegrade LDPE, contributing to the breakdown of plastic pollutants."</data>
