    <edge source="&quot;MEALWORMS&quot;" target="&quot;PS (POLYSTYRENE)&quot;">
      <data key="d3">106.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Different mealworms metabolize PS differently, impacting the chemical composition and degradation stages."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume PS as part of their diet, which provides them with partial energy and carbon for maintenance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms were fed PS as part of a study to determine its impact on their survival, growth, and ability to depolymerize and degrade the polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms were fed with PS to study its mass loss due to ingestion in various regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are used to consume PS achieving conversion into CO₂ and biomass over various incubation periods."&lt;SEP&gt;"PS is one type of plastic feed given to mealworms to study consumption and gut microbiome changes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms are capable of consuming and degrading PS, highlighting their potential role in plastic biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms showed the highest rate of consumption for Polystyrene, indicating it as their most favored plastic type in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and biodegrade PS, contributing to its depolymerization over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms consume and degrade PS, illustrating a biological process where the polymer is broken down during digestion."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms were used to test the biodegradability of PS through consumption and conversion to frass."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mealworms ingested and degraded PS foams, as indicated by changes in frass composition and mass balance data."</data>
