    <edge source="&quot;LDPE&quot;" target="&quot;T. OBSCURUS&quot;">
      <data key="d3">102.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Similar to T. molitor, T. obscurus larvae can degrade LDPE, highlighting a shared biodegradation capability."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus contributes to the process of breaking down LDPE through biodegradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus demonstrates a more effective degradation capacity with LDPE compared to HDPE, similar to T. molitor."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae are also fed LDPE to observe its degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae are shown to biodegrade LDPE, contributing to its breakdown in the study."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae biodegrade LDPE effectively, leading to distinct oxidative stress and physiological responses."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae ingest LDPE, leading to significant depolymerization and biodegradation of the polymer."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae show a similar pattern as T. molitor, with significant depolymerization and biodegradation of LDPE."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae were included in the study, and fed LDPE to study degradation patterns."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae also play a role in the depolymerization of LDPE, contributing to the insight that gut microbes are not solely responsible."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus larvae are responsible for biodegrading LDPE, as part of the study's findings."&lt;SEP&gt;"T. obscurus performs similar biodegradation of LDPE as T. molitor, converting a portion of LDPE into CO2, which highlights its potential for plastic waste management."</data>
